[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.a4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/3ff4f3cd-c7a8-4750-87bc-967620b1c959/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course on the 3...a6 Queen’s Gambit Declined! In this first chapter we’ll explore what happens when White allows us to capture on c4 with 4...dxc4. As you’ll see, our move 3...a6 carries real venom, and if White isn’t precise, not even 5.a4 will help them recover the pawn. White begins with 1.d4, a solid move supported by the queen that prepares solid development. We respond symmetrically with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {Many players now go for the Queen’s Gambit. This challenges our central control, but instead of taking on c4, we support our own center. We play 2...e6, defending the d5-pawn with another pawn in true QGD style.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White often develops the queenside knight to c3, increasing pressure on d5. Now comes our key move: 3...a6. This multipurpose move fights for b5 control, prevents a white piece from landing there, and prepares ...b5. Combined with 4...dxc4, 3...a6 can even become a real threat to hold on to the c-pawn.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {Most players are taught that the Queen's Gambit isn't a true gambit: Black cannot win and hold on to the c-pawn. But this isn't the case in the 3...a6 QGD! A simple, developing move like 4. Nf3, while a natural continuation that many players choose to go for, actually allows us to take on c4. Thanks to ...a6, we can capture on c4, posing difficult problems for White.} 4...dxc4 5.a4 {The first variation of this chapter arises after 5.a4, aiming to prevent ...b5. But I’ve prepared a way to complicate matters for White despite this move. We continue with 5...Nc6, intending to jump to a5, where the knight defends the c4-pawn and eyes b3.} 5...Nc6 6.Bg5 {6.Bg5 is the third most popular option. It attacks our queen, but we can calmly block and develop with 6...Be7, proposing an exchange and neutralizing the threat.} 6...Be7 7.Bxe7 {White accepts the trade. Our development speeds up after 7...Qxe7. Our queen gains freedom on the dark squares, and without White’s dark-squared bishop, it’s much harder for them to exploit any dark-square weaknesses.} 7...Qxe7 8.e3 {When White opens an attack on c4 with 8.e3... Our queen, already on e7, can quickly jump to b4, defending the c4-pawn while pressuring b2.} 8...Qb4 9.Qc2 {9.Qc2 defends b2 and places the queen on a very common square in the QGD, where it usually works with Bd3 to form a battery. But with our pawn on c4, White can’t play Bd3 yet. We continue simple development with ...Nf6. There’s no need for ...Na5 immediately; we can improve our kingside first and prepare to castle.} 9...Nf6 10.Be2 {White develops their kingside bishop and gets ready to castle. Now we play ...Na5, aiming at the b3-square and clearing the path for our c-pawn.} 10...Na5 11.O-O {When White castles...} 11...O-O {We can castle kingside ourselves and play ...c5 next.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.a4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/3ff4f3cd-c7a8-4750-87bc-967620b1c959/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course on the 3...a6 Queen’s Gambit Declined! In this first chapter we’ll explore what happens when White allows us to capture on c4 with 4...dxc4. As you’ll see, our move 3...a6 carries real venom, and if White isn’t precise, not even 5.a4 will help them recover the pawn. White begins with 1.d4, a solid move supported by the queen that prepares solid development. We respond symmetrically with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {Many players now go for the Queen’s Gambit. This challenges our central control, but instead of taking on c4, we support our own center. We play 2...e6, defending the d5-pawn with another pawn in true QGD style.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White often develops the queenside knight to c3, increasing pressure on d5. Now comes our key move: 3...a6. This multipurpose move fights for b5 control, prevents a white piece from landing there, and prepares ...b5. Combined with 4...dxc4, 3...a6 can even become a real threat to hold on to the c-pawn.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {Most players are taught that the Queen's Gambit isn't a true gambit: Black cannot win and hold on to the c-pawn. But this isn't the case in the 3...a6 QGD! A simple, developing move like 4. Nf3, while a natural continuation that many players choose to go for, actually allows us to take on c4. Thanks to ...a6, we can capture on c4, posing difficult problems for White.} 4...dxc4 5.a4 {The first variation of this chapter arises after 5.a4, aiming to prevent ...b5. But I’ve prepared a way to complicate matters for White despite this move. We continue with 5...Nc6, intending to jump to a5, where the knight defends the c4-pawn and eyes b3.} 5...Nc6 6.e3 {6.e3 is the second most popular move, and it also opens an attack on c4. We can continue with our usual idea: 6...Na5, defending the c4-pawn with the knight.} 6...Na5 7.Ne5 {If White adds another attacker with 7.Ne5... We simply ignore the threat to c4 and play 7...Nf6. It’s actually good for us that White had to twist their pieces around with Nf3–Ne5–Nxc4; that lost time won’t come back.} 7...Nf6 8.Nxc4 {White recaptures. We follow with 8...c5 - one of the core points behind 6...Na5 - freeing the c-pawn.} 8...c5 9.Nxa5 {White exchanges, the most popular choice. This lets us recapture and develop our queen at the same time, pinning the knight.} 9...Qxa5 10.Bd2 {The bishop comes to d2, lining up with our queen. But if we take on d4, White has no useful discovered attacks, so we simply capture in the center.} 10...cxd4 11.exd4 {White recaptures, leaving themselves with an isolated queen’s pawn, a structural weakness in endgames. Now we develop our bishop to b4. This both shields our queen from any discoveries and ensures we’ll trade pieces if the knight moves. When playing against an IQP, trades generally favor the side attacking it.} 11...Bb4 12.Be2 {When White prepares castling...} 12...O-O {We can castle. In the next moves, you can bring your rook to d8 to attack d4, play ...Bd7 and ...Bc6.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.a4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/3ff4f3cd-c7a8-4750-87bc-967620b1c959/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course on the 3...a6 Queen’s Gambit Declined! In this first chapter we’ll explore what happens when White allows us to capture on c4 with 4...dxc4. As you’ll see, our move 3...a6 carries real venom, and if White isn’t precise, not even 5.a4 will help them recover the pawn. White begins with 1.d4, a solid move supported by the queen that prepares solid development. We respond symmetrically with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {Many players now go for the Queen’s Gambit. This challenges our central control, but instead of taking on c4, we support our own center. We play 2...e6, defending the d5-pawn with another pawn in true QGD style.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White often develops the queenside knight to c3, increasing pressure on d5. Now comes our key move: 3...a6. This multipurpose move fights for b5 control, prevents a white piece from landing there, and prepares ...b5. Combined with 4...dxc4, 3...a6 can even become a real threat to hold on to the c-pawn.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {Most players are taught that the Queen's Gambit isn't a true gambit: Black cannot win and hold on to the c-pawn. But this isn't the case in the 3...a6 QGD! A simple, developing move like 4. Nf3, while a natural continuation that many players choose to go for, actually allows us to take on c4. Thanks to ...a6, we can capture on c4, posing difficult problems for White.} 4...dxc4 5.a4 {The first variation of this chapter arises after 5.a4, aiming to prevent ...b5. But I’ve prepared a way to complicate matters for White despite this move. We continue with 5...Nc6, intending to jump to a5, where the knight defends the c4-pawn and eyes b3.} 5...Nc6 6.e4 {Now 6.e4 is the most popular attempt, opening a direct attack on our c4-pawn with the f1-bishop. Here we reveal the point of our setup: 6...Na5!, defending c4 and forcing White to work hard to recover the pawn.} 6...Na5 7.Be2 {Some players choose simple development with 7.Be2. You might see this even from opponents who aren’t sure of the plan. If they do know the idea, they’ll try to reroute the bishop to f3, but that requires preparing the knight move with Be3 first. In the meantime, we calmly develop with 7...Nf6.} 7...Nf6 8.O-O {White castles. We also prepare to castle with 8...Be7.} 8...Be7 9.Be3 {As mentioned, 9.Be3 is the only real plan to recapture the pawn. White first defends the d4-pawn, then aims for Nd2 to attack c4. Since Nd2 is coming, we play 9...c6 with the idea of following up with ...b5 to secure our c4-pawn.} 9...c6 10.Nd2 {When Nd2 arrives... We’re right on time: 10...b5 defends c4.} 10...b5 11.axb5 {White may choose to exchange their a-pawn, hoping to open the a-file for the rook. Always be careful in these situations, don’t blunder the knight on a5, which is currently defended by our queen. In this position we can simply recapture.} 11...cxb5 12.Bf3 {And if the bishop heads to f3 to attack our a8-rook... We just castle.} 12...O-O 13.e5 {Don’t fear the discovered attack after 13.e5. We can block with the knight on d5. For White to keep pressing, they’d need to dive into some wild Rxa5 ideas. But if they try to break through the center, we’ll emerge with a superior pawn structure, active pieces, and the bishop pair.} 13...Nd5 14.Nxd5 {For illustration - no need to memorize this part - if White takes on d5... We recapture with the e-pawn.} 14...exd5 15.Bxd5 {And if White continues naturally by taking again on d5, restoring material balance... We centralize our queen with the recapture.} 15...Qxd5 16.Rxa5 {And once White captures on a5, we've got many strong ideas. ...f6 to undermine e5, followed by ...Bb4. Or ...Bb4 right away is strong for Black. The bishop pair reigns supreme in this position.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.a4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/3ff4f3cd-c7a8-4750-87bc-967620b1c959/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course on the 3...a6 Queen’s Gambit Declined! In this first chapter we’ll explore what happens when White allows us to capture on c4 with 4...dxc4. As you’ll see, our move 3...a6 carries real venom, and if White isn’t precise, not even 5.a4 will help them recover the pawn. White begins with 1.d4, a solid move supported by the queen that prepares solid development. We respond symmetrically with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {Many players now go for the Queen’s Gambit. This challenges our central control, but instead of taking on c4, we support our own center. We play 2...e6, defending the d5-pawn with another pawn in true QGD style.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White often develops the queenside knight to c3, increasing pressure on d5. Now comes our key move: 3...a6. This multipurpose move fights for b5 control, prevents a white piece from landing there, and prepares ...b5. Combined with 4...dxc4, 3...a6 can even become a real threat to hold on to the c-pawn.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {Most players are taught that the Queen's Gambit isn't a true gambit: Black cannot win and hold on to the c-pawn. But this isn't the case in the 3...a6 QGD! A simple, developing move like 4. Nf3, while a natural continuation that many players choose to go for, actually allows us to take on c4. Thanks to ...a6, we can capture on c4, posing difficult problems for White.} 4...dxc4 5.a4 {The first variation of this chapter arises after 5.a4, aiming to prevent ...b5. But I’ve prepared a way to complicate matters for White despite this move. We continue with 5...Nc6, intending to jump to a5, where the knight defends the c4-pawn and eyes b3.} 5...Nc6 6.e4 {Now 6.e4 is the most popular attempt, opening a direct attack on our c4-pawn with the f1-bishop. Here we reveal the point of our setup: 6...Na5!, defending c4 and forcing White to work hard to recover the pawn.} 6...Na5 7.Be3 {7.Be3 defends the d4-pawn and prepares to maneuver toward the c4-pawn. Against this we have a neat idea starting with 7...Nf6, but it relies on White playing Ne5 and f3 in the next couple of moves.} 7...Nf6 8.Ne5 {If White goes 8.Ne5, adding another attacker to c4, we develop our bishop, pinning the knight on c3. The point is to build enough pressure on e4 that White feels forced to defend with f3.} 8...Bb4 9.f3 {White plays 9.f3, but this weakens the dark squares, making our future ...c5 much stronger. It also removes the natural retreat square for the e5-knight. Thanks to all this, the beautiful 9...Nd5 becomes possible.} 9...Nd5 10.exd5 {White may happily take our knight. We simply recapture, and now c4 is completely consolidated. We’ve traded a knight for two powerful central pawns, that’s our first source of compensation.} 10...exd5 11.Be2 {White continues developing with 10.Be2, preparing to castle. Our second source of compensation is that the f3-square is blocked by a pawn, so 11...f6 hits the knight with greater force.} 11...f6 12.Ng4 {The knight must retreat to g4, where its only idea is to eye f2. It’s a miserable piece, and with our d5/c4 structure we can jump our knight into b3.} 12...Nb3 13.Ra2 {If White places the rook on the “safest” square, they’ll face a problem: the rook on a2 is completely passive. It can’t go to a1 (covered by our knight), nor to a3 (covered by our bishop), and the entire second rank is blocked by its own b2-pawn. We develop our light-squared bishop to e6 to support the whole pawn chain. We need that support because we won’t be playing ...c6, our idea is the thematic ...c5!} 13...Be6 14.O-O {White castles.} 14...c5 {And here comes 14...c5, to finally undermine White's last central pawn. It’s worth analyzing positions after natural moves like Kh1 or Re1; Black’s central control is superb, and in many lines White even has to give back material.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.a4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/3ff4f3cd-c7a8-4750-87bc-967620b1c959/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course on the 3...a6 Queen’s Gambit Declined! In this first chapter we’ll explore what happens when White allows us to capture on c4 with 4...dxc4. As you’ll see, our move 3...a6 carries real venom, and if White isn’t precise, not even 5.a4 will help them recover the pawn. White begins with 1.d4, a solid move supported by the queen that prepares solid development. We respond symmetrically with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {Many players now go for the Queen’s Gambit. This challenges our central control, but instead of taking on c4, we support our own center. We play 2...e6, defending the d5-pawn with another pawn in true QGD style.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White often develops the queenside knight to c3, increasing pressure on d5. Now comes our key move: 3...a6. This multipurpose move fights for b5 control, prevents a white piece from landing there, and prepares ...b5. Combined with 4...dxc4, 3...a6 can even become a real threat to hold on to the c-pawn.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {Most players are taught that the Queen's Gambit isn't a true gambit: Black cannot win and hold on to the c-pawn. But this isn't the case in the 3...a6 QGD! A simple, developing move like 4. Nf3, while a natural continuation that many players choose to go for, actually allows us to take on c4. Thanks to ...a6, we can capture on c4, posing difficult problems for White.} 4...dxc4 5.a4 {The first variation of this chapter arises after 5.a4, aiming to prevent ...b5. But I’ve prepared a way to complicate matters for White despite this move. We continue with 5...Nc6, intending to jump to a5, where the knight defends the c4-pawn and eyes b3.} 5...Nc6 6.e3 {6.e3 is the second most popular move, and it also opens an attack on c4. We can continue with our usual idea: 6...Na5, defending the c4-pawn with the knight.} 6...Na5 7.Be2 {Simple development with 7.Be2, aiming for quick castling, is also an option for White. We continue naturally with 7...Nf6.} 7...Nf6 8.O-O {White castles. Now we strike with our strong ...c5 pawn break, undermining White’s central control.} 8...c5 9.dxc5 {Some players can’t handle the tension and release it by taking on c5. Here we play 9...Bd7 to avoid a queen trade. It’s actually White who feels cramped because of our c4-pawn, so we shouldn’t give them an easy way out.} 9...Bd7 10.Ne5 {If the knight jumps to e5, attacking our bishop and the c4-pawn... We simply develop our queen to c7, preparing ...Bxc5 and clearing d8 for one of the rooks.} 10...Qc7 11.Nxc4 {When the knight eventually takes on c4, this shouldn’t concern us. White had to go Nf3–Ne5–Nxc4 to recover the pawn. We capture on c4, trading knights.} 11...Nxc4 12.Bxc4 {White recaptures, and this trade conveniently places the bishop on c4 where it’s undefended.} 12...Bxc5 {So after ...Bxc5, our queen indirectly sees the bishop on c4. We'll continue with moves like ...Bc6, 0-0 and Rfd8.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.a4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/3ff4f3cd-c7a8-4750-87bc-967620b1c959/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course on the 3...a6 Queen’s Gambit Declined! In this first chapter we’ll explore what happens when White allows us to capture on c4 with 4...dxc4. As you’ll see, our move 3...a6 carries real venom, and if White isn’t precise, not even 5.a4 will help them recover the pawn. White begins with 1.d4, a solid move supported by the queen that prepares solid development. We respond symmetrically with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {Many players now go for the Queen’s Gambit. This challenges our central control, but instead of taking on c4, we support our own center. We play 2...e6, defending the d5-pawn with another pawn in true QGD style.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White often develops the queenside knight to c3, increasing pressure on d5. Now comes our key move: 3...a6. This multipurpose move fights for b5 control, prevents a white piece from landing there, and prepares ...b5. Combined with 4...dxc4, 3...a6 can even become a real threat to hold on to the c-pawn.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {Most players are taught that the Queen's Gambit isn't a true gambit: Black cannot win and hold on to the c-pawn. But this isn't the case in the 3...a6 QGD! A simple, developing move like 4. Nf3, while a natural continuation that many players choose to go for, actually allows us to take on c4. Thanks to ...a6, we can capture on c4, posing difficult problems for White.} 4...dxc4 5.a4 {The first variation of this chapter arises after 5.a4, aiming to prevent ...b5. But I’ve prepared a way to complicate matters for White despite this move. We continue with 5...Nc6, intending to jump to a5, where the knight defends the c4-pawn and eyes b3.} 5...Nc6 6.e4 {Now 6.e4 is the most popular attempt, opening a direct attack on our c4-pawn with the f1-bishop. Here we reveal the point of our setup: 6...Na5!, defending c4 and forcing White to work hard to recover the pawn.} 6...Na5 7.Ne5 {White’s most popular reply, 7.Ne5, appears to pressure c4 but actually fails tactically. It allows our knight to hop into b3, hitting both d4 and the rook.} 7...Nb3 8.Rb1 {Once the rook moves...} 8...Qxd4 {Black can win a pawn. Our ideas against 5. a4 are powerful, and can result in completely winning positions like this one if White isn't careful.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e4 or 5.e3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/a52bc332-65f1-42ae-8613-f0c61fa17c98/lines"]

1.d4 {This second study will show you how to handle the very natural 5.e4 and 5.e3. After Black plays ...dxc4, many Queen’s Gambit players instinctively push the e-pawn to attack c4 with a discovered threat from the bishop. But this actually gives us everything we want from the opening - and in some lines, we even keep the pawn! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with our own, aiming for our traditional QGD setup.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit, 2.c4. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White usually follows with 3.Nc3. As mentioned in the previous study, c3 is the ideal square for the queenside knight in the QGD because it reinforces pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5, stopping White’s pieces from using b5, and supporting our light-square structure.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {White develops with 4.Nf3. Their logic is that the Queen’s Gambit isn’t a true gambit, that they can always recover the c-pawn. But 4.Nf3 actually allows us to capture it immediately.} 4...dxc4 5.e4 {After our capture, the reflex move 5.e4 is very common. White wants to claim the central e4-square we just vacated and hit c4 at the same time. Thanks to 3...a6, we can play 5...b5 to prevent Bxc4 - you’ll soon see that the a6/b5 structure not only defends the pawn but can also enable ...b4 to kick the knight and disrupt White’s coordination.} 5...b5 6.a4 {6.a4 is White’s most popular attempt to undermine the c4-pawn. But 6...b4 hits the knight and asks a serious question: where should it go? The best engine move is 7.Ne2, but it’s not even the most common choice.} 6...b4 7.Ne2 {Finally, 7.Ne2 is liked by the engine but not very popular, and it creates coordination issues. The knight on e2 blocks the f1-bishop, and since that bishop is shut in, we can freely develop our knight to c6. This gives us extra time to play ...Na5 and defend c4.} 7...Nc6 8.Ng3 {Once the knight moves to g3 to reopen the bishop's diagonal... We can jump to a5 and secure the c4-pawn.} 8...Na5 9.Be2 {If White develops the bishop to prepare castling, we continue with 9...Bd7. We’ll follow up with ...Nf6 and ...Be7, putting pressure on e4 while enjoying our active queenside knight and pawn structure.} 9...Bb7 10.O-O {White may castle at this point.} 10...Nf6 {But our moves are very natural, and our pieces are active on the squares that are available to them (in contrast to White's knight on g3 and the e2 bishop).} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e4 or 5.e3 / Variation 12"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/a52bc332-65f1-42ae-8613-f0c61fa17c98/lines"]

1.d4 {This second study will show you how to handle the very natural 5.e4 and 5.e3. After Black plays ...dxc4, many Queen’s Gambit players instinctively push the e-pawn to attack c4 with a discovered threat from the bishop. But this actually gives us everything we want from the opening - and in some lines, we even keep the pawn! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with our own, aiming for our traditional QGD setup.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit, 2.c4. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White usually follows with 3.Nc3. As mentioned in the previous study, c3 is the ideal square for the queenside knight in the QGD because it reinforces pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5, stopping White’s pieces from using b5, and supporting our light-square structure.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {White develops with 4.Nf3. Their logic is that the Queen’s Gambit isn’t a true gambit, that they can always recover the c-pawn. But 4.Nf3 actually allows us to capture it immediately.} 4...dxc4 5.e3 {5.e3 is a more modest way of targeting the c4-pawn. It doesn’t try to claim both central squares with e4, but simply aims to stay solid and recover the pawn. Against 5.e3, we can already pose problems by threatening to hold on to it with 5...b5.} 5...b5 6.a4 {But White can undermine our structure with 6.a4. We respond just as in the main line: the familiar …b4 push.} 6...b4 7.Nb1 {Some players bring the knight back to b1. This is obviously not ideal for development. Our moves stay the same: We'll start with 7...Bb7, then ...Nf6, and ...c5. The bishop goes to b7 to control the long diagonal, and our grip on e4 and d5 becomes a major asset.} 7...Bb7 8.Bxc4 {The bishop finally recaptures the pawn. We continue with 8...Nf6, we’ve achieved very natural development while White still has a knight sitting on b1.} 8...Nf6 9.O-O {White castles. We challenge the center with ...c5.} 9...c5 10.Nbd2 {White may also refuse to take on c5 with 10.Nbd2. In this case, I like adding pressure on d4 with 10...Nc6 to force them into releasing the tension.} 10...Nc6 11.dxc5 {When that capture finally happens, we’re quite happy - White has given up their d4-pawn and loosened their central control. We simply recapture the c5-pawn.} 11...Bxc5 12.b3 {And once again, with e3 on the board, you'll most likely see b3 for White to play Bb2.} 12...O-O {Here we'll castle and after Bb2 we may even go for ...Na5 and ...Nxc4. We'll also centralize rooks on d8 and c8. I like Black's piece coordination here.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e4 or 5.e3 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/a52bc332-65f1-42ae-8613-f0c61fa17c98/lines"]

1.d4 {This second study will show you how to handle the very natural 5.e4 and 5.e3. After Black plays ...dxc4, many Queen’s Gambit players instinctively push the e-pawn to attack c4 with a discovered threat from the bishop. But this actually gives us everything we want from the opening - and in some lines, we even keep the pawn! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with our own, aiming for our traditional QGD setup.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit, 2.c4. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White usually follows with 3.Nc3. As mentioned in the previous study, c3 is the ideal square for the queenside knight in the QGD because it reinforces pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5, stopping White’s pieces from using b5, and supporting our light-square structure.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {White develops with 4.Nf3. Their logic is that the Queen’s Gambit isn’t a true gambit, that they can always recover the c-pawn. But 4.Nf3 actually allows us to capture it immediately.} 4...dxc4 5.e3 {5.e3 is a more modest way of targeting the c4-pawn. It doesn’t try to claim both central squares with e4, but simply aims to stay solid and recover the pawn. Against 5.e3, we can already pose problems by threatening to hold on to it with 5...b5.} 5...b5 6.a4 {But White can undermine our structure with 6.a4. We respond just as in the main line: the familiar …b4 push.} 6...b4 7.Ne4 {Since White hasn’t placed a pawn on e4, they actually gain an extra square for the knight. This is the most popular move. We answer with 7…Bb7, attacking the knight and strengthening our grip on the light squares.} 7...Bb7 8.Ng3 {White, of course, must move the knight - this is the main continuation. I suggest we develop our knight to f6, taking control of key central squares like e4 and d5.} 8...Nf6 9.Bxc4 {Once White recovers the c4-pawn... We continue with …c5. This move undermines the center, and White cannot capture without losing castling rights. Even though we’re no longer a pawn up, we reach a completely healthy position.} 9...c5 10.O-O {Most players will castle here. In that case, as in other lines where White doesn’t take on c5, I like …Nc6 to increase pressure on d4. This move often provokes dxc5 because of the tension on the pawn.} 10...Nc6 11.dxc5 {And indeed, we may see that capture. I recommend taking back with the bishop, which reinforces the b4-pawn that controls key queenside squares and restricts White’s normal development. If White trades queens on d8, we recapture with the rook and enjoy the open file.} 11...Bxc5 12.Qe2 {White might try a move like this to prepare Rd1. But continuing with 12.Na5, attacking White's bishop, is always a good strategy.} 12...Na5 13.Rd1 {If Rd1...} 13...Qb6 {Our queen is safe on b6. We're on the light-squared bishop, which we'll take if we get the chance. ...0-0 and centralizing our rooks on d8 and c8 are our next moves if we do not take on c4 first.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e4 or 5.e3 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/a52bc332-65f1-42ae-8613-f0c61fa17c98/lines"]

1.d4 {This second study will show you how to handle the very natural 5.e4 and 5.e3. After Black plays ...dxc4, many Queen’s Gambit players instinctively push the e-pawn to attack c4 with a discovered threat from the bishop. But this actually gives us everything we want from the opening - and in some lines, we even keep the pawn! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with our own, aiming for our traditional QGD setup.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit, 2.c4. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White usually follows with 3.Nc3. As mentioned in the previous study, c3 is the ideal square for the queenside knight in the QGD because it reinforces pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5, stopping White’s pieces from using b5, and supporting our light-square structure.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {White develops with 4.Nf3. Their logic is that the Queen’s Gambit isn’t a true gambit, that they can always recover the c-pawn. But 4.Nf3 actually allows us to capture it immediately.} 4...dxc4 5.e3 {5.e3 is a more modest way of targeting the c4-pawn. It doesn’t try to claim both central squares with e4, but simply aims to stay solid and recover the pawn. Against 5.e3, we can already pose problems by threatening to hold on to it with 5...b5.} 5...b5 6.a4 {But White can undermine our structure with 6.a4. We respond just as in the main line: the familiar …b4 push.} 6...b4 7.Na2 {7.Na2 places the knight on a bad square. The b4-pawn is defended by our bishop on f8, and after 7...Bb7 our bishop exerts long-range control over the light squares. Without a pawn on e4, that bishop becomes even more powerful.} 7...Bb7 8.Bxc4 {White recaptures on c4. Our moves here are very easy to remember: 8...Nf6, and ...c5. The knight on f6 helps control e4 and d5. And remember: if 9.Bd2, you can simply play 9...a5.} 8...Nf6 9.O-O {White castles. We immediately undermine the d4-pawn with 9...c5.} 9...c5 10.b3 {White may also choose to ignore the pawn with 10.b3. In that case, we continue exactly as before: ...Nc6, increasing the pressure on d4 and trying to provoke White into taking on c5.} 10...Nc6 11.Bb2 {If White continues to ignore the tension... I like capturing on d4, giving them an IQP. They can recapture with the e-pawn and accept the isolated pawn, or recapture with a piece and surrender central control.} 11...cxd4 12.Nxd4 {Capturing with the knight is the move you’ll see most often, avoiding the IQP and keeping the dark-squared bishop on the board. Here I really like 12...Na5, going after White's light-squared bishop.} 12...Na5 13.Be2 {Supposing they save the bishop...} 13...Be7 {After 13...Be7 Black has the same advantages related to piece activity and coordination. We'll play ...0-0, ...Rc8, ...Qb6 to develop our pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e4 or 5.e3 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/a52bc332-65f1-42ae-8613-f0c61fa17c98/lines"]

1.d4 {This second study will show you how to handle the very natural 5.e4 and 5.e3. After Black plays ...dxc4, many Queen’s Gambit players instinctively push the e-pawn to attack c4 with a discovered threat from the bishop. But this actually gives us everything we want from the opening - and in some lines, we even keep the pawn! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with our own, aiming for our traditional QGD setup.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit, 2.c4. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White usually follows with 3.Nc3. As mentioned in the previous study, c3 is the ideal square for the queenside knight in the QGD because it reinforces pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5, stopping White’s pieces from using b5, and supporting our light-square structure.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {White develops with 4.Nf3. Their logic is that the Queen’s Gambit isn’t a true gambit, that they can always recover the c-pawn. But 4.Nf3 actually allows us to capture it immediately.} 4...dxc4 5.e3 {5.e3 is a more modest way of targeting the c4-pawn. It doesn’t try to claim both central squares with e4, but simply aims to stay solid and recover the pawn. Against 5.e3, we can already pose problems by threatening to hold on to it with 5...b5.} 5...b5 6.a4 {But White can undermine our structure with 6.a4. We respond just as in the main line: the familiar …b4 push.} 6...b4 7.Na2 {7.Na2 places the knight on a bad square. The b4-pawn is defended by our bishop on f8, and after 7...Bb7 our bishop exerts long-range control over the light squares. Without a pawn on e4, that bishop becomes even more powerful.} 7...Bb7 8.a5 {What if White plays 8.a5 themselves, preparing Bd2 to attack our b4-pawn? Here I like the immediate 8...c5. This move creates practical problems: ...c5 helps defend b4, and if dxc5, we get a favorable queen trade. White becomes even more cramped on the queenside.} 8...c5 9.Bd2 {And what if White tries Bd2 to avoid the queen trade and increase pressure on b4? They delay dxc5, so it looks logical. Yet 9...Nc6 solves everything: it adds another defender to b4, and if dxc5 now, ...Bxc5 follows and the bishop defends b4 as well.} 9...Nc6 10.Bxc4 {So at some point White will have to resort to Bxc4. We simply keep developing, maintaining control over the d5-square with 10.Nf6.} 10...Nf6 11.dxc5 {If dxc5 ever...} 11...Bxc5 {Black recaptures with the bishop, defending b4. The a5-pawn isn’t the strongest, but we must stay alert since the rook is on the a-file. ...0-0, ...Qe7 at some point also adds a defender on b4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e4 or 5.e3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/a52bc332-65f1-42ae-8613-f0c61fa17c98/lines"]

1.d4 {This second study will show you how to handle the very natural 5.e4 and 5.e3. After Black plays ...dxc4, many Queen’s Gambit players instinctively push the e-pawn to attack c4 with a discovered threat from the bishop. But this actually gives us everything we want from the opening - and in some lines, we even keep the pawn! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with our own, aiming for our traditional QGD setup.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit, 2.c4. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White usually follows with 3.Nc3. As mentioned in the previous study, c3 is the ideal square for the queenside knight in the QGD because it reinforces pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5, stopping White’s pieces from using b5, and supporting our light-square structure.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {White develops with 4.Nf3. Their logic is that the Queen’s Gambit isn’t a true gambit, that they can always recover the c-pawn. But 4.Nf3 actually allows us to capture it immediately.} 4...dxc4 5.e4 {After our capture, the reflex move 5.e4 is very common. White wants to claim the central e4-square we just vacated and hit c4 at the same time. Thanks to 3...a6, we can play 5...b5 to prevent Bxc4 - you’ll soon see that the a6/b5 structure not only defends the pawn but can also enable ...b4 to kick the knight and disrupt White’s coordination.} 5...b5 6.a4 {6.a4 is White’s most popular attempt to undermine the c4-pawn. But 6...b4 hits the knight and asks a serious question: where should it go? The best engine move is 7.Ne2, but it’s not even the most common choice.} 6...b4 7.Na2 {The main move is 7.Na2. White avoids the attack but places the knight on the rim, and it’s not even threatening b4, since our bishop on f8 defends it. We continue with ...Bb7, a perfect square for the bishop, where it cuts across the long diagonal and targets the e4-pawn.} 7...Bb7 8.Bxc4 {If White recaptures on c4... We simply take on e4.} 8...Bxe4 9.O-O {Castling is the most common move afterwards. White often tries to compensate for the pawn with activity - especially along the e-file with Re1. But we develop harmoniously with 9...Nc6, controlling key light squares and defending our bishop, while maintaining excellent coordination. White still has lingering issues, especially the misplaced Na2.} 9...Nf6 10.Re1 {10.Re1 is a very natural idea, pressuring the e-file and eyeing our bishop. I recommend developing the knight to c6 and soon bringing the bishop to d5.} 10...Nc6 11.Be3 {If the bishop comes to e3 to defend the d4 pawn...} 11...Be7 {We can develop with 11...Be7, looking to castle soon and place our bishop on d5 where it will have a safer home. Black is a pawn up out of the opening, and White still has to solve the knight on a2 situation.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e4 or 5.e3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/a52bc332-65f1-42ae-8613-f0c61fa17c98/lines"]

1.d4 {This second study will show you how to handle the very natural 5.e4 and 5.e3. After Black plays ...dxc4, many Queen’s Gambit players instinctively push the e-pawn to attack c4 with a discovered threat from the bishop. But this actually gives us everything we want from the opening - and in some lines, we even keep the pawn! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with our own, aiming for our traditional QGD setup.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit, 2.c4. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White usually follows with 3.Nc3. As mentioned in the previous study, c3 is the ideal square for the queenside knight in the QGD because it reinforces pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5, stopping White’s pieces from using b5, and supporting our light-square structure.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {White develops with 4.Nf3. Their logic is that the Queen’s Gambit isn’t a true gambit, that they can always recover the c-pawn. But 4.Nf3 actually allows us to capture it immediately.} 4...dxc4 5.e4 {After our capture, the reflex move 5.e4 is very common. White wants to claim the central e4-square we just vacated and hit c4 at the same time. Thanks to 3...a6, we can play 5...b5 to prevent Bxc4 - you’ll soon see that the a6/b5 structure not only defends the pawn but can also enable ...b4 to kick the knight and disrupt White’s coordination.} 5...b5 6.a4 {6.a4 is White’s most popular attempt to undermine the c4-pawn. But 6...b4 hits the knight and asks a serious question: where should it go? The best engine move is 7.Ne2, but it’s not even the most common choice.} 6...b4 7.Na2 {The main move is 7.Na2. White avoids the attack but places the knight on the rim, and it’s not even threatening b4, since our bishop on f8 defends it. We continue with ...Bb7, a perfect square for the bishop, where it cuts across the long diagonal and targets the e4-pawn.} 7...Bb7 8.e5 {If White makes this pawn push, they give up control of the e4- and the d5-squares. We immediately make use of that by placing our bishop on d5 as an outpost. From d5, the bishop also defends the c4-pawn.} 8...Bd5 9.Bd2 {White can’t take either pawn and plays 9.Bd2, targeting b4. We respond with 9...Nc6, which defends the b4-pawn.} 9...Nc6 10.Rc1 {The very natural Rc1, adding an attacker on c4, runs into a tactic:} 10...c3 {10...c3, attacking the bishop on d2 and with a discovered attack on the a2 knight!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e4 or 5.e3 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/a52bc332-65f1-42ae-8613-f0c61fa17c98/lines"]

1.d4 {This second study will show you how to handle the very natural 5.e4 and 5.e3. After Black plays ...dxc4, many Queen’s Gambit players instinctively push the e-pawn to attack c4 with a discovered threat from the bishop. But this actually gives us everything we want from the opening - and in some lines, we even keep the pawn! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with our own, aiming for our traditional QGD setup.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit, 2.c4. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White usually follows with 3.Nc3. As mentioned in the previous study, c3 is the ideal square for the queenside knight in the QGD because it reinforces pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5, stopping White’s pieces from using b5, and supporting our light-square structure.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {White develops with 4.Nf3. Their logic is that the Queen’s Gambit isn’t a true gambit, that they can always recover the c-pawn. But 4.Nf3 actually allows us to capture it immediately.} 4...dxc4 5.e3 {5.e3 is a more modest way of targeting the c4-pawn. It doesn’t try to claim both central squares with e4, but simply aims to stay solid and recover the pawn. Against 5.e3, we can already pose problems by threatening to hold on to it with 5...b5.} 5...b5 6.a4 {But White can undermine our structure with 6.a4. We respond just as in the main line: the familiar …b4 push.} 6...b4 7.Nb1 {Some players bring the knight back to b1. This is obviously not ideal for development. Our moves stay the same: We'll start with 7...Bb7, then ...Nf6, and ...c5. The bishop goes to b7 to control the long diagonal, and our grip on e4 and d5 becomes a major asset.} 7...Bb7 8.Bxc4 {The bishop finally recaptures the pawn. We continue with 8...Nf6, we’ve achieved very natural development while White still has a knight sitting on b1.} 8...Nf6 9.O-O {White castles. We challenge the center with ...c5.} 9...c5 10.dxc5 {If White chooses to take... We place our queen on c7. We’re not interested in trading pieces and helping White coordinate - coordination becomes easier with fewer pieces. We’d rather watch them untangle the b1-knight and the c1-bishop while we keep developing smoothly.} 10...Qc7 11.b3 {Once again, you’ll often see b3 from White to get their pieces out. There’s no need to play ...Qxc5; the queen is safer on c7 than on c5. We’re also not forced to recapture immediately, so we play 11...Nbd7.} 11...Nbd7 12.Bb2 {White develops their bishop to b2. Fortunately, after ...Nbd7, our knight on f6 gains support. This setup is very sound for Black after 12...Be7, delaying the capture on c5 so the knight on f6 remains protected.} 12...Be7 13.Nbd2 {White may develop the knight, preparing Rc1. Now is a good moment for us to recapture on c5 with the knight.} 13...Nxc5 14.Rc1 {If Rc1...} 14...Rc8 {You can always overprotect your knight on c5. ...0-0, ...Rfd8 are sound moves you could continue with, centralizing your pieces. When the rook comes to d8, ...Nd5 will also become a solid choice.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e4 or 5.e3 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/a52bc332-65f1-42ae-8613-f0c61fa17c98/lines"]

1.d4 {This second study will show you how to handle the very natural 5.e4 and 5.e3. After Black plays ...dxc4, many Queen’s Gambit players instinctively push the e-pawn to attack c4 with a discovered threat from the bishop. But this actually gives us everything we want from the opening - and in some lines, we even keep the pawn! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with our own, aiming for our traditional QGD setup.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit, 2.c4. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White usually follows with 3.Nc3. As mentioned in the previous study, c3 is the ideal square for the queenside knight in the QGD because it reinforces pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5, stopping White’s pieces from using b5, and supporting our light-square structure.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {White develops with 4.Nf3. Their logic is that the Queen’s Gambit isn’t a true gambit, that they can always recover the c-pawn. But 4.Nf3 actually allows us to capture it immediately.} 4...dxc4 5.e3 {5.e3 is a more modest way of targeting the c4-pawn. It doesn’t try to claim both central squares with e4, but simply aims to stay solid and recover the pawn. Against 5.e3, we can already pose problems by threatening to hold on to it with 5...b5.} 5...b5 6.a4 {But White can undermine our structure with 6.a4. We respond just as in the main line: the familiar …b4 push.} 6...b4 7.Na2 {7.Na2 places the knight on a bad square. The b4-pawn is defended by our bishop on f8, and after 7...Bb7 our bishop exerts long-range control over the light squares. Without a pawn on e4, that bishop becomes even more powerful.} 7...Bb7 8.Bxc4 {White recaptures on c4. Our moves here are very easy to remember: 8...Nf6, and ...c5. The knight on f6 helps control e4 and d5. And remember: if 9.Bd2, you can simply play 9...a5.} 8...Nf6 9.O-O {White castles. We immediately undermine the d4-pawn with 9...c5.} 9...c5 10.dxc5 {And if White takes on c5, once again, we’re not trading queens. We develop our queen instead and watch White struggle to untangle their queenside pieces - the knight on a2 and the bishop on c1.} 10...Qc7 11.b3 {White will likely have to adopt a b3–Bb2 setup to get developed, since they played e3. We can capture on c5 with the bishop, clearing the way for castling.} 11...Bxc5 12.Bb2 {The awkward thing for White is that they'd like to play Rc1 but they also have to retrieve their knight with some Nc1 maneuver.} 12...Nbd7 {Noting that Rc1 may come, ...Nbd7 protects the c5-bishop. We won't be surprised on the c-file. We've got ...0-0 coming up and if Rc1 White will be cramped, without any way out for their a2-knight. We even have ...Rd8 coming to x-ray their queen, and in this case we have a more direct threat to White's own queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e4 or 5.e3 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/a52bc332-65f1-42ae-8613-f0c61fa17c98/lines"]

1.d4 {This second study will show you how to handle the very natural 5.e4 and 5.e3. After Black plays ...dxc4, many Queen’s Gambit players instinctively push the e-pawn to attack c4 with a discovered threat from the bishop. But this actually gives us everything we want from the opening - and in some lines, we even keep the pawn! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with our own, aiming for our traditional QGD setup.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit, 2.c4. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White usually follows with 3.Nc3. As mentioned in the previous study, c3 is the ideal square for the queenside knight in the QGD because it reinforces pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5, stopping White’s pieces from using b5, and supporting our light-square structure.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {White develops with 4.Nf3. Their logic is that the Queen’s Gambit isn’t a true gambit, that they can always recover the c-pawn. But 4.Nf3 actually allows us to capture it immediately.} 4...dxc4 5.e3 {5.e3 is a more modest way of targeting the c4-pawn. It doesn’t try to claim both central squares with e4, but simply aims to stay solid and recover the pawn. Against 5.e3, we can already pose problems by threatening to hold on to it with 5...b5.} 5...b5 6.a4 {But White can undermine our structure with 6.a4. We respond just as in the main line: the familiar …b4 push.} 6...b4 7.Na2 {7.Na2 places the knight on a bad square. The b4-pawn is defended by our bishop on f8, and after 7...Bb7 our bishop exerts long-range control over the light squares. Without a pawn on e4, that bishop becomes even more powerful.} 7...Bb7 8.Bd2 {If you ever see 8.Bd2... Remember you have ...a5 to defend everything. This keeps the b-pawn protected and maintains annoying control over the queenside squares. White’s development becomes awkward.} 8...a5 9.Bxc4 {White has already lost time with Bd2. They could take on c4 now, but our development flows smoothly. We continue with kingside development with 9...Nf6 since we’re going to castle. We’re perfectly happy with the a5–b4 pawn chain, there’s not much to do on the queenside for the moment.} 9...Nf6 10.O-O {White castles.} 10...Bd6 {And we're placing our bishop on d6, on a more active diagonal than if it were placed on e7. We've got ...0-0 coming up, followed by ...Nbd7 and we'll prepare the e5-pawn break for middlegame counterplay.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e4 or 5.e3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/a52bc332-65f1-42ae-8613-f0c61fa17c98/lines"]

1.d4 {This second study will show you how to handle the very natural 5.e4 and 5.e3. After Black plays ...dxc4, many Queen’s Gambit players instinctively push the e-pawn to attack c4 with a discovered threat from the bishop. But this actually gives us everything we want from the opening - and in some lines, we even keep the pawn! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with our own, aiming for our traditional QGD setup.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit, 2.c4. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White usually follows with 3.Nc3. As mentioned in the previous study, c3 is the ideal square for the queenside knight in the QGD because it reinforces pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5, stopping White’s pieces from using b5, and supporting our light-square structure.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {White develops with 4.Nf3. Their logic is that the Queen’s Gambit isn’t a true gambit, that they can always recover the c-pawn. But 4.Nf3 actually allows us to capture it immediately.} 4...dxc4 5.e4 {After our capture, the reflex move 5.e4 is very common. White wants to claim the central e4-square we just vacated and hit c4 at the same time. Thanks to 3...a6, we can play 5...b5 to prevent Bxc4 - you’ll soon see that the a6/b5 structure not only defends the pawn but can also enable ...b4 to kick the knight and disrupt White’s coordination.} 5...b5 6.a3 {6.a3 is occasionally played with the idea of preventing ...b4. The problem is that it lets us keep our strong pawn chain led by the extra pawn. We can simply fianchetto our light-squared bishop on a beautiful diagonal. Our light-square control becomes extensive: the a6–c4 chain dominates the queenside, while the bishop on b7 targets the kingside.} 6...Bb7 7.Be2 {Many players now just continue developing, thinking their central pawns and the stable knight on c3 are enough compensation. But that isn’t true. With 7...Nf6, we already put pressure on the center.} 7...Nf6 8.e5 {If they try to fix that problem by pushing the e-pawn, they only weaken the light squares further. It allows us to immediately take advantage with a knight jump to d5.} 8...Nd5 9.O-O {White castles.} 9...Be7 {And we'll get castled soon enough. We're up a pawn with a better position!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.e4 or 5.e3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/a52bc332-65f1-42ae-8613-f0c61fa17c98/lines"]

1.d4 {This second study will show you how to handle the very natural 5.e4 and 5.e3. After Black plays ...dxc4, many Queen’s Gambit players instinctively push the e-pawn to attack c4 with a discovered threat from the bishop. But this actually gives us everything we want from the opening - and in some lines, we even keep the pawn! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with our own, aiming for our traditional QGD setup.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit, 2.c4. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White usually follows with 3.Nc3. As mentioned in the previous study, c3 is the ideal square for the queenside knight in the QGD because it reinforces pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5, stopping White’s pieces from using b5, and supporting our light-square structure.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {White develops with 4.Nf3. Their logic is that the Queen’s Gambit isn’t a true gambit, that they can always recover the c-pawn. But 4.Nf3 actually allows us to capture it immediately.} 4...dxc4 5.e4 {After our capture, the reflex move 5.e4 is very common. White wants to claim the central e4-square we just vacated and hit c4 at the same time. Thanks to 3...a6, we can play 5...b5 to prevent Bxc4 - you’ll soon see that the a6/b5 structure not only defends the pawn but can also enable ...b4 to kick the knight and disrupt White’s coordination.} 5...b5 6.a4 {6.a4 is White’s most popular attempt to undermine the c4-pawn. But 6...b4 hits the knight and asks a serious question: where should it go? The best engine move is 7.Ne2, but it’s not even the most common choice.} 6...b4 7.Nb1 {7. Nb1, a hard reset on the knight, is what many others have played.} 7...Bb7 {We can play Bb7 with a tempo on e4, and now White has no convenient defense. If 8.Bxc4 Bxe4. If 8.Nbd2 c3! If 8.e5 Bd5 And we proceed with c7-c5 when necessary, as well as kingside development.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 3: Other White 5th Moves / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/ef23d05b-a5ed-40fd-bfb1-282f386dc29d/lines"]

1.d4 {This third study reviews the sidelines White may choose after 4...dxc4. Some options, like 5.Bf4 or 5.Bg5, allow harmonious development. Others, such as 5.Qa4+ and 5.g3, are more dubious and let us comfortably fight for an advantage. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with our own, heading toward our standard QGD structure.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit. We decline it with ...e6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White usually develops with 3.Nc3, since from c3 the knight puts pressure on d5. Once again, we follow with our signature move 3...a6, taking control of b5 and preparing ...b5 should we need it.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {White develops normally with 4.Nf3, but this move allows us to enter our desired line with 4...dxc4. We’re temporarily a pawn up, and as seen in the previous studies, recovering it is far from trivial for White.} 4...dxc4 5.Bg5 {5.Bg5, attacking our queen, is the most popular sideline. But we can calmly block the attack with 5...Be7, offering a bishop trade. With dark-squared bishops off, White will struggle to attack our dark squares.} 5...Be7 6.Bxe7 {If they accept the trade, which is the most common choice... We recapture with the queen, activating it on the dark squares.} 6...Qxe7 7.e3 {Among beginners, 7.e3 is the most popular continuation, aiming at our c4-pawn. We respond with 7...b5, reinforcing the c4-pawn.} 7...b5 8.a4 {White’s most logical reply is to challenge our b-pawn and exploit the pin on the a-file. We solve this by developing our light-squared bishop to b7, removing the pin so that after axb5 axb5 our rook remains defended.} 8...Bb7 9.axb5 {And indeed, White may play exactly that. We recapture, and because our queen is on e7, the tactical idea ...Qb4+ becomes available if Nxb5.} 9...axb5 10.Rxa8 {White exchanges rooks. We recapture.} 10...Bxa8 11.Nxb5 {The knight takes on b5, grabbing material. We give a check with the queen, attacking both the knight and the b2-pawn.} 11...Qb4+ 12.Nc3 {White must block with the knight to save the piece. Now comes a nice detail: we take on f3 first, damaging their pawn structure.} 12...Bxf3 13.gxf3 {White recaptures, ending up with doubled f-pawns. These pawns will be weak in endgames, and even in the middlegame Black can often generate play against the king. Now we take on b2, since c4 is weak and doubled regardless.} 13...Qxb2 14.Qa4+ {The game could continue with 14. Qa4+.} 14...Nd7 {We'll develop a piece and block the check. After this, we'll bring our other knight out and castle kingside. Black has an active queen and a better pawn structure out of the opening.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 3: Other White 5th Moves / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/ef23d05b-a5ed-40fd-bfb1-282f386dc29d/lines"]

1.d4 {This third study reviews the sidelines White may choose after 4...dxc4. Some options, like 5.Bf4 or 5.Bg5, allow harmonious development. Others, such as 5.Qa4+ and 5.g3, are more dubious and let us comfortably fight for an advantage. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with our own, heading toward our standard QGD structure.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit. We decline it with ...e6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White usually develops with 3.Nc3, since from c3 the knight puts pressure on d5. Once again, we follow with our signature move 3...a6, taking control of b5 and preparing ...b5 should we need it.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {White develops normally with 4.Nf3, but this move allows us to enter our desired line with 4...dxc4. We’re temporarily a pawn up, and as seen in the previous studies, recovering it is far from trivial for White.} 4...dxc4 5.Bf4 {5.Bf4, developing the bishop, is the second-most popular choice. The bishop eyes c7, but thanks to our earlier 3...a6, Nb5 is impossible. I suggest we pin the knight here with 5...Bb4. If we later trade it and the b-pawn recaptures, controlling the queenside becomes much easier since White will only have one pawn break left (a4).} 5...Bb4 6.e3 {Many players follow up with e3 to attack c4. With the dark-squared bishop already outside the pawn chain on f4, they feel comfortable doing so. But we have our signature ...b5, defending the pawn. Once again, White lacks an effective way to pressure c4, and we may simply remain a pawn up.} 6...b5 7.Be2 {Some players just continue developing and prepare to castle. We do the same with 7...Nf6, aiming at the central squares e4 and d5, and getting ready to castle.} 7...Nf6 8.O-O {Once White castles... We can take the knight. After 9.bxc3, we will control the queenside more easily because White no longer has the b3 pawn break. With only a4 left, their queenside play is much easier to handle.} 8...Bxc3 9.bxc3 {White recaptures. We develop our bishop to b7, onto the strong diagonal.} 9...Bb7 10.a4 {If White goes a4...} 10...Nbd7 {We won't have any trouble dealing with the queenside counterplay. We'll castle kingside and enjoy being a pawn up.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 3: Other White 5th Moves / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/ef23d05b-a5ed-40fd-bfb1-282f386dc29d/lines"]

1.d4 {This third study reviews the sidelines White may choose after 4...dxc4. Some options, like 5.Bf4 or 5.Bg5, allow harmonious development. Others, such as 5.Qa4+ and 5.g3, are more dubious and let us comfortably fight for an advantage. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with our own, heading toward our standard QGD structure.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit. We decline it with ...e6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White usually develops with 3.Nc3, since from c3 the knight puts pressure on d5. Once again, we follow with our signature move 3...a6, taking control of b5 and preparing ...b5 should we need it.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {White develops normally with 4.Nf3, but this move allows us to enter our desired line with 4...dxc4. We’re temporarily a pawn up, and as seen in the previous studies, recovering it is far from trivial for White.} 4...dxc4 5.g3 {White may try to play 5.g3 in a Catalan style, aiming for compensation and quick control of the h1–a8 diagonal. But this idea is flawed: after 5...b5, our bishop will reach the same diagonal just as quickly, so White gains no special advantage.} 5...b5 6.Bg2 {White gets their bishop onto the diagonal. We do the same, and the position offers White nothing extra.} 6...Bb7 7.a4 {If 7. a4...} 7...b4 {We can attack that knight, and it'll have to retreat to b1. White is worse out of the opening. We'll continue with moves like ...Nf6 and ...c5, going for comfortable development that we have earned.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 3: Other White 5th Moves / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/ef23d05b-a5ed-40fd-bfb1-282f386dc29d/lines"]

1.d4 {This third study reviews the sidelines White may choose after 4...dxc4. Some options, like 5.Bf4 or 5.Bg5, allow harmonious development. Others, such as 5.Qa4+ and 5.g3, are more dubious and let us comfortably fight for an advantage. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with our own, heading toward our standard QGD structure.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit. We decline it with ...e6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White usually develops with 3.Nc3, since from c3 the knight puts pressure on d5. Once again, we follow with our signature move 3...a6, taking control of b5 and preparing ...b5 should we need it.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {White develops normally with 4.Nf3, but this move allows us to enter our desired line with 4...dxc4. We’re temporarily a pawn up, and as seen in the previous studies, recovering it is far from trivial for White.} 4...dxc4 5.Bg5 {5.Bg5, attacking our queen, is the most popular sideline. But we can calmly block the attack with 5...Be7, offering a bishop trade. With dark-squared bishops off, White will struggle to attack our dark squares.} 5...Be7 6.Bxe7 {If they accept the trade, which is the most common choice... We recapture with the queen, activating it on the dark squares.} 6...Qxe7 7.Qa4+ {This check is a good move. We cannot play 7...b5 here because the knight can take on b5 (8.Nxb5), and if we try to pin with 8...Bd7, White has Nxc7+. That’s why we need a different idea: 7...c6, preparing ...b5.} 7...c6 8.Qxc4 {White recovers the pawn, but there’s a price to pay: we will gain a tempo on the queen and build a strong queenside pawn structure. After 8...b5 we hit the queen and create a square for our bishop. Getting our light-squared bishop to b7 with tempo is a big win - normally in the QGD the light-squared bishop is a problem piece, sometimes needing a long route via d7–e8 and even ...f6 to activate it!} 8...b5 9.Qb3 {b3 is a very natural square for the queen. I'm willing to bet most d4 players would go here.} 9...Bb7 {At which point, you can just get your bishop on b7, and in the following moves, conclude your development adopting this setup: ...Nd7, ...Ngf6 and ...0-0. Black has sound development and a very robust queenside pawn diamond.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 3: Other White 5th Moves / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/6bc47d63-3d38-41cf-bf53-c06ff958f5c8/studies/ef23d05b-a5ed-40fd-bfb1-282f386dc29d/lines"]

1.d4 {This third study reviews the sidelines White may choose after 4...dxc4. Some options, like 5.Bf4 or 5.Bg5, allow harmonious development. Others, such as 5.Qa4+ and 5.g3, are more dubious and let us comfortably fight for an advantage. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with our own, heading toward our standard QGD structure.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit. We decline it with ...e6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White usually develops with 3.Nc3, since from c3 the knight puts pressure on d5. Once again, we follow with our signature move 3...a6, taking control of b5 and preparing ...b5 should we need it.} 3...a6 4.Nf3 {White develops normally with 4.Nf3, but this move allows us to enter our desired line with 4...dxc4. We’re temporarily a pawn up, and as seen in the previous studies, recovering it is far from trivial for White.} 4...dxc4 5.Qa4+ {5.Qa4+, developing the queen with check, is a very popular choice. White’s idea is to recapture the pawn immediately, and they may also assume - incorrectly - that ...b5 is impossible because of Nxb5 and the pin on the a-file. But we can play 5...b5.} 5...b5 6.Nxb5 {If White takes this pawn, yes 6...axb5 loses the rook on a8. But we don't have to take. We can just counter pin the knight and win it.} 6...Bd7 {6...Bd7 pins the knight and it's game over.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 1: White Plays 6.Bg5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/80447d14-d10c-4b92-833a-faf6e7da58f2/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 2! Some players like to exchange pawns with cxd5. If this happens, does it make our 3...a6 idea useless? Not at all. In fact, cxd5 often helps us develop, many players capture because they’re afraid of our ...a6–b5 idea! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with ours.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen's Gambit. These first moves are the same as the previous chapter. As before, we decline it with 2...e6.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight to c3, increasing pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, keeping all our ideas of ...b5, holding the pawn, and building queenside counterplay.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White may avoid the tricky 4.Nf3 and choose the “safe” approach with 4.cxd5. Whatever the reason, we recapture - and we get an added bonus: our c8-bishop will no longer be a problem piece. In many QGD lines, that bishop suffers, but with the diagonal open, development becomes effortless.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, reinforcing central control. We continue with simple principles: develop our knight to f6 first, supporting d5 and avoiding Qb3 tactics before activating our bishops.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {White plays 6.Bg5, a typical QGD move. The pin on our knight removes a defender of d5, and Qb3 ideas start to appear. That’s why I suggest solidifying the center with ...c6. One bonus of ...c6 is that it frees our c8-bishop - we’ll be able to play ...Bf5 before White goes Bd3.} 6...c6 7.e3 {7.e3 is the most natural move, enabling the development the light-squared bishop. And since White’s dark-squared bishop is already outside the chain, e3 is even more appealing. But we should exploit the fact that we can enter the h7–b1 diagonal first. If we trade light-squared bishops, we’re happy, our pawn structure already forms a strong light-square complex.} 7...Bf5 8.Qb3 {Some players, whenever you go …Bf5 in Queen’s Pawn games, like to place their queen on b3 to pressure the now undefended b7-pawn. But since we already played …c6 to defend d5, all we need to do is protect b7 - and we can do that easily. Thus, we play 8…b5, achieving a strong queenside pawn structure and neutralizing the threat.} 8...b5 9.a4 {9.a4 tries to break apart our queenside and threatens axb5, not only to win a pawn but also to activate the rook. That’s why 9...Nbd7 is necessary: it connects our queen and rook and reduces the danger of axb5.} 9...Nbd7 10.axb5 {White can still trade on a8, which is their best way of activating the rook. We simply recapture with the a-pawn - capturing with the c-pawn would drop d5.} 10...axb5 11.Rxa8 {After a trade of rooks... We recapture. Our queen sits well and fully controls the a-file.} 11...Qxa8 12.Be2 {White develops the bishop to its only square and prepares to castle. We also get ready to castle, placing our bishop on d6. Notice how after 12...Bd6 both our bishops have better diagonals and are more active than White’s.} 12...Bd6 13.O-O {White will now castle...} 13...O-O {And so will we. Because our queen controls the a-file, White will most likely play their rook to c1, x-raying our one weakness: the c6 pawn. Thus, Rc8 from us should be considered, along with further destabilizing ideas like b4 at some point.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 1: White Plays 6.Bg5 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/80447d14-d10c-4b92-833a-faf6e7da58f2/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 2! Some players like to exchange pawns with cxd5. If this happens, does it make our 3...a6 idea useless? Not at all. In fact, cxd5 often helps us develop, many players capture because they’re afraid of our ...a6–b5 idea! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with ours.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen's Gambit. These first moves are the same as the previous chapter. As before, we decline it with 2...e6.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight to c3, increasing pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, keeping all our ideas of ...b5, holding the pawn, and building queenside counterplay.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White may avoid the tricky 4.Nf3 and choose the “safe” approach with 4.cxd5. Whatever the reason, we recapture - and we get an added bonus: our c8-bishop will no longer be a problem piece. In many QGD lines, that bishop suffers, but with the diagonal open, development becomes effortless.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, reinforcing central control. We continue with simple principles: develop our knight to f6 first, supporting d5 and avoiding Qb3 tactics before activating our bishops.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {White plays 6.Bg5, a typical QGD move. The pin on our knight removes a defender of d5, and Qb3 ideas start to appear. That’s why I suggest solidifying the center with ...c6. One bonus of ...c6 is that it frees our c8-bishop - we’ll be able to play ...Bf5 before White goes Bd3.} 6...c6 7.Qc2 {7.Qc2 is a natural queen move in many Queen’s Pawn openings. It prevents …Bf5, which is why we develop our light-squared bishop to e6 instead.} 7...Be6 8.e3 {White plays 8.e3 to develop the bishop to d3 and pressure the d3–h7 diagonal. We challenge the bishop once with 8...h6; if White trades, we’re happy to win the bishop pair. If it moves to h4, White's Bd3/Qc2 battery won’t threaten h7.} 8...h6 9.Bh4 {Good players avoid taking on f6 and retreat the bishop to h4. Here we develop our queenside knight to d7.} 9...Nbd7 10.Bd3 {The bishop arrives on d3, but thanks to …h6 it lacks its usual impact. We develop our bishop on the long diagonal.} 10...Bd6 11.O-O {White castles. We castle as well. Now White has two typical choices: e4 or Rab1 for queenside play.} 11...O-O 12.Rab1 {12.Rab1 aims for queenside play. We respond with 12...a5, controlling the b4-square. Together with our bishop’s influence, this makes White’s idea much harder to execute.} 12...a5 13.a3 {f White insists, they may prepare b4 with 13.a3. The point of …a5 is that when b4 finally comes, we’ll capture it and activate our a8-rook. So we'll allow b5 and bring our other rook into play with 13.Re8, improving our position.} 13...Re8 14.b4 {White gets their desired b4 push. We simply take it.} 14...axb4 15.axb4 {And once they recapture, b5 is a positional threat. Just as a fork on e5 with a pawn is a tactical threat, b5 threatens to weaken our structure and activate the White pieces on the kingside.} 15...b5 {Hence I suggest we play b5 ourselves. We have the idea of playing Nb6-c4, making sure we don't have queenside weaknesses.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 1: White Plays 6.Bg5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/80447d14-d10c-4b92-833a-faf6e7da58f2/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 2! Some players like to exchange pawns with cxd5. If this happens, does it make our 3...a6 idea useless? Not at all. In fact, cxd5 often helps us develop, many players capture because they’re afraid of our ...a6–b5 idea! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with ours.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen's Gambit. These first moves are the same as the previous chapter. As before, we decline it with 2...e6.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight to c3, increasing pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, keeping all our ideas of ...b5, holding the pawn, and building queenside counterplay.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White may avoid the tricky 4.Nf3 and choose the “safe” approach with 4.cxd5. Whatever the reason, we recapture - and we get an added bonus: our c8-bishop will no longer be a problem piece. In many QGD lines, that bishop suffers, but with the diagonal open, development becomes effortless.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, reinforcing central control. We continue with simple principles: develop our knight to f6 first, supporting d5 and avoiding Qb3 tactics before activating our bishops.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {White plays 6.Bg5, a typical QGD move. The pin on our knight removes a defender of d5, and Qb3 ideas start to appear. That’s why I suggest solidifying the center with ...c6. One bonus of ...c6 is that it frees our c8-bishop - we’ll be able to play ...Bf5 before White goes Bd3.} 6...c6 7.Qc2 {7.Qc2 is a natural queen move in many Queen’s Pawn openings. It prevents …Bf5, which is why we develop our light-squared bishop to e6 instead.} 7...Be6 8.e3 {White plays 8.e3 to develop the bishop to d3 and pressure the d3–h7 diagonal. We challenge the bishop once with 8...h6; if White trades, we’re happy to win the bishop pair. If it moves to h4, White's Bd3/Qc2 battery won’t threaten h7.} 8...h6 9.Bh4 {Good players avoid taking on f6 and retreat the bishop to h4. Here we develop our queenside knight to d7.} 9...Nbd7 10.Bd3 {The bishop arrives on d3, but thanks to …h6 it lacks its usual impact. We develop our bishop on the long diagonal.} 10...Bd6 11.O-O {White castles. We castle as well. Now White has two typical choices: e4 or Rab1 for queenside play.} 11...O-O 12.e4 {If e4, remember that e5, forking our bishop and knight, is a threat. So we simply capture on e4 to remove it.} 12...dxe4 13.Nxe4 {The knight recaptures, adding pressure to our pinned Nf6 and attacking our bishop.} 13...Be7 {We simply retreat the bishop to e7, both defending the knight and breaking the pin. And you’ll notice that after the 12.e4 operation, White is left with an IQP, which isn’t easy to manage. Our pawn structure is far more solid, and in any endgame we’ll be better off.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 1: White Plays 6.Bg5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/80447d14-d10c-4b92-833a-faf6e7da58f2/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 2! Some players like to exchange pawns with cxd5. If this happens, does it make our 3...a6 idea useless? Not at all. In fact, cxd5 often helps us develop, many players capture because they’re afraid of our ...a6–b5 idea! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with ours.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen's Gambit. These first moves are the same as the previous chapter. As before, we decline it with 2...e6.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight to c3, increasing pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, keeping all our ideas of ...b5, holding the pawn, and building queenside counterplay.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White may avoid the tricky 4.Nf3 and choose the “safe” approach with 4.cxd5. Whatever the reason, we recapture - and we get an added bonus: our c8-bishop will no longer be a problem piece. In many QGD lines, that bishop suffers, but with the diagonal open, development becomes effortless.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, reinforcing central control. We continue with simple principles: develop our knight to f6 first, supporting d5 and avoiding Qb3 tactics before activating our bishops.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {White plays 6.Bg5, a typical QGD move. The pin on our knight removes a defender of d5, and Qb3 ideas start to appear. That’s why I suggest solidifying the center with ...c6. One bonus of ...c6 is that it frees our c8-bishop - we’ll be able to play ...Bf5 before White goes Bd3.} 6...c6 7.e3 {7.e3 is the most natural move, enabling the development the light-squared bishop. And since White’s dark-squared bishop is already outside the chain, e3 is even more appealing. But we should exploit the fact that we can enter the h7–b1 diagonal first. If we trade light-squared bishops, we’re happy, our pawn structure already forms a strong light-square complex.} 7...Bf5 8.Bd3 {White plays 8.Bd3, proposing a trade. And as mentioned, we should absolutely accept it. In QGD structures, Black gladly trades the light-squared bishop.} 8...Bxd3 9.Qxd3 {White recaptures with the queen, activating it. One of the queen’s main ideas is to support the upcoming e4 push. We continue developing with 9...Nbd7, supporting our f6-knight.} 9...Nbd7 10.e4 {The immediate 10.e4 is also possible. We simply take, and when White recaptures, we have a very common idea.} 10...dxe4 11.Nxe4 {The knight recaptures, allowing us accelerated development: ...Bb4+, developing with tempo and forcing White to make a difficult choice.} 11...Bb4+ 12.Kf1 {12.Kf1 steps out of the check and essentially claims that the knight on e4 will stay put - after all, it pressures the pinned knight on f6. But from Black’s perspective, we can castle and have a much safer king, while the knight on e4 doesn’t create any real threats. We have more than enough resources to handle the pin.} 12...O-O 13.a3 {If a3...} 13...Be7 {We can retreat to e7 and simply enjoy the advantages of a safer king, connected rooks, and a healthier pawn structure - since we’re once again fighting against an IQP.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 1: White Plays 6.Bg5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/80447d14-d10c-4b92-833a-faf6e7da58f2/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 2! Some players like to exchange pawns with cxd5. If this happens, does it make our 3...a6 idea useless? Not at all. In fact, cxd5 often helps us develop, many players capture because they’re afraid of our ...a6–b5 idea! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with ours.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen's Gambit. These first moves are the same as the previous chapter. As before, we decline it with 2...e6.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight to c3, increasing pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, keeping all our ideas of ...b5, holding the pawn, and building queenside counterplay.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White may avoid the tricky 4.Nf3 and choose the “safe” approach with 4.cxd5. Whatever the reason, we recapture - and we get an added bonus: our c8-bishop will no longer be a problem piece. In many QGD lines, that bishop suffers, but with the diagonal open, development becomes effortless.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, reinforcing central control. We continue with simple principles: develop our knight to f6 first, supporting d5 and avoiding Qb3 tactics before activating our bishops.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {White plays 6.Bg5, a typical QGD move. The pin on our knight removes a defender of d5, and Qb3 ideas start to appear. That’s why I suggest solidifying the center with ...c6. One bonus of ...c6 is that it frees our c8-bishop - we’ll be able to play ...Bf5 before White goes Bd3.} 6...c6 7.e3 {7.e3 is the most natural move, enabling the development the light-squared bishop. And since White’s dark-squared bishop is already outside the chain, e3 is even more appealing. But we should exploit the fact that we can enter the h7–b1 diagonal first. If we trade light-squared bishops, we’re happy, our pawn structure already forms a strong light-square complex.} 7...Bf5 8.Be2 {8.Be2 is a natural move, avoiding the bishop trade. Without the exchange, our bishop on f5 becomes very strong, controlling the h7–b1 diagonal. We calmly develop our b-knight to d7 - Be2 is passive and poses no immediate issues.} 8...Nbd7 9.O-O {White castles. We continue normal development, placing our bishop on the diagonal toward h2. White’s setup isn’t challenging, and the pin on f6 means little if d5 isn’t under pressure.} 9...Bd6 10.Rc1 {The rook comes to c1, becoming active on the semi-open c-file. We can now get our king to safety by castling short.} 10...O-O 11.Qb3 {If the queen comes to attack the base of our pawn chain...} 11...Rb8 {The cool, calm and collected 11...Rb8 defends the important b7 pawn. We'll continue with moves like ...Qc7 (always wary of not losing a pawn with Nxd5) and ...Re8. We don't have any weaknesses that White could attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 1: White Plays 6.Bg5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/80447d14-d10c-4b92-833a-faf6e7da58f2/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 2! Some players like to exchange pawns with cxd5. If this happens, does it make our 3...a6 idea useless? Not at all. In fact, cxd5 often helps us develop, many players capture because they’re afraid of our ...a6–b5 idea! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with ours.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen's Gambit. These first moves are the same as the previous chapter. As before, we decline it with 2...e6.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight to c3, increasing pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, keeping all our ideas of ...b5, holding the pawn, and building queenside counterplay.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White may avoid the tricky 4.Nf3 and choose the “safe” approach with 4.cxd5. Whatever the reason, we recapture - and we get an added bonus: our c8-bishop will no longer be a problem piece. In many QGD lines, that bishop suffers, but with the diagonal open, development becomes effortless.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, reinforcing central control. We continue with simple principles: develop our knight to f6 first, supporting d5 and avoiding Qb3 tactics before activating our bishops.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {White plays 6.Bg5, a typical QGD move. The pin on our knight removes a defender of d5, and Qb3 ideas start to appear. That’s why I suggest solidifying the center with ...c6. One bonus of ...c6 is that it frees our c8-bishop - we’ll be able to play ...Bf5 before White goes Bd3.} 6...c6 7.e3 {7.e3 is the most natural move, enabling the development the light-squared bishop. And since White’s dark-squared bishop is already outside the chain, e3 is even more appealing. But we should exploit the fact that we can enter the h7–b1 diagonal first. If we trade light-squared bishops, we’re happy, our pawn structure already forms a strong light-square complex.} 7...Bf5 8.Bd3 {White plays 8.Bd3, proposing a trade. And as mentioned, we should absolutely accept it. In QGD structures, Black gladly trades the light-squared bishop.} 8...Bxd3 9.Qxd3 {White recaptures with the queen, activating it. One of the queen’s main ideas is to support the upcoming e4 push. We continue developing with 9...Nbd7, supporting our f6-knight.} 9...Nbd7 10.O-O {Castling is the slower - but better - approach. Getting the king safe first is more logical than rushing e4 and walking into ...Bb4+. Since e4 is coming, we also want to develop and get our king out of the center.} 10...Be7 11.Rfe1 {White places the rook on e1 to prepare the e-pawn push. Thus we get our king out of the center quickly by castling.} 11...O-O 12.e4 {When e4 comes... We take on e4 and leave White with an isolated queen’s pawn.} 12...dxe4 13.Nxe4 {White must recapture with the knight. We welcome the trades, it is well known that exchanges favor the side playing against the IQP.} 13...Nxe4 14.Bxe7 {White trades on e7, as our bishop and queen were pressuring g5. We recapture.} 14...Qxe7 15.Rxe4 {White recaptures on e4 with tempo on our queen. We escape the threat by centralizing it to d6, hitting d5 and keeping the IQP under pressure.} 15...Qd6 16.Rh4 {White may try to play actively with their rook, attacking h7. This is exactly what IQP players must do: create activity. We simply defend h7 by pushing the h-pawn. It becomes considerably tougher to target the kingside pawns after 16...h6.} 16...h6 17.Ng5 {If the knight tries to be cool by coming to g5 and threaten checkmate with the queen, by all means, don't take! Because that's checkmate with the queen and rook!} 17...Nf6 {Just defend against checkmate with the knight. Up next, you should play moves like Rae8 and take control of the open e-file! Black has a small edge out of the opening because of a better pawn structure (no IQP), a robust kingside defense, and the control of the e-file coming up.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 1: White Plays 6.Bg5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/80447d14-d10c-4b92-833a-faf6e7da58f2/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 2! Some players like to exchange pawns with cxd5. If this happens, does it make our 3...a6 idea useless? Not at all. In fact, cxd5 often helps us develop, many players capture because they’re afraid of our ...a6–b5 idea! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with ours.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen's Gambit. These first moves are the same as the previous chapter. As before, we decline it with 2...e6.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight to c3, increasing pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, keeping all our ideas of ...b5, holding the pawn, and building queenside counterplay.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White may avoid the tricky 4.Nf3 and choose the “safe” approach with 4.cxd5. Whatever the reason, we recapture - and we get an added bonus: our c8-bishop will no longer be a problem piece. In many QGD lines, that bishop suffers, but with the diagonal open, development becomes effortless.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, reinforcing central control. We continue with simple principles: develop our knight to f6 first, supporting d5 and avoiding Qb3 tactics before activating our bishops.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {White plays 6.Bg5, a typical QGD move. The pin on our knight removes a defender of d5, and Qb3 ideas start to appear. That’s why I suggest solidifying the center with ...c6. One bonus of ...c6 is that it frees our c8-bishop - we’ll be able to play ...Bf5 before White goes Bd3.} 6...c6 7.e3 {7.e3 is the most natural move, enabling the development the light-squared bishop. And since White’s dark-squared bishop is already outside the chain, e3 is even more appealing. But we should exploit the fact that we can enter the h7–b1 diagonal first. If we trade light-squared bishops, we’re happy, our pawn structure already forms a strong light-square complex.} 7...Bf5 8.Qb3 {Some players, whenever you go …Bf5 in Queen’s Pawn games, like to place their queen on b3 to pressure the now undefended b7-pawn. But since we already played …c6 to defend d5, all we need to do is protect b7 - and we can do that easily. Thus, we play 8…b5, achieving a strong queenside pawn structure and neutralizing the threat.} 8...b5 9.Be2 {9.Be2 is simple and reasonable development. Here we just bring out our queenside knight.} 9...Nbd7 10.O-O {White castles kingside. Our bishop comes to d6. Once again, both of our bishops are more active than White’s in this position.} 10...Bd6 11.Rac1 {White places a rook on the c-file, x-raying the c6-pawn. We castle as well - c6 isn’t under direct attack yet, so we have time to bring the king to safety.} 11...O-O 12.Nd1 {12.Nd1 reveals an attack on the c6-pawn.} 12...Rc8 {But after 12...Rc8, we're defending our only weakness, our bishops are better, and the burden is on White to try to trade one of the bishops. Moves like Bd3 or Bf4 come to mind. If Bd3, we can play ...Be4. If Bf4, we can keep the bishop with ...Be7 and play ...Re8.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 1: White Plays 6.Bg5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/80447d14-d10c-4b92-833a-faf6e7da58f2/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 2! Some players like to exchange pawns with cxd5. If this happens, does it make our 3...a6 idea useless? Not at all. In fact, cxd5 often helps us develop, many players capture because they’re afraid of our ...a6–b5 idea! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with ours.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen's Gambit. These first moves are the same as the previous chapter. As before, we decline it with 2...e6.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight to c3, increasing pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, keeping all our ideas of ...b5, holding the pawn, and building queenside counterplay.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White may avoid the tricky 4.Nf3 and choose the “safe” approach with 4.cxd5. Whatever the reason, we recapture - and we get an added bonus: our c8-bishop will no longer be a problem piece. In many QGD lines, that bishop suffers, but with the diagonal open, development becomes effortless.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, reinforcing central control. We continue with simple principles: develop our knight to f6 first, supporting d5 and avoiding Qb3 tactics before activating our bishops.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {White plays 6.Bg5, a typical QGD move. The pin on our knight removes a defender of d5, and Qb3 ideas start to appear. That’s why I suggest solidifying the center with ...c6. One bonus of ...c6 is that it frees our c8-bishop - we’ll be able to play ...Bf5 before White goes Bd3.} 6...c6 7.e3 {7.e3 is the most natural move, enabling the development the light-squared bishop. And since White’s dark-squared bishop is already outside the chain, e3 is even more appealing. But we should exploit the fact that we can enter the h7–b1 diagonal first. If we trade light-squared bishops, we’re happy, our pawn structure already forms a strong light-square complex.} 7...Bf5 8.Qb3 {Some players, whenever you go …Bf5 in Queen’s Pawn games, like to place their queen on b3 to pressure the now undefended b7-pawn. But since we already played …c6 to defend d5, all we need to do is protect b7 - and we can do that easily. Thus, we play 8…b5, achieving a strong queenside pawn structure and neutralizing the threat.} 8...b5 9.Ne5 {We’ve discussed the Ne5 idea before, but it’s worth repeating: from e5 the knight pressures c6 and supports a potential Bd3. We simply develop the bishop to d6 and prepare to castle.} 9...Bd6 10.Bd3 {If the bishop comes to d3, we can trade and set a small trap. We take on d3.} 10...Bxd3 11.Nxd3 {The knight recaptures. We castle immediately. This often tempts White to castle as well, and if they do, 12.0-0 runs straight into Bxh2+!} 11...O-O 12.O-O {Many players will castle here automatically. And now we have the classic Greek Gift sacrifice on h2.} 12...Bxh2+ 13.Kxh2 {After Kxh2... We play the discovered attack 13...Ng4+, and the bishop on d3 will fall next.} 13...Ng4+ 14.Kg1 {White's best bet is to hide away on g1.} 14...Qxg5 {After which we can recover our piece with interest, and we're up a pawn with a strong attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 1: White Plays 6.Bg5 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/80447d14-d10c-4b92-833a-faf6e7da58f2/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 2! Some players like to exchange pawns with cxd5. If this happens, does it make our 3...a6 idea useless? Not at all. In fact, cxd5 often helps us develop, many players capture because they’re afraid of our ...a6–b5 idea! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with ours.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen's Gambit. These first moves are the same as the previous chapter. As before, we decline it with 2...e6.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight to c3, increasing pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, keeping all our ideas of ...b5, holding the pawn, and building queenside counterplay.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White may avoid the tricky 4.Nf3 and choose the “safe” approach with 4.cxd5. Whatever the reason, we recapture - and we get an added bonus: our c8-bishop will no longer be a problem piece. In many QGD lines, that bishop suffers, but with the diagonal open, development becomes effortless.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, reinforcing central control. We continue with simple principles: develop our knight to f6 first, supporting d5 and avoiding Qb3 tactics before activating our bishops.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {White plays 6.Bg5, a typical QGD move. The pin on our knight removes a defender of d5, and Qb3 ideas start to appear. That’s why I suggest solidifying the center with ...c6. One bonus of ...c6 is that it frees our c8-bishop - we’ll be able to play ...Bf5 before White goes Bd3.} 6...c6 7.Bxf6 {This capture, after we’ve played …c6 to reinforce d5, lacks toughness. We simply recapture and develop our queen.} 7...Qxf6 8.e4 {8.e4 attacks our d5-pawn, but it won’t help White. It opens the position, which favors us because we have the bishop pair. Pawn exchanges benefit our bishops, so 8...dxe4 is strong. Tactically, after 9.Nxe4, we also have 9...Bb4+.} 8...dxe4 9.Nxe4 {When the knight recaptures, it leaves the c3-square, opening the diagonal for our bishop. With 9...Bb4+ we develop and probe the dark squares.} 9...Bb4+ 10.Nc3 {White has nothing better than to retreat to block the check...} 10...O-O {After which we can just castle kingside. We've castled before White, and we're playing ...Be6 and ...Nd7, with moves like ...Rfe8 coming in the future.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 1: White Plays 6.Bg5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/80447d14-d10c-4b92-833a-faf6e7da58f2/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 2! Some players like to exchange pawns with cxd5. If this happens, does it make our 3...a6 idea useless? Not at all. In fact, cxd5 often helps us develop, many players capture because they’re afraid of our ...a6–b5 idea! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with ours.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen's Gambit. These first moves are the same as the previous chapter. As before, we decline it with 2...e6.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight to c3, increasing pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, keeping all our ideas of ...b5, holding the pawn, and building queenside counterplay.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White may avoid the tricky 4.Nf3 and choose the “safe” approach with 4.cxd5. Whatever the reason, we recapture - and we get an added bonus: our c8-bishop will no longer be a problem piece. In many QGD lines, that bishop suffers, but with the diagonal open, development becomes effortless.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, reinforcing central control. We continue with simple principles: develop our knight to f6 first, supporting d5 and avoiding Qb3 tactics before activating our bishops.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {White plays 6.Bg5, a typical QGD move. The pin on our knight removes a defender of d5, and Qb3 ideas start to appear. That’s why I suggest solidifying the center with ...c6. One bonus of ...c6 is that it frees our c8-bishop - we’ll be able to play ...Bf5 before White goes Bd3.} 6...c6 7.e4 {7.e4, immediately challenging the d5-pawn, is also popular, but it shouldn’t worry us. We simply capture the pawn, and after the knight recaptures, our f8-bishop develops more easily.} 7...dxe4 8.Nxe4 {White recaptures, putting pressure on f6… But our bishop checks on b4, forcing White to block with a piece.} 8...Bb4+ 9.Nc3 {When the knight comes back to c3...} 9...O-O {We can castle, with ideas of Re8 and Be6. Black is fighting against the IQP, so we should look to control the d5 square and take advantage of our e-file pressure.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 1: White Plays 6.Bg5 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/80447d14-d10c-4b92-833a-faf6e7da58f2/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 2! Some players like to exchange pawns with cxd5. If this happens, does it make our 3...a6 idea useless? Not at all. In fact, cxd5 often helps us develop, many players capture because they’re afraid of our ...a6–b5 idea! White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with ours.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen's Gambit. These first moves are the same as the previous chapter. As before, we decline it with 2...e6.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight to c3, increasing pressure on d5. We respond with 3...a6, keeping all our ideas of ...b5, holding the pawn, and building queenside counterplay.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White may avoid the tricky 4.Nf3 and choose the “safe” approach with 4.cxd5. Whatever the reason, we recapture - and we get an added bonus: our c8-bishop will no longer be a problem piece. In many QGD lines, that bishop suffers, but with the diagonal open, development becomes effortless.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, reinforcing central control. We continue with simple principles: develop our knight to f6 first, supporting d5 and avoiding Qb3 tactics before activating our bishops.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {White plays 6.Bg5, a typical QGD move. The pin on our knight removes a defender of d5, and Qb3 ideas start to appear. That’s why I suggest solidifying the center with ...c6. One bonus of ...c6 is that it frees our c8-bishop - we’ll be able to play ...Bf5 before White goes Bd3.} 6...c6 7.Qb3 {7.Qb3, played after we’ve reinforced d5 with …c6, is another futile attempt to create pressure. With d5 covered, we simply play 7...b5, solving any b7 issues. This gives us a strong queenside pawn formation that dominates the light squares, while our bishop is freed now that b7 is no longer a weakness.} 7...b5 8.e3 {White plays e3 to develop their light-squared bishop, as they often do.} 8...Bf5 {And we can get our bishop out to f5, controlling e4 and d3, which are squares White would like to control themselves. One of the best ideas White has now is placing their knight on e5, followed by Bd3 at some point. If they don't find that, our development is simple: ...Bd6 and ...0-0.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bf4 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/7759150f-4a1a-4d9d-baa0-a1b7610fec23/lines"]

1.d4 {In this second study, we examine what happens when White trades on d5 and develops the bishop to f4 instead of g5. Against this setup, we immediately challenge that bishop with 6...Bd6, proposing a trade. Our goal is to simplify, develop quickly, and reach a sound position. White starts with the queen’s pawn. We mirror.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We decline as usual.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, once again putting pressure on d5. We follow with 3...a6, with the familiar ideas of preparing ...b5 and restricting White’s pieces from using b5.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {To avoid our ...dxc4 ideas, White may take on d5. We recapture, opening the diagonal for our traditionally problematic c8-bishop.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight, increasing central control. We answer with 5...Nf6, overprotecting d5 and keeping Bg5/Qb3 ideas under control.} 5...Nf6 6.Bf4 {In this line White plays the natural 6.Bf4. One of the best reactions is simply to challenge that bishop. That’s why I like 6...Bd6, proposing a trade and getting quick development.} 6...Bd6 7.e3 {The last option is 7.e3, opening the diagonal for the f1-bishop and defending the bishop on f4 with a pawn. White likely wants to meet …Bf5 with Bd3 and trade bishops, but this setup doesn’t challenge us at all. We activate our bishop to f5 once again.} 7...Bf5 8.Qb3 {White may also go for the b7-pawn. Here I recommend the very tricky 8…Nc6.} 8...Nc6 9.Bxd6 {White may try to eliminate our bishop first. After we recapture, our queen no longer protects the a8-rook.} 9...Qxd6 10.Qxb7 {So can they take the pawn now? Not really - the queen is still nearly trapped, and White loses too much time trying to save it. We castle our king.} 10...O-O 11.Qb3 {When White finally retreats the queen... We slide our a-rook to b8, attacking it and x-raying the b2-pawn.} 11...Rab8 12.Qd1 {If the queen retreats to its home square...} 12...Rxb2 {Not only do we recover the pawn, but we activate all our pieces on the queenside. If Be2, ...Qb4. If Rc1, ...Nb4 for ...Nc2+. White is totally lost.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bf4 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/7759150f-4a1a-4d9d-baa0-a1b7610fec23/lines"]

1.d4 {In this second study, we examine what happens when White trades on d5 and develops the bishop to f4 instead of g5. Against this setup, we immediately challenge that bishop with 6...Bd6, proposing a trade. Our goal is to simplify, develop quickly, and reach a sound position. White starts with the queen’s pawn. We mirror.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We decline as usual.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, once again putting pressure on d5. We follow with 3...a6, with the familiar ideas of preparing ...b5 and restricting White’s pieces from using b5.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {To avoid our ...dxc4 ideas, White may take on d5. We recapture, opening the diagonal for our traditionally problematic c8-bishop.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight, increasing central control. We answer with 5...Nf6, overprotecting d5 and keeping Bg5/Qb3 ideas under control.} 5...Nf6 6.Bf4 {In this line White plays the natural 6.Bf4. One of the best reactions is simply to challenge that bishop. That’s why I like 6...Bd6, proposing a trade and getting quick development.} 6...Bd6 7.e3 {The last option is 7.e3, opening the diagonal for the f1-bishop and defending the bishop on f4 with a pawn. White likely wants to meet …Bf5 with Bd3 and trade bishops, but this setup doesn’t challenge us at all. We activate our bishop to f5 once again.} 7...Bf5 8.Qb3 {White may also go for the b7-pawn. Here I recommend the very tricky 8…Nc6.} 8...Nc6 9.Bxd6 {White may try to eliminate our bishop first. After we recapture, our queen no longer protects the a8-rook.} 9...Qxd6 10.Qxb7 {So can they take the pawn now? Not really - the queen is still nearly trapped, and White loses too much time trying to save it. We castle our king.} 10...O-O 11.Qb3 {When White finally retreats the queen... We slide our a-rook to b8, attacking it and x-raying the b2-pawn.} 11...Rab8 12.Qa3 {12.Qa3 is another logical try, defending b2 and proposing a queen trade.} 12...Nb4 {But we've got the c2-square for the knight. If Rc1, ...Nc2+ wins the exchange. If 0-0-0, ...Rfc8 is strong, for ...c5. White is lost here as well.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bf4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/7759150f-4a1a-4d9d-baa0-a1b7610fec23/lines"]

1.d4 {In this second study, we examine what happens when White trades on d5 and develops the bishop to f4 instead of g5. Against this setup, we immediately challenge that bishop with 6...Bd6, proposing a trade. Our goal is to simplify, develop quickly, and reach a sound position. White starts with the queen’s pawn. We mirror.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We decline as usual.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, once again putting pressure on d5. We follow with 3...a6, with the familiar ideas of preparing ...b5 and restricting White’s pieces from using b5.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {To avoid our ...dxc4 ideas, White may take on d5. We recapture, opening the diagonal for our traditionally problematic c8-bishop.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight, increasing central control. We answer with 5...Nf6, overprotecting d5 and keeping Bg5/Qb3 ideas under control.} 5...Nf6 6.Bf4 {In this line White plays the natural 6.Bf4. One of the best reactions is simply to challenge that bishop. That’s why I like 6...Bd6, proposing a trade and getting quick development.} 6...Bd6 7.Bg3 {Many players familiar with London-style positions will drop the bishop back to g3, hoping that if we take on g3, hxg3 activates the rook on the h-file. We should avoid helping White - so instead of taking, we castle and activate our own rook.} 7...O-O 8.e3 {e3 is a very common move in structures where the bishop went to f4. White only needs to develop the light-squared bishop, so e3 is enough. But we answer just in time with 8...Bf5, preventing White from taking over key diagonals.} 8...Bf5 9.Qb3 {If White targets b7 immediately with the queen... 9...Nc6 is a tricky response because of the queen trap on b7.} 9...Nc6 10.Qxb7 {Once again, this cannot be played.} 10...Na5 {The queen would fall.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bf4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/7759150f-4a1a-4d9d-baa0-a1b7610fec23/lines"]

1.d4 {In this second study, we examine what happens when White trades on d5 and develops the bishop to f4 instead of g5. Against this setup, we immediately challenge that bishop with 6...Bd6, proposing a trade. Our goal is to simplify, develop quickly, and reach a sound position. White starts with the queen’s pawn. We mirror.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We decline as usual.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, once again putting pressure on d5. We follow with 3...a6, with the familiar ideas of preparing ...b5 and restricting White’s pieces from using b5.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {To avoid our ...dxc4 ideas, White may take on d5. We recapture, opening the diagonal for our traditionally problematic c8-bishop.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight, increasing central control. We answer with 5...Nf6, overprotecting d5 and keeping Bg5/Qb3 ideas under control.} 5...Nf6 6.Bf4 {In this line White plays the natural 6.Bf4. One of the best reactions is simply to challenge that bishop. That’s why I like 6...Bd6, proposing a trade and getting quick development.} 6...Bd6 7.Bg3 {Many players familiar with London-style positions will drop the bishop back to g3, hoping that if we take on g3, hxg3 activates the rook on the h-file. We should avoid helping White - so instead of taking, we castle and activate our own rook.} 7...O-O 8.e3 {e3 is a very common move in structures where the bishop went to f4. White only needs to develop the light-squared bishop, so e3 is enough. But we answer just in time with 8...Bf5, preventing White from taking over key diagonals.} 8...Bf5 9.Qb3 {If White targets b7 immediately with the queen... 9...Nc6 is a tricky response because of the queen trap on b7.} 9...Nc6 10.Be2 {Instead of taking on b7, White may opt for simple development once they realize they cannot win any pawns. In that case, we capture on g3 and bring our queen to d6, securing control over the dark squares.} 10...Bxg3 11.hxg3 {White would recapture.} 11...Qd6 {And our control is quite nice. Our bishop is on f5 so h7 has some reinforcement. We've got sound moves like ...b6 and ...g6 to safeguard our weak points. If Qxb7 here, the queen still gets trapped with ...Na5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bf4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/7759150f-4a1a-4d9d-baa0-a1b7610fec23/lines"]

1.d4 {In this second study, we examine what happens when White trades on d5 and develops the bishop to f4 instead of g5. Against this setup, we immediately challenge that bishop with 6...Bd6, proposing a trade. Our goal is to simplify, develop quickly, and reach a sound position. White starts with the queen’s pawn. We mirror.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We decline as usual.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, once again putting pressure on d5. We follow with 3...a6, with the familiar ideas of preparing ...b5 and restricting White’s pieces from using b5.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {To avoid our ...dxc4 ideas, White may take on d5. We recapture, opening the diagonal for our traditionally problematic c8-bishop.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight, increasing central control. We answer with 5...Nf6, overprotecting d5 and keeping Bg5/Qb3 ideas under control.} 5...Nf6 6.Bf4 {In this line White plays the natural 6.Bf4. One of the best reactions is simply to challenge that bishop. That’s why I like 6...Bd6, proposing a trade and getting quick development.} 6...Bd6 7.e3 {The last option is 7.e3, opening the diagonal for the f1-bishop and defending the bishop on f4 with a pawn. White likely wants to meet …Bf5 with Bd3 and trade bishops, but this setup doesn’t challenge us at all. We activate our bishop to f5 once again.} 7...Bf5 8.Qb3 {White may also go for the b7-pawn. Here I recommend the very tricky 8…Nc6.} 8...Nc6 9.Qxb7 {If White takes thinking that ...Rb8 cannot be played, they would be right.} 9...Na5 {But 9...Na5 can be played and the queen doesn't have any squares!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bf4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/7759150f-4a1a-4d9d-baa0-a1b7610fec23/lines"]

1.d4 {In this second study, we examine what happens when White trades on d5 and develops the bishop to f4 instead of g5. Against this setup, we immediately challenge that bishop with 6...Bd6, proposing a trade. Our goal is to simplify, develop quickly, and reach a sound position. White starts with the queen’s pawn. We mirror.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We decline as usual.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, once again putting pressure on d5. We follow with 3...a6, with the familiar ideas of preparing ...b5 and restricting White’s pieces from using b5.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {To avoid our ...dxc4 ideas, White may take on d5. We recapture, opening the diagonal for our traditionally problematic c8-bishop.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight, increasing central control. We answer with 5...Nf6, overprotecting d5 and keeping Bg5/Qb3 ideas under control.} 5...Nf6 6.Bf4 {In this line White plays the natural 6.Bf4. One of the best reactions is simply to challenge that bishop. That’s why I like 6...Bd6, proposing a trade and getting quick development.} 6...Bd6 7.e3 {The last option is 7.e3, opening the diagonal for the f1-bishop and defending the bishop on f4 with a pawn. White likely wants to meet …Bf5 with Bd3 and trade bishops, but this setup doesn’t challenge us at all. We activate our bishop to f5 once again.} 7...Bf5 8.Bd3 {This time White wants to trade immediately. We don’t mind - developing the light-squared bishop is often difficult for Black in QGD positions anyway.} 8...Bxd3 9.Qxd3 {Once the queen takes...} 9...O-O {We get our king to safety. White has given us everything we need out of the opening; we are developed and castled, with no real weaknesses. Next, we'll reinforce the center with ...c6, and after dealing with the dark-squared bishop tension, develop the knight and centralize the rooks.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bf4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/7759150f-4a1a-4d9d-baa0-a1b7610fec23/lines"]

1.d4 {In this second study, we examine what happens when White trades on d5 and develops the bishop to f4 instead of g5. Against this setup, we immediately challenge that bishop with 6...Bd6, proposing a trade. Our goal is to simplify, develop quickly, and reach a sound position. White starts with the queen’s pawn. We mirror.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We decline as usual.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, once again putting pressure on d5. We follow with 3...a6, with the familiar ideas of preparing ...b5 and restricting White’s pieces from using b5.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {To avoid our ...dxc4 ideas, White may take on d5. We recapture, opening the diagonal for our traditionally problematic c8-bishop.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight, increasing central control. We answer with 5...Nf6, overprotecting d5 and keeping Bg5/Qb3 ideas under control.} 5...Nf6 6.Bf4 {In this line White plays the natural 6.Bf4. One of the best reactions is simply to challenge that bishop. That’s why I like 6...Bd6, proposing a trade and getting quick development.} 6...Bd6 7.Bxd6 {The most common response is for White to trade. We recapture with the queen, placing it actively on d6.} 7...Qxd6 8.e3 {White plays the usual e3. And once again, we develop our light-squared bishop faster than White with ...Bf5.} 8...Bf5 9.Bd3 {White immediately looks for a trade of light-squared bishops, which is no problem for us. We accept the trade.} 9...Bxd3 10.Qxd3 {White recaptures, activating the queen. We castle.} 10...O-O 11.O-O {And so does our opponent.} 11...Nbd7 {After 11...Nbd7 the development is complete. As per usual, we will reinforce the center with ...c6 next. In the future, we'll bring the rooks to the center and possibly try to jump into e4 with our knight! This line just goes to show that sometimes chess can be very calm, with many trades and balanced, equal positions.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 2: White Plays 6.Bf4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/7759150f-4a1a-4d9d-baa0-a1b7610fec23/lines"]

1.d4 {In this second study, we examine what happens when White trades on d5 and develops the bishop to f4 instead of g5. Against this setup, we immediately challenge that bishop with 6...Bd6, proposing a trade. Our goal is to simplify, develop quickly, and reach a sound position. White starts with the queen’s pawn. We mirror.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We decline as usual.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, once again putting pressure on d5. We follow with 3...a6, with the familiar ideas of preparing ...b5 and restricting White’s pieces from using b5.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {To avoid our ...dxc4 ideas, White may take on d5. We recapture, opening the diagonal for our traditionally problematic c8-bishop.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight, increasing central control. We answer with 5...Nf6, overprotecting d5 and keeping Bg5/Qb3 ideas under control.} 5...Nf6 6.Bf4 {In this line White plays the natural 6.Bf4. One of the best reactions is simply to challenge that bishop. That’s why I like 6...Bd6, proposing a trade and getting quick development.} 6...Bd6 7.Bg5 {Some players who don’t want to trade will move the bishop again to g5, pinning our knight and threatening to win d5. We simply reinforce the center with 7…c6, forming the familiar pawn chain.} 7...c6 8.e3 {With 8.e3, White opens the door for their own bishop on f1...} 8...Bf5 {...But before they get to develop it, we jump in with ...Bf5 and control the d3-square, as in the Bg3 line! We are ready to castle and develop the knight from b8. If White tries to be aggressive with moves like Qb3 now, ...b5 is very strong again. As mentioned, some of the themes we play are repetitive, which makes them important to remember!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 3: White Plays 6.e3 or 6.g3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/c8cb7070-418e-44dd-8781-51f526d2f120/lines"]

1.d4 {In this third study of Chapter 2, we look at what happens when White skips both 6.Bg5 and 6.Bf4 and instead plays 6.e3 or 6.g3. There are a few simple ideas to remember. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror, aiming for our QGD structure.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit is on the board. We decline it with ...e6, supporting the center.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops with more pressure on d5. As we’ve already learned, 3...a6 is the main idea of this course - preparing to capture on c4 and support the pawn with ...b5.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {But here White prevents that and captures on d5 first. We recapture, activating our light-squared bishop, which is the biggest benefit for Black when White plays cxd5 early.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {The knight jumps to its natural f3-square... We also continue developing with 5...Nf6, and now the fun begins.} 5...Nf6 6.g3 {Another line is 6.g3, aiming to fianchetto the light-squared bishop instead of developing it normally. We’ll eventually push ...c6 to reinforce the center. First, we bring the bishop out to e7 and prepare to castle.} 6...Be7 7.Bg2 {White continues with their plan, also preparing to castle. Before the battle begins, both kings must be safe, so we castle short.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White castles too. So we implement ...c6 idea, creating a solid pawn chain and stabilizing the center.} 8...c6 9.Re1 {The rook comes to the e-file, hinting at a future e4. We continue developing for now, 9...Nbd7 is our next move. Our plan in this variation is to place the knight on f8, from where it helps defend the kingside and can reroute to g6.} 9...Nbd7 10.Bf4 {White activates the dark-squared bishop before pushing e4, which is one of the key benefits of playing 6.g3. We stay with our idea: activating our rook on the semi-open e-file and freeing the f8-square for the knight.} 10...Re8 11.a3 {White's 11.a3 prevents a possible ...Bb4, pinning the knight to the rook.} 11...Nf8 {We finish our knight maneuver by landing it on f8. Notice we’ve delayed developing our light-squared bishop - it can easily become a target of Ng5 or Nh4. But once the knight reaches g6, Nh4 won’t be possible, as we can trade. Only then do we bring the bishop out to f5!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 3: White Plays 6.e3 or 6.g3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/c8cb7070-418e-44dd-8781-51f526d2f120/lines"]

1.d4 {In this third study of Chapter 2, we look at what happens when White skips both 6.Bg5 and 6.Bf4 and instead plays 6.e3 or 6.g3. There are a few simple ideas to remember. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror, aiming for our QGD structure.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit is on the board. We decline it with ...e6, supporting the center.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops with more pressure on d5. As we’ve already learned, 3...a6 is the main idea of this course - preparing to capture on c4 and support the pawn with ...b5.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {But here White prevents that and captures on d5 first. We recapture, activating our light-squared bishop, which is the biggest benefit for Black when White plays cxd5 early.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {The knight jumps to its natural f3-square... We also continue developing with 5...Nf6, and now the fun begins.} 5...Nf6 6.e3 {One sixth-move option for White is 6.e3. White wants to develop the light-squared bishop naturally and strengthen the center, but this also shuts in the dark-squared bishop. We take advantage of the open diagonal and develop our bishop to f5 so it won’t be stuck anymore.} 6...Bf5 7.Bd3 {Instead of launching queen pressure, White may try to trade our active light-squared bishop with 7.Bd3. Trading is completely fine - it makes our development smooth and leaves our position free of problems.} 7...Bxd3 8.Qxd3 {White recaptures. Since they already played 6.e3, we don’t have to worry about Bg5 ideas. That’s why 8...Bd6 is the best developing move for our dark-squared bishop.} 8...Bd6 9.O-O {Once White brings the king to safety...} 9...O-O {We do the same. While White might try to push e4 at some point and activate the dark-squared bishop, we will counter that by either taking or playing ...c6. Then, we will develop our knight and enjoy a comfortable position!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 3: White Plays 6.e3 or 6.g3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/c8cb7070-418e-44dd-8781-51f526d2f120/lines"]

1.d4 {In this third study of Chapter 2, we look at what happens when White skips both 6.Bg5 and 6.Bf4 and instead plays 6.e3 or 6.g3. There are a few simple ideas to remember. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror, aiming for our QGD structure.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen's Gambit is on the board. We decline it with ...e6, supporting the center.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops with more pressure on d5. As we’ve already learned, 3...a6 is the main idea of this course - preparing to capture on c4 and support the pawn with ...b5.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {But here White prevents that and captures on d5 first. We recapture, activating our light-squared bishop, which is the biggest benefit for Black when White plays cxd5 early.} 4...exd5 5.Nf3 {The knight jumps to its natural f3-square... We also continue developing with 5...Nf6, and now the fun begins.} 5...Nf6 6.e3 {One sixth-move option for White is 6.e3. White wants to develop the light-squared bishop naturally and strengthen the center, but this also shuts in the dark-squared bishop. We take advantage of the open diagonal and develop our bishop to f5 so it won’t be stuck anymore.} 6...Bf5 7.Qb3 {However, that does leave the b7-pawn undefended and vulnerable to various queen attacks...} 7...b5 {But we simply go with our standard approach and push ...b5! In the future, we might support our structure with ...c6, develop our knight to d7, move our dark-squared bishop and castle. On the other hand, White has blocked their dark-squared bishop in, and now has trouble developing the light-squared one too! The only available square is e2.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 4: Other White 5th Moves / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/d695c056-8741-43a9-8645-03356107366b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 4 of Chapter 2 in our QGD course. Here we examine White’s 5th-move deviations, such as 5.Bf4, 5.e3, and 5.g3. In the 5.Bf4 line, White can even avoid Nf3 entirely! White begins with 1.d4, taking space in the center. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our center.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops naturally, adding pressure to d5. We once again play our thematic 3...a6, dreaming of ...dxc4 and ...b5 - but White has other plans.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White tries to neutralize 3...a6 by taking on d5, but they only help our development. We recapture on d5, and our light-squared bishop gains a clear path.} 4...exd5 5.e4 {5.e4 is a popular try, but White isn’t ready to open the position. After 5…dxe4, White is left with a weak d4-pawn and must play with an isolated queen’s pawn.} 5...dxe4 6.Nxe4 {White can recapture, of course. But after 6...Qe7, we guarantee trades - always good for the side playing against an IQP.} 6...Qe7 7.Qe2 {White must defend the knight while breaking the pin with 7.Qe2. We develop our knight to c6 with tempo, attacking d4. White has no good way to meet this pressure.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {The natural pawn push only gives us another tempo. We jump into d4, hitting the queen.} 8...Nd4 9.Qd3 {Once the queen retreats to d3 to defend the knight, cover c2, and keep an eye on our knight... We attack the knight with our light-squared bishop. As mentioned, trades favor the side playing against the IQP.} 9...Bf5 10.Qxd4 {White takes on d4. We capture on e4, forcing a queen trade.} 10...Qxe4+ 11.Qxe4+ {White is forced to exchange queens. We recapture, also attacking d5.} 11...Bxe4 12.Bc4 {White cannot defend the pawn.} 12...Bxg2 {Because g2 is unprotected. This should be an easy win for Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 4: Other White 5th Moves / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/d695c056-8741-43a9-8645-03356107366b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 4 of Chapter 2 in our QGD course. Here we examine White’s 5th-move deviations, such as 5.Bf4, 5.e3, and 5.g3. In the 5.Bf4 line, White can even avoid Nf3 entirely! White begins with 1.d4, taking space in the center. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our center.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops naturally, adding pressure to d5. We once again play our thematic 3...a6, dreaming of ...dxc4 and ...b5 - but White has other plans.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White tries to neutralize 3...a6 by taking on d5, but they only help our development. We recapture on d5, and our light-squared bishop gains a clear path.} 4...exd5 5.e4 {5.e4 is a popular try, but White isn’t ready to open the position. After 5…dxe4, White is left with a weak d4-pawn and must play with an isolated queen’s pawn.} 5...dxe4 6.Nxe4 {White can recapture, of course. But after 6...Qe7, we guarantee trades - always good for the side playing against an IQP.} 6...Qe7 7.Qe2 {White must defend the knight while breaking the pin with 7.Qe2. We develop our knight to c6 with tempo, attacking d4. White has no good way to meet this pressure.} 7...Nc6 8.Nf3 {If White tries to simply develop... We’ll get the trades we want, and the IQP will soon come under unbearable pressure. We start by adding pressure on Ne4 with 8...Bf5.} 8...Bf5 9.Nc3 {If White tries to save the knight... The problems only increase after we play 9...Nb4 threatening ...Nc2+. Just to illustrate, suppose White trades here.} 9...Nb4 10.Qxe7+ {Trading queens just accelerates our development.} 10...Nxe7 {We recapture, and ...Nc2+ is still a problem. ...Rd8 is coming, but not before ...Nc2, attacking the rook on a1. White's position is crumbling and most likely castling won't be possible for them.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 4: Other White 5th Moves / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/d695c056-8741-43a9-8645-03356107366b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 4 of Chapter 2 in our QGD course. Here we examine White’s 5th-move deviations, such as 5.Bf4, 5.e3, and 5.g3. In the 5.Bf4 line, White can even avoid Nf3 entirely! White begins with 1.d4, taking space in the center. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our center.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops naturally, adding pressure to d5. We once again play our thematic 3...a6, dreaming of ...dxc4 and ...b5 - but White has other plans.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White tries to neutralize 3...a6 by taking on d5, but they only help our development. We recapture on d5, and our light-squared bishop gains a clear path.} 4...exd5 5.Qb3 {White can choose to attack the d5-pawn and put pressure on b7. We respond with a sound developing move that also defends the pawn, 5...Nf6.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {If White pins our knight... We support the center with ...c6. From here, White has a few natural developing tries.} 6...c6 7.e3 {One is 7.e3, reinforcing d4 and opening the diagonal for the light-squared bishop. But this slow move prevents us from playing …b5 (too committal) and …Bf5 is impossible due to the b7-weakness. When there’s nothing active to do, we follow opening principles and develop calmly, 7...Be7 is our move.} 7...Be7 8.Bd3 {This is one of the few lines where White stops our bishop from reaching f5. We simply develop the pieces we can, now with 8...Nbd7.} 8...Nbd7 9.Nf3 {White will usually continue developing as well. We push h6, forcing the g5-bishop to make a decision. It has three legitimate choices.} 9...h6 10.Bh4 {The most logical is 10.Bh4, keeping influence along the diagonal. Here we castle and prepare …Re8.} 10...O-O 11.O-O {White castles as well. With 11…Re8, we prepare …Ne4, which gives us chances to trade pieces.} 11...Re8 12.Rac1 {In most QGD structures, White places a rook on c1. Now we go for 12…Ne4, with a discovered attack on the bishop on h4 and forcing White to react.} 12...Ne4 13.Bxe7 {The bishop will most often take. We recapture.} 13...Qxe7 14.Bxe4 {White usually trades off our strong e4-knight next. We take with the pawn, forcing the f3-knight away.} 14...dxe4 15.Nd2 {This knight may come to d2, putting pressure on our pawn.} 15...Nf6 {But our knight arrives on f6 right on time. We have ...Bf5 ideas and ...Rad8 to continue our development. In the middlegame, we're looking to exploit the fact that our e4-pawn helps us control White's position. If we can trade our light-squared bishop for a knight, this would also be good since our pawns are mostly on light squares.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 4: Other White 5th Moves / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/d695c056-8741-43a9-8645-03356107366b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 4 of Chapter 2 in our QGD course. Here we examine White’s 5th-move deviations, such as 5.Bf4, 5.e3, and 5.g3. In the 5.Bf4 line, White can even avoid Nf3 entirely! White begins with 1.d4, taking space in the center. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our center.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops naturally, adding pressure to d5. We once again play our thematic 3...a6, dreaming of ...dxc4 and ...b5 - but White has other plans.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White tries to neutralize 3...a6 by taking on d5, but they only help our development. We recapture on d5, and our light-squared bishop gains a clear path.} 4...exd5 5.Bf4 {The most critical choice is 5.Bf4. White delays Nf3, and we’ll soon see why. We simply continue developing and strengthen our center with 5...Nf6.} 5...Nf6 6.e3 {With the dark-squared bishop developed, 6.e3 prepares to develop the light-squared bishop as well. We aim to trade our bishop for White’s active f4-bishop. This line resembles the 6.Bf4 variation, except White hasn’t played Nf3 yet - allowing them tricky alternatives we’ll look at.} 6...Bd6 7.Bxd6 {White chooses to trade bishops, develop quickly and castle. We recapture with the queen.} 7...Qxd6 8.Bd3 {A simple developing move from White, 8.Bd3. We bring our king to safety.} 8...O-O 9.Nge2 {Instead of Nf3, White plays 9.Nge2, planning a knight route toward g3 and maybe f5 if allowed. We activate our rook, discouraging the e4 break.} 9...Re8 10.O-O {Before moving forward with their idea, White castles. We answer with the peculiar-looking 10...Nc6. Developing the knight to c6 isn’t typical here, but White’s setup allows it. The knight pressures d4, making e4 much harder to achieve.} 10...Nc6 11.Ng3 {The knight continues its journey toward g3, probably aiming for f5. Although it weakens our king a little, 11...g6 is necessary - we must stop anything from landing on f5 or h5.} 11...g6 12.Re1 {White activates the rook on the e-file, with plans of playing e4 in the future if possible.} 12...Bd7 {And we complete queenside development with 12...Bd7. White cannot push e4 yet because the d4 pawn would be hanging. Next, we will activate the a8 rook, and sometimes go for the ...h5 push, to try and put pressure on White's oddly placed knight on g3. Our knight could also rotate to e7 and become useful on the kingside, in some positions.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 4: Other White 5th Moves / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/d695c056-8741-43a9-8645-03356107366b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 4 of Chapter 2 in our QGD course. Here we examine White’s 5th-move deviations, such as 5.Bf4, 5.e3, and 5.g3. In the 5.Bf4 line, White can even avoid Nf3 entirely! White begins with 1.d4, taking space in the center. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our center.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops naturally, adding pressure to d5. We once again play our thematic 3...a6, dreaming of ...dxc4 and ...b5 - but White has other plans.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White tries to neutralize 3...a6 by taking on d5, but they only help our development. We recapture on d5, and our light-squared bishop gains a clear path.} 4...exd5 5.g3 {Another line, often interchangeable with 5.e3, is 5.g3. White prepares to fianchetto the light-squared bishop. We develop our knight to f6, adding central support.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg2 {White continues with their plan. As seen in the previous study, ...c6 is often useful against g3/Bg2 structures.} 6...c6 7.Nf3 {White finishes kingside development and prepares to castle. We do the same by playing 7...Bd6.} 7...Bd6 8.O-O {With the king safe, White now begins to think seriously about e4. We also get our king out of the center.} 8...O-O 9.Bg5 {White uses the fact that they haven’t moved the e-pawn yet and pins our knight. We reinforce it with 9...Nbd7, developing.} 9...Nbd7 10.e4 {10.e4 strikes in the center! We must not allow e5 and the fork, so we capture. This gives White the chance to activate their knight…} 10...dxe4 11.Nxe4 {…and they do, now attacking our pinned knight and our bishop simultaneously. 11...Be7 is essentially forced - retreating the bishop and unpinning the knight. After the bishop retreat, we threaten to capture on e4.} 11...Be7 12.Nxf6+ {That's why White trades...} 12...Nxf6 {...And we take back, activating our knight from d7. We have successfully dealt with White's quick development, advancement in the center and kingside aggression! Next, we will develop our light-squared bishop, kick out White from g5 and bring our rooks to the game, having equalized.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 4: Other White 5th Moves / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/d695c056-8741-43a9-8645-03356107366b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 4 of Chapter 2 in our QGD course. Here we examine White’s 5th-move deviations, such as 5.Bf4, 5.e3, and 5.g3. In the 5.Bf4 line, White can even avoid Nf3 entirely! White begins with 1.d4, taking space in the center. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our center.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops naturally, adding pressure to d5. We once again play our thematic 3...a6, dreaming of ...dxc4 and ...b5 - but White has other plans.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White tries to neutralize 3...a6 by taking on d5, but they only help our development. We recapture on d5, and our light-squared bishop gains a clear path.} 4...exd5 5.Qb3 {White can choose to attack the d5-pawn and put pressure on b7. We respond with a sound developing move that also defends the pawn, 5...Nf6.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {If White pins our knight... We support the center with ...c6. From here, White has a few natural developing tries.} 6...c6 7.e3 {One is 7.e3, reinforcing d4 and opening the diagonal for the light-squared bishop. But this slow move prevents us from playing …b5 (too committal) and …Bf5 is impossible due to the b7-weakness. When there’s nothing active to do, we follow opening principles and develop calmly, 7...Be7 is our move.} 7...Be7 8.Bd3 {This is one of the few lines where White stops our bishop from reaching f5. We simply develop the pieces we can, now with 8...Nbd7.} 8...Nbd7 9.Nf3 {White will usually continue developing as well. We push h6, forcing the g5-bishop to make a decision. It has three legitimate choices.} 9...h6 10.Bf4 {The bishop can stay on the board by retreating to f4, keeping an eye on e5. We calmly castle.} 10...O-O 11.O-O {In all likelihood, White will do the same. Since we cannot trade dark-squared bishops, we can aim for it with our knight - 11…Nh5 fits the QGD strategy perfectly, since dark squares are key.} 11...Nh5 12.Bg3 {The bishop may try to keep structure by going to g3. We take.} 12...Nxg3 13.hxg3 {White will recapture.} 13...Nf6 {And now with the knight on f6 we control the e4-square, because White may try to push e4 at some point. We have ideas of ...Bd6 and ...Re8 to improve our pieces, and even a5-a4 to kick the queen out of b3. With the bishop pair, we should have a slightly more comfortable position.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 4: Other White 5th Moves / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/d695c056-8741-43a9-8645-03356107366b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 4 of Chapter 2 in our QGD course. Here we examine White’s 5th-move deviations, such as 5.Bf4, 5.e3, and 5.g3. In the 5.Bf4 line, White can even avoid Nf3 entirely! White begins with 1.d4, taking space in the center. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our center.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops naturally, adding pressure to d5. We once again play our thematic 3...a6, dreaming of ...dxc4 and ...b5 - but White has other plans.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White tries to neutralize 3...a6 by taking on d5, but they only help our development. We recapture on d5, and our light-squared bishop gains a clear path.} 4...exd5 5.Qb3 {White can choose to attack the d5-pawn and put pressure on b7. We respond with a sound developing move that also defends the pawn, 5...Nf6.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {If White pins our knight... We support the center with ...c6. From here, White has a few natural developing tries.} 6...c6 7.e3 {One is 7.e3, reinforcing d4 and opening the diagonal for the light-squared bishop. But this slow move prevents us from playing …b5 (too committal) and …Bf5 is impossible due to the b7-weakness. When there’s nothing active to do, we follow opening principles and develop calmly, 7...Be7 is our move.} 7...Be7 8.Bd3 {This is one of the few lines where White stops our bishop from reaching f5. We simply develop the pieces we can, now with 8...Nbd7.} 8...Nbd7 9.Nf3 {White will usually continue developing as well. We push h6, forcing the g5-bishop to make a decision. It has three legitimate choices.} 9...h6 10.Bxf6 {If White gives up this bishop, we'll gladly accept it.} 10...Nxf6 {The dark-squared bishop is the most important bishop in Queen's Gambit Declined positions because Black has placed many pawns on dark squares. Black can aim for ...0-0, Bd6 and Qc7, controlling the dark squares.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 4: Other White 5th Moves / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/d695c056-8741-43a9-8645-03356107366b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 4 of Chapter 2 in our QGD course. Here we examine White’s 5th-move deviations, such as 5.Bf4, 5.e3, and 5.g3. In the 5.Bf4 line, White can even avoid Nf3 entirely! White begins with 1.d4, taking space in the center. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our center.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops naturally, adding pressure to d5. We once again play our thematic 3...a6, dreaming of ...dxc4 and ...b5 - but White has other plans.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White tries to neutralize 3...a6 by taking on d5, but they only help our development. We recapture on d5, and our light-squared bishop gains a clear path.} 4...exd5 5.Qb3 {White can choose to attack the d5-pawn and put pressure on b7. We respond with a sound developing move that also defends the pawn, 5...Nf6.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {If White pins our knight... We support the center with ...c6. From here, White has a few natural developing tries.} 6...c6 7.e4 {White can try to break in the center with 7.e4. Some aggressive players think the pin makes this possible. We simply take, there’s no reason to fear Nxe4 or Bc4.} 7...dxe4 8.Bc4 {If Bc4 appears, targeting f7... We defend both pawns with the calm 8...Qe7.} 8...Qe7 9.a4 {White is advised to play a4 to stop b5. Our reply is to kick the bishop off the diagonal with 9...h6.} 9...h6 10.Bxf6 {White will often take, damaging our pawns. But since we are a pawn up, this is acceptable. We recapture with the g-pawn and then follow with …f5 and a kingside fianchetto, controlling central squares. It’s White who must find a way to challenge our e4-pawn.} 10...gxf6 11.Nge2 {After White develops their other knight... We support the e4-pawn with 11...f5. The structure may not be pretty, but our opening problems are solved. With …Bg7 coming, our entire kingside becomes developed.} 11...f5 12.O-O {White castles. We fianchetto and begin pressuring the isolated queen’s pawn.} 12...Bg7 13.f3 {If 13.f3, we cannot let White open the f-file - the f-file is where our weaknesses live. So we push the pawn forward.} 13...e3 14.Nd1 {And even though White can win this pawn...} 14...O-O {We should allow them to take, because we're not in the business of giving up excessive piece activity for a pawn. We'll continue with ...Re8, ...Nd7 to develop, keeping in mind we always have pressure on d4 if White isn't careful with that pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 2 Knights Setup After 4.cxd5 / Study 4: Other White 5th Moves / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/48a1ccf1-98aa-49d1-a69f-cf5944873102/studies/d695c056-8741-43a9-8645-03356107366b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 4 of Chapter 2 in our QGD course. Here we examine White’s 5th-move deviations, such as 5.Bf4, 5.e3, and 5.g3. In the 5.Bf4 line, White can even avoid Nf3 entirely! White begins with 1.d4, taking space in the center. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our center.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops naturally, adding pressure to d5. We once again play our thematic 3...a6, dreaming of ...dxc4 and ...b5 - but White has other plans.} 3...a6 4.cxd5 {White tries to neutralize 3...a6 by taking on d5, but they only help our development. We recapture on d5, and our light-squared bishop gains a clear path.} 4...exd5 5.Qb3 {White can choose to attack the d5-pawn and put pressure on b7. We respond with a sound developing move that also defends the pawn, 5...Nf6.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {If White pins our knight... We support the center with ...c6. From here, White has a few natural developing tries.} 6...c6 7.e4 {White can try to break in the center with 7.e4. Some aggressive players think the pin makes this possible. We simply take, there’s no reason to fear Nxe4 or Bc4.} 7...dxe4 8.Nxe4 {If White just captures on e4... We break the pin with 8…Be7, as is common in QGD structures.} 8...Be7 9.Nxf6+ {White has nothing better than to trade pieces. We recapture with the bishop, keeping a clean structure.} 9...Bxf6 10.Bxf6 {White continues trading. We recapture and develop our queen, with pressure on d4.} 10...Qxf6 11.Nf3 {White defends and develops. We castle, bringing our rook into the game.} 11...O-O 12.Be2 {White will develop and block the e-file.} 12...Re8 {But after 12...Re8, it's safe to say we have some nice pressure in this position because of the pin. To increase the pressure, we have ideas of trading White's strong queen on b3 with ...Qe6. We'll also aim to develop our queenside knight to d7 and then to f6.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 1: White Plays 3.e3 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/5833d446-ca8a-46fa-a9ad-3425bf9268cb/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 3! We now cover White’s second most popular reply to 3...a6: 4.e3. White opens with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with 1...d5, fighting for the center.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears on the board. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, adding pressure on d5. We play the key move once again: 3...a6. Later we may expand on the queenside with ...b5.} 3...a6 4.e3 {White chooses the quiet 4.e3, simply aiming to develop the pieces. We bring out our knight to its natural square, f6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White does the same. Here the knights come out quickly to support a solid center, but White’s bishops aren’t very active. We prepare to castle with 5...Be7.} 5...Be7 6.b3 {White may defend c4 with 6.b3, avoiding an immediate ...b5. This also prepares to develop the dark-squared bishop, which was blocked by the early e3. It's time for us to hide our king.} 6...O-O 7.Bb2 {White develops their dark-squared bishop on the main diagonal. Our next move, 7...b6, supports ...c5 and prepares the b7-square for our bishop.} 7...b6 8.Bd3 {White plays 8.Bd3, developing the light-squared bishop on an active diagonal toward h7. We fianchetto ours, which sits well on the a8–h1 diagonal.} 8...Bb7 9.O-O {White castles. We take on c4, releasing the tension immediately after. This leaves our light-squared bishop with a much better scope.} 9...dxc4 10.bxc4 {White recaptures. We challenge White’s center with 10...c5.} 10...c5 11.Qe2 {White connects the rooks and gets the queen off the dangerous d-file. We now have multiple development options. One is 11...Nbd7, supporting the c5-pawn without blocking our b7-bishop.} 11...Nbd7 12.Rad1 {Another sensible continuation.} 12...Qc7 {We respond by simply stepping out of the d-file, also connecting the rooks. White will most likely play for the d5 pawn push. In that case, the plan for Black revolves around playing ...b5 and expanding on the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 1: White Plays 3.e3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/5833d446-ca8a-46fa-a9ad-3425bf9268cb/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 3! We now cover White’s second most popular reply to 3...a6: 4.e3. White opens with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with 1...d5, fighting for the center.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears on the board. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, adding pressure on d5. We play the key move once again: 3...a6. Later we may expand on the queenside with ...b5.} 3...a6 4.e3 {White chooses the quiet 4.e3, simply aiming to develop the pieces. We bring out our knight to its natural square, f6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White does the same. Here the knights come out quickly to support a solid center, but White’s bishops aren’t very active. We prepare to castle with 5...Be7.} 5...Be7 6.b3 {White may defend c4 with 6.b3, avoiding an immediate ...b5. This also prepares to develop the dark-squared bishop, which was blocked by the early e3. It's time for us to hide our king.} 6...O-O 7.Bb2 {White develops their dark-squared bishop on the main diagonal. Our next move, 7...b6, supports ...c5 and prepares the b7-square for our bishop.} 7...b6 8.Bd3 {White plays 8.Bd3, developing the light-squared bishop on an active diagonal toward h7. We fianchetto ours, which sits well on the a8–h1 diagonal.} 8...Bb7 9.O-O {White castles. We take on c4, releasing the tension immediately after. This leaves our light-squared bishop with a much better scope.} 9...dxc4 10.bxc4 {White recaptures. We challenge White’s center with 10...c5.} 10...c5 11.Qe2 {White connects the rooks and gets the queen off the dangerous d-file. We now have multiple development options. One is 11...Nbd7, supporting the c5-pawn without blocking our b7-bishop.} 11...Nbd7 12.Rfd1 {White slides their f-rook to d1, which now eyes our queen, so we must be careful about tactics if White opens the d-file with dxc5. Whenever the rook comes to d1, it’s wise to move away from discovered attacks, so we play 12...Qc7.} 12...Qc7 13.d5 {White confronts our pawn structure by pushing their d-pawn. d5 should always be taken, so that's what we do, 13...exd5 comes next.} 13...exd5 14.cxd5 {White recaptures on d5. Now the plan of queenside expansion with ... b5 is very strong.} 14...b5 {We have a very strong queenside pawn chain that can be pushed with ...c4 or ...b4 should good, tactical opportunities present themselves. ...Rfe8 and ...Bd6 are also some plans, playing for control of the e5-square.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 1: White Plays 3.e3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/5833d446-ca8a-46fa-a9ad-3425bf9268cb/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 3! We now cover White’s second most popular reply to 3...a6: 4.e3. White opens with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with 1...d5, fighting for the center.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears on the board. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, adding pressure on d5. We play the key move once again: 3...a6. Later we may expand on the queenside with ...b5.} 3...a6 4.e3 {White chooses the quiet 4.e3, simply aiming to develop the pieces. We bring out our knight to its natural square, f6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White does the same. Here the knights come out quickly to support a solid center, but White’s bishops aren’t very active. We prepare to castle with 5...Be7.} 5...Be7 6.Bd3 {White develops the bishop on an active diagonal. Now we make use of our 3...a6 idea and capture on c4.} 6...dxc4 7.Bxc4 {White must recapture, wasting another tempo with the bishop. We attack the bishop while gaining queenside space with 7...b5. The b7-square is now open for our own bishop.} 7...b5 8.Bd3 {White retreats to d3. Our bishop will be beautifully placed on b7.} 8...Bb7 9.O-O {White castles. We finish development with 9...Nbd7. Our plan is simple: play ...c5 and castle with a very comfortable position} 9...Nbd7 10.a4 {White tries to challenge our queenside pawns... But a4 is always met by ...b4.} 10...b4 11.Ne2 {White retreats. We challenge the center with ...c5 while expanding on the queenside. Black’s position is extremely comfortable - White’s c1-bishop has yet to move, and our development is nearly complete.} 11...c5 12.Ng3 {A popular move for White is 12.Ng3, activating the knight. Now White's knight on g3, we strike on the kingside with 12...h5.} 12...h5 13.h4 {White tries to block the attack but simultaneously makes some weaknesses around their king.} 13...Ng4 {We end the variation with 13...Ng4, placing the knight on a very active square. h4 is now under some serious pressure and Black has great attacking potential. In addition, the ...g5 move is always an idea, initiating the attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 1: White Plays 3.e3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/5833d446-ca8a-46fa-a9ad-3425bf9268cb/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 3! We now cover White’s second most popular reply to 3...a6: 4.e3. White opens with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with 1...d5, fighting for the center.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears on the board. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, adding pressure on d5. We play the key move once again: 3...a6. Later we may expand on the queenside with ...b5.} 3...a6 4.e3 {White chooses the quiet 4.e3, simply aiming to develop the pieces. We bring out our knight to its natural square, f6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White does the same. Here the knights come out quickly to support a solid center, but White’s bishops aren’t very active. We prepare to castle with 5...Be7.} 5...Be7 6.Bd3 {White develops the bishop on an active diagonal. Now we make use of our 3...a6 idea and capture on c4.} 6...dxc4 7.Bxc4 {White must recapture, wasting another tempo with the bishop. We attack the bishop while gaining queenside space with 7...b5. The b7-square is now open for our own bishop.} 7...b5 8.Bd3 {White retreats to d3. Our bishop will be beautifully placed on b7.} 8...Bb7 9.O-O {White castles. We finish development with 9...Nbd7. Our plan is simple: play ...c5 and castle with a very comfortable position} 9...Nbd7 10.e4 {Instead of 10.a4, White expands in the center with 10.e4. We continue with our ...c5 plan.} 10...c5 11.e5 {White pushes the e-pawn, attacking our knight. We place it on d5, its best square.} 11...Nd5 12.Nxd5 {White gets rid of our strongly positioned d5-knight.} 12...Bxd5 {We recapture with our bishop. We will castle next. The backward d-pawn will be successfully undermined. The e5-pawn presents some attacking chances for White, so we must be careful and defend our king accordingly.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 1: White Plays 3.e3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/5833d446-ca8a-46fa-a9ad-3425bf9268cb/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 3! We now cover White’s second most popular reply to 3...a6: 4.e3. White opens with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with 1...d5, fighting for the center.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears on the board. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, adding pressure on d5. We play the key move once again: 3...a6. Later we may expand on the queenside with ...b5.} 3...a6 4.e3 {White chooses the quiet 4.e3, simply aiming to develop the pieces. We bring out our knight to its natural square, f6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White does the same. Here the knights come out quickly to support a solid center, but White’s bishops aren’t very active. We prepare to castle with 5...Be7.} 5...Be7 6.Bd3 {White develops the bishop on an active diagonal. Now we make use of our 3...a6 idea and capture on c4.} 6...dxc4 7.Bxc4 {White must recapture, wasting another tempo with the bishop. We attack the bishop while gaining queenside space with 7...b5. The b7-square is now open for our own bishop.} 7...b5 8.Bd3 {White retreats to d3. Our bishop will be beautifully placed on b7.} 8...Bb7 9.O-O {White castles. We finish development with 9...Nbd7. Our plan is simple: play ...c5 and castle with a very comfortable position} 9...Nbd7 10.Re1 {Another option for White is to develop the rook to e1. Our …c5 advance remains essential.} 10...c5 11.dxc5 {White releases the tension. This allows our knight to recapture on c5 and occupy an excellent square. The bishop comes under attack, and our pieces are more active than White’s.} 11...Nxc5 12.Bc2 {White retreats.} 12...O-O {We castle, leaving us with a lead in development and good control of the e4 square. In case White trades the queens, we recapture on d8 with the f-rook, so the a-rook can be placed on c8. Otherwise, we play ...Qc7 with the idea of playing ...Rfd8 and ...Rac8, centralizing the rooks.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 1: White Plays 3.e3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/5833d446-ca8a-46fa-a9ad-3425bf9268cb/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 3! We now cover White’s second most popular reply to 3...a6: 4.e3. White opens with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with 1...d5, fighting for the center.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears on the board. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, adding pressure on d5. We play the key move once again: 3...a6. Later we may expand on the queenside with ...b5.} 3...a6 4.e3 {White chooses the quiet 4.e3, simply aiming to develop the pieces. We bring out our knight to its natural square, f6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White does the same. Here the knights come out quickly to support a solid center, but White’s bishops aren’t very active. We prepare to castle with 5...Be7.} 5...Be7 6.Bd3 {White develops the bishop on an active diagonal. Now we make use of our 3...a6 idea and capture on c4.} 6...dxc4 7.Bxc4 {White must recapture, wasting another tempo with the bishop. We attack the bishop while gaining queenside space with 7...b5. The b7-square is now open for our own bishop.} 7...b5 8.Bd3 {White retreats to d3. Our bishop will be beautifully placed on b7.} 8...Bb7 9.O-O {White castles. We finish development with 9...Nbd7. Our plan is simple: play ...c5 and castle with a very comfortable position} 9...Nbd7 10.a4 {White tries to challenge our queenside pawns... But a4 is always met by ...b4.} 10...b4 11.Ne2 {White retreats. We challenge the center with ...c5 while expanding on the queenside. Black’s position is extremely comfortable - White’s c1-bishop has yet to move, and our development is nearly complete.} 11...c5 12.a5 {Another popular move here is to play a5, fixing our pawn on a6.} 12...Qc7 {In that case, we should put the queen on c7 in order to prepare a "battery" with ...Bd6. The rooks will also sit nicely on the d- and c-files.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 1: White Plays 3.e3 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/5833d446-ca8a-46fa-a9ad-3425bf9268cb/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 3! We now cover White’s second most popular reply to 3...a6: 4.e3. White opens with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with 1...d5, fighting for the center.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears on the board. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, adding pressure on d5. We play the key move once again: 3...a6. Later we may expand on the queenside with ...b5.} 3...a6 4.e3 {White chooses the quiet 4.e3, simply aiming to develop the pieces. We bring out our knight to its natural square, f6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White does the same. Here the knights come out quickly to support a solid center, but White’s bishops aren’t very active. We prepare to castle with 5...Be7.} 5...Be7 6.b3 {White may defend c4 with 6.b3, avoiding an immediate ...b5. This also prepares to develop the dark-squared bishop, which was blocked by the early e3. It's time for us to hide our king.} 6...O-O 7.Bb2 {White develops their dark-squared bishop on the main diagonal. Our next move, 7...b6, supports ...c5 and prepares the b7-square for our bishop.} 7...b6 8.Bd3 {White plays 8.Bd3, developing the light-squared bishop on an active diagonal toward h7. We fianchetto ours, which sits well on the a8–h1 diagonal.} 8...Bb7 9.O-O {White castles. We take on c4, releasing the tension immediately after. This leaves our light-squared bishop with a much better scope.} 9...dxc4 10.bxc4 {White recaptures. We challenge White’s center with 10...c5.} 10...c5 11.dxc5 {White opts to take on c5 immediately.} 11...Bxc5 {Capturing with the bishop keeps the b- and a-pawns intact. The queen as usual will be moved to c7 and we will play ...Nbd7. The c4-pawn could be a weakness that we could attack at some point.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 1: White Plays 3.e3 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/5833d446-ca8a-46fa-a9ad-3425bf9268cb/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 3! We now cover White’s second most popular reply to 3...a6: 4.e3. White opens with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with 1...d5, fighting for the center.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears on the board. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our d5-pawn.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3, adding pressure on d5. We play the key move once again: 3...a6. Later we may expand on the queenside with ...b5.} 3...a6 4.e3 {White chooses the quiet 4.e3, simply aiming to develop the pieces. We bring out our knight to its natural square, f6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 {White does the same. Here the knights come out quickly to support a solid center, but White’s bishops aren’t very active. We prepare to castle with 5...Be7.} 5...Be7 6.b3 {White may defend c4 with 6.b3, avoiding an immediate ...b5. This also prepares to develop the dark-squared bishop, which was blocked by the early e3. It's time for us to hide our king.} 6...O-O 7.Bb2 {White develops their dark-squared bishop on the main diagonal. Our next move, 7...b6, supports ...c5 and prepares the b7-square for our bishop.} 7...b6 8.Bd3 {White plays 8.Bd3, developing the light-squared bishop on an active diagonal toward h7. We fianchetto ours, which sits well on the a8–h1 diagonal.} 8...Bb7 9.O-O {White castles. We take on c4, releasing the tension immediately after. This leaves our light-squared bishop with a much better scope.} 9...dxc4 10.bxc4 {White recaptures. We challenge White’s center with 10...c5.} 10...c5 11.Qc2 {White instead chooses 11.Qc2, planning to form a battery along the b1–h7 diagonal, but this is a mistake. The knight now has only one defender - the pawn in front of White’s king - so we capture it to ruin White’s structure.} 11...Bxf3 12.gxf3 {White recaptures.} 12...cxd4 {It is a good idea to open things up since White's King can become weak. We also have ideas of playing ...Nc6 to b4. After exd4, White will have doubled isolated f-pawns. It is worth noting that the immediate ...Qxd4 wouldn't be a good move because of a discovered attack on our queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 or 4.e4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/2445ab39-61f4-4f9a-902f-2b9c61545837/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second lesson of this chapter! Here we examine White’s less popular reply to 3...a6: 4.Bf4. After that, we’ll also look at 4.e4. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our d-pawn and opening the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight, fighting for the center. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5.} 3...a6 4.Bf4 {White immediately develops the bishop to f4 - similar to a London setup, avoiding the issue of the bishop being stuck on c1. We simply take on c4, the most reliable counterplay in the position.} 4...dxc4 5.a4 {White tries to stop ...b5 with 5.a4, but this is not a good approach. They should play e3 first. Our plan is to put the knight on a5 to defend the c4-pawn, so 5...Nc6 is our move. Usually White defends with 6.e3, because that puts pressure on c4. If 6.Nf3, we play ...Bb4 first and then ...Na5.} 5...Nc6 6.e3 {White opens the light-squared bishop's diagonal and attacks c4. A knight on the rim is often bad, but here it’s perfect, White will struggle to attack our c4-pawn once we place our knight on a5.} 6...Na5 7.Nf3 {White develops the knight. We place our dark-squared bishop on its typical active square in this variation, b4, pinning the knight on c3.} 7...Bb4 8.Be2 {White prepares to castle. We develop the knight to f6, controlling e4 and d5.} 8...Nf6 9.O-O {White castles, and the knight is no longer pinned. We increase pressure on c3, threatening to win another pawn with 9...Nd5.} 9...Nd5 10.Nxd5 {White sees the threat and captures on d5.} 10...exd5 {We recapture with our e-pawn. White will most likely aim for the e4 pawn push in the future. Our plan is to castle, put the rook on e8, and push ...b5 to support the pawn on c4. With a pawn up and space advantage on the queenside, we have a great position.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 or 4.e4 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/2445ab39-61f4-4f9a-902f-2b9c61545837/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second lesson of this chapter! Here we examine White’s less popular reply to 3...a6: 4.Bf4. After that, we’ll also look at 4.e4. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our d-pawn and opening the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight, fighting for the center. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5.} 3...a6 4.e4 {Another option is 4.e4, a very confrontational move. White tries to seize central control, but 4.e4 causes no real problems for Black. We take immediately to prevent any expansion that could lead to a passive position.} 4...dxe4 5.Nxe4 {White recaptures. We challenge the centralized knight as it is unprotected.} 5...Nf6 6.Nc3 {White may choose to retreat the knight. The thematic ...c5 is excellent here, targeting d4 and undermining White’s center. If White advances, we capture, develop the dark-squared bishop, and castle.} 6...c5 7.Nf3 {White defends the pawn. We develop the knight to c6, attacking d4.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {White pushes their d-pawn with tempo on our knight. We capture on d5 first before advancing our knight to b4.} 8...exd5 9.cxd5 {White recaptures, and our knight must move. We jump to b4, escaping danger and attacking the d5-pawn.} 9...Nb4 10.Bc4 {White develops their light-squared bishop to c4, defending d5 and getting ready to castle.} 10...b5 {But our typical move ... b5 gives us a better position. We have to be careful a little bit since our king isn't safe yet, but a quick ...Be7 and castling is good for us.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 or 4.e4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/2445ab39-61f4-4f9a-902f-2b9c61545837/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second lesson of this chapter! Here we examine White’s less popular reply to 3...a6: 4.Bf4. After that, we’ll also look at 4.e4. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our d-pawn and opening the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight, fighting for the center. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5.} 3...a6 4.Bf4 {White immediately develops the bishop to f4 - similar to a London setup, avoiding the issue of the bishop being stuck on c1. We simply take on c4, the most reliable counterplay in the position.} 4...dxc4 5.e3 {5.e3 is White’s best and only try to avoid permanently losing the c4-pawn. We play 5...b4, reinforcing c4.} 5...b5 6.a4 {White plays 6.a4, trying to break our queenside chain. 6...b4 is a typical response to a4 in the 3...a6 QGD: we gain space and hit the knight. Next, ...Bb7 stops White from taking on c4 because g2 would be hanging.} 6...b4 7.Ne4 {The last knight move for White is 7.Ne4. We continue with a light-squared bishop fianchetto, developing a piece and attacking the e4-knight.} 7...Bb7 8.Nc5 {White attacks our bishop. We capture the strong knight - there's no better use for our dark-squared bishop.} 8...Bxc5 9.dxc5 {White recaptures. We go for a queen trade.} 9...Qxd1+ 10.Rxd1 {White recaptures with the rook. We pressure c5 with 10...Nd7 - Bxc4 is impossible because of ...Bxg2.} 10...Nd7 11.Nf3 {White develops the knight. If Bxc7, we simply take on c5. We capture on c5 anyway.} 11...Nxc5 12.Bxc4 {White can finally take the c-pawn now that g2 isn’t hanging… But now a4 is free for us to capture.} 12...Nxa4 13.b3 {White reinforces the c4-bishop and attacks our knight. We retreat the knight to b6. If allowed, we’ll take on c4 and get a passed b-pawn.} 13...Nb6 14.Bxc7 {White takes the free pawn. We take the bishop on c4 as planned.} 14...Nxc4 15.bxc4 {White recaptures. With two passed pawns, simplifying is perfectly fine for us, so we trade more pieces with 15...Bxf3.} 15...Bxf3 16.gxf3 {White captures. We develop the knight to e7, from where it has better routes.} 16...Ne7 17.Bd6 {White attacks b4. We simply defend the passed pawn by advancing our a-pawn.} 17...a5 18.Rg1 {White activates the rook by placing it on the semi-open g-file, from where it attacks our g7-pawn.} 18...g6 {We defend the attacked pawn by pushing it to g6. The plan is to utilize the passed pawns on b4, and a5, later supporting them with ...Nc6. The king could be developed via playing ... f6 and ...Kf7.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 or 4.e4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/2445ab39-61f4-4f9a-902f-2b9c61545837/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second lesson of this chapter! Here we examine White’s less popular reply to 3...a6: 4.Bf4. After that, we’ll also look at 4.e4. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our d-pawn and opening the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight, fighting for the center. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5.} 3...a6 4.Bf4 {White immediately develops the bishop to f4 - similar to a London setup, avoiding the issue of the bishop being stuck on c1. We simply take on c4, the most reliable counterplay in the position.} 4...dxc4 5.e3 {5.e3 is White’s best and only try to avoid permanently losing the c4-pawn. We play 5...b4, reinforcing c4.} 5...b5 6.a4 {White plays 6.a4, trying to break our queenside chain. 6...b4 is a typical response to a4 in the 3...a6 QGD: we gain space and hit the knight. Next, ...Bb7 stops White from taking on c4 because g2 would be hanging.} 6...b4 7.Na2 {The first knight retreat is 7.Na2. After 7...Bb7 our bishop eyes g2. White cannot capture on c4 immediately - ...Bxg2 wins the exchange.} 7...Bb7 8.Nf3 {White plays 8.Nf3, blocking our bishop’s view of g2. Our response is to anchor our light-squared bishop on d5, reinforcing the c4-pawn.} 8...Bd5 9.Ne5 {White's knight jumps to an active square, attacking c4. We develop our dark-squared bishop to d6.} 9...Bd6 10.Nxc4 {White finally takes the pawn… And we exchange White’s active dark-squared bishop.} 10...Bxf4 11.exf4 {White recaptures.} 11...Nc6 {We end the variation by developing the knight. The plan is to play ...Nh6-f5 and castle. The d4-pawn is under pressure, and the bishop on f1 cannot be developed so easily since g2 is hanging. The knight on a2 is placed very poorly. If White attacks the pawn, just defend with ...a5.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 or 4.e4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/2445ab39-61f4-4f9a-902f-2b9c61545837/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second lesson of this chapter! Here we examine White’s less popular reply to 3...a6: 4.Bf4. After that, we’ll also look at 4.e4. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our d-pawn and opening the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight, fighting for the center. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5.} 3...a6 4.Bf4 {White immediately develops the bishop to f4 - similar to a London setup, avoiding the issue of the bishop being stuck on c1. We simply take on c4, the most reliable counterplay in the position.} 4...dxc4 5.e3 {5.e3 is White’s best and only try to avoid permanently losing the c4-pawn. We play 5...b4, reinforcing c4.} 5...b5 6.a4 {White plays 6.a4, trying to break our queenside chain. 6...b4 is a typical response to a4 in the 3...a6 QGD: we gain space and hit the knight. Next, ...Bb7 stops White from taking on c4 because g2 would be hanging.} 6...b4 7.Na2 {The first knight retreat is 7.Na2. After 7...Bb7 our bishop eyes g2. White cannot capture on c4 immediately - ...Bxg2 wins the exchange.} 7...Bb7 8.Nf3 {White plays 8.Nf3, blocking our bishop’s view of g2. Our response is to anchor our light-squared bishop on d5, reinforcing the c4-pawn.} 8...Bd5 9.Rc1 {White may try to attack c4 with Rc1, but this is a mistake. We reveal a discovered attack on the knight with 9...c3.} 9...c3 10.b3 {White closes the diagonal...} 10...Nf6 {...but trap their own knight on a2. We continue with normal development and we have a very good position. We are up a pawn and with a5 coming. White's position is very hard to untangle.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 or 4.e4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/2445ab39-61f4-4f9a-902f-2b9c61545837/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second lesson of this chapter! Here we examine White’s less popular reply to 3...a6: 4.Bf4. After that, we’ll also look at 4.e4. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our d-pawn and opening the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight, fighting for the center. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5.} 3...a6 4.Bf4 {White immediately develops the bishop to f4 - similar to a London setup, avoiding the issue of the bishop being stuck on c1. We simply take on c4, the most reliable counterplay in the position.} 4...dxc4 5.e3 {5.e3 is White’s best and only try to avoid permanently losing the c4-pawn. We play 5...b4, reinforcing c4.} 5...b5 6.a4 {White plays 6.a4, trying to break our queenside chain. 6...b4 is a typical response to a4 in the 3...a6 QGD: we gain space and hit the knight. Next, ...Bb7 stops White from taking on c4 because g2 would be hanging.} 6...b4 7.Na2 {The first knight retreat is 7.Na2. After 7...Bb7 our bishop eyes g2. White cannot capture on c4 immediately - ...Bxg2 wins the exchange.} 7...Bb7 8.Nf3 {White plays 8.Nf3, blocking our bishop’s view of g2. Our response is to anchor our light-squared bishop on d5, reinforcing the c4-pawn.} 8...Bd5 9.Qc1 {Another option for White is 9.Qc1, attacking our pawn. We respond with 9...b3, attacking the knight.} 9...b3 10.Nc3 {10.Nc3 is the only move for White.} 10...Nf6 {We play 10...Nf6, developing the knight. With 9... b3 we have allowed the knight back in the game, but now White blocked the queen's attack on c4. We have a nice space advantage at our disposal on the queenside and future plans of playing ... c5.} {Another plan is to play ...Nc6 and ...Na5, furthermore strengthening the pawn chain, with ...c5 coming later.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 or 4.e4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/2445ab39-61f4-4f9a-902f-2b9c61545837/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second lesson of this chapter! Here we examine White’s less popular reply to 3...a6: 4.Bf4. After that, we’ll also look at 4.e4. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our d-pawn and opening the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight, fighting for the center. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5.} 3...a6 4.Bf4 {White immediately develops the bishop to f4 - similar to a London setup, avoiding the issue of the bishop being stuck on c1. We simply take on c4, the most reliable counterplay in the position.} 4...dxc4 5.Qa4+ {White is trying to pick up the pawn on c4 next...} 5...b5 {...b5 supports the pawn.} 6.Nxb5 {White's point is that they can capture on b5 with the knight since the rook on a8 is hanging. This is a mistake.} 6...Bd7 {We can pin the knight to the queen.} 7.Nxc7+ {Doesn't save White.} 7...Qxc7 {Simply capturing the knight.} 8.Bxc7 {White takes our queen...} 8...Bxa4 {...but we take theirs, leaving us up a piece.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 or 4.e4 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/2445ab39-61f4-4f9a-902f-2b9c61545837/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second lesson of this chapter! Here we examine White’s less popular reply to 3...a6: 4.Bf4. After that, we’ll also look at 4.e4. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our d-pawn and opening the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight, fighting for the center. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5.} 3...a6 4.e4 {Another option is 4.e4, a very confrontational move. White tries to seize central control, but 4.e4 causes no real problems for Black. We take immediately to prevent any expansion that could lead to a passive position.} 4...dxe4 5.Nxe4 {White recaptures. We challenge the centralized knight as it is unprotected.} 5...Nf6 6.Nxf6+ {White usually trades knights, retreating only wastes time. We recapture on f6 with the queen, which now sits well on f6, where it cannot be challenged.} 6...Qxf6 7.Nf3 {White develops the knight to a natural square allowing Bg5, which would attack our queen. We respond by developing our bishop with check, 7...Bb4+.} 7...Bb4+ 8.Bd2 {White blocks. We’re happy to trade the dark-squared bishops - ours didn't have a better use.} 8...Bxd2+ 9.Qxd2 {White recaptures.} 9...b6 {9...b6 allows for the bishop to be placed on the strong a8-h1 diagonal. We will castle, place the knight on d7, connect and centralize the rooks, and have a very comfortable position. We can eventually plan the move ... c5 to challenge White's d-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 or 4.e4 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/2445ab39-61f4-4f9a-902f-2b9c61545837/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second lesson of this chapter! Here we examine White’s less popular reply to 3...a6: 4.Bf4. After that, we’ll also look at 4.e4. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. We decline it with 2...e6, supporting our d-pawn and opening the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight, fighting for the center. We respond with 3...a6, preparing ...b5.} 3...a6 4.e4 {Another option is 4.e4, a very confrontational move. White tries to seize central control, but 4.e4 causes no real problems for Black. We take immediately to prevent any expansion that could lead to a passive position.} 4...dxe4 5.Nxe4 {White recaptures. We challenge the centralized knight as it is unprotected.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {Instead of retreating, White pins the knight to our queen. We break the pin by developing our dark-squared bishop to e7, and now the knight on e4 is hanging.} 6...Be7 7.Nxf6+ {White trades the knights. We recapture.} 7...Bxf6 8.Bxf6 {White also trades dark-squared bishops, since retreating allows us to keep an active bishop on f6. We recapture with the queen. Without dark-squared bishops on the board, we no longer have to fear any attack on our queen.} 8...Qxf6 9.Nf3 {White develops the kingside knight.} 9...Nc6 {We end the variation with 9...Nc6. The plan is to castle and put pressure on the d-pawn by playing ...Rd8. If White pushes d5, we simply trade first and retreat our knight to e7.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.g3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/e2c79b71-d998-4a3a-86d6-f5508ed66062/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the third lesson of the third chapter of the a6 Queen’s Gambit Declined! In this study, we examine the move 4.g3 for White. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We mirror with our own.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. This is a very solid way to begin the game. With 2...e6 we support d5, open the f8-bishop, and prepare our a6 setup.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White continues developing. We play 3...a6, with the usual plan of ...dxc4 followed by ...b5.} 3...a6 4.g3 {But White can choose 4.g3, entering a Catalan-style setup. We keep to our theme and capture on c4.} 4...dxc4 5.Bg2 {After 5.Bg2 ...b5 is impossible... So we develop the knight, simultaneously controlling the central e4 and d5 squares.} 5...Nf6 6.Nf3 {White may choose to develop their knight instead. But now the bishop on g2 is blocked, allowing us to push the b-pawn and protect the c-pawn.} 6...b5 7.Ne5 {White centralizes the knight and attacks our rook. We move out of the attack, 7...Ra7 is our move.} 7...Ra7 8.a4 {White challenges our queenside pawns with 8.a4. We fianchetto our light-squared bishop, attacking White's.} 8...Bb7 9.Bxb7 {White trades. We recapture, also reinforcing the b-pawn.} 9...Rxb7 10.axb5 {White exchanges again to open the a-file for the rook. We recapture the pawn.} 10...axb5 11.Ra8 {White tries to activate the rook. We must get our king to safety quickly, so we develop the bishop and prepare to castle.} 11...Bb4 12.O-O {White castles.} 12...O-O {We castle as well. In this position, we are up a pawn. White will most likely develop their bishop to g5. In that case, we can even retreat with the bishop to e7 and prepare ...Nd5 and ...c5 with pressure on the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.g3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/e2c79b71-d998-4a3a-86d6-f5508ed66062/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the third lesson of the third chapter of the a6 Queen’s Gambit Declined! In this study, we examine the move 4.g3 for White. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We mirror with our own.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. This is a very solid way to begin the game. With 2...e6 we support d5, open the f8-bishop, and prepare our a6 setup.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White continues developing. We play 3...a6, with the usual plan of ...dxc4 followed by ...b5.} 3...a6 4.g3 {But White can choose 4.g3, entering a Catalan-style setup. We keep to our theme and capture on c4.} 4...dxc4 5.Bg2 {After 5.Bg2 ...b5 is impossible... So we develop the knight, simultaneously controlling the central e4 and d5 squares.} 5...Nf6 6.Qa4+ {White goes after the c4-pawn with the queen. Note that 6...b5 doesn’t work here because the a8-rook hangs. We block the check with the knight; otherwise, b7 would be hanging.} 6...Nbd7 7.Qxc4 {White captures the pawn. We strike back in the center with 7...c5. Both ...c5 and ...b5 are thematic for Black in this structure.} 7...c5 8.dxc5 {White trades. We recapture with the bishop, enabling castling.} 8...Bxc5 9.Nf3 {White develops the knight to a natural square. Now that the knight blocks the bishop's vision, we play our typical ...b5 plan.} 9...b5 10.Qh4 {White brings the queen toward the kingside. We place our bishop on b7, countering White’s Catalan bishop.} 10...Bb7 11.O-O {White castles.} 11...O-O {We also castle. The plan is to put the rooks on d8 and c8 and attack on the queenside. We might anticipate a kingside attack from White. With active pieces and space on the queenside, we have a comfortable position.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.g3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/e2c79b71-d998-4a3a-86d6-f5508ed66062/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the third lesson of the third chapter of the a6 Queen’s Gambit Declined! In this study, we examine the move 4.g3 for White. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We mirror with our own.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. This is a very solid way to begin the game. With 2...e6 we support d5, open the f8-bishop, and prepare our a6 setup.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White continues developing. We play 3...a6, with the usual plan of ...dxc4 followed by ...b5.} 3...a6 4.g3 {But White can choose 4.g3, entering a Catalan-style setup. We keep to our theme and capture on c4.} 4...dxc4 5.Qa4+ {White tries to immediately recover the pawn on c4. But 5.Qa4+ is a blunder because the bishop is not on g2. We block the check while also protecting c4 with 5...b5.} 5...b5 6.Nxb5 {White assumes they can take on b5 since the rook on a8 is hanging… But we now pin the knight to the queen.} 6...Bd7 7.Bg2 {White tries to intensify the threat by attacking the a8-rook. But we simply capture the knight with tempo on the queen.} 7...Bxb5 8.Qc2 {White retreats. When ahead in material, trading pieces is always a good idea, so we give an in-between check with our dark-squared bishop, aiming to trade it for White’s before moving our rook out of danger.} 8...Bb4+ 9.Bd2 {White blocks. We trade as planned.} 9...Bxd2+ 10.Qxd2 {White recaptures.} 10...Bc6 {And now we trade off White's strong bishop to save our rook. We are up a knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.g3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/e2c79b71-d998-4a3a-86d6-f5508ed66062/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the third lesson of the third chapter of the a6 Queen’s Gambit Declined! In this study, we examine the move 4.g3 for White. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We mirror with our own.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. This is a very solid way to begin the game. With 2...e6 we support d5, open the f8-bishop, and prepare our a6 setup.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {White continues developing. We play 3...a6, with the usual plan of ...dxc4 followed by ...b5.} 3...a6 4.g3 {But White can choose 4.g3, entering a Catalan-style setup. We keep to our theme and capture on c4.} 4...dxc4 5.Bg2 {After 5.Bg2 ...b5 is impossible... So we develop the knight, simultaneously controlling the central e4 and d5 squares.} 5...Nf6 6.Nf3 {White may choose to develop their knight instead. But now the bishop on g2 is blocked, allowing us to push the b-pawn and protect the c-pawn.} 6...b5 7.O-O {White castles instead.} 7...Bb7 {That allows for our bishop to be developed nicely, opposing White's bishop on g2.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 4: White Plays 4.a4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/bae61b6c-0261-42fc-94ed-3dd3e092290e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this new study, which I’m adding post-launch based on the 7th most popular move after 3...a6: 4.a4. The idea is to stop any …b5 ideas. However, as you’ll see, 4.a4 gives up the b5-square and is actually premature - White falls behind in development trying to prevent …b5. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit. We are ready for this and go for the Queen’s Gambit Declined.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {This line can transpose into the 3.Nf3 variations with an early 4.a4, but there are still some unique positions that may arise. We play 3...a6 here as well, just like in earlier studies.} 3...a6 4.a4 {The difference now is the immediate 4.a4. Nevertheless, we strike back right away with …c5. The point is simple: we undermine the center the moment White wastes time with a4.} 4...c5 5.cxd5 {If White plays 5.cxd5, this does not transpose back to our earlier chapters - but that’s fine, because the capture is simply bad for White! We gain a tempo on the knight by taking on d5 first. If White recaptures with the queen, we gain yet another tempo hitting the queen.} 5...cxd4 6.Qxd4 {When the queen captures on d4... We develop with tempo. The pawn is pinned, so the queen must move again.} 6...Nc6 7.Qd1 {7.Qd1 is the most natural and popular retreat. But we simply capture on d5. It may look like White will emerge a pawn up, but if they recapture with the knight, …Be6 gives Black a clear advantage.} 7...exd5 8.Qxd5 {The queen moves yet again to recapture.} 8...Qc7 {But we've got multiple moves to attack the queen. Our development is better and after ...Be6, ...Nf6, ...Bb4 to pin the c3-knight, we'll have a better middlegame and enough compensation for the pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 4: White Plays 4.a4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/bae61b6c-0261-42fc-94ed-3dd3e092290e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this new study, which I’m adding post-launch based on the 7th most popular move after 3...a6: 4.a4. The idea is to stop any …b5 ideas. However, as you’ll see, 4.a4 gives up the b5-square and is actually premature - White falls behind in development trying to prevent …b5. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit. We are ready for this and go for the Queen’s Gambit Declined.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {This line can transpose into the 3.Nf3 variations with an early 4.a4, but there are still some unique positions that may arise. We play 3...a6 here as well, just like in earlier studies.} 3...a6 4.a4 {The difference now is the immediate 4.a4. Nevertheless, we strike back right away with …c5. The point is simple: we undermine the center the moment White wastes time with a4.} 4...c5 5.cxd5 {If White plays 5.cxd5, this does not transpose back to our earlier chapters - but that’s fine, because the capture is simply bad for White! We gain a tempo on the knight by taking on d5 first. If White recaptures with the queen, we gain yet another tempo hitting the queen.} 5...cxd4 6.Qxd4 {When the queen captures on d4... We develop with tempo. The pawn is pinned, so the queen must move again.} 6...Nc6 7.Qe3 {White may also try the “clever-looking” 7.Qe3, trying to pin the e-pawn. But 7...Nb4 wins on the spot - we’re threatening a fork.} 7...Nb4 8.Qd2 {Once the queen moves again, we have enough pressure to win back the pawn. White won’t even keep extra material for their poor position. We take on d5, and …d4 next becomes a strong threat that White cannot easily stop. If 9.e4, then 9...Nf6 increases the pressure, with …Bf5 coming once the pawn leaves e4 after something like exd5.} 8...exd5 9.e3 {If 9. e3 to control the d4-square, White cannot stop Black's other idea.} 9...Bf5 {9...Bf5 adds another attacker to the c2-square. ...Nc2+ is coming. Kd1 is one of the best moves given by the engine, but it's already -6.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 4: White Plays 4.a4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/bae61b6c-0261-42fc-94ed-3dd3e092290e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this new study, which I’m adding post-launch based on the 7th most popular move after 3...a6: 4.a4. The idea is to stop any …b5 ideas. However, as you’ll see, 4.a4 gives up the b5-square and is actually premature - White falls behind in development trying to prevent …b5. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit. We are ready for this and go for the Queen’s Gambit Declined.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {This line can transpose into the 3.Nf3 variations with an early 4.a4, but there are still some unique positions that may arise. We play 3...a6 here as well, just like in earlier studies.} 3...a6 4.a4 {The difference now is the immediate 4.a4. Nevertheless, we strike back right away with …c5. The point is simple: we undermine the center the moment White wastes time with a4.} 4...c5 5.dxc5 {White may insert this capture, but it’s never good - because we immediately gain control of the d4-square. Our pawn push, 5...d4, becomes very strong, dislodging the knight from its best square. White’s knight choices here are unpleasant.} 5...d4 6.Ne4 {White may jump into the center. But we challenge the knight right away with ours, 6...Nf6 is our move. If White trades, we simply activate our queen.} 6...Nf6 7.Nd6+ {White can try this check, but it isn’t challenging at all. We take White’s only developed piece, recapture the pawn, and keep our knight developed on f6 - a win-win for Black.} 7...Bxd6 8.cxd6 {The pawn recaptures… But we take it back immediately, and our dominant d-pawn remains the strongest force in the position.} 8...Qxd6 9.e3 {White may try to undermine the pawn.} 9...Nc6 {But we can develop and support the pawn. Notice our superior development. If exd4, ...Nxd4 and we've got ...Qb4+ for ...Qxb2 ideas.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 4: White Plays 4.a4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/bae61b6c-0261-42fc-94ed-3dd3e092290e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this new study, which I’m adding post-launch based on the 7th most popular move after 3...a6: 4.a4. The idea is to stop any …b5 ideas. However, as you’ll see, 4.a4 gives up the b5-square and is actually premature - White falls behind in development trying to prevent …b5. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit. We are ready for this and go for the Queen’s Gambit Declined.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {This line can transpose into the 3.Nf3 variations with an early 4.a4, but there are still some unique positions that may arise. We play 3...a6 here as well, just like in earlier studies.} 3...a6 4.a4 {The difference now is the immediate 4.a4. Nevertheless, we strike back right away with …c5. The point is simple: we undermine the center the moment White wastes time with a4.} 4...c5 5.dxc5 {White may insert this capture, but it’s never good - because we immediately gain control of the d4-square. Our pawn push, 5...d4, becomes very strong, dislodging the knight from its best square. White’s knight choices here are unpleasant.} 5...d4 6.Ne4 {White may jump into the center. But we challenge the knight right away with ours, 6...Nf6 is our move. If White trades, we simply activate our queen.} 6...Nf6 7.Nxf6+ {If this trade occurs... We gain full control of d4 and our queen develops in the process. We’re also threatening to take on c5, which White cannot realistically prevent.} 7...Qxf6 8.b4 {8. b4 defends for one more turn.} 8...a5 {But 8...a5 undermines the pawn chain and the defense of c5. White won't be able to hold on to c5. We're going to take on b4 and then win c5. We should be better because of our strong d-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 4: White Plays 4.a4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/bae61b6c-0261-42fc-94ed-3dd3e092290e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this new study, which I’m adding post-launch based on the 7th most popular move after 3...a6: 4.a4. The idea is to stop any …b5 ideas. However, as you’ll see, 4.a4 gives up the b5-square and is actually premature - White falls behind in development trying to prevent …b5. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit. We are ready for this and go for the Queen’s Gambit Declined.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {This line can transpose into the 3.Nf3 variations with an early 4.a4, but there are still some unique positions that may arise. We play 3...a6 here as well, just like in earlier studies.} 3...a6 4.a4 {The difference now is the immediate 4.a4. Nevertheless, we strike back right away with …c5. The point is simple: we undermine the center the moment White wastes time with a4.} 4...c5 5.e3 {5. e3 will also transpose to the 3. Nf3 lines. We simply develop the kingside knight to f6.} 5...Nf6 6.Nf3 {And after White develops their knight...} 6...Nc6 {We have this 4 knights position with tension between the c- and d-pawns just like in the 3. Nf3 lines.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nc3 / Study 4: White Plays 4.a4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d740e581-1cc5-440e-b03d-31e5656f28f7/studies/bae61b6c-0261-42fc-94ed-3dd3e092290e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this new study, which I’m adding post-launch based on the 7th most popular move after 3...a6: 4.a4. The idea is to stop any …b5 ideas. However, as you’ll see, 4.a4 gives up the b5-square and is actually premature - White falls behind in development trying to prevent …b5. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White continues with the Queen’s Gambit. We are ready for this and go for the Queen’s Gambit Declined.} 2...e6 3.Nc3 {This line can transpose into the 3.Nf3 variations with an early 4.a4, but there are still some unique positions that may arise. We play 3...a6 here as well, just like in earlier studies.} 3...a6 4.a4 {The difference now is the immediate 4.a4. Nevertheless, we strike back right away with …c5. The point is simple: we undermine the center the moment White wastes time with a4.} 4...c5 5.Nf3 {If this knight is brought out at move 5, we can transpose.} 5...cxd4 {This transposes to the 2 knights with 4. a4 and we've got ...e5 coming up. This is found in the 4. a4 study after 3. Nf3.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 1: The Catalan / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/4925e2e4-6f2e-4e1e-9e63-47839433068b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 4! Here we look at White’s setup with 3.Nf3, specifically when they choose the Catalan lines with g3. White starts with 1.d4. We mirror by bringing our own d-pawn to the center.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. We support d5 with 2...e6, opening the dark-squared bishop.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {White develops the kingside knight first. We prepare our ...b5 setup with our typical 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.g3 {White goes for the Catalan system with g3. Staying true to our theme, we take on c4 and aim for ...b5.} 4...dxc4 5.Bg2 {White places the bishop on g2. As usual, we follow up with the key move of the variation: 5...b5.} 5...b5 6.O-O {White chooses to castle instead- this isn’t the best way to play. We respond with a natural move, challenging White’s strong bishop on g2 with our own.} 6...Bb7 7.a4 {White uses the typical a4 push to pressure our queenside. We place the knight on the f6 square, eyeing e4 and d5.} 7...Nf6 8.axb5 {White immediately releases the tension by taking on b5. We recapture.} 8...axb5 9.Rxa8 {White trades the rooks. We take back.} 9...Bxa8 10.b3 {White tries to disrupt our pawn chain with 10...b3. We don’t want to leave tension on our c4-pawn, so our standard answer to b3 in this chapter is simply to capture on b3.} 10...cxb3 11.Qxb3 {White recaptures, and the b5-pawn is now attacked. We push it to b4, from where it's defended by our dark-squared bishop.} 11...b4 12.Qa4+ {White creates a double attack on our bishop and king with 12.Qa4+. We defend with tempo, 12...Bc6 is our move.} 12...Bc6 13.Qc2 {The queen retreats.} 13...Be7 {The plan is to castle. We are up a pawn. ...Bb5 and ...c6 might be played later in order to activate the pieces on the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 1: The Catalan / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/4925e2e4-6f2e-4e1e-9e63-47839433068b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 4! Here we look at White’s setup with 3.Nf3, specifically when they choose the Catalan lines with g3. White starts with 1.d4. We mirror by bringing our own d-pawn to the center.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. We support d5 with 2...e6, opening the dark-squared bishop.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {White develops the kingside knight first. We prepare our ...b5 setup with our typical 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.g3 {White goes for the Catalan system with g3. Staying true to our theme, we take on c4 and aim for ...b5.} 4...dxc4 5.Bg2 {White places the bishop on g2. As usual, we follow up with the key move of the variation: 5...b5.} 5...b5 6.Ne5 {White jumps to e5, with a discovered attack on our rook. We simply block it with a move that appears to lose a pawn, 6...c6. After 6...c6, White should react energetically with either Bxc6 or 7.a4. If they castle instead, we’ll develop the light-squared bishop, complete kingside development, and castle safely.} 6...c6 7.Nxc6 {White grabs the pawn. The position may look dangerous for us, but there are no real problems. Our queen was hit, yet after 7...Qb6 White’s knight is hanging.} 7...Qb6 8.Nxb8 {White trades knights - there’s really nothing else, otherwise we play ...Bb7 next. We recapture with the rook since it was under attack.} 8...Rxb8 9.O-O {White castles. Of course, if Bf4, we would have played ...Bd6 and not much would have changed.} 9...Bb7 {We end the variation with 9...Bb7, challenging the defender of White's camp. Next, we finish up development on the kingside and castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 1: The Catalan / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/4925e2e4-6f2e-4e1e-9e63-47839433068b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 4! Here we look at White’s setup with 3.Nf3, specifically when they choose the Catalan lines with g3. White starts with 1.d4. We mirror by bringing our own d-pawn to the center.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. We support d5 with 2...e6, opening the dark-squared bishop.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {White develops the kingside knight first. We prepare our ...b5 setup with our typical 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.g3 {White goes for the Catalan system with g3. Staying true to our theme, we take on c4 and aim for ...b5.} 4...dxc4 5.Bg2 {White places the bishop on g2. As usual, we follow up with the key move of the variation: 5...b5.} 5...b5 6.Ne5 {White jumps to e5, with a discovered attack on our rook. We simply block it with a move that appears to lose a pawn, 6...c6. After 6...c6, White should react energetically with either Bxc6 or 7.a4. If they castle instead, we’ll develop the light-squared bishop, complete kingside development, and castle safely.} 6...c6 7.a4 {White often repeats the same ideas in different positions, and usually the resulting structures remain similar. We respond by developing our bishop to its typical square in this variation, 7...Bb7.} 7...Bb7 8.axb5 {Once again, White trades on the queenside. We recapture with the a-pawn.} 8...axb5 9.Rxa8 {White trades rooks. We recapture.} 9...Bxa8 10.Nc3 {The knight now “threatens” to take on b5, which would win our a8-bishop, but the threat isn’t real. We can safely ignore Nxb5 and continue developing with 10...Nf6.} 10...Nf6 11.O-O {White castles. We also prepare castling with 11...Be7. But White has one final trick…} 11...Be7 12.Nxb5 {Capturing on b5. We recapture, and then…} 12...cxb5 13.Bxa8 {The point is revealed - White recovers the piece, reaching material equality.} 13...O-O {But we can just castle and have a completely safe position, with some more queenside space. Pressure d4, finish up your development, and you'll be great.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 1: The Catalan / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/4925e2e4-6f2e-4e1e-9e63-47839433068b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 4! Here we look at White’s setup with 3.Nf3, specifically when they choose the Catalan lines with g3. White starts with 1.d4. We mirror by bringing our own d-pawn to the center.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. We support d5 with 2...e6, opening the dark-squared bishop.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {White develops the kingside knight first. We prepare our ...b5 setup with our typical 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.g3 {White goes for the Catalan system with g3. Staying true to our theme, we take on c4 and aim for ...b5.} 4...dxc4 5.Bg2 {White places the bishop on g2. As usual, we follow up with the key move of the variation: 5...b5.} 5...b5 6.Ne5 {White jumps to e5, with a discovered attack on our rook. We simply block it with a move that appears to lose a pawn, 6...c6. After 6...c6, White should react energetically with either Bxc6 or 7.a4. If they castle instead, we’ll develop the light-squared bishop, complete kingside development, and castle safely.} 6...c6 7.a4 {White often repeats the same ideas in different positions, and usually the resulting structures remain similar. We respond by developing our bishop to its typical square in this variation, 7...Bb7.} 7...Bb7 8.axb5 {Once again, White trades on the queenside. We recapture with the a-pawn.} 8...axb5 9.Rxa8 {White trades rooks. We recapture.} 9...Bxa8 10.Nc3 {The knight now “threatens” to take on b5, which would win our a8-bishop, but the threat isn’t real. We can safely ignore Nxb5 and continue developing with 10...Nf6.} 10...Nf6 11.Nxb5 {If White impatiently grabs the b5-pawn, it’s a blunder. We simply capture the knight.} 11...cxb5 12.Bxa8 {White takes our bishop...} 12...Qa5+ {...but ...Qa5+ picks up the piece since White hasn't castled yet.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 1: The Catalan / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/4925e2e4-6f2e-4e1e-9e63-47839433068b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 4! Here we look at White’s setup with 3.Nf3, specifically when they choose the Catalan lines with g3. White starts with 1.d4. We mirror by bringing our own d-pawn to the center.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. We support d5 with 2...e6, opening the dark-squared bishop.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {White develops the kingside knight first. We prepare our ...b5 setup with our typical 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.g3 {White goes for the Catalan system with g3. Staying true to our theme, we take on c4 and aim for ...b5.} 4...dxc4 5.a4 {White tries to stop our ...b5 idea. Since we can’t push the b-pawn, we play ...b6 and prepare ...Bb7.} 5...b6 6.Bg2 {White develops the bishop. We oppose the strong g2-bishop with our own.} 6...Bb7 7.O-O {White castles. We place our kingisde knight on its best square, f6.} 7...Nf6 8.Qc2 {White attacks the c4-pawn with the queen. But after ...Bb7, we can defend c4 with 8...b5 - the a8-rook is covered.} 8...b5 9.axb5 {White trades to open up their rook. We recapture.} 9...axb5 10.Rxa8 {White trades the rooks.} 10...Bxa8 {Recapturing. This is a typical position, which may arise a lot of the time. We develop with ...Be7 and ...Nd7. If b3, we can play ...cxb3 and push ...b4. There are also plans of exchanging our light-squared bishop for either the knight on f3 or bishop on g2 with ...c6 coming later.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 1: The Catalan / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/4925e2e4-6f2e-4e1e-9e63-47839433068b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 4! Here we look at White’s setup with 3.Nf3, specifically when they choose the Catalan lines with g3. White starts with 1.d4. We mirror by bringing our own d-pawn to the center.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. We support d5 with 2...e6, opening the dark-squared bishop.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {White develops the kingside knight first. We prepare our ...b5 setup with our typical 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.g3 {White goes for the Catalan system with g3. Staying true to our theme, we take on c4 and aim for ...b5.} 4...dxc4 5.Ne5 {5.Ne5 tries to win the pawn immediately, but it’s simply a mistake! 5…Nc6 challenges the centralized knight. Keep an eye on ...Qd5 next!} 5...Nc6 6.Nxc6 {White trades knights. If instead White had captured on c4, we had ...Qd5, forking h1 and c4 and winning on the spot. We don’t want to ruin our pawn structure by recapturing with the b-pawn, so we first attack the h1-rook with our queen.} 6...Qd5 7.Rg1 {White moves the rook. Now we recapture the knight. This queen maneuver avoids tripling our pawns on the c-file - a sound structural decision.} 7...Qxc6 8.Bg2 {White develops the bishop and attacks our queen. We place it on b6. Notice how poor the rook on g1 is. The real question is: where will White castle?} 8...Qb6 9.Qa4+ {White wants to take the pawn on c4 by giving a check with the queen. We develop and block the check.} 9...Bd7 10.Qxc4 {White gains the pawn back.} 10...O-O-O {We now castle queenside. Black develops very fast here. ...Nf6, ...Bb5 coming quickly with pressure on d4. White's king is also still in the center and we might try to open up the position.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 1: The Catalan / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/4925e2e4-6f2e-4e1e-9e63-47839433068b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 4! Here we look at White’s setup with 3.Nf3, specifically when they choose the Catalan lines with g3. White starts with 1.d4. We mirror by bringing our own d-pawn to the center.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. We support d5 with 2...e6, opening the dark-squared bishop.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {White develops the kingside knight first. We prepare our ...b5 setup with our typical 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.g3 {White goes for the Catalan system with g3. Staying true to our theme, we take on c4 and aim for ...b5.} 4...dxc4 5.Bg2 {White places the bishop on g2. As usual, we follow up with the key move of the variation: 5...b5.} 5...b5 6.O-O {White chooses to castle instead- this isn’t the best way to play. We respond with a natural move, challenging White’s strong bishop on g2 with our own.} 6...Bb7 7.Nc3 {White focuses on development first. We play 7...Nd7, from where it supports the upcoming …c5 break.} 7...Nd7 8.a4 {Once again, White plays the typical a4.} 8...b4 {After 8...b4 White's knight doesn't have many squares to move to and will be placed very awkwardly. With ...c5, ...Nf6, ...Be7, and castling coming we have a great position. We might play ...a5 to strengthen the pawn on b4.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 1: The Catalan / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/4925e2e4-6f2e-4e1e-9e63-47839433068b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 4! Here we look at White’s setup with 3.Nf3, specifically when they choose the Catalan lines with g3. White starts with 1.d4. We mirror by bringing our own d-pawn to the center.} 1...d5 2.c4 {The Queen’s Gambit appears. We support d5 with 2...e6, opening the dark-squared bishop.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {White develops the kingside knight first. We prepare our ...b5 setup with our typical 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.g3 {White goes for the Catalan system with g3. Staying true to our theme, we take on c4 and aim for ...b5.} 4...dxc4 5.Bg2 {White places the bishop on g2. As usual, we follow up with the key move of the variation: 5...b5.} 5...b5 6.a4 {A sensible move. Yet again utilizing the a4 plan.} 6...Bb7 {...Bb7 also defends the rook on a8 in case of axb5.} 7.b3 {White pressures the c- and b-pawns and frees up the b2-square for the bishop.} 7...cxb3 {Our usual response is to take on b3 when this pawn tension arises.} 8.Qxb3 {White captures.} 8...b4 {And as in many similar lines, ...b4 is played to protect the pawn and gain some space on the queenside. We are up a pawn and the plan is to play ... Nf6, ... Be7, ... Nbd7, and castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.e3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/baa8bdab-ba50-4bdb-82d1-82b1e8206966/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second lesson of Chapter 4! In this study we cover White’s setup with 4.e3, which usually transposes into positions from previous chapters. White prepares a standard QGD structure. We also bring our central pawn into play.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We reinforce d5.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {White develops the kingside knight first. We go for step one of our setup, 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.e3 {4.e3 is a very non-specific move and often transposes into well-known lines. Our plans remain the same: take on c4, advance with …b5, and prepare …c5. We first develop naturally with 4...Nf6 - we don’t want to take on c4 yet and help White develop with Bxc4.} 4...Nf6 5.Bd3 {Only after White develops the bishop... We take the pawn on c4.} 5...dxc4 6.Bxc4 {White recaptures. We play the desired 6…b5 with tempo.} 6...b5 7.Bd3 {White retreats. The QGD light-squared bishop - usually a problem piece for us - won’t bother us here. With 7...c5, using the support of our earlier ...b5, we strongly undermine White’s center.} 7...c5 8.dxc5 {White captures on c5, a common idea. They do this to avoid …cxd4 followed by exd4 (creating an IQP), and to avoid …cxd4 Nxd4, where the knight can later be hit by …e5. We recapture on c5 with the bishop.} 8...Bxc5 9.O-O {White castles. We develop our light-squared bishop to its ideal square, b7.} 9...Bb7 10.a4 {White threatens the b-pawn.} 10...b4 {a4 is always met by ...b4 in this setup. The development plan for us is very simple. We will castle, play ...Nbd7, and place the rooks on d8 and c8.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.e3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/baa8bdab-ba50-4bdb-82d1-82b1e8206966/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second lesson of Chapter 4! In this study we cover White’s setup with 4.e3, which usually transposes into positions from previous chapters. White prepares a standard QGD structure. We also bring our central pawn into play.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We reinforce d5.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {White develops the kingside knight first. We go for step one of our setup, 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.e3 {4.e3 is a very non-specific move and often transposes into well-known lines. Our plans remain the same: take on c4, advance with …b5, and prepare …c5. We first develop naturally with 4...Nf6 - we don’t want to take on c4 yet and help White develop with Bxc4.} 4...Nf6 5.Bd3 {Only after White develops the bishop... We take the pawn on c4.} 5...dxc4 6.Bxc4 {White recaptures. We play the desired 6…b5 with tempo.} 6...b5 7.Bd3 {White retreats. The QGD light-squared bishop - usually a problem piece for us - won’t bother us here. With 7...c5, using the support of our earlier ...b5, we strongly undermine White’s center.} 7...c5 8.O-O {Let’s say White castles in this position… We simply take on d4.} 8...cxd4 9.Nxd4 {If White takes on d4 with the knight... It can be attacked with 9...e5.} 9...e5 10.Nf3 {And White cannot go back to the knight's ideal square because of a fork.} 10...e4 {Therefore, many players just capture on c5 when they can.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.Bg5, 4.Bf4, or 4.c5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/32b32c09-30f1-4e17-95b8-b9e336a34847/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the third lesson of this chapter! Here we look at the only unique move order from White that we haven’t seen before: developing the dark-squared bishop before e3 or Nc3. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We mirror.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We choose one of the most reliable defenses and support the center.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {White keeps things flexible with 3.Nf3 - many setups are still possible. We continue with our plan and play 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.Bg5 {White develops the dark-squared bishop immediately, attacking our queen. We don’t mind trading off this bishop and offer the exchange with 4...Be7.} 4...Be7 5.Bxe7 {White captures - there’s no benefit in retreating. We recapture with the queen, keeping the …Nf6 development available. This position also appeared in Chapter 1, though by a different move order.} 5...Qxe7 6.cxd5 {White takes on d5, preventing any …dxc4 …b5 ideas. We recapture, and the upside is that our light-squared bishop now has a clear development route.} 6...exd5 7.Nc3 {White develops the knight with an attack on the d5 pawn.} 7...Nf6 {We continue by placing the knight on a natural square, 7...Nf6, defending the pawn. The next steps are to castle, play ...Nbd7, activate the bishop via ...Bf5 and centralize the rooks. The pawn on d5 will be supported with ...c6.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.Bg5, 4.Bf4, or 4.c5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/32b32c09-30f1-4e17-95b8-b9e336a34847/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the third lesson of this chapter! Here we look at the only unique move order from White that we haven’t seen before: developing the dark-squared bishop before e3 or Nc3. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We mirror.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We choose one of the most reliable defenses and support the center.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {White keeps things flexible with 3.Nf3 - many setups are still possible. We continue with our plan and play 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.c5 {White plays 4.c5, a very uncommon move, but still possible. The idea is to slow down …b5 and …c5. Although it may look annoying, 4.c5 poses no real threat. The advanced pawn is easily targeted with 4…b6. So that's what we play, immediately threatening the pawn.} 4...b6 5.cxb6 {White doesn't want to lose the pawn, so they capture it. But that doesn’t stop us from playing 5…c5. Notice how the c5 push has weakened White’s queenside. With their structure undermined, we get excellent queenside play.} 5...c5 6.dxc5 {White decides to take it again...} 6...Bxc5 {...allowing for the dark-squared bishop to be developed. We will recapture the pawn on b6, and develop very logically with ... Nf6 and ... Nc6. With a slight lead in development, we have a great position. Our open lines on the queenside will also give us great attacking chances.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.Bg5, 4.Bf4, or 4.c5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/32b32c09-30f1-4e17-95b8-b9e336a34847/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the third lesson of this chapter! Here we look at the only unique move order from White that we haven’t seen before: developing the dark-squared bishop before e3 or Nc3. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We mirror.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We choose one of the most reliable defenses and support the center.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {White keeps things flexible with 3.Nf3 - many setups are still possible. We continue with our plan and play 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.c5 {White plays 4.c5, a very uncommon move, but still possible. The idea is to slow down …b5 and …c5. Although it may look annoying, 4.c5 poses no real threat. The advanced pawn is easily targeted with 4…b6. So that's what we play, immediately threatening the pawn.} 4...b6 5.b4 {White tries to protect c5 with 5.b4, but this is a bad move. After 5…a5, White has a serious problem: the b4-pawn is hanging, and the defense of c5 collapses.} 5...a5 6.a3 {If White tries to protect the pawn on b4...} 6...axb4 {...we win a pawn since the rook on a1 is hanging.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.Bg5, 4.Bf4, or 4.c5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/32b32c09-30f1-4e17-95b8-b9e336a34847/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the third lesson of this chapter! Here we look at the only unique move order from White that we haven’t seen before: developing the dark-squared bishop before e3 or Nc3. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We mirror.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We choose one of the most reliable defenses and support the center.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {White keeps things flexible with 3.Nf3 - many setups are still possible. We continue with our plan and play 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.Bf4 {Another choice for White is 4.Bf4. This is very similar to Chapter 1 after we take on c4, just without the knight on c3. 4.Bf4 doesn't serve White any deeper purpose and will likely transpose once again. The ideas of ...dxc4, ...b5, developing with ...Nf6 still remain.} 4...dxc4 {Just remember the chapter 1 ideas. If a4, for instance, ...Nc6 for ...Na5 is our idea.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 4: White Plays 4.a4 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/4d010d95-8bde-4259-95c8-1dab41c379b5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this new study, added post-launch based on the 7th most popular move after 3...a6: 4.a4. The idea is to stop any …b5 plans. However, 4.a4 gives up the b5-square and is actually premature - White falls behind in development trying to prevent …b5. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with ours.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We’re prepared and enter the QGD.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {In this study we’ll emphasize the 3.Nf3 move order. Many 3.Nc3 lines are either inaccurate or simply transpose back to 3.Nf3 anyway. We get ready to capture on c4 by playing 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.a4 {And now comes the premature 4.a4. It stops …b5, but gives us the b4-square and doesn’t develop a piece. That’s why I recommend the immediate 4...c5, challenging the center right away. White usually chooses between 5.cxd5, 5.e3, or 5.Nc3. If they take on c5, we recapture with the bishop and develop quickly.} 4...c5 5.Nc3 {White can go for the Two Knights setup here, but it isn’t particularly strong. Yes, a4 is already on the board -but so is our c5. That’s why we can insert a favorable trade, 5...cxd4, reducing White’s central control.} 5...cxd4 6.Qxd4 {This move isn’t popular because the queen is unsafe on d4. We develop and attack it, with tempo.} 6...Nc6 7.Qd1 {The queen is forced all the way back, which is terrible for White’s development. The best way to challenge White’s setup is to push in the center with 7...d4; it becomes very hard for White to defend. 8.Na2 is one of their better moves.} 7...d4 8.Ne4 {The knight may try e4, but centralized knights can be pushed around. With 8…f5 we control e4 and kick it away.} 8...f5 9.Ng3 {The knight jumps to g3, looking safe - but now we switch gears. We play 9...e5, with ideas of …e4.} 9...e5 10.e3 {10.e3 is the only way White can develop the light-squared bishop. But it allows check on b4.} 10...Bb4+ 11.Bd2 {After the bishop blocks, the queen no longer works to control d3.} 11...d3 {Which allows us to advance 11...d3. Now, if Bxb4, ...Nxb4 and the d3-pawn is defended by the knight. We have ideas of ...e4 to reinforce the d3-pawn, normal developing moves like ...Nf6 and ...Qe7. We have more space and are restricting White's movement.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 4: White Plays 4.a4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/4d010d95-8bde-4259-95c8-1dab41c379b5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this new study, added post-launch based on the 7th most popular move after 3...a6: 4.a4. The idea is to stop any …b5 plans. However, 4.a4 gives up the b5-square and is actually premature - White falls behind in development trying to prevent …b5. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with ours.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We’re prepared and enter the QGD.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {In this study we’ll emphasize the 3.Nf3 move order. Many 3.Nc3 lines are either inaccurate or simply transpose back to 3.Nf3 anyway. We get ready to capture on c4 by playing 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.a4 {And now comes the premature 4.a4. It stops …b5, but gives us the b4-square and doesn’t develop a piece. That’s why I recommend the immediate 4...c5, challenging the center right away. White usually chooses between 5.cxd5, 5.e3, or 5.Nc3. If they take on c5, we recapture with the bishop and develop quickly.} 4...c5 5.cxd5 {This capture is the second most popular move - but also the engine’s top choice. We simply recapture, opening the diagonal for our light-squared bishop.} 5...exd5 6.Nc3 {White may now develop the knight to c3. We develop our own knight to f6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 6...Nf6 7.g3 {Some players may try a fianchetto with 7.g3. With 7...h6 we control the g5-square and prevent Bg5.} 7...h6 8.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. The bishop on g2 may add pressure on d5 once the f3-knight moves. We simply develop our queenside knight to its best square.} 8...Nc6 9.O-O {White may also choose to ignore the c5-pawn and castle. In that case, development is the name of the game. If there’s nothing better to do, we keep developing with 9...Be7. Taking on d4 now brings no structural or dynamic benefit, so we improve our pieces. Whenever White captures on c5, we recapture with the bishop and castle.} 9...Be7 10.Bf4 {White could develop their bishop, refusing to take on c5.} 10...O-O {In this case we'll castle as well. Next we develop the rest of our pieces and stabilize our center.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 4: White Plays 4.a4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/4d010d95-8bde-4259-95c8-1dab41c379b5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this new study, added post-launch based on the 7th most popular move after 3...a6: 4.a4. The idea is to stop any …b5 plans. However, 4.a4 gives up the b5-square and is actually premature - White falls behind in development trying to prevent …b5. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with ours.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We’re prepared and enter the QGD.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {In this study we’ll emphasize the 3.Nf3 move order. Many 3.Nc3 lines are either inaccurate or simply transpose back to 3.Nf3 anyway. We get ready to capture on c4 by playing 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.a4 {And now comes the premature 4.a4. It stops …b5, but gives us the b4-square and doesn’t develop a piece. That’s why I recommend the immediate 4...c5, challenging the center right away. White usually chooses between 5.cxd5, 5.e3, or 5.Nc3. If they take on c5, we recapture with the bishop and develop quickly.} 4...c5 5.e3 {White may choose to reinforce d4 with 5.e3, but this blocks their dark-squared bishop. We usually develop the knight to f6 first in QGD positions, supporting d5.} 5...Nf6 6.Nc3 {White may add pressure by developing their knight to c3. We play 6...Nc6, reaching a Four Knights setup. White next chooses between bishop development or pawn captures.} 6...Nc6 7.Be2 {The simple developing move preparing castling is normal and not challenging. We mirror and get ready to castle as well.} 7...Be7 8.O-O {White castles.} 8...O-O {And we castle too. We have ideas of ...b6 and ...Bb7, going for development. Our rook is coming to e8 and if d5 is ever taken we'll recapture with the pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 4: White Plays 4.a4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/4d010d95-8bde-4259-95c8-1dab41c379b5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this new study, added post-launch based on the 7th most popular move after 3...a6: 4.a4. The idea is to stop any …b5 plans. However, 4.a4 gives up the b5-square and is actually premature - White falls behind in development trying to prevent …b5. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with ours.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We’re prepared and enter the QGD.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {In this study we’ll emphasize the 3.Nf3 move order. Many 3.Nc3 lines are either inaccurate or simply transpose back to 3.Nf3 anyway. We get ready to capture on c4 by playing 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.a4 {And now comes the premature 4.a4. It stops …b5, but gives us the b4-square and doesn’t develop a piece. That’s why I recommend the immediate 4...c5, challenging the center right away. White usually chooses between 5.cxd5, 5.e3, or 5.Nc3. If they take on c5, we recapture with the bishop and develop quickly.} 4...c5 5.Nc3 {White can go for the Two Knights setup here, but it isn’t particularly strong. Yes, a4 is already on the board -but so is our c5. That’s why we can insert a favorable trade, 5...cxd4, reducing White’s central control.} 5...cxd4 6.Nxd4 {Capturing with the knight is the most popular move. But with the knight centralized, we attack it with 6…e5 and gain space. Wherever the knight moves, we follow with 7...d4 and then develop normally. If this feels too sharp, you can simply develop and castle - just avoid the e6–e5 push.} 6...e5 7.Nc2 {White may choose 7.Nc2, but our center is mobile. Sometimes simply pushing is strong, and so is 7...d4.} 7...d4 8.Nd5 {White jumps into d5 with the knight. Our pawn structure is solid, and the d5-knight is not an issue. We develop our bishop to f5, controlling e4.} 8...Bf5 9.e3 {You’ll often see 9.e3, challenging our pawn chain. We go after the d5-knight with 9...Ne7. The idea is simple: we invite Nxe7 so we can answer with …Bxe7 and develop smoothly.} 9...Ne7 10.Nxe7 {So if White takes... We recapture.} 10...Bxe7 11.exd4 {And if White trades on d4, we don’t have to take back immediately... We insert 11…Bxc2 first, capturing the knight and attacking the queen.} 11...Bxc2 12.Qxc2 {And once the queen arrives on c2... We can even sacrifice a pawn with 12…Nc6. Even if White keeps a pawn, our development is superior and we gain tempi against the queen.} 12...Nc6 13.d5 {This pawn push, for example, allows us a great knight outpost.} 13...Nd4 {We have ideas of ...Bb4 if Qc3 or Qd2, so White has to be careful not to lose their queen. If Qd3, moves like ...b5 are good to open up the position. If there's a rook trade on a8, the queen will become active on the a-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 4: White Plays 4.a4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/4d010d95-8bde-4259-95c8-1dab41c379b5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this new study, added post-launch based on the 7th most popular move after 3...a6: 4.a4. The idea is to stop any …b5 plans. However, 4.a4 gives up the b5-square and is actually premature - White falls behind in development trying to prevent …b5. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with ours.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We’re prepared and enter the QGD.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {In this study we’ll emphasize the 3.Nf3 move order. Many 3.Nc3 lines are either inaccurate or simply transpose back to 3.Nf3 anyway. We get ready to capture on c4 by playing 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.a4 {And now comes the premature 4.a4. It stops …b5, but gives us the b4-square and doesn’t develop a piece. That’s why I recommend the immediate 4...c5, challenging the center right away. White usually chooses between 5.cxd5, 5.e3, or 5.Nc3. If they take on c5, we recapture with the bishop and develop quickly.} 4...c5 5.cxd5 {This capture is the second most popular move - but also the engine’s top choice. We simply recapture, opening the diagonal for our light-squared bishop.} 5...exd5 6.Nc3 {White may now develop the knight to c3. We develop our own knight to f6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 6...Nf6 7.Bg5 {White may try to pin our knight and undermine d5. But because our e6-pawn recaptured on d5, we defend comfortably with 7…Be6.} 7...Be6 8.dxc5 {White may think they’re giving us an IQP, but we’re not afraid of it - this actually accelerates our development. After we recapture on c5, our bishop is out, castling is near, and …Nc6 is coming. White’s pressure on d5 isn’t significant.} 8...Bxc5 9.e3 {9.e3 tries to control d4 in front of the isolated pawn, but we develop and reinforce that square even more. After 9...Nc6 we threaten to push and trade the isolated pawn, which is never good news for the side playing against it.} 9...Nc6 10.Rc1 {For example, if the standard 10.Rc1 is played… 10…d4 is simply better for Black, both dynamically and structurally. Structurally, we eliminate our biggest weakness; dynamically, the position opens and White may not be ready.} 10...d4 11.exd4 {After the trade... We recapture with the knight to preserve our bishop.} 11...Nxd4 12.Nxd4 {White will most often recapture...} 12...Bxd4 {...and we have to recapture as well. If a natural move like Bd3, we can capture on c3 with check (...Bxc3), followed by ...Qd5, forking g2 and the bishop on g5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 4: White Plays 4.a4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/4d010d95-8bde-4259-95c8-1dab41c379b5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this new study, added post-launch based on the 7th most popular move after 3...a6: 4.a4. The idea is to stop any …b5 plans. However, 4.a4 gives up the b5-square and is actually premature - White falls behind in development trying to prevent …b5. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with ours.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We’re prepared and enter the QGD.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {In this study we’ll emphasize the 3.Nf3 move order. Many 3.Nc3 lines are either inaccurate or simply transpose back to 3.Nf3 anyway. We get ready to capture on c4 by playing 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.a4 {And now comes the premature 4.a4. It stops …b5, but gives us the b4-square and doesn’t develop a piece. That’s why I recommend the immediate 4...c5, challenging the center right away. White usually chooses between 5.cxd5, 5.e3, or 5.Nc3. If they take on c5, we recapture with the bishop and develop quickly.} 4...c5 5.cxd5 {This capture is the second most popular move - but also the engine’s top choice. We simply recapture, opening the diagonal for our light-squared bishop.} 5...exd5 6.Nc3 {White may now develop the knight to c3. We develop our own knight to f6, supporting the d5-pawn.} 6...Nf6 7.Bg5 {White may try to pin our knight and undermine d5. But because our e6-pawn recaptured on d5, we defend comfortably with 7…Be6.} 7...Be6 8.e3 {Players usually play e3 after developing the bishop. The most natural square for our knight is c6, so that's what we play, 8...Nc6.} 8...Nc6 9.Be2 {White will likely develop the bishop to e2 to avoid …c4 against Bd3. We challenge the dark-squared bishop with 9...h6. If White trades, we gain the bishop pair; if not, 9…h6 is useful to control g5.} 9...h6 10.Bh4 {If White keeps the pin... We can even go 10…g5. It’s not always a QGD idea, but here we can afford it - we’re going straight after the dark-squared bishop, forcing it to go to g3.} 10...g5 11.Bg3 {And once it reaches g3, it becomes quite a strong piece - so trading it is ideal. We go for the trade with 11...Ne4, attacking the bishop that has nowhere to go. If White castles, we capture it, follow with …Bg7, and castle.} 11...Ne4 12.Nxe4 {If White chooses to trade the knight off...} 12...dxe4 {We get a lot of pressure on the d4-pawn. It will be rather hard for White to keep the pawn structure intact. ...cxd4 will be our next move no matter what.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 4: White Plays 4.a4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d0fbddff-af74-42de-a143-449ff0f5eaa5/studies/4d010d95-8bde-4259-95c8-1dab41c379b5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this new study, added post-launch based on the 7th most popular move after 3...a6: 4.a4. The idea is to stop any …b5 plans. However, 4.a4 gives up the b5-square and is actually premature - White falls behind in development trying to prevent …b5. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We mirror with ours.} 1...d5 2.c4 {White plays the Queen’s Gambit. We’re prepared and enter the QGD.} 2...e6 3.Nf3 {In this study we’ll emphasize the 3.Nf3 move order. Many 3.Nc3 lines are either inaccurate or simply transpose back to 3.Nf3 anyway. We get ready to capture on c4 by playing 3...a6.} 3...a6 4.a4 {And now comes the premature 4.a4. It stops …b5, but gives us the b4-square and doesn’t develop a piece. That’s why I recommend the immediate 4...c5, challenging the center right away. White usually chooses between 5.cxd5, 5.e3, or 5.Nc3. If they take on c5, we recapture with the bishop and develop quickly.} 4...c5 5.Nc3 {White can go for the Two Knights setup here, but it isn’t particularly strong. Yes, a4 is already on the board -but so is our c5. That’s why we can insert a favorable trade, 5...cxd4, reducing White’s central control.} 5...cxd4 6.Nxd4 {Capturing with the knight is the most popular move. But with the knight centralized, we attack it with 6…e5 and gain space. Wherever the knight moves, we follow with 7...d4 and then develop normally. If this feels too sharp, you can simply develop and castle - just avoid the e6–e5 push.} 6...e5 7.Nf3 {White’s most natural reaction is to drop back to f3. Even so, our center is mobile, and we can bully the knights with our pawns. With 7…d4 we destabilize the queenside knight.} 7...d4 8.Ne4 {The knight may try e4, thinking it’s safer. Unfortunately for White, it isn’t - next comes 8...f5!} 8...f5 9.Ng3 {After 9.Ng3, the knight remains a target. We push our f-pawn again, attacking it. The good news is that our f-pawn also controls e3, discouraging White from playing their usual e3 setup.} 9...f4 10.Ne4 {The knight never finds a good square in this variation. We attack it with our bishop, developing with tempo.} 10...Bf5 11.Qc2 {And when the queen defends...} 11...Nc6 {We just develop further. Our pawn chain is dominant and well-defended. We've got ideas of ...Bb4+ and ...Nf6 for quick development.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Tricky 2.Nf3 Move Order / Study 1: White Plays 3.g3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/e8b10932-62e5-486e-8cde-a69054dc1a92/studies/e00b113b-6d54-43a4-b839-cc2e109cb04e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 5! Here we deal with 2.Nf3 move-orders. White may go for a g3–fianchetto or something more unconventional, like an early 3.Bg5 supported by the knight on f3. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We reply with …d5, aiming for our usual QGD setups.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {Instead of entering known territory, White may play this vague developing move. It could lead to London-type positions, Torre ideas with Bg5, or just flexible setups - but it doesn’t reveal much. So we calmly play 2...e6. Our plan is simple: if 3.c4, then 3...a6 and we return to our Chapter 4 territory.} 2...e6 3.g3 {But White may insert 3.g3, going for something unique - dark-square control with the knight and d-pawn, and light-square control with the g2-bishop. Against this, I recommend completely preventing c4 with 3...b5. This takes Catalan players out of their comfort zone. …Bb7 also activates our light-squared bishop.} 3...b5 4.Bg2 {White continues logically with 4.Bg2. Only now do we bring out our knight to f6. When there are no direct issues to address, we focus on simple development.} 4...Nf6 5.O-O {White often castles here after completing kingside development. If instead White plays 5.a4, we just push …b4 - leading us back to main-line positions. We place our bishop on b7 to coordinate our queenside. Next comes …Nbd7 and …c5 if allowed. Whether White develops the bishop or knight makes no difference: we develop, castle, and play …c5.} 5...Bb7 6.a4 {White may try to get rid of our annoying b-pawn - usually the only logical plan. But after 6…b4, we continue being irritating, controlling the c3-square.} 6...b4 7.a5 {If White delays moving the c-pawn, I recommend a different approach as well. We start with simple development, 7...Be7.} 7...Be7 8.c3 {8. c3 is also possible here. We should just castle - our b4-pawn is defended.} 8...O-O 9.cxb4 {After the pawn takes... We recapture.} 9...Bxb4 10.Qb3 {This move attacks our dark-squared bishop and x-rays the one on b7… But we calmly defend the attacked bishop with 10...Qe7.} 10...Qe7 11.Bd2 {11.Bd2 aims to remove our b4-bishop so the one on b7 becomes vulnerable. 11…Na6 is a solid defensive answer.} 11...Na6 12.Rc1 {And after our pawn is attacked with the rook...} 12...Rfc8 {We defend once more, with ...c5 to come at the right time. We need to think about the b-file pressure as well, so ...Rab8 will also exert counter-pressure on the queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Tricky 2.Nf3 Move Order / Study 1: White Plays 3.g3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/e8b10932-62e5-486e-8cde-a69054dc1a92/studies/e00b113b-6d54-43a4-b839-cc2e109cb04e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 5! Here we deal with 2.Nf3 move-orders. White may go for a g3–fianchetto or something more unconventional, like an early 3.Bg5 supported by the knight on f3. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We reply with …d5, aiming for our usual QGD setups.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {Instead of entering known territory, White may play this vague developing move. It could lead to London-type positions, Torre ideas with Bg5, or just flexible setups - but it doesn’t reveal much. So we calmly play 2...e6. Our plan is simple: if 3.c4, then 3...a6 and we return to our Chapter 4 territory.} 2...e6 3.g3 {But White may insert 3.g3, going for something unique - dark-square control with the knight and d-pawn, and light-square control with the g2-bishop. Against this, I recommend completely preventing c4 with 3...b5. This takes Catalan players out of their comfort zone. …Bb7 also activates our light-squared bishop.} 3...b5 4.Bg2 {White continues logically with 4.Bg2. Only now do we bring out our knight to f6. When there are no direct issues to address, we focus on simple development.} 4...Nf6 5.O-O {White often castles here after completing kingside development. If instead White plays 5.a4, we just push …b4 - leading us back to main-line positions. We place our bishop on b7 to coordinate our queenside. Next comes …Nbd7 and …c5 if allowed. Whether White develops the bishop or knight makes no difference: we develop, castle, and play …c5.} 5...Bb7 6.a4 {White may try to get rid of our annoying b-pawn - usually the only logical plan. But after 6…b4, we continue being irritating, controlling the c3-square.} 6...b4 7.a5 {If White delays moving the c-pawn, I recommend a different approach as well. We start with simple development, 7...Be7.} 7...Be7 8.c4 {White can go for c4 now. We simply castle.} 8...O-O 9.c5 {If White played a5, it makes sense they push c5 - trying to isolate our b-pawn. So we switch gears: c5 and a5 weaken the light squares, and our bishop can take a strong diagonal from a6, x-raying the f1-rook.} 9...Ba6 10.Ne5 {Ne5 is a typical idea, preventing …Nc6. We challenge the knight immediately with 10...Nfd7 - if we take it, we win the c5-pawn.} 10...Nfd7 11.Nxd7 {That's why White will have to take on d7...} 11...Qxd7 {And we'll recapture with the queen, with ...Nc6 to come and defend the b4-pawn, ...Rab8, and even ...b3 ideas to use the b4-square for our pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Tricky 2.Nf3 Move Order / Study 1: White Plays 3.g3 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/e8b10932-62e5-486e-8cde-a69054dc1a92/studies/e00b113b-6d54-43a4-b839-cc2e109cb04e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 5! Here we deal with 2.Nf3 move-orders. White may go for a g3–fianchetto or something more unconventional, like an early 3.Bg5 supported by the knight on f3. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We reply with …d5, aiming for our usual QGD setups.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {Instead of entering known territory, White may play this vague developing move. It could lead to London-type positions, Torre ideas with Bg5, or just flexible setups - but it doesn’t reveal much. So we calmly play 2...e6. Our plan is simple: if 3.c4, then 3...a6 and we return to our Chapter 4 territory.} 2...e6 3.g3 {But White may insert 3.g3, going for something unique - dark-square control with the knight and d-pawn, and light-square control with the g2-bishop. Against this, I recommend completely preventing c4 with 3...b5. This takes Catalan players out of their comfort zone. …Bb7 also activates our light-squared bishop.} 3...b5 4.Bg2 {White continues logically with 4.Bg2. Only now do we bring out our knight to f6. When there are no direct issues to address, we focus on simple development.} 4...Nf6 5.O-O {White often castles here after completing kingside development. If instead White plays 5.a4, we just push …b4 - leading us back to main-line positions. We place our bishop on b7 to coordinate our queenside. Next comes …Nbd7 and …c5 if allowed. Whether White develops the bishop or knight makes no difference: we develop, castle, and play …c5.} 5...Bb7 6.a4 {White may try to get rid of our annoying b-pawn - usually the only logical plan. But after 6…b4, we continue being irritating, controlling the c3-square.} 6...b4 7.a5 {If White delays moving the c-pawn, I recommend a different approach as well. We start with simple development, 7...Be7.} 7...Be7 8.c4 {White can go for c4 now. We simply castle.} 8...O-O 9.c5 {If White played a5, it makes sense they push c5 - trying to isolate our b-pawn. So we switch gears: c5 and a5 weaken the light squares, and our bishop can take a strong diagonal from a6, x-raying the f1-rook.} 9...Ba6 10.Re1 {If 10.Re1, aiming to go e4 at some point...} 10...Nc6 {Our knight has the c6-square to land on. We've got moves like ...Rb8 to defend the b4-pawn and get out of the diagonal, ...Ne4 to centralize the knight and ...Qe8 to defend the c6-knight if Qa5.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Tricky 2.Nf3 Move Order / Study 1: White Plays 3.g3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/e8b10932-62e5-486e-8cde-a69054dc1a92/studies/e00b113b-6d54-43a4-b839-cc2e109cb04e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 5! Here we deal with 2.Nf3 move-orders. White may go for a g3–fianchetto or something more unconventional, like an early 3.Bg5 supported by the knight on f3. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We reply with …d5, aiming for our usual QGD setups.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {Instead of entering known territory, White may play this vague developing move. It could lead to London-type positions, Torre ideas with Bg5, or just flexible setups - but it doesn’t reveal much. So we calmly play 2...e6. Our plan is simple: if 3.c4, then 3...a6 and we return to our Chapter 4 territory.} 2...e6 3.g3 {But White may insert 3.g3, going for something unique - dark-square control with the knight and d-pawn, and light-square control with the g2-bishop. Against this, I recommend completely preventing c4 with 3...b5. This takes Catalan players out of their comfort zone. …Bb7 also activates our light-squared bishop.} 3...b5 4.Bg2 {White continues logically with 4.Bg2. Only now do we bring out our knight to f6. When there are no direct issues to address, we focus on simple development.} 4...Nf6 5.O-O {White often castles here after completing kingside development. If instead White plays 5.a4, we just push …b4 - leading us back to main-line positions. We place our bishop on b7 to coordinate our queenside. Next comes …Nbd7 and …c5 if allowed. Whether White develops the bishop or knight makes no difference: we develop, castle, and play …c5.} 5...Bb7 6.a4 {White may try to get rid of our annoying b-pawn - usually the only logical plan. But after 6…b4, we continue being irritating, controlling the c3-square.} 6...b4 7.a5 {If White delays moving the c-pawn, I recommend a different approach as well. We start with simple development, 7...Be7.} 7...Be7 8.c3 {8. c3 is also possible here. We should just castle - our b4-pawn is defended.} 8...O-O 9.cxb4 {After the pawn takes... We recapture.} 9...Bxb4 10.Qb3 {This move attacks our dark-squared bishop and x-rays the one on b7… But we calmly defend the attacked bishop with 10...Qe7.} 10...Qe7 11.Nc3 {If 11.Nc3, White doesn't add pressure on our bishops so we can be a bit safer. Still, I like 11...Na6 here.} 11...Na6 {11...Na6 is a solid move that covers many weaknesses and freezes the a5-pawn. We'll continue with ...Rab8 and ...Bd6 back to fight for control of the e5-square and have our rook threaten the queen on the b-file to a greater capacity.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Tricky 2.Nf3 Move Order / Study 1: White Plays 3.g3 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/e8b10932-62e5-486e-8cde-a69054dc1a92/studies/e00b113b-6d54-43a4-b839-cc2e109cb04e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 5! Here we deal with 2.Nf3 move-orders. White may go for a g3–fianchetto or something more unconventional, like an early 3.Bg5 supported by the knight on f3. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We reply with …d5, aiming for our usual QGD setups.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {Instead of entering known territory, White may play this vague developing move. It could lead to London-type positions, Torre ideas with Bg5, or just flexible setups - but it doesn’t reveal much. So we calmly play 2...e6. Our plan is simple: if 3.c4, then 3...a6 and we return to our Chapter 4 territory.} 2...e6 3.g3 {But White may insert 3.g3, going for something unique - dark-square control with the knight and d-pawn, and light-square control with the g2-bishop. Against this, I recommend completely preventing c4 with 3...b5. This takes Catalan players out of their comfort zone. …Bb7 also activates our light-squared bishop.} 3...b5 4.Bg2 {White continues logically with 4.Bg2. Only now do we bring out our knight to f6. When there are no direct issues to address, we focus on simple development.} 4...Nf6 5.O-O {White often castles here after completing kingside development. If instead White plays 5.a4, we just push …b4 - leading us back to main-line positions. We place our bishop on b7 to coordinate our queenside. Next comes …Nbd7 and …c5 if allowed. Whether White develops the bishop or knight makes no difference: we develop, castle, and play …c5.} 5...Bb7 6.a4 {White may try to get rid of our annoying b-pawn - usually the only logical plan. But after 6…b4, we continue being irritating, controlling the c3-square.} 6...b4 7.a5 {If White delays moving the c-pawn, I recommend a different approach as well. We start with simple development, 7...Be7.} 7...Be7 8.Ne5 {White always has this Ne5 idea. But we can castle - it doesn’t create any real pressure yet.} 8...O-O 9.c4 {And after c4...} 9...c5 {We've got 9...c5, weakening the d4-pawn which supports the knight. We've got ...Qc7 to develop the queen, clearing the way for ...Rd8. We're not worried about the tension on d5 because we've got two minor pieces on that square.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Tricky 2.Nf3 Move Order / Study 1: White Plays 3.g3 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/e8b10932-62e5-486e-8cde-a69054dc1a92/studies/e00b113b-6d54-43a4-b839-cc2e109cb04e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 5! Here we deal with 2.Nf3 move-orders. White may go for a g3–fianchetto or something more unconventional, like an early 3.Bg5 supported by the knight on f3. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We reply with …d5, aiming for our usual QGD setups.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {Instead of entering known territory, White may play this vague developing move. It could lead to London-type positions, Torre ideas with Bg5, or just flexible setups - but it doesn’t reveal much. So we calmly play 2...e6. Our plan is simple: if 3.c4, then 3...a6 and we return to our Chapter 4 territory.} 2...e6 3.g3 {But White may insert 3.g3, going for something unique - dark-square control with the knight and d-pawn, and light-square control with the g2-bishop. Against this, I recommend completely preventing c4 with 3...b5. This takes Catalan players out of their comfort zone. …Bb7 also activates our light-squared bishop.} 3...b5 4.e3 {White may go after the b5-pawn with 4.e3, but this is short-sighted - it blocks the dark-squared bishop, which normally belongs on g5. We solve the problem with 4...a6. If White increases the pressure with 5.a4, then 5...b4 escapes the attack and controls c3.} 4...a6 5.Bg2 {White may still fianchetto. But remember: one of the points of …b5 is that it supports …Bb7 as well.} 5...Bb7 6.O-O {After White castles... If there’s no direct pressure, we simply develop with 6...Nf6. Then we castle - simple and strong.} 6...Nf6 7.a4 {If 7.a4 to attack the pawn chain...} 7...Nbd7 {Our pawn chain is already sufficiently defended. After this point, White has to think about developing their queenside pieces since 4. e3 blocked the bishop in. This isn't an easy problem to solve.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Tricky 2.Nf3 Move Order / Study 1: White Plays 3.g3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/e8b10932-62e5-486e-8cde-a69054dc1a92/studies/e00b113b-6d54-43a4-b839-cc2e109cb04e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 5! Here we deal with 2.Nf3 move-orders. White may go for a g3–fianchetto or something more unconventional, like an early 3.Bg5 supported by the knight on f3. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We reply with …d5, aiming for our usual QGD setups.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {Instead of entering known territory, White may play this vague developing move. It could lead to London-type positions, Torre ideas with Bg5, or just flexible setups - but it doesn’t reveal much. So we calmly play 2...e6. Our plan is simple: if 3.c4, then 3...a6 and we return to our Chapter 4 territory.} 2...e6 3.g3 {But White may insert 3.g3, going for something unique - dark-square control with the knight and d-pawn, and light-square control with the g2-bishop. Against this, I recommend completely preventing c4 with 3...b5. This takes Catalan players out of their comfort zone. …Bb7 also activates our light-squared bishop.} 3...b5 4.Bg2 {White continues logically with 4.Bg2. Only now do we bring out our knight to f6. When there are no direct issues to address, we focus on simple development.} 4...Nf6 5.O-O {White often castles here after completing kingside development. If instead White plays 5.a4, we just push …b4 - leading us back to main-line positions. We place our bishop on b7 to coordinate our queenside. Next comes …Nbd7 and …c5 if allowed. Whether White develops the bishop or knight makes no difference: we develop, castle, and play …c5.} 5...Bb7 6.a4 {White may try to get rid of our annoying b-pawn - usually the only logical plan. But after 6…b4, we continue being irritating, controlling the c3-square.} 6...b4 7.c4 {White gains access to the c4 break once we push …b4. Yet we simply take en passant.} 7...bxc3 8.Nxc3 {The knight recaptures.} 8...a5 {I suggest we control the b4-square here and stop the a-pawn's march up the board. Next: ...Be7, ...0-0, ...Nbd7, ...Ba6, ...c5. A very easy plan to execute.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Tricky 2.Nf3 Move Order / Study 1: White Plays 3.g3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/e8b10932-62e5-486e-8cde-a69054dc1a92/studies/e00b113b-6d54-43a4-b839-cc2e109cb04e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Chapter 5! Here we deal with 2.Nf3 move-orders. White may go for a g3–fianchetto or something more unconventional, like an early 3.Bg5 supported by the knight on f3. White begins with the queen’s pawn. We reply with …d5, aiming for our usual QGD setups.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {Instead of entering known territory, White may play this vague developing move. It could lead to London-type positions, Torre ideas with Bg5, or just flexible setups - but it doesn’t reveal much. So we calmly play 2...e6. Our plan is simple: if 3.c4, then 3...a6 and we return to our Chapter 4 territory.} 2...e6 3.g3 {But White may insert 3.g3, going for something unique - dark-square control with the knight and d-pawn, and light-square control with the g2-bishop. Against this, I recommend completely preventing c4 with 3...b5. This takes Catalan players out of their comfort zone. …Bb7 also activates our light-squared bishop.} 3...b5 4.Bg2 {White continues logically with 4.Bg2. Only now do we bring out our knight to f6. When there are no direct issues to address, we focus on simple development.} 4...Nf6 5.O-O {White often castles here after completing kingside development. If instead White plays 5.a4, we just push …b4 - leading us back to main-line positions. We place our bishop on b7 to coordinate our queenside. Next comes …Nbd7 and …c5 if allowed. Whether White develops the bishop or knight makes no difference: we develop, castle, and play …c5.} 5...Bb7 6.a4 {White may try to get rid of our annoying b-pawn - usually the only logical plan. But after 6…b4, we continue being irritating, controlling the c3-square.} 6...b4 7.c4 {White gains access to the c4 break once we push …b4. Yet we simply take en passant.} 7...bxc3 8.bxc3 {If the pawn takes, the b-file becomes fully open and the c3-pawn is backwards.} 8...Nbd7 {I like going for quick development here since the bishop is on b7 and it could come under fire soon. Also, ...Be7 and castling is always good. The position is balanced. If White targets our b7-bishop with their rook, we slide it forward one square.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Tricky 2.Nf3 Move Order / Study 2: Other 3rd Moves / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/e8b10932-62e5-486e-8cde-a69054dc1a92/studies/8b47a723-d464-4a09-b418-d24f55b4c0de/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 5! Here we cover the Torre Attack and some other unconventional 3rd-move options from White. White starts with 1.d4, claiming space in the center. We mirror with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {2.Nf3 is very flexible and keeps White’s intentions hidden. We must be ready for everything. Once again, we go 2...e6 to remain prepared to transpose into known QGD territory.} 2...e6 3.e3 {3.e3 is a very rare move order. You won’t face this often, and you don’t need to memorize much. White usually sets up pawns on e3, d4, c3 with the bishop on d3 - but the dark-squared bishop becomes passive, so this opening shouldn’t concern us. We develop our knight to f6 and wait for White’s next step.} 3...Nf6 4.Bd3 {As seen in the previous study, Bd3 is very typical when White places many pawns on dark squares. 4...a6 is our best move, transposing back to the course. If c4, then …dxc4 and …b5.} 4...a6 5.O-O {White may castle, preparing their main break: e4. That’s why we play 5...b6, aiming to develop our bishop to b7 - trying to make e4 harder to achieve.} 5...b6 6.Nbd2 {White may reinforce e4... But so do we, 6...Bb7 is coming.} 6...Bb7 7.Re1 {Eventually White might get e4 in... But only by allowing us counterplay in the center with the 7...c5 pawn break!} 7...c5 8.c3 {You’ll often see 8.c3 as White tries to keep the center solid. We develop our other knight, 8...Nbd7.} 8...Nbd7 9.e4 {White manages to play their desired move, 9.e4, but it weakens their d4-pawn. We may not win it immediately, but we will isolate it with 9...cxd4 - because Nxd4 runs into …Nc5, hitting both the bishop and the e4-pawn.} 9...cxd4 10.cxd4 {If White recaptures on d4... Taking on e4 favors us, since we isolate the d-pawn.} 10...dxe4 11.Nxe4 {White recaptures with the knight, centralizing a piece.} 11...Be7 {White has an IQP they won't easily get rid of. We'll continue with ...0-0, ...b5 to expand on the queenside, and we'll develop the queen depending on what White does. If they trade on f6, Nxf6+, we can recapture with the knight and develop the queen to d6.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Tricky 2.Nf3 Move Order / Study 2: Other 3rd Moves / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/e8b10932-62e5-486e-8cde-a69054dc1a92/studies/8b47a723-d464-4a09-b418-d24f55b4c0de/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 5! Here we cover the Torre Attack and some other unconventional 3rd-move options from White. White starts with 1.d4, claiming space in the center. We mirror with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {2.Nf3 is very flexible and keeps White’s intentions hidden. We must be ready for everything. Once again, we go 2...e6 to remain prepared to transpose into known QGD territory.} 2...e6 3.Bg5 {3.Bg5 attacks our queen. In this course, whenever Bg5 appears, our standard answer is …Be7, blocking the attack.} 3...Be7 4.Bxe7 {Trading is the most logical continuation. After we recapture with the queen, ideas of Qb4+ come into play.} 4...Qxe7 5.c3 {5.c3 stops …Qb4+, but White is heading toward a c3–d4–e3 London structure without the London bishop - making the setup far less convincing. With 5...Nf6 we increase control over e4, so e3 is the most likely follow-up.} 5...Nf6 6.e3 {This becomes an inferior London. We continue in a principled way, castling our king.} 6...O-O 7.Bd3 {d3 is the best square for White's light-squared bishop in these positions. And …c5 is our thematic break to undermine d4.} 7...c5 8.O-O {White castles and keeps playing typical London moves. We respond with 8...b6 - we want to use b7 for our bishop, since the f7–e6–d5 chain is currently locked.} 8...b6 9.Nbd2 {White develops the knight. From d2 it supports e4 and c4. We continue with 9...Bb7, an excellent square - reinforcing e4 and making the pawn push difficult. 10.Re1 is the usual way White prepares the advance.} 9...Bb7 10.Re1 {10.Re1 appears to support e4. We bring our knight out to d7.} 10...Nbd7 11.e4 {e4 arrives. Since e5 is a threat, we capture on e4, but only once. We don’t want to move the f6-knight because it helps defend h7.} 11...dxe4 12.Nxe4 {And after the knight recaptures, we're not going for massive trades in the center.} 12...Rad8 {Rather we'll place a rook on d8 to x-ray many of White's pieces on the d-file. The position is fairly equal. ...h6 is a safe defensive move to keep a piece out of g5. ...Nd5 is also a good move to centralize the knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Tricky 2.Nf3 Move Order / Study 2: Other 3rd Moves / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/e8b10932-62e5-486e-8cde-a69054dc1a92/studies/8b47a723-d464-4a09-b418-d24f55b4c0de/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 5! Here we cover the Torre Attack and some other unconventional 3rd-move options from White. White starts with 1.d4, claiming space in the center. We mirror with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {2.Nf3 is very flexible and keeps White’s intentions hidden. We must be ready for everything. Once again, we go 2...e6 to remain prepared to transpose into known QGD territory.} 2...e6 3.e3 {3.e3 is a very rare move order. You won’t face this often, and you don’t need to memorize much. White usually sets up pawns on e3, d4, c3 with the bishop on d3 - but the dark-squared bishop becomes passive, so this opening shouldn’t concern us. We develop our knight to f6 and wait for White’s next step.} 3...Nf6 4.Bd3 {As seen in the previous study, Bd3 is very typical when White places many pawns on dark squares. 4...a6 is our best move, transposing back to the course. If c4, then …dxc4 and …b5.} 4...a6 5.O-O {White may castle, preparing their main break: e4. That’s why we play 5...b6, aiming to develop our bishop to b7 - trying to make e4 harder to achieve.} 5...b6 6.Nbd2 {White may reinforce e4... But so do we, 6...Bb7 is coming.} 6...Bb7 7.Re1 {Eventually White might get e4 in... But only by allowing us counterplay in the center with the 7...c5 pawn break!} 7...c5 8.e4 {In essence, it looks like we've stopped e4.} 8...c4 {Because we can chase one of the pieces that supports the e4-pawn. We'll be taking on e4 next. White's dark-squared bishop is still buried on c1 and soon the attacked one will be on f1 as well.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Tricky 2.Nf3 Move Order / Study 2: Other 3rd Moves / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/e8b10932-62e5-486e-8cde-a69054dc1a92/studies/8b47a723-d464-4a09-b418-d24f55b4c0de/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 5! Here we cover the Torre Attack and some other unconventional 3rd-move options from White. White starts with 1.d4, claiming space in the center. We mirror with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {2.Nf3 is very flexible and keeps White’s intentions hidden. We must be ready for everything. Once again, we go 2...e6 to remain prepared to transpose into known QGD territory.} 2...e6 3.Bg5 {3.Bg5 attacks our queen. In this course, whenever Bg5 appears, our standard answer is …Be7, blocking the attack.} 3...Be7 4.Bxe7 {Trading is the most logical continuation. After we recapture with the queen, ideas of Qb4+ come into play.} 4...Qxe7 5.Nc3 {White may go 5.Nc3 immediately. This blocks the c-pawn, but White’s idea is a fast e4 break. We develop with 5...Nf6 and challenge that plan.} 5...Nf6 6.e3 {Many players resort to 6.e3 because 6.e4 is no longer possible. This is a small admission that 5.Nc3 may not have been ideal. Meanwhile, we do have a pawn break: 6…c5 undermines d4. If White takes on c5, they lose control of e5, which is often White’s key square in these QP structures.} 6...c5 7.Bb5+ {Bb5+ is the most popular response. But this check accomplishes nothing - we simply block the check with our bishop.} 7...Bd7 8.Bxd7+ {And after this trade...} 8...Nbxd7 {White has traded Black's worst piece in symmetrical d4 d5 positions: the light-squared bishop. We'll castle kingside and expand on the queenside with ...a6 and ...b5, taking advantage of the fact that the c3-knight is badly placed.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Tricky 2.Nf3 Move Order / Study 2: Other 3rd Moves / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/e8b10932-62e5-486e-8cde-a69054dc1a92/studies/8b47a723-d464-4a09-b418-d24f55b4c0de/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 5! Here we cover the Torre Attack and some other unconventional 3rd-move options from White. White starts with 1.d4, claiming space in the center. We mirror with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {2.Nf3 is very flexible and keeps White’s intentions hidden. We must be ready for everything. Once again, we go 2...e6 to remain prepared to transpose into known QGD territory.} 2...e6 3.Bg5 {3.Bg5 attacks our queen. In this course, whenever Bg5 appears, our standard answer is …Be7, blocking the attack.} 3...Be7 4.Bxe7 {Trading is the most logical continuation. After we recapture with the queen, ideas of Qb4+ come into play.} 4...Qxe7 5.c4 {5.c4 is a good move, a logical pawn break to undermine our center. We don’t go for …Qb4+ because White can simply meet it with Qd2, defending b2. Instead, we bring out the knight to f6, enabling castling and reinforcing the e4-square.} 5...Nf6 6.Nc3 {Once the c-pawn moves, it’s natural for White to place the knight on c3. Against 6.Nc3, we follow a similar plan to the 6.e3 lines and castle.} 6...O-O 7.e3 {The reason being is that Nc3 and e3 are interchangeable many times. So we can support d5 with 7...Rd8 and place the rook opposite White's queen.} 7...Rd8 8.Qc2 {After 8.Qc2 it looks like a fairly normal Queen's Gambit Declined Position.} 8...b6 {We end the variation with 8...b6 and will look to play ...Bb7 and ...Nbd7 for our c5-pawn break. The position is fairly stable, but after a few pawn trades in the center Black's pieces are more active on the queenside and the bishop's diagonal on b7 may even become open if the d5-pawn comes off the board.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Tricky 2.Nf3 Move Order / Study 2: Other 3rd Moves / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/e8b10932-62e5-486e-8cde-a69054dc1a92/studies/8b47a723-d464-4a09-b418-d24f55b4c0de/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 5! Here we cover the Torre Attack and some other unconventional 3rd-move options from White. White starts with 1.d4, claiming space in the center. We mirror with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {2.Nf3 is very flexible and keeps White’s intentions hidden. We must be ready for everything. Once again, we go 2...e6 to remain prepared to transpose into known QGD territory.} 2...e6 3.Bg5 {3.Bg5 attacks our queen. In this course, whenever Bg5 appears, our standard answer is …Be7, blocking the attack.} 3...Be7 4.Bxe7 {Trading is the most logical continuation. After we recapture with the queen, ideas of Qb4+ come into play.} 4...Qxe7 5.c4 {5.c4 is a good move, a logical pawn break to undermine our center. We don’t go for …Qb4+ because White can simply meet it with Qd2, defending b2. Instead, we bring out the knight to f6, enabling castling and reinforcing the e4-square.} 5...Nf6 6.e3 {You may also face 6. e3. Again, ...Qb4+ isn't relevant anymore.} 6...O-O {So we'll just castle. The position is about equal. We'll go ...b6, ...Bb7 and ...c5 at some point, with a stable position.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Tricky 2.Nf3 Move Order / Study 2: Other 3rd Moves / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/e8b10932-62e5-486e-8cde-a69054dc1a92/studies/8b47a723-d464-4a09-b418-d24f55b4c0de/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 5! Here we cover the Torre Attack and some other unconventional 3rd-move options from White. White starts with 1.d4, claiming space in the center. We mirror with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {2.Nf3 is very flexible and keeps White’s intentions hidden. We must be ready for everything. Once again, we go 2...e6 to remain prepared to transpose into known QGD territory.} 2...e6 3.Bg5 {3.Bg5 attacks our queen. In this course, whenever Bg5 appears, our standard answer is …Be7, blocking the attack.} 3...Be7 4.Bxe7 {Trading is the most logical continuation. After we recapture with the queen, ideas of Qb4+ come into play.} 4...Qxe7 5.e3 {5. e3, allowing Qb4+, is the most popular continuation. So we give the check and target the b2-pawn.} 5...Qb4+ 6.Nbd2 {Blocking the check with 6.Nbd2 is the main reply. White stays coordinated and willingly gives up the b2-pawn to activate the rook. We can take on b2 and remain a pawn up.} 6...Qxb2 7.Bd3 {7.Bd3 develops. If 7.Rb1 instead, the a2-pawn hangs. e4 becomes an idea in many of these positions, so we should place our knight on f6 to fight for the square.} 7...Nf6 8.O-O {White castles. We castle as well. Being a pawn up, our job is simply to finish development.} 8...O-O 9.Ne5 {9.Ne5 is a popular try: since White controls the e5-square, they may try to get a knight there. But this isn’t the right moment - White should have tried pawn breaks or activated other pieces. 9…c5 undermines the d4-pawn, as we’ve done throughout the course.} 9...c5 10.Rb1 {White may try to relieve the pressure on d4 by moving the queen away from b2. But whenever the rook slides to b1, we can grab yet another pawn, 10...Qxa2.} 10...Qxa2 11.dxc5 {If White resolves the tension with a capture… We slide our queen to a5. The idea isn't to go after the pawn, it is to get the queen back into a safer square where it helps defend the Black position.} 11...Qa5 12.Nb3 {12.Nb3 defends c5 and attacks the queen...} 12...Qc7 {But we've got pressure against the knight on e5. ...Nbd7 will challenge that knight successfully. With the queen on c7, the ...Bd7-c6 maneuver becomes possible. Black is up a pawn and should have long-lasting pressure against the doubled c-pawns.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Tricky 2.Nf3 Move Order / Study 2: Other 3rd Moves / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/e8b10932-62e5-486e-8cde-a69054dc1a92/studies/8b47a723-d464-4a09-b418-d24f55b4c0de/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 5! Here we cover the Torre Attack and some other unconventional 3rd-move options from White. White starts with 1.d4, claiming space in the center. We mirror with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {2.Nf3 is very flexible and keeps White’s intentions hidden. We must be ready for everything. Once again, we go 2...e6 to remain prepared to transpose into known QGD territory.} 2...e6 3.Bg5 {3.Bg5 attacks our queen. In this course, whenever Bg5 appears, our standard answer is …Be7, blocking the attack.} 3...Be7 4.Bxe7 {Trading is the most logical continuation. After we recapture with the queen, ideas of Qb4+ come into play.} 4...Qxe7 5.g3 {This move is too slow. It aims for a Bg2 setup, but it allows 5…Qb4+ while White is far behind in development. We’re fully justified in going pawn-hunting!} 5...Qb4+ 6.Nbd2 {White may block...} 6...Qxb2 {And White still has to go Bg2 and 0-0 to develop. If Bg2, we can even go 7...Nc6 for ...Nb4. If our queen is attacked with Rb1, taking on a2 is strong.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Tricky 2.Nf3 Move Order / Study 2: Other 3rd Moves / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/e8b10932-62e5-486e-8cde-a69054dc1a92/studies/8b47a723-d464-4a09-b418-d24f55b4c0de/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 5! Here we cover the Torre Attack and some other unconventional 3rd-move options from White. White starts with 1.d4, claiming space in the center. We mirror with 1...d5.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {2.Nf3 is very flexible and keeps White’s intentions hidden. We must be ready for everything. Once again, we go 2...e6 to remain prepared to transpose into known QGD territory.} 2...e6 3.Bf4 {This is the London System with 2. Nf3 and 3. Bf4. Refer to Chapter 6 for this one!} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 1: The 2. Nf3 London / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/64f93e5c-6955-4e5f-ad92-f56bd9cc4c3b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our first study of this chapter. T he 2.Bf4 London is a simple approach, and you won’t see many deviations - especially when White follows with 3.e3. White opens with 1.d4. We reply with 1…d5, ready for a Queen’s Gambit structure.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {White may go 2.Nf3. We play 2…e6, keeping the option to face c4. We don’t know whether White will choose Bf4, c4, or g3, so …e6 ensures we can always transpose into familiar territory. If we play 2...Nf6, White could enter a Catalan, and without our pawn on e6 we cannot steer the game back to our course.} 2...e6 3.Bf4 {But White may instead bring the bishop out immediately and take us into the London. Since this move order is different, we can also do something different. We challenge the bishop on f4 right away by playing 3...Bd6.} 3...Bd6 4.e3 {White may defend the bishop with 4.e3, hoping that if we take on f4, they recapture with the pawn and strengthen e5. So we do not capture. Instead we place a knight on f6. This allows White the tricky Ne5 idea.} 4...Nf6 5.Ne5 {Because our knight sits on f6 and White has played e3, we cannot kick the knight from e5 with …f6. This is a serious line played even by top GMs. We should castle early to avoid any potential tactics.} 5...O-O 6.Bd3 {White brings the bishop to d3. We counter with 6…c5 immediately.} 6...c5 7.c3 {London players rarely take on c5. They usually reinforce the pawn and create the c3–d4–e3 triangle. We play 7…Qc7, keeping pressure on the diagonal without losing sight of the c5-pawn.} 7...Qc7 8.Nd2 {White develops the other knight. We continue with 8...Nbd7, developing while maintaining pressure on c5.} 8...Nbd7 9.Ndf3 {White brings the knight to f3, adding more control over e5. Without losing time, we set up queenside counterplay, starting with 9...Rb8.} 9...Rb8 10.g4 {London structures can get sharp quickly - White attacks on the kingside. Our counter is to attack on the queenside, 10...b5 is our next move!} 10...b5 11.g5 {If White advances with g5, they do gain space, but the pawn also takes away an important square from their pieces. We jump to e4 with our knight.} 11...Ne4 12.Qc2 {White increases pressure on e4… We reinforce the knight with 12...f5. If White avoids en passant, we maintain a strong outpost on e4.} 12...f5 13.gxf6 {If White does take on en passant...} 13...Ndxf6 {We'll also get to support our knight on e4. You've got ideas of ...b4 and ...c4. If dxc5, we recapture with the knight and attack the bishop on d3. We have to keep our king safe and attack on the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 1: The 2. Nf3 London / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/64f93e5c-6955-4e5f-ad92-f56bd9cc4c3b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our first study of this chapter. T he 2.Bf4 London is a simple approach, and you won’t see many deviations - especially when White follows with 3.e3. White opens with 1.d4. We reply with 1…d5, ready for a Queen’s Gambit structure.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {White may go 2.Nf3. We play 2…e6, keeping the option to face c4. We don’t know whether White will choose Bf4, c4, or g3, so …e6 ensures we can always transpose into familiar territory. If we play 2...Nf6, White could enter a Catalan, and without our pawn on e6 we cannot steer the game back to our course.} 2...e6 3.Bf4 {But White may instead bring the bishop out immediately and take us into the London. Since this move order is different, we can also do something different. We challenge the bishop on f4 right away by playing 3...Bd6.} 3...Bd6 4.Bxd6 {First, we check what happens if White accepts the trade. If they do, we’re happy - we’ve traded off White’s best piece: the London Bishop. Our queen lands on d6, eyeing b4. Our plan is simple: develop, castle kingside, and then expand on the queenside with …c5, …b6, and so on.} 4...Qxd6 5.e3 {5.e3 is standard London play, activating the light-squared bishop and queen. But 5.c3 was also possible, especially since after the bishop trade on d6. 5.e3 allows us to throw in a check on b4 with the queen.} 5...Qb4+ 6.c3 {This block doesn’t work - there will be too much pressure on White’s undeveloped queenside. After we take on b2, we attack the rook.} 6...Qxb2 7.Nbd2 {And when the knight moves so the queen can defend it... We capture on c3. Essentially, c3 was played only to be taken.} 7...Qxc3 8.Rc1 {The rook might try to get active, and White may claim compensation… But queens excel at hitting multiple weaknesses by sliding to a3. a2 becomes the next target.} 8...Qa3 9.Rxc7 {White cannot even take this pawn... After 9...Nc6 the rook is trapped.} 9...Nc6 10.Bb5 {White may try to attack the knight...} 10...Qd6 {But this move attacks the rook and defends the knight. White is lost.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 1: The 2. Nf3 London / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/64f93e5c-6955-4e5f-ad92-f56bd9cc4c3b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our first study of this chapter. T he 2.Bf4 London is a simple approach, and you won’t see many deviations - especially when White follows with 3.e3. White opens with 1.d4. We reply with 1…d5, ready for a Queen’s Gambit structure.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {White may go 2.Nf3. We play 2…e6, keeping the option to face c4. We don’t know whether White will choose Bf4, c4, or g3, so …e6 ensures we can always transpose into familiar territory. If we play 2...Nf6, White could enter a Catalan, and without our pawn on e6 we cannot steer the game back to our course.} 2...e6 3.Bf4 {But White may instead bring the bishop out immediately and take us into the London. Since this move order is different, we can also do something different. We challenge the bishop on f4 right away by playing 3...Bd6.} 3...Bd6 4.Bxd6 {First, we check what happens if White accepts the trade. If they do, we’re happy - we’ve traded off White’s best piece: the London Bishop. Our queen lands on d6, eyeing b4. Our plan is simple: develop, castle kingside, and then expand on the queenside with …c5, …b6, and so on.} 4...Qxd6 5.c3 {Playing the pawn to c3 aims to develop the queen and prevent …Qb4+. We simply develop the kingside with 5...Nf6 and prepare to castle.} 5...Nf6 6.e3 {White can continue with standard London pawn-and-piece play. We develop another piece, 6...Nbd7. White has spent a lot of time moving pawns, so we might even prepare central counterplay.} 6...Nbd7 7.Bd3 {This move is very common. It’s interchangeable with Nbd2 and can transpose. We go for …b6 and …Bb7.} 7...b6 8.O-O {White castles. As I said, Nbd2 is equally likely - London players follow their system. We fianchetto.} 8...Bb7 9.Nbd2 {The knight develops to d2. Fortunately, in this variation we control e5, so White’s plan of planting a knight there won’t work. We castle.} 9...O-O 10.Re1 {White’s only counterplay is to prepare the e4-pawn push. We strike with 10…c5.} 10...c5 11.e4 {After 11.e4... We take, removing the tension.} 11...dxe4 12.Nxe4 {And after the knight recaptures in the center attacking our queen...} 12...Qc7 {We won't go for trades in the center, rather we'll keep our pieces as they are. If White doesn't take on c5, we'll take on d4. If they take on c5, we'll trade bishop and knight on e4 and only then recapture on c5 with the knight. We've got a rook coming into the d8-square and we have a stable middlegame.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 1: The 2. Nf3 London / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/64f93e5c-6955-4e5f-ad92-f56bd9cc4c3b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our first study of this chapter. T he 2.Bf4 London is a simple approach, and you won’t see many deviations - especially when White follows with 3.e3. White opens with 1.d4. We reply with 1…d5, ready for a Queen’s Gambit structure.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {White may go 2.Nf3. We play 2…e6, keeping the option to face c4. We don’t know whether White will choose Bf4, c4, or g3, so …e6 ensures we can always transpose into familiar territory. If we play 2...Nf6, White could enter a Catalan, and without our pawn on e6 we cannot steer the game back to our course.} 2...e6 3.Bf4 {But White may instead bring the bishop out immediately and take us into the London. Since this move order is different, we can also do something different. We challenge the bishop on f4 right away by playing 3...Bd6.} 3...Bd6 4.Bg3 {4.Bg3 is the most common move. London players like falling back so that hxg3 opens the h-file if we take. We simply continue developing and play 4...Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.e3 {White goes for the usual e3. We respond with 5…c5, our thematic break against the London.} 5...c5 6.c3 {Many players won’t take on c5, so you’ll often see 6.c3. Our defense is to build a bishop –queen battery with 6...Qc7.} 6...Qc7 7.Bd3 {White may bring the bishop to d3, their dream square in the London. And again, we develop the queenside with 7...Nbd7, also defending the c5-pawn - exactly as explained in the video.} 7...Nbd7 8.O-O {After White castles... We can now take on g3 - and we should. Exchanging bishops helps reduce White’s control of e5. Also, after 8.0-0, hxg3 does not activate the rook on the h-file.} 8...Bxg3 9.hxg3 {White recaptures. We continue improving our position with 9...b6, our bishop will soon be developed on b7.} 9...b6 10.Nbd2 {White goes for Standard London development.} 10...Bb7 {And we go for sound development as well. Our bishop helps control the e4-square. Play may continue with Qe2 and ...0-0. We'll take on e4 if White pushes e4 and quickly get our f-rook into d8.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 1: The 2. Nf3 London / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/64f93e5c-6955-4e5f-ad92-f56bd9cc4c3b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our first study of this chapter. T he 2.Bf4 London is a simple approach, and you won’t see many deviations - especially when White follows with 3.e3. White opens with 1.d4. We reply with 1…d5, ready for a Queen’s Gambit structure.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {White may go 2.Nf3. We play 2…e6, keeping the option to face c4. We don’t know whether White will choose Bf4, c4, or g3, so …e6 ensures we can always transpose into familiar territory. If we play 2...Nf6, White could enter a Catalan, and without our pawn on e6 we cannot steer the game back to our course.} 2...e6 3.Bf4 {But White may instead bring the bishop out immediately and take us into the London. Since this move order is different, we can also do something different. We challenge the bishop on f4 right away by playing 3...Bd6.} 3...Bd6 4.Bxd6 {First, we check what happens if White accepts the trade. If they do, we’re happy - we’ve traded off White’s best piece: the London Bishop. Our queen lands on d6, eyeing b4. Our plan is simple: develop, castle kingside, and then expand on the queenside with …c5, …b6, and so on.} 4...Qxd6 5.c3 {Playing the pawn to c3 aims to develop the queen and prevent …Qb4+. We simply develop the kingside with 5...Nf6 and prepare to castle.} 5...Nf6 6.Ne5 {White may try 6.Ne5, though it’s odd - the knight is the only developed piece. Since the pawn is still on e2, we can answer with 6…Nfd7 and later …f6 to chase it.} 6...Nfd7 7.f4 {White reinforces their knight with 7.f4. This early Stonewall attempt allows us …f6. It’s a one-piece attack that is easily repelled.} 7...f6 8.Nxd7 {After the trade... We recapture.} 8...Nxd7 9.e3 {9.e3 builds a Stonewall, but with no knight capable of reaching e5. It’s not a strong setup. We castle.} 9...O-O 10.Bd3 {If White tries 10.Bd3 pressure... We control e5 - so we push and aim for this pawn break.} 10...e5 11.Qh5 {White has no strategic solution and goes for mate. After 11…e4, the bishop gets chased from the diagonal and no longer pressures h7 - a key idea of any Stonewall.} 11...e4 12.Be2 {This isn't ideal coordination for White. We also control f3 so Nbd2 to f3 isn't possible. c4 seems like the only option for White, which we can begin to play against with ...Nb6 and ...Be6. We'll have a complete grip on the position.} 12...Nb6 {And like I said, we control the c4-square. ...Be6 will follow. One of the only productive things White could do is castling kingside, but after ...Be6 they' ll be tasked with playing something useful once again. Our moves are easier to play because of strong our central control.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 1: The 2. Nf3 London / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/64f93e5c-6955-4e5f-ad92-f56bd9cc4c3b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our first study of this chapter. T he 2.Bf4 London is a simple approach, and you won’t see many deviations - especially when White follows with 3.e3. White opens with 1.d4. We reply with 1…d5, ready for a Queen’s Gambit structure.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {White may go 2.Nf3. We play 2…e6, keeping the option to face c4. We don’t know whether White will choose Bf4, c4, or g3, so …e6 ensures we can always transpose into familiar territory. If we play 2...Nf6, White could enter a Catalan, and without our pawn on e6 we cannot steer the game back to our course.} 2...e6 3.Bf4 {But White may instead bring the bishop out immediately and take us into the London. Since this move order is different, we can also do something different. We challenge the bishop on f4 right away by playing 3...Bd6.} 3...Bd6 4.e3 {White may defend the bishop with 4.e3, hoping that if we take on f4, they recapture with the pawn and strengthen e5. So we do not capture. Instead we place a knight on f6. This allows White the tricky Ne5 idea.} 4...Nf6 5.Ne5 {Because our knight sits on f6 and White has played e3, we cannot kick the knight from e5 with …f6. This is a serious line played even by top GMs. We should castle early to avoid any potential tactics.} 5...O-O 6.Bd3 {White brings the bishop to d3. We counter with 6…c5 immediately.} 6...c5 7.c3 {London players rarely take on c5. They usually reinforce the pawn and create the c3–d4–e3 triangle. We play 7…Qc7, keeping pressure on the diagonal without losing sight of the c5-pawn.} 7...Qc7 8.Bg3 {Some players secure the bishop on g3 and prepare f4. We develop the knight to d7 and keep everything defended. On d7 it doesn’t block the queen’s view of c5, and it can reroute to f6 later.} 8...Nbd7 9.f4 {9.f4 reinforces the knight on e5 even more. But this allows us to also get a knight into a central square.} 9...Ne4 10.Bxe4 {If White gives up their important light-squared bishop... We recapture and welcome the concession.} 10...dxe4 11.Nd2 {White may try to attack the pawn. But we’re perfectly fine if we simply defend it with 11...Nf6.} 11...Nf6 12.Ndc4 {Even though White could jump into c4 and attack our bishop...} 12...Be7 {We can just move our bishop away. We've got a nice d5-square to work with: we'll play ...Nd5 and if White tries setting up discoveries with f5, we'll just get out of the way with ...Qd8.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 1: The 2. Nf3 London / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/64f93e5c-6955-4e5f-ad92-f56bd9cc4c3b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our first study of this chapter. T he 2.Bf4 London is a simple approach, and you won’t see many deviations - especially when White follows with 3.e3. White opens with 1.d4. We reply with 1…d5, ready for a Queen’s Gambit structure.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {White may go 2.Nf3. We play 2…e6, keeping the option to face c4. We don’t know whether White will choose Bf4, c4, or g3, so …e6 ensures we can always transpose into familiar territory. If we play 2...Nf6, White could enter a Catalan, and without our pawn on e6 we cannot steer the game back to our course.} 2...e6 3.Bf4 {But White may instead bring the bishop out immediately and take us into the London. Since this move order is different, we can also do something different. We challenge the bishop on f4 right away by playing 3...Bd6.} 3...Bd6 4.Ne5 {White may place the knight on e5 early, but if it arrives before we play …Nf6... We can kick it out immediately. Since White isn’t optimally placed, we can prepare the …e5 break. …f6 is also possible because the pawn on e2 stops Qh5+ ideas.} 4...f6 5.Nd3 {The knight must retreat to d3 to protect the bishop. We continue developing with 5…Ne7 - our kingside is now fully developed.} 5...Ne7 6.e3 {White continues in typical London fashion with 6.e3. We develop our other knight to c6. This blocks our c-pawn for now, but White’s setup is also suboptimal because their bishop usually belongs on d3.} 6...Nbc6 7.Nd2 {White develops and aims for Nf3. But one reason to play …Nc6 early is to prepare …e5. 7…e5 hits the bishop, gains space, and opens lines for our pieces.} 7...e5 8.dxe5 {White may trade… But …f6 and …e5 is a dream pawn break against the London. We recapture. Here, White has already lost the d4-pawn - meaning their grip on e5 is gone.} 8...fxe5 9.Bg5 {White may try to recover something strategically with a pin. But there’s no real way to exploit our position after Bg5. We simply activate our bishop.} 9...Bf5 10.Be2 {And after White puts their bishop on the suboptimal e2-square...} 10...O-O {Our rook is even active on the semi-open f-file. A short-term plan is to unpin and improve the queen: ...Qe8 for ...Qg6. Of course, our pawn center is mobile and strong: if 11. 0-0, 11...e4 is a strong move.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 1: The 2. Nf3 London / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/64f93e5c-6955-4e5f-ad92-f56bd9cc4c3b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our first study of this chapter. T he 2.Bf4 London is a simple approach, and you won’t see many deviations - especially when White follows with 3.e3. White opens with 1.d4. We reply with 1…d5, ready for a Queen’s Gambit structure.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {White may go 2.Nf3. We play 2…e6, keeping the option to face c4. We don’t know whether White will choose Bf4, c4, or g3, so …e6 ensures we can always transpose into familiar territory. If we play 2...Nf6, White could enter a Catalan, and without our pawn on e6 we cannot steer the game back to our course.} 2...e6 3.Bf4 {But White may instead bring the bishop out immediately and take us into the London. Since this move order is different, we can also do something different. We challenge the bishop on f4 right away by playing 3...Bd6.} 3...Bd6 4.Bxd6 {First, we check what happens if White accepts the trade. If they do, we’re happy - we’ve traded off White’s best piece: the London Bishop. Our queen lands on d6, eyeing b4. Our plan is simple: develop, castle kingside, and then expand on the queenside with …c5, …b6, and so on.} 4...Qxd6 5.Nc3 {White may choose fast knight development, aiming for a quick e4. But Nc3 blocks the c-pawn, so we must focus on controlling e4. Our solution is to develop with 5...Nf6, controlling the e4-square.} 5...Nf6 6.Nb5 {Many players lash out with this attacking move - but it’s a huge blunder. We reply with a queen check, attacking both the knight and the b2-pawn.} 6...Qb4+ 7.Nc3 {Once the knight blocks... We attack the knight on c3 with pressure on the rook (though it is defended by the queen, so we don’t take it). The real issue is that White has few good squares here…} 7...Qxb2 8.Na4 {8.Na4 is the only safe square. We go for 8...Qa3, attacking the knight once again.} 8...Qa3 9.c3 {c3 does defend the knight for one more move… But with the pawn still on e2, 9…b5 is possible. White runs into problems by not developing the e-pawn early.} 9...b5 10.Nc5 {Once again, only one square remains. We now take on c3 with check.} 10...Qxc3+ 11.Nd2 {The knight has to block or the rook falls.} 11...Qxd4 {And now we can take on d4. We're up two pawns out of the opening. It's now time to convert.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 1: The 2. Nf3 London / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/64f93e5c-6955-4e5f-ad92-f56bd9cc4c3b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our first study of this chapter. T he 2.Bf4 London is a simple approach, and you won’t see many deviations - especially when White follows with 3.e3. White opens with 1.d4. We reply with 1…d5, ready for a Queen’s Gambit structure.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {White may go 2.Nf3. We play 2…e6, keeping the option to face c4. We don’t know whether White will choose Bf4, c4, or g3, so …e6 ensures we can always transpose into familiar territory. If we play 2...Nf6, White could enter a Catalan, and without our pawn on e6 we cannot steer the game back to our course.} 2...e6 3.Bf4 {But White may instead bring the bishop out immediately and take us into the London. Since this move order is different, we can also do something different. We challenge the bishop on f4 right away by playing 3...Bd6.} 3...Bd6 4.Bxd6 {First, we check what happens if White accepts the trade. If they do, we’re happy - we’ve traded off White’s best piece: the London Bishop. Our queen lands on d6, eyeing b4. Our plan is simple: develop, castle kingside, and then expand on the queenside with …c5, …b6, and so on.} 4...Qxd6 5.e3 {5.e3 is standard London play, activating the light-squared bishop and queen. But 5.c3 was also possible, especially since after the bishop trade on d6. 5.e3 allows us to throw in a check on b4 with the queen.} 5...Qb4+ 6.Nbd2 {White could block and develop the knight. This is very sound because it argues that Black will lose valuable development time hunting pawns.} 6...Qxb2 {Yet we want to be able to take and refute White's gambit, in essence. Our plan is simple: Escape with the queen via a3 to e7, and play Nf6 and castle. Capturing on a2 is possible, but not recommended.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 1: The 2. Nf3 London / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/64f93e5c-6955-4e5f-ad92-f56bd9cc4c3b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our first study of this chapter. T he 2.Bf4 London is a simple approach, and you won’t see many deviations - especially when White follows with 3.e3. White opens with 1.d4. We reply with 1…d5, ready for a Queen’s Gambit structure.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {White may go 2.Nf3. We play 2…e6, keeping the option to face c4. We don’t know whether White will choose Bf4, c4, or g3, so …e6 ensures we can always transpose into familiar territory. If we play 2...Nf6, White could enter a Catalan, and without our pawn on e6 we cannot steer the game back to our course.} 2...e6 3.Bf4 {But White may instead bring the bishop out immediately and take us into the London. Since this move order is different, we can also do something different. We challenge the bishop on f4 right away by playing 3...Bd6.} 3...Bd6 4.Bxd6 {First, we check what happens if White accepts the trade. If they do, we’re happy - we’ve traded off White’s best piece: the London Bishop. Our queen lands on d6, eyeing b4. Our plan is simple: develop, castle kingside, and then expand on the queenside with …c5, …b6, and so on.} 4...Qxd6 5.e3 {5.e3 is standard London play, activating the light-squared bishop and queen. But 5.c3 was also possible, especially since after the bishop trade on d6. 5.e3 allows us to throw in a check on b4 with the queen.} 5...Qb4+ 6.Nc3 {Nc3 is also very unstable. It allows us to take on b2, putting pressure on two pieces.} 6...Qxb2 7.Nb5 {Jumping to b5 might be White's idea... But it’s quickly refuted with 7...Qb4+. If 8.Qd2, then …Qe7.} 7...Qb4+ 8.c3 {After White blocks...} 8...Qa5 {The queen defends c7. We're kicking the knight out soon with ...a6 and developing with ...Nd7. White doesn't have compensation.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 1: The 2. Nf3 London / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/64f93e5c-6955-4e5f-ad92-f56bd9cc4c3b/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our first study of this chapter. T he 2.Bf4 London is a simple approach, and you won’t see many deviations - especially when White follows with 3.e3. White opens with 1.d4. We reply with 1…d5, ready for a Queen’s Gambit structure.} 1...d5 2.Nf3 {White may go 2.Nf3. We play 2…e6, keeping the option to face c4. We don’t know whether White will choose Bf4, c4, or g3, so …e6 ensures we can always transpose into familiar territory. If we play 2...Nf6, White could enter a Catalan, and without our pawn on e6 we cannot steer the game back to our course.} 2...e6 3.Bf4 {But White may instead bring the bishop out immediately and take us into the London. Since this move order is different, we can also do something different. We challenge the bishop on f4 right away by playing 3...Bd6.} 3...Bd6 4.e3 {White may defend the bishop with 4.e3, hoping that if we take on f4, they recapture with the pawn and strengthen e5. So we do not capture. Instead we place a knight on f6. This allows White the tricky Ne5 idea.} 4...Nf6 5.Bd3 {If White continues with a typical London setup, we’re completely fine. This line is much less tricky than 5.Ne5. I suggest we simply trade bishops.} 5...Bxf4 6.exf4 {White ends up with doubled f-pawns but also strong control of e5. We develop the queen with tempo, 6...Qd6.} 6...Qd6 7.Qd2 {White will usually defend with the queen, placing it on a square that normally belongs to the knight in London setups. White is still fine; the position remains equal. We castle.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White castles. With our f7–e6–d5 pawn chain, the best development for our light-squared bishop is via b7 or a6, so 8...b6 is our move.} 8...b6 9.Ne5 {Expect this move. White centralizes the knight.} 9...c5 {And now we should go for 9...c5, undermining d4. A nice way of reducing the pressure in the position is to go for a trade with ...Ba6, removing one of White's best and most aggressive pieces. ...a6 to keep a piece out of b5 is also an option in the future, trying to play for ...Nc6 and a stable queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 2: The 2.Bf4 London / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/50cc6d7d-f272-4343-839e-dc6615b6a349/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study we cover the 2.Bf4 London, usually followed by 3.e3. It’s one of the most common amateur setups because the ideas are simple. We answer with 3...Bf5 to develop our light-squared bishop early. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn Opening. We mirror with 1…d5, ready for a QGD structure.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {White immediately develops the dark-squared bishop, so e3 won’t trap it. White also gains control over e5. This is clearly the London, which means we can safely play 2...Nf6 without worry of transpositions.} 2...Nf6 3.e3 {White opens the diagonal for the light-squared bishop. Our idea is to mirror White and play 3...Bf5, getting our bishop out of the pawn chain. We don’t want it stuck behind our pawns.} 3...Bf5 4.c4 {A typical move in Queen’s Pawn openings. We respond with 4...e6, preparing to develop the dark-squared bishop.} 4...e6 5.Qb3 {White brings out the queen early, targeting b7. We don’t need to defend the pawn and instead play 5...Nc6.} 5...Nc6 6.Qxb7 {Capturing on b7 is a common mistake - White is now in trouble. We jump with our knight to b4, threatening to fork the king and rook.} 6...Nb4 7.Na3 {White plays 7.Na3, defending c2. We take on c4 and free up the e4-square for our bishop.} 7...dxc4 8.Bxc4 {White recaptures. We attack both the queen on b7 and the weak pawn on g2 with 8...Be4.} 8...Be4 9.Bb5+ {White tries to attack our king with 9.Bb5 +. We calmly step out to e7 - there are no threats.} 9...Ke7 10.Qxc7+ {The queen takes on c7 with check, it had no other squares to go to. We trade queens.} 10...Qxc7 11.Bxc7 {White recaptures.} 11...Bxg2 {And we win the rook.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 2: The 2.Bf4 London / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/50cc6d7d-f272-4343-839e-dc6615b6a349/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study we cover the 2.Bf4 London, usually followed by 3.e3. It’s one of the most common amateur setups because the ideas are simple. We answer with 3...Bf5 to develop our light-squared bishop early. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn Opening. We mirror with 1…d5, ready for a QGD structure.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {White immediately develops the dark-squared bishop, so e3 won’t trap it. White also gains control over e5. This is clearly the London, which means we can safely play 2...Nf6 without worry of transpositions.} 2...Nf6 3.e3 {White opens the diagonal for the light-squared bishop. Our idea is to mirror White and play 3...Bf5, getting our bishop out of the pawn chain. We don’t want it stuck behind our pawns.} 3...Bf5 4.c4 {A typical move in Queen’s Pawn openings. We respond with 4...e6, preparing to develop the dark-squared bishop.} 4...e6 5.Qb3 {White brings out the queen early, targeting b7. We don’t need to defend the pawn and instead play 5...Nc6.} 5...Nc6 6.Nc3 {White develops the knight. We pin it, strengthening our control of the e4-square.} 6...Bb4 {This position is a transposition to 5. Qb3 Nc6 6. Nc3 Bb4 The plan is to play ...a5, going for the queenside expansion. Trading off the dark-squared bishop is also an idea. If White's queen ever moves out of the protection of the c3 knight, we will capture the knight and double White's c-pawns.} 7.a3 {White attacks our bishop. We capture on c3 to maintain better control over e4.} 7...Bxc3+ 8.bxc3 {White recaptures. We maneuver our knight to a5, hitting the queen.} 8...Na5 9.Qa4+ {White gives a check forking the king and the knight on a5. We block with our c-pawn - now the queen defends the knight.} 9...c6 10.cxd5 {White gets rid of the doubled c-pawns.} 10...Nxd5 {We recapture with the knight, which is now nicely placed on the d5-square. We are putting pressure on the c3-pawn and the bishop on f4. ...Qb6 and castling are the ideas here, with ...Rc8 preparing c5.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 2: The 2.Bf4 London / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/50cc6d7d-f272-4343-839e-dc6615b6a349/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study we cover the 2.Bf4 London, usually followed by 3.e3. It’s one of the most common amateur setups because the ideas are simple. We answer with 3...Bf5 to develop our light-squared bishop early. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn Opening. We mirror with 1…d5, ready for a QGD structure.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {White immediately develops the dark-squared bishop, so e3 won’t trap it. White also gains control over e5. This is clearly the London, which means we can safely play 2...Nf6 without worry of transpositions.} 2...Nf6 3.e3 {White opens the diagonal for the light-squared bishop. Our idea is to mirror White and play 3...Bf5, getting our bishop out of the pawn chain. We don’t want it stuck behind our pawns.} 3...Bf5 4.c4 {A typical move in Queen’s Pawn openings. We respond with 4...e6, preparing to develop the dark-squared bishop.} 4...e6 5.Nc3 {White develops the knight behind the c-pawn. We play a common idea in these structures - 5...Bb4, pinning the knight to the king and strengthening our control of e4.} 5...Bb4 6.Qb3 {b3 is a common square for the queen. 6. a3 is always met with 6... Bxc3.} 6...Nc6 {This position is a transposition to 5. Qb3 Nc6 6. Nc3 Bb4 The plan is to play ...a5, going for the queenside expansion. Trading off the dark-squared bishop is also an idea. If White's queen ever moves out of the protection of the c3 knight, we will capture the knight and double White's c-pawns.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 2: The 2.Bf4 London / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/50cc6d7d-f272-4343-839e-dc6615b6a349/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study we cover the 2.Bf4 London, usually followed by 3.e3. It’s one of the most common amateur setups because the ideas are simple. We answer with 3...Bf5 to develop our light-squared bishop early. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn Opening. We mirror with 1…d5, ready for a QGD structure.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {White immediately develops the dark-squared bishop, so e3 won’t trap it. White also gains control over e5. This is clearly the London, which means we can safely play 2...Nf6 without worry of transpositions.} 2...Nf6 3.e3 {White opens the diagonal for the light-squared bishop. Our idea is to mirror White and play 3...Bf5, getting our bishop out of the pawn chain. We don’t want it stuck behind our pawns.} 3...Bf5 4.c4 {A typical move in Queen’s Pawn openings. We respond with 4...e6, preparing to develop the dark-squared bishop.} 4...e6 5.Nc3 {White develops the knight behind the c-pawn. We play a common idea in these structures - 5...Bb4, pinning the knight to the king and strengthening our control of e4.} 5...Bb4 6.Nf3 {White frenquently plays this move in these variations.} 6...O-O {We simply castle, with the plans of ...Ne4. Play can develop in many ways here, but keep in mind the following ideas: 1. Ne4 jump 2. c7-c5 followed by ...Qa5 3. ...Bxc3, damaging White's pawns} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 2: The 2.Bf4 London / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/50cc6d7d-f272-4343-839e-dc6615b6a349/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study we cover the 2.Bf4 London, usually followed by 3.e3. It’s one of the most common amateur setups because the ideas are simple. We answer with 3...Bf5 to develop our light-squared bishop early. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn Opening. We mirror with 1…d5, ready for a QGD structure.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {White immediately develops the dark-squared bishop, so e3 won’t trap it. White also gains control over e5. This is clearly the London, which means we can safely play 2...Nf6 without worry of transpositions.} 2...Nf6 3.e3 {White opens the diagonal for the light-squared bishop. Our idea is to mirror White and play 3...Bf5, getting our bishop out of the pawn chain. We don’t want it stuck behind our pawns.} 3...Bf5 4.Bd3 {White may challenge our light-squared bishop. We trade on d3, the upside is that exchanging this bishop reduces White’s attacking chances on our king.} 4...Bxd3 5.Qxd3 {White recaptures with the queen.} 5...Nbd7 {Now I like this flexible move, 5...Nbd7. From here, we try to play ...e6, ...c5 (or even ...c6), develop and castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 2: The 2.Bf4 London / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/50cc6d7d-f272-4343-839e-dc6615b6a349/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study we cover the 2.Bf4 London, usually followed by 3.e3. It’s one of the most common amateur setups because the ideas are simple. We answer with 3...Bf5 to develop our light-squared bishop early. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn Opening. We mirror with 1…d5, ready for a QGD structure.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {White immediately develops the dark-squared bishop, so e3 won’t trap it. White also gains control over e5. This is clearly the London, which means we can safely play 2...Nf6 without worry of transpositions.} 2...Nf6 3.e3 {White opens the diagonal for the light-squared bishop. Our idea is to mirror White and play 3...Bf5, getting our bishop out of the pawn chain. We don’t want it stuck behind our pawns.} 3...Bf5 4.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3 first. We respond with 4...e6 - aiming to develop the dark-squared bishop and build an e6–d5 structure. 5.c4 becomes another move order.} 4...e6 5.Bd3 {White fights for e4 with Bd3. As usual, we take on d3.} 5...Bxd3 6.Qxd3 {White recaptures.} 6...Nbd7 {Again this flexible ...Nbd7 move. We will again finish up development and go c7-c5 (or the more solid ...c6). The position is balanced.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 3: 1. d4 d5 (2. Bf4, 3. Nf3 Move Order) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/c80f1128-131c-4711-a9c5-33540bab7bd9/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 3. Some London players develop the kingside knight first. It’s mostly a matter of preference, but it gives us extra time - White’s queenside development is delayed. White starts with the Queen’s Pawn. We reply with 1…d5, ready for the Queen’s Gambit.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {This setup is the London System. White plays 2.Bf4 to control e5. 2...Nf6 is solid - we support d5 and control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White develops the knight in typical London fashion. Ne5 may come later. We want to play …e6, but first we develop our bishop to f5 so it doesn’t get stuck.} 3...Bf5 4.c4 {White challenges our d5-pawn in a Queen’s Gambit fashion. We support with 4…e6; our bishop is nicely placed outside the pawn chain.} 4...e6 5.Qb3 {A natural continuation from White, attacking b7 is 5.Qb3. We set a trap by leaving the pawn hanging and play 5...Nbd7.} 5...Nbd7 6.Qxb7 {White grabs the “free” pawn - this is the most popular try. We attack the queen and get our rook on the semi-open file.} 6...Rb8 7.Qxa7 {If White chooses to capture the pawn on a7... We take on b2 - now the knight on b1 is hanging.} 7...Rxb2 8.Bxc7 {Taking on c7 with the bishop loses. We step out of the attack, 8...Qc8. There are two threats: …Rxb1 and …Rb7, attacking queen and bishop.} 8...Qc8 9.c5 {White tries to close the position, but cannot defend both threats.} 9...Rb7 {This picks up the piece.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 3: 1. d4 d5 (2. Bf4, 3. Nf3 Move Order) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/c80f1128-131c-4711-a9c5-33540bab7bd9/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 3. Some London players develop the kingside knight first. It’s mostly a matter of preference, but it gives us extra time - White’s queenside development is delayed. White starts with the Queen’s Pawn. We reply with 1…d5, ready for the Queen’s Gambit.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {This setup is the London System. White plays 2.Bf4 to control e5. 2...Nf6 is solid - we support d5 and control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White develops the knight in typical London fashion. Ne5 may come later. We want to play …e6, but first we develop our bishop to f5 so it doesn’t get stuck.} 3...Bf5 4.c4 {White challenges our d5-pawn in a Queen’s Gambit fashion. We support with 4…e6; our bishop is nicely placed outside the pawn chain.} 4...e6 5.Qb3 {A natural continuation from White, attacking b7 is 5.Qb3. We set a trap by leaving the pawn hanging and play 5...Nbd7.} 5...Nbd7 6.Qxb7 {White grabs the “free” pawn - this is the most popular try. We attack the queen and get our rook on the semi-open file.} 6...Rb8 7.Qxc7 {White takes another pawn. We trade queens.} 7...Qxc7 8.Bxc7 {White recaptures. We take on b2, attacking the knight.} 8...Rxb2 9.Nbd2 {White steps out of the attack.} 9...Bb4 {And with ...Bb4 we pin the knight on d2. We can always capture the pawn on a2 later. With ...Ne4 coming it is very hard for White to untangle this position.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 3: 1. d4 d5 (2. Bf4, 3. Nf3 Move Order) / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/c80f1128-131c-4711-a9c5-33540bab7bd9/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 3. Some London players develop the kingside knight first. It’s mostly a matter of preference, but it gives us extra time - White’s queenside development is delayed. White starts with the Queen’s Pawn. We reply with 1…d5, ready for the Queen’s Gambit.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {This setup is the London System. White plays 2.Bf4 to control e5. 2...Nf6 is solid - we support d5 and control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White develops the knight in typical London fashion. Ne5 may come later. We want to play …e6, but first we develop our bishop to f5 so it doesn’t get stuck.} 3...Bf5 4.c4 {White challenges our d5-pawn in a Queen’s Gambit fashion. We support with 4…e6; our bishop is nicely placed outside the pawn chain.} 4...e6 5.Nc3 {5.Nc3 is a different approach where White develops the queenside knight first. We take on c4 - something we’re used to doing.} 5...dxc4 6.e3 {White tries to regain the pawn by opening up their light-squared bishop. We centralize the knight, 6...Nd5, looking to win the bishop pair.} 6...Nd5 7.Bxc4 {White recaptures the pawn. We get rid of White’s strong dark-squared bishop.} 7...Nxf4 8.exf4 {White recaptures. We play 8…c6, guarding d5 and b5.} 8...c6 9.O-O {White castles. We develop the knight; from d7 it can later go to f6 or b6.} 9...Nd7 10.Re1 {White now eyes our king after 10.Re1. We must be careful about d5 pushes. We reduce the rook’s scope by preparing ...0-0 with 10...Be7.} 10...Be7 11.Bd3 {The bishop isn't placed well on c4, so White may elect to trade it. We accept the trade since we don't want our pawn structure to be disrupted. In addition, this trade favors Black, because we get rid of White's attacking chances, and our pawns are already on light squares.} 11...Bxd3 12.Qxd3 {White recaptures, activating the queen.} 12...O-O {We castle. The next moves will be ...Qc7 to connect the rooks and get the knight into the game with ...Nf6 or ...Nb6.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 3: 1. d4 d5 (2. Bf4, 3. Nf3 Move Order) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/c80f1128-131c-4711-a9c5-33540bab7bd9/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 3. Some London players develop the kingside knight first. It’s mostly a matter of preference, but it gives us extra time - White’s queenside development is delayed. White starts with the Queen’s Pawn. We reply with 1…d5, ready for the Queen’s Gambit.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {This setup is the London System. White plays 2.Bf4 to control e5. 2...Nf6 is solid - we support d5 and control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White develops the knight in typical London fashion. Ne5 may come later. We want to play …e6, but first we develop our bishop to f5 so it doesn’t get stuck.} 3...Bf5 4.c4 {White challenges our d5-pawn in a Queen’s Gambit fashion. We support with 4…e6; our bishop is nicely placed outside the pawn chain.} 4...e6 5.Qb3 {A natural continuation from White, attacking b7 is 5.Qb3. We set a trap by leaving the pawn hanging and play 5...Nbd7.} 5...Nbd7 6.e3 {Another option is 6.e3, delaying pressure on d5. We usually take on c4.} 6...dxc4 7.Bxc4 {White recaptures with the bishop. We try to eliminate the bishop pair with 7...Nb6.} 7...Nb6 8.Be2 {White retreats. We play 8...a5, looking to later attack the queen with …a4.} 8...a5 9.O-O {White castles. We push 9…a4 to chase the queen off the b-file.} 9...a4 10.Qd1 {White retreats.} 10...Be7 {We prepare to castle. With the plans of ...Ne4 and ...c5 Black has a very comfortable game here.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 3: 1. d4 d5 (2. Bf4, 3. Nf3 Move Order) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/c80f1128-131c-4711-a9c5-33540bab7bd9/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 3. Some London players develop the kingside knight first. It’s mostly a matter of preference, but it gives us extra time - White’s queenside development is delayed. White starts with the Queen’s Pawn. We reply with 1…d5, ready for the Queen’s Gambit.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {This setup is the London System. White plays 2.Bf4 to control e5. 2...Nf6 is solid - we support d5 and control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White develops the knight in typical London fashion. Ne5 may come later. We want to play …e6, but first we develop our bishop to f5 so it doesn’t get stuck.} 3...Bf5 4.c4 {White challenges our d5-pawn in a Queen’s Gambit fashion. We support with 4…e6; our bishop is nicely placed outside the pawn chain.} 4...e6 5.Qb3 {A natural continuation from White, attacking b7 is 5.Qb3. We set a trap by leaving the pawn hanging and play 5...Nbd7.} 5...Nbd7 6.e3 {Another option is 6.e3, delaying pressure on d5. We usually take on c4.} 6...dxc4 7.Bxc4 {White recaptures with the bishop. We try to eliminate the bishop pair with 7...Nb6.} 7...Nb6 8.Be2 {White retreats. We play 8...a5, looking to later attack the queen with …a4.} 8...a5 9.a4 {White stops the pawn. We want to trade queens to improve our b6-knight, so 9...Qd5 is our move.} 9...Qd5 10.Qxd5 {White trades. We recapture and our knight becomes nicely centralized.} 10...Nbxd5 11.Bg3 {White wants to keep the bishop pair.} 11...Bb4+ {Making it harder for White to develop naturally. This is a classic idea in this line - occupy the b4 square. We have a greater piece activity, we will castle next and centralize the rooks with ...Rfd8 and ...Rac8. The knight will be placed well on e4.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 3: 1. d4 d5 (2. Bf4, 3. Nf3 Move Order) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/c80f1128-131c-4711-a9c5-33540bab7bd9/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 3. Some London players develop the kingside knight first. It’s mostly a matter of preference, but it gives us extra time - White’s queenside development is delayed. White starts with the Queen’s Pawn. We reply with 1…d5, ready for the Queen’s Gambit.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {This setup is the London System. White plays 2.Bf4 to control e5. 2...Nf6 is solid - we support d5 and control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White develops the knight in typical London fashion. Ne5 may come later. We want to play …e6, but first we develop our bishop to f5 so it doesn’t get stuck.} 3...Bf5 4.c4 {White challenges our d5-pawn in a Queen’s Gambit fashion. We support with 4…e6; our bishop is nicely placed outside the pawn chain.} 4...e6 5.Qb3 {A natural continuation from White, attacking b7 is 5.Qb3. We set a trap by leaving the pawn hanging and play 5...Nbd7.} 5...Nbd7 6.Nc3 {White ignores the pawn and instead increases pressure on d5. We remove the pressure by taking on c4.} 6...dxc4 7.Qxc4 {White recaptures and attacks our c-pawn. We defend it by pushing it one square.} 7...c6 8.e3 {White opens the diagonal for the light-squared bishop. 8...Qb6 is our next move. Our queen on b6 is very active, pressuring b2. Tactics often revolve around controlling b4 - White can blunder easily.} 8...Qb6 9.Qb3 {White wants to trade queens because their king might become vulnerable. We’re happy to trade.} 9...Qxb3 10.axb3 {White recaptures.} 10...Bb4 {Now we pin the knight. Black has a very good position now. White's doubled b-pawns are soft, and with ...Nd5 coming it is easy for us to put pressure on White. The b4-square is also perpetually ours.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 3: 1. d4 d5 (2. Bf4, 3. Nf3 Move Order) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/c80f1128-131c-4711-a9c5-33540bab7bd9/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 3. Some London players develop the kingside knight first. It’s mostly a matter of preference, but it gives us extra time - White’s queenside development is delayed. White starts with the Queen’s Pawn. We reply with 1…d5, ready for the Queen’s Gambit.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {This setup is the London System. White plays 2.Bf4 to control e5. 2...Nf6 is solid - we support d5 and control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White develops the knight in typical London fashion. Ne5 may come later. We want to play …e6, but first we develop our bishop to f5 so it doesn’t get stuck.} 3...Bf5 4.c4 {White challenges our d5-pawn in a Queen’s Gambit fashion. We support with 4…e6; our bishop is nicely placed outside the pawn chain.} 4...e6 5.Qb3 {A natural continuation from White, attacking b7 is 5.Qb3. We set a trap by leaving the pawn hanging and play 5...Nbd7.} 5...Nbd7 6.Nc3 {White ignores the pawn and instead increases pressure on d5. We remove the pressure by taking on c4.} 6...dxc4 7.Qxc4 {White recaptures and attacks our c-pawn. We defend it by pushing it one square.} 7...c6 8.e3 {White opens the diagonal for the light-squared bishop. 8...Qb6 is our next move. Our queen on b6 is very active, pressuring b2. Tactics often revolve around controlling b4 - White can blunder easily.} 8...Qb6 9.b3 {White may try to defend b2 - this is a subtle blunder. We pin the knight with our queen; …Nb6 and …Bb4 are unstoppable.} 9...Qa5 10.Be2 {White cannot escape.} 10...Nb6 {The queen is trapped.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 3: 1. d4 d5 (2. Bf4, 3. Nf3 Move Order) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/c80f1128-131c-4711-a9c5-33540bab7bd9/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 3. Some London players develop the kingside knight first. It’s mostly a matter of preference, but it gives us extra time - White’s queenside development is delayed. White starts with the Queen’s Pawn. We reply with 1…d5, ready for the Queen’s Gambit.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {This setup is the London System. White plays 2.Bf4 to control e5. 2...Nf6 is solid - we support d5 and control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White develops the knight in typical London fashion. Ne5 may come later. We want to play …e6, but first we develop our bishop to f5 so it doesn’t get stuck.} 3...Bf5 4.c4 {White challenges our d5-pawn in a Queen’s Gambit fashion. We support with 4…e6; our bishop is nicely placed outside the pawn chain.} 4...e6 5.Qb3 {A natural continuation from White, attacking b7 is 5.Qb3. We set a trap by leaving the pawn hanging and play 5...Nbd7.} 5...Nbd7 6.Nc3 {White ignores the pawn and instead increases pressure on d5. We remove the pressure by taking on c4.} 6...dxc4 7.Qxc4 {White recaptures and attacks our c-pawn. We defend it by pushing it one square.} 7...c6 8.e3 {White opens the diagonal for the light-squared bishop. 8...Qb6 is our next move. Our queen on b6 is very active, pressuring b2. Tactics often revolve around controlling b4 - White can blunder easily.} 8...Qb6 9.Na4 {White attacks our queen, but this is also a mistake. We check the king, 9...Qa5+.} 9...Qa5+ 10.Nc3 {White has to retreat, othervise the attack is even harder to defend with ...Nd5 coming.} 10...Nd5 {Black is very active here with ...Bb4 coming. For instance, after 11. a3 we can still play 11... Bb4, because the rook on a1 is undefended.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 3: 1. d4 d5 (2. Bf4, 3. Nf3 Move Order) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/c80f1128-131c-4711-a9c5-33540bab7bd9/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 3. Some London players develop the kingside knight first. It’s mostly a matter of preference, but it gives us extra time - White’s queenside development is delayed. White starts with the Queen’s Pawn. We reply with 1…d5, ready for the Queen’s Gambit.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {This setup is the London System. White plays 2.Bf4 to control e5. 2...Nf6 is solid - we support d5 and control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White develops the knight in typical London fashion. Ne5 may come later. We want to play …e6, but first we develop our bishop to f5 so it doesn’t get stuck.} 3...Bf5 4.c4 {White challenges our d5-pawn in a Queen’s Gambit fashion. We support with 4…e6; our bishop is nicely placed outside the pawn chain.} 4...e6 5.Qb3 {A natural continuation from White, attacking b7 is 5.Qb3. We set a trap by leaving the pawn hanging and play 5...Nbd7.} 5...Nbd7 6.Nc3 {White ignores the pawn and instead increases pressure on d5. We remove the pressure by taking on c4.} 6...dxc4 7.Qxc4 {White recaptures and attacks our c-pawn. We defend it by pushing it one square.} 7...c6 8.e3 {White opens the diagonal for the light-squared bishop. 8...Qb6 is our next move. Our queen on b6 is very active, pressuring b2. Tactics often revolve around controlling b4 - White can blunder easily.} 8...Qb6 9.Bd3 {White develops the bishop.} 9...Nd5 {Centralizing the knight here is good, gaining control of the crucial b4-square with both the bishop and the knight. An idea is to just let White capture on f5 because the pawn on b2 hangs. White must be careful!} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 3: 1. d4 d5 (2. Bf4, 3. Nf3 Move Order) / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/c80f1128-131c-4711-a9c5-33540bab7bd9/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to Study 3. Some London players develop the kingside knight first. It’s mostly a matter of preference, but it gives us extra time - White’s queenside development is delayed. White starts with the Queen’s Pawn. We reply with 1…d5, ready for the Queen’s Gambit.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {This setup is the London System. White plays 2.Bf4 to control e5. 2...Nf6 is solid - we support d5 and control e4.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White develops the knight in typical London fashion. Ne5 may come later. We want to play …e6, but first we develop our bishop to f5 so it doesn’t get stuck.} 3...Bf5 4.c4 {White challenges our d5-pawn in a Queen’s Gambit fashion. We support with 4…e6; our bishop is nicely placed outside the pawn chain.} 4...e6 5.Nc3 {5.Nc3 is a different approach where White develops the queenside knight first. We take on c4 - something we’re used to doing.} 5...dxc4 6.Qa4+ {White may try to regain the pawn with Qa4+. We block the check with our c-pawn.} 6...c6 7.Qxc4 {White captures the pawn.} 7...Qb6 {And we play the traditional 7...Qb6, with ...Be7 and castling next. Our bishop stands dominant on f5, and White won't get their standard London play out of this opening.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 4: 1. d4 d5 (2. Bf4 Nf6 3. Nc3) - The Jobava London / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/ef247620-9b1c-44c6-afa4-e52cfb27d95f/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the last study in the London chapter! Here we look at the Jobava London System, popularized by Baadur Jobava. It’s defined by the early 3.Nc3, before any c4 commitment. White begins with the central d-pawn. We mirror with 1…d5.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {White immediately chooses to develop the London bishop. We develop our knight naturally, 2...Nf6, eyeing central squares.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight early. This is the Jobava London - unusual because blocking the c-pawn is rarely ideal in d4/d5 openings. Still, White gets active piece play. We continue with the standard move 3...e6, opening our bishop’s diagonal.} 3...e6 4.e3 {White prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. We develop with 4...Bb4, with the idea of capturing on c3 and damaging White’s pawn structure.} 4...Bb4 5.a3 {White attacks our bishop. As planned, we capture and damage the queenside structure.} 5...Bxc3+ 6.bxc3 {White recaptures. We strike with 6…c5, opening the diagonal for the queen and targeting the weak queenside pawns.} 6...c5 7.dxc5 {If White takes on c5, they’re left with tripled, very weak pawns. This is the second most popular option after the bad 7.Nf3. 7…Qa5 puts pressure on all the pawns.} 7...Qa5 8.Qd3 {8.Qd3 defends for one more move and prepares Qb5+. We block the diagonal with 8...Nc6 to prevent Qb5+. We’re not letting the pressure fade.} 8...Nc6 9.Nf3 {After 9.Nf3...} 9...Ne4 {With 9...Ne4 we should be winning some of those doubled isolated c-pawns. ...Nxc5 is coming with a tempo on the queen almost no matter what. ...0-0 will come eventually, and we'll continue playing against the c-pawns.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 4: 1. d4 d5 (2. Bf4 Nf6 3. Nc3) - The Jobava London / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/ef247620-9b1c-44c6-afa4-e52cfb27d95f/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the last study in the London chapter! Here we look at the Jobava London System, popularized by Baadur Jobava. It’s defined by the early 3.Nc3, before any c4 commitment. White begins with the central d-pawn. We mirror with 1…d5.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {White immediately chooses to develop the London bishop. We develop our knight naturally, 2...Nf6, eyeing central squares.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight early. This is the Jobava London - unusual because blocking the c-pawn is rarely ideal in d4/d5 openings. Still, White gets active piece play. We continue with the standard move 3...e6, opening our bishop’s diagonal.} 3...e6 4.Nb5 {White attacks c7 - very typical in Jobava lines. We defend by developing the bishop with check.} 4...Bb4+ 5.c3 {And when White blocks, attacking our bishop... We defend c7 from a5.} 5...Ba5 6.e3 {White opens the diagonal for the light-squared bishop. We attack the knight with 6...a6.} 6...a6 7.Na3 {White must retreat to a passive square. We castle.} 7...O-O 8.Nf3 {White gets on with kingside development. We also bring our knight into the game, 8...Nbd7.} 8...Nbd7 9.Be2 {White prepares to castle.} 9...c6 {We play 9...c6 with the idea of retreating the bishop to c7 and trading off White's strong f4 bishop. Black has ideas of ...b5 and ...c5, devleoping with ...Bb7 too.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 4: 1. d4 d5 (2. Bf4 Nf6 3. Nc3) - The Jobava London / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/ef247620-9b1c-44c6-afa4-e52cfb27d95f/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the last study in the London chapter! Here we look at the Jobava London System, popularized by Baadur Jobava. It’s defined by the early 3.Nc3, before any c4 commitment. White begins with the central d-pawn. We mirror with 1…d5.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {White immediately chooses to develop the London bishop. We develop our knight naturally, 2...Nf6, eyeing central squares.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight early. This is the Jobava London - unusual because blocking the c-pawn is rarely ideal in d4/d5 openings. Still, White gets active piece play. We continue with the standard move 3...e6, opening our bishop’s diagonal.} 3...e6 4.Nb5 {White attacks c7 - very typical in Jobava lines. We defend by developing the bishop with check.} 4...Bb4+ 5.c3 {And when White blocks, attacking our bishop... We defend c7 from a5.} 5...Ba5 6.Qa4 {White threatens discovered attacks with the knight. We cover the diagonal and defend the bishop with 6...Nc6. We’re now ready for …a6 and …b5 if we want it.} 6...Nc6 7.Nf3 {White develops a knight. We push the knight to a worse square with 7...a6, c3 isn't available anymore.} 7...a6 8.Na3 {White retreats. We activate our f-knight to e4 - threatening to take on c3 and win the rook after bxc3.} 8...Ne4 9.Rc1 {White stops the threat.} 9...b5 {We end the variation with 9...b5, attacking the queen. The moves ...g5 and ...f5 are definitely a plan, going for the attack. White will be excessively passive as we attack on the kingside. Black is much better.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 4: 1. d4 d5 (2. Bf4 Nf6 3. Nc3) - The Jobava London / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/ef247620-9b1c-44c6-afa4-e52cfb27d95f/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the last study in the London chapter! Here we look at the Jobava London System, popularized by Baadur Jobava. It’s defined by the early 3.Nc3, before any c4 commitment. White begins with the central d-pawn. We mirror with 1…d5.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {White immediately chooses to develop the London bishop. We develop our knight naturally, 2...Nf6, eyeing central squares.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight early. This is the Jobava London - unusual because blocking the c-pawn is rarely ideal in d4/d5 openings. Still, White gets active piece play. We continue with the standard move 3...e6, opening our bishop’s diagonal.} 3...e6 4.e3 {White prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. We develop with 4...Bb4, with the idea of capturing on c3 and damaging White’s pawn structure.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd3 {White places their light-squared bishop on an active square - the most popular move after the pin. We challenge White’s center immediately with our typical 5...c5.} 5...c5 6.dxc5 {White trades. We don’t rush to take on c5. We instead play 6...Nbd7, aiming to take on c5 with the knight. If 7.Nf3, we may play ...Bxc3, doubling the pawns, then …Nxc5. If 7...Ne2, then …Bxc5, bringing our bishop back.} 6...Nbd7 7.Ne2 {Supporting the knight on c3 is the most popular move, so here’s the antidote: We capture on c5 with the bishop. 8.Nb5 isn’t good for White because of 8…e5 and 9…a6.} 7...Bxc5 8.O-O {White may castle and seem perfectly developed with no obvious weaknesses. But we’ll go after the bishop pair with 8...Nh5. The f4-bishop is one of White’s best pieces.} 8...Nh5 9.Bg3 {White usually brings the bishop back. We take anyway.} 9...Nxg3 10.Nxg3 {And after the knight takes...} 10...O-O {We get the king to safety. We're not afraid of Qh5 on h7 because we have ...g6, and since we have the dark-squared bishop our dark squares will be safe. A neat idea is to bring the bishop back to e7 so the knight can land on c5. Black is better out of the opening.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 4: 1. d4 d5 (2. Bf4 Nf6 3. Nc3) - The Jobava London / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/ef247620-9b1c-44c6-afa4-e52cfb27d95f/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the last study in the London chapter! Here we look at the Jobava London System, popularized by Baadur Jobava. It’s defined by the early 3.Nc3, before any c4 commitment. White begins with the central d-pawn. We mirror with 1…d5.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {White immediately chooses to develop the London bishop. We develop our knight naturally, 2...Nf6, eyeing central squares.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight early. This is the Jobava London - unusual because blocking the c-pawn is rarely ideal in d4/d5 openings. Still, White gets active piece play. We continue with the standard move 3...e6, opening our bishop’s diagonal.} 3...e6 4.e3 {White prepares to develop the light-squared bishop. We develop with 4...Bb4, with the idea of capturing on c3 and damaging White’s pawn structure.} 4...Bb4 5.a3 {White attacks our bishop. As planned, we capture and damage the queenside structure.} 5...Bxc3+ 6.bxc3 {White recaptures. We strike with 6…c5, opening the diagonal for the queen and targeting the weak queenside pawns.} 6...c5 7.Nf3 {Keep in mind the most popular move, 7. Nf3, runs into ...Qa5.} 7...Qa5 {And if you can tell, White cannot really defend the c3-pawn! If 8Qd3, ...c4! If Qd2, ...Ne4!} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The London System / Study 4: 1. d4 d5 (2. Bf4 Nf6 3. Nc3) - The Jobava London / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/ca538f62-9da4-4192-9cd6-c08bf3b17dee/studies/ef247620-9b1c-44c6-afa4-e52cfb27d95f/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the last study in the London chapter! Here we look at the Jobava London System, popularized by Baadur Jobava. It’s defined by the early 3.Nc3, before any c4 commitment. White begins with the central d-pawn. We mirror with 1…d5.} 1...d5 2.Bf4 {White immediately chooses to develop the London bishop. We develop our knight naturally, 2...Nf6, eyeing central squares.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight early. This is the Jobava London - unusual because blocking the c-pawn is rarely ideal in d4/d5 openings. Still, White gets active piece play. We continue with the standard move 3...e6, opening our bishop’s diagonal.} 3...e6 4.Nb5 {White attacks c7 - very typical in Jobava lines. We defend by developing the bishop with check.} 4...Bb4+ 5.c3 {And when White blocks, attacking our bishop... We defend c7 from a5.} 5...Ba5 6.b4 {White expands on the queenside, attacking our bishop. We retreat the bishop to b6.} 6...Bb6 7.a4 {White is trying to trap our bishop on b6.} 7...a5 {But here comes 7...a5, disrupting the advanced pawns on the queenside. The b4-pawn will be very weak. ...Qe7 and ...Nc6 are ideas to attack the b4-pawn. If bxa5, ...Bxa5 and Black gets everything they want, because White's queenside is destroyed.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: 1. d4 Sidelines / Study 1: 2.e3 and 2.e4 — The Blackmar-Diemer Gambit / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/dba0ef12-520e-49de-b17c-699cf02c44ae/studies/375ca717-b9a5-4775-b08c-747ac30f51e5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the first study of Chapter 7! Here and in the next study we cover White’s second-move deviations. In this one, we look at 2.e3 and 2.e4. White fights for the center with 1.d4. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.e3 {2.e3 looks dubious, blocking the c1-bishop, but White may aim for a Stonewall with several dark-square pawns and Bd3. They may also still go for c4, so we don’t over-commit with …Bf5. We develop naturally with 2...Nf6.} 2...Nf6 3.Bd3 {White develops the bishop to d3, the usual square in QP positions. 3...c5 is a very interesting idea! If White takes, we have …e5 ideas with strong central control.} 3...c5 4.c3 {Again White sets up the classic triangle of dark-square pawns. We continue with 4…Nc6 - no need to block our bishop with …Nd7. We still leave c5 hanging.} 4...Nc6 5.dxc5 {White chooses 5.dxc5, relieving tension and grabbing a pawn. Now our plan begins: 5...e5, taking over the center and eyeing c5 with the f8-bishop.} 5...e5 6.b4 {White’s entire point was to hold the pawn. That’s fine - our central control is strong, we’re almost fully developed, and ready to castle. We continue with 6...Be7.} 6...Be7 7.Ne2 {White develops the final kingside piece. 7.Nf3? loses to 7...e4. We finally castle.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White castles as well. Although 8…e4 concedes d4 and f4, it gains space and tempo, prepares e5 for the knight, and restricts White’s light-squared bishop. We’re taking over!} 8...e4 9.Bc2 {The bishop steps out of the way. We now address the queenside clamp: 9…b6 undermines the structure and forces White to react.} 9...b6 10.cxb6 {White captures, and instead of recapturing... We set a trap: 10...Bd6!} 10...Bd6 11.bxa7 {White falls for it - we’re winning. This bishop sacrifice on h2 is the famous Greek Gift.} 11...Bxh2+ 12.Kxh2 {White accepts the sacrifice but... The follow-up check with the knight opens the queen’s diagonal…} 12...Ng4+ 13.Kg1 {...And after the king moves...} 13...Qh4 {Our queen arrives at the h4 square, and together with the knight, they attack the undefendable h2 square. Checkmate is now unstoppable. Good game!} *

[Event "Chapter 7: 1. d4 Sidelines / Study 1: 2.e3 and 2.e4 — The Blackmar-Diemer Gambit / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/dba0ef12-520e-49de-b17c-699cf02c44ae/studies/375ca717-b9a5-4775-b08c-747ac30f51e5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the first study of Chapter 7! Here and in the next study we cover White’s second-move deviations. In this one, we look at 2.e3 and 2.e4. White fights for the center with 1.d4. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.e3 {2.e3 looks dubious, blocking the c1-bishop, but White may aim for a Stonewall with several dark-square pawns and Bd3. They may also still go for c4, so we don’t over-commit with …Bf5. We develop naturally with 2...Nf6.} 2...Nf6 3.f4 {Very aggressive! 3.f4 supports e5 and is central to Stonewall setups. But if White now plays c4, they'd be totally overextended. We strike immediately with 3...Bf5, claiming the light squares.} 3...Bf5 4.Bd3 {White tries to trade our active bishop. 4…e6 is key - we don’t mind trading, since a pawn on f5 becomes a shield and strengthens our grip on e4.} 4...e6 5.Nf3 {Instead, White may continue with 5.Nf3, targeting e5. But this overlooks 5…c5!, our thematic pawn break. We already equalize.} 5...c5 6.c3 {6.c3 is most popular, connecting the pawns. We develop the knight to c6 after …c5. If Bxf5, exf5 followed by …Bd6 and …g6 is excellent.} 6...Nc6 7.O-O {White may castle. We prepare castling as well, 7...Bd6 comes next.} 7...Bd6 8.Ne5 {8.Ne5 is one of White’s main Stonewall ideas. We don’t fear it - our pieces develop around it and we keep firm control of e4. We continue with 8...Qc7.} 8...Qc7 9.Qc2 {If White avoids the bishop trade and moves the queen… We go for the bishop trade, forcing White to move the queen again.} 9...Bxd3 10.Qxd3 {White recaptures.} 10...O-O {And we can castle. Expect Stonewall players to go Nd2-Ndf3. However, White's hole on e4 cannot be ignored. We've got plan of ...Rac8, ...a6, ...b5 and ...Ne4 at the right time. We have a positional edge not only because of our e4 control but because of our piece quality: the bishop on c1 is dead.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: 1. d4 Sidelines / Study 1: 2.e3 and 2.e4 — The Blackmar-Diemer Gambit / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/dba0ef12-520e-49de-b17c-699cf02c44ae/studies/375ca717-b9a5-4775-b08c-747ac30f51e5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the first study of Chapter 7! Here and in the next study we cover White’s second-move deviations. In this one, we look at 2.e3 and 2.e4. White fights for the center with 1.d4. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.e3 {2.e3 looks dubious, blocking the c1-bishop, but White may aim for a Stonewall with several dark-square pawns and Bd3. They may also still go for c4, so we don’t over-commit with …Bf5. We develop naturally with 2...Nf6.} 2...Nf6 3.f4 {Very aggressive! 3.f4 supports e5 and is central to Stonewall setups. But if White now plays c4, they'd be totally overextended. We strike immediately with 3...Bf5, claiming the light squares.} 3...Bf5 4.Bd3 {White tries to trade our active bishop. 4…e6 is key - we don’t mind trading, since a pawn on f5 becomes a shield and strengthens our grip on e4.} 4...e6 5.Bxf5 {This is the most common continuation. After 5...exf5 our control of e4 is overwhelming - an outpost for the entire middlegame and endgame.} 5...exf5 6.Nf3 {The kingside knight is usually White’s first developed minor piece. If they ever pressure f5 with Qd3, then …g6 holds everything. If the pawn remains untargeted, we simply develop - the knight is best on d7 because it may go to f6 later to support the other knight on e4.} 6...Nbd7 7.O-O {White often just castles... We develop our remaining kingside minor piece to prepare castling.} 7...Bd6 8.Ne5 {If White gets their knight to e5 as is their plan in the Stonewall...} 8...O-O {We castle and will get our knight into e5 soon enough. e4 will be a permanent outpost for us, while e5 won't be a permanent outpost for White because we can even go ...f6 at some point to kick the knight out. We have plans of ...c6 and ...g6 to consolidate our pawns. If at any point White trades on f5, you'll have similar ideas revolving around control of e4.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: 1. d4 Sidelines / Study 1: 2.e3 and 2.e4 — The Blackmar-Diemer Gambit / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/dba0ef12-520e-49de-b17c-699cf02c44ae/studies/375ca717-b9a5-4775-b08c-747ac30f51e5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the first study of Chapter 7! Here and in the next study we cover White’s second-move deviations. In this one, we look at 2.e3 and 2.e4. White fights for the center with 1.d4. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.e4 {2.e4 is the Blackmar–Diemer Gambit. White sacrifices a pawn to speed up development - but with precise play, the move is simply bad. We accept the pawn and go up material.} 2...dxe4 3.Nc3 {White develops with tempo, attacking e4. We defend and develop with 2...Nf6.} 3...Nf6 4.f3 {White’s idea is to give up the e-pawn, trade on f3, and accelerate development. We take on f3 and leave White with choices.} 4...exf3 5.Nxf3 {If 5.Nxf3, White has a small lead in development and wants to castle quickly. Since defending with …e6 wasn’t needed earlier, we develop the light-squared bishop to f5 first.} 5...Bf5 6.Bc4 {White continues developing and prepares to castle. Now we play 6…e6, opening the bishop and defending f7.} 6...e6 7.Bg5 {7.Bg5 pins our knight, but this won’t work. We develop our b-knight to d7, defending f6, and keeping a solid extra pawn.} 7...Nbd7 8.O-O {White castles. With our king still in the center, White wants d5 and pressure on the e-file. That’s why 8...c6 is important - it adds another defender to d5.} 8...c6 9.Nh4 {White moves the knight to the edge of the board with goals of capturing our bishop.} 9...Bg6 {We move the bishop away from the dangerous f5 square, and are ready to capture with the h-pawn if the trade happens. Apart from that, our plan is simple: Develop the bishop to e7, castle, and enjoy a much better position being up a pawn. Sounds great!} *

[Event "Chapter 7: 1. d4 Sidelines / Study 1: 2.e3 and 2.e4 — The Blackmar-Diemer Gambit / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/dba0ef12-520e-49de-b17c-699cf02c44ae/studies/375ca717-b9a5-4775-b08c-747ac30f51e5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the first study of Chapter 7! Here and in the next study we cover White’s second-move deviations. In this one, we look at 2.e3 and 2.e4. White fights for the center with 1.d4. We respond symmetrically.} 1...d5 2.e4 {2.e4 is the Blackmar–Diemer Gambit. White sacrifices a pawn to speed up development - but with precise play, the move is simply bad. We accept the pawn and go up material.} 2...dxe4 3.Nc3 {White develops with tempo, attacking e4. We defend and develop with 2...Nf6.} 3...Nf6 4.f3 {White’s idea is to give up the e-pawn, trade on f3, and accelerate development. We take on f3 and leave White with choices.} 4...exf3 5.Qxf3 {If White recaptures with the queen, offering yet another pawn, this is even worse. White is lost. We simply capture - now two pawns up.} 5...Qxd4 6.Be3 {6.Be3 develops with tempo - White’s whole idea. Since we’re two pawns ahead, we’re happy to trade queens, so we offer the exchange on g4.} 6...Qg4 7.Qf2 {White avoids it, as expected… But we insist with 7...Qf5.} 7...Qf5 8.Nf3 {A developing move by White, blocking the queens from seeing eachother. But it simply loses.} 8...Ng4 {8...Ng4! forks White's queen and bishop, and we are guaranteed to trade a piece. After that, we will develop the kingside, castle, and then develop the queenside. Now you know how to fight against the Blackmar-Diemer gambit!} *

[Event "Chapter 7: 1. d4 Sidelines / Study 2: 2.Bg5 and 2.Nc3 Sidelines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/dba0ef12-520e-49de-b17c-699cf02c44ae/studies/e0e44f2d-9663-4c04-a07a-44afe7e3e9a8/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the last study of this chapter! Here we analyze positions where White’s second move is either 2.Bg5 or 2.Nc3. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We respond symmetrically with 1…d5.} 1...d5 2.Bg5 {2.Bg5 is an immediate attempt to annoy us, pinning the e-pawn. The bishop won’t stay there long. We kick it with 2…h6.} 2...h6 3.Bh4 {The natural reaction is to keep the pin. We answer with 3...c6, defending d5 and giving the queen a diagonal - on b6 we will attack b2.} 3...c6 4.Nf3 {White may go for simple development with 4.Nf3. We can attack b2 with the queen here as well.} 4...Qb6 5.b3 {Pushing the pawn is the natural way to defend it. We immediately undermine d4 with our c-pawn - this also frees the c6-square for our knight.} 5...c5 6.e3 {White may refuse to capture and simply support d4. We pin the defender with 6...Bg4 - adding central pressure before …e6.} 6...Bg4 7.h3 {White tries to provoke a capture. Here, we should take on f3 - the d4-pawn becomes weak.} 7...Bxf3 8.Qxf3 {The queen recaptures. White attacks d5, we attack d4. We take on d4 - it looks equal, but it isn’t.} 8...cxd4 9.Qxd5 {The problem is that when White takes on d5, the queen isn't safe. We develop the knight and support d4 while clearing the way for …Rd8.} 9...Nc6 10.exd4 {White may try to exchange pawns to avoid structural damage... But 10…Rd8 activates the rook - …Rxd4 is coming.} 10...Rd8 11.Qe4 {So if the queen retreats to the wrong square...} 11...Rxd4 {We're winning a bishop. Of course, White isn't forced to go to e4. But even if 11. Qf3 had been played, 11...Nxd4 would have been a tempo on the queen as well, with a winning advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: 1. d4 Sidelines / Study 2: 2.Bg5 and 2.Nc3 Sidelines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/dba0ef12-520e-49de-b17c-699cf02c44ae/studies/e0e44f2d-9663-4c04-a07a-44afe7e3e9a8/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the last study of this chapter! Here we analyze positions where White’s second move is either 2.Bg5 or 2.Nc3. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We respond symmetrically with 1…d5.} 1...d5 2.Bg5 {2.Bg5 is an immediate attempt to annoy us, pinning the e-pawn. The bishop won’t stay there long. We kick it with 2…h6.} 2...h6 3.Bh4 {The natural reaction is to keep the pin. We answer with 3...c6, defending d5 and giving the queen a diagonal - on b6 we will attack b2.} 3...c6 4.e3 {4.e3 is the most popular move, connecting White’s pawn chain. And this is where our setup shines - we attack the vulnerable b2-pawn with the queen after the early bishop development.} 4...Qb6 5.b3 {White may defend b2 by pushing it, but that creates plenty of dark-square weaknesses. Since the knight isn’t on f3 to fight for e5, we can play 5…e5 here.} 5...e5 6.Nf3 {White targets our e5-pawn. We defend with 6...Nd7 - the same ...Qb4+ trick remains possible.} 6...Nd7 7.Be2 {The bishop goes to e2 since Bd3 is impossible because of e5. We still attack the f3-knight with our e-pawn - our pawn chain grants us space and central control.} 7...e4 8.Nfd2 {The knight must retreat. We develop and challenge the h4-bishop with our own, 8...Be7.} 8...Be7 9.Bxe7 {After the trade, we get rid of White's only active piece.} 9...Nxe7 {We recapture with our knight. We'll be playing ...f5 and ...Nf6 after the f-pawn moves. We'll castle kingside and go for an attack on that side of the board.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: 1. d4 Sidelines / Study 2: 2.Bg5 and 2.Nc3 Sidelines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/dba0ef12-520e-49de-b17c-699cf02c44ae/studies/e0e44f2d-9663-4c04-a07a-44afe7e3e9a8/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the last study of this chapter! Here we analyze positions where White’s second move is either 2.Bg5 or 2.Nc3. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We respond symmetrically with 1…d5.} 1...d5 2.Bg5 {2.Bg5 is an immediate attempt to annoy us, pinning the e-pawn. The bishop won’t stay there long. We kick it with 2…h6.} 2...h6 3.Bh4 {The natural reaction is to keep the pin. We answer with 3...c6, defending d5 and giving the queen a diagonal - on b6 we will attack b2.} 3...c6 4.Nf3 {White may go for simple development with 4.Nf3. We can attack b2 with the queen here as well.} 4...Qb6 5.b3 {Pushing the pawn is the natural way to defend it. We immediately undermine d4 with our c-pawn - this also frees the c6-square for our knight.} 5...c5 6.dxc5 {Another natural reaction to ...c5 is for White to take it. We'll recapture with the queen, with some pressure against the c-pawn.} 6...Qxc5 7.e3 {White continues developing, so expect 7.e3. With 7...g5, we gain a tempo and prepare a great diagonal for our dark-squared bishop.} 7...g5 8.Bg3 {The bishop must go to g3 - only move. Now we hit the rook by completing the fianchetto.} 8...Bg7 9.c3 {White blocks the diagonal. We don't want to take on c3; instead, we continue developing and play 9...Nc6 - it improves our control of e5 and d4.} 9...Nc6 10.Be2 {White could also go for development. The nice thing is that their queenside is under attack by our bishop and queen, so they cannot easily develop it.} 10...Nf6 {We should just develop as well though. Amazingly, the engine gives Black an advantage with moves like ...h5 for ...h4, but this is really sharp. You could also centralize the knight with ...Ne4, castle kingside, and continue attacking the weak points with your pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: 1. d4 Sidelines / Study 2: 2.Bg5 and 2.Nc3 Sidelines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/dba0ef12-520e-49de-b17c-699cf02c44ae/studies/e0e44f2d-9663-4c04-a07a-44afe7e3e9a8/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the last study of this chapter! Here we analyze positions where White’s second move is either 2.Bg5 or 2.Nc3. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We respond symmetrically with 1…d5.} 1...d5 2.Bg5 {2.Bg5 is an immediate attempt to annoy us, pinning the e-pawn. The bishop won’t stay there long. We kick it with 2…h6.} 2...h6 3.Bh4 {The natural reaction is to keep the pin. We answer with 3...c6, defending d5 and giving the queen a diagonal - on b6 we will attack b2.} 3...c6 4.e3 {4.e3 is the most popular move, connecting White’s pawn chain. And this is where our setup shines - we attack the vulnerable b2-pawn with the queen after the early bishop development.} 4...Qb6 5.b3 {White may defend b2 by pushing it, but that creates plenty of dark-square weaknesses. Since the knight isn’t on f3 to fight for e5, we can play 5…e5 here.} 5...e5 6.Nf3 {White targets our e5-pawn. We defend with 6...Nd7 - the same ...Qb4+ trick remains possible.} 6...Nd7 7.dxe5 {White captures, but this trade favors us. We take on e5 with the knight.} 7...Nxe5 8.Nxe5 {White takes a knight...} 8...Qb4+ {But we can go after the bishop. We'll get the bishop pair and get our queen into f6. We'll have a slight positional edge.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: 1. d4 Sidelines / Study 2: 2.Bg5 and 2.Nc3 Sidelines / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/dba0ef12-520e-49de-b17c-699cf02c44ae/studies/e0e44f2d-9663-4c04-a07a-44afe7e3e9a8/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the last study of this chapter! Here we analyze positions where White’s second move is either 2.Bg5 or 2.Nc3. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We respond symmetrically with 1…d5.} 1...d5 2.Nc3 {Another move you’ll face after 1.d4 d5 is 2.Nc3, the Veresov. White wants a quick e4. We develop the kingside knight to f6 first.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg5 {3.Bg5 targets f6 and tries to ruin our pawn structure. We defend with 3…Nbd7, ready to recapture on f6 with the knight.} 3...Nbd7 4.Nf3 {White continues developing. We strengthen the center with 4...e6, opening the diagonal for the dark-squared bishop.} 4...e6 5.e3 {White plays 5.e3 to activate the light-squared bishop. We gain a tempo with 5...h6, asking the bishop a question.} 5...h6 6.Bh4 {The bishop retreats to h4, keeping the pin. White may consider 7.Bb5, pinning our d7-knight. So we play 6…a6, preventing Bb5 and preparing …c5.} 6...a6 7.Bd3 {7.Bd3 is the most popular continuation. We unpin the knight with 7..Be7, gaining more control of e4.} 7...Be7 8.O-O {When White castles… 8…c5 is already very strong. We’re threatening to chase the bishop from d3.} 8...c5 9.dxc5 {If White takes on c5...} 9...Nxc5 {Black has a better position and one extra pawn in the center, thus more central control. We'll continue with ...0-0, ...b6 and ...Bb7.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: 1. d4 Sidelines / Study 2: 2.Bg5 and 2.Nc3 Sidelines / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/dba0ef12-520e-49de-b17c-699cf02c44ae/studies/e0e44f2d-9663-4c04-a07a-44afe7e3e9a8/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the last study of this chapter! Here we analyze positions where White’s second move is either 2.Bg5 or 2.Nc3. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We respond symmetrically with 1…d5.} 1...d5 2.Nc3 {Another move you’ll face after 1.d4 d5 is 2.Nc3, the Veresov. White wants a quick e4. We develop the kingside knight to f6 first.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg5 {3.Bg5 targets f6 and tries to ruin our pawn structure. We defend with 3…Nbd7, ready to recapture on f6 with the knight.} 3...Nbd7 4.Nf3 {White continues developing. We strengthen the center with 4...e6, opening the diagonal for the dark-squared bishop.} 4...e6 5.e3 {White plays 5.e3 to activate the light-squared bishop. We gain a tempo with 5...h6, asking the bishop a question.} 5...h6 6.Bh4 {The bishop retreats to h4, keeping the pin. White may consider 7.Bb5, pinning our d7-knight. So we play 6…a6, preventing Bb5 and preparing …c5.} 6...a6 7.Bd3 {7.Bd3 is the most popular continuation. We unpin the knight with 7..Be7, gaining more control of e4.} 7...Be7 8.O-O {When White castles… 8…c5 is already very strong. We’re threatening to chase the bishop from d3.} 8...c5 9.Re1 {If White prepares e4… We chase the bishop from d3 with 9...c4.} 9...c4 10.Bf1 {No matter where the bishop goes...} 10...b5 {We have a lot of space on the queenside and a robust pawn structure; meanwhile White's pieces aren't so well-coordinated. ...Bb7 and ...0-0 are in the horizon, and we can even consider queenside play because we're so far advanced on that side of the board.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: 1. d4 Sidelines / Study 2: 2.Bg5 and 2.Nc3 Sidelines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/dba0ef12-520e-49de-b17c-699cf02c44ae/studies/e0e44f2d-9663-4c04-a07a-44afe7e3e9a8/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the last study of this chapter! Here we analyze positions where White’s second move is either 2.Bg5 or 2.Nc3. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We respond symmetrically with 1…d5.} 1...d5 2.Bg5 {2.Bg5 is an immediate attempt to annoy us, pinning the e-pawn. The bishop won’t stay there long. We kick it with 2…h6.} 2...h6 3.Bh4 {The natural reaction is to keep the pin. We answer with 3...c6, defending d5 and giving the queen a diagonal - on b6 we will attack b2.} 3...c6 4.e3 {4.e3 is the most popular move, connecting White’s pawn chain. And this is where our setup shines - we attack the vulnerable b2-pawn with the queen after the early bishop development.} 4...Qb6 5.Qc1 {White may defend b2 with 5.Qc1, but the queen is passive. Without Nf3, we can undermine d4 in a very tricky way, with 5...e5 - White should not capture.} 5...e5 6.c3 {The most popular response is defending with c3. We take on d4.} 6...exd4 7.exd4 {White usually recaptures with the e-pawn. That gives White's queen extra squares, but Black’s queen is far more active. With 7...Bf5, our development becomes harmonious.} 7...Bf5 8.Nd2 {White starts queenside development. We do the same, 8...Nd7 - our pieces go to more natural squares.} 8...Nd7 9.Ngf3 {White brings the knight out. We mirror the development, 9...Ngf6.} 9...Ngf6 10.Be2 {White develops their bishop to e2, and we see one benefit of …Bf5 - we control d3, preventing Bd3. Our dark-squared bishop goes to a strong diagonal via d6.} 10...Bd6 11.O-O {White castles.} 11...O-O {And we castle too. We're going to centralize the rooks. If the bishop goes to g3, we'll go for a trade. We've got superior piece placement and so we should have a stable edge in the middlegame.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: 1. d4 Sidelines / Study 2: 2.Bg5 and 2.Nc3 Sidelines / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/dba0ef12-520e-49de-b17c-699cf02c44ae/studies/e0e44f2d-9663-4c04-a07a-44afe7e3e9a8/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the last study of this chapter! Here we analyze positions where White’s second move is either 2.Bg5 or 2.Nc3. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We respond symmetrically with 1…d5.} 1...d5 2.Bg5 {2.Bg5 is an immediate attempt to annoy us, pinning the e-pawn. The bishop won’t stay there long. We kick it with 2…h6.} 2...h6 3.Bh4 {The natural reaction is to keep the pin. We answer with 3...c6, defending d5 and giving the queen a diagonal - on b6 we will attack b2.} 3...c6 4.Nf3 {White may go for simple development with 4.Nf3. We can attack b2 with the queen here as well.} 4...Qb6 5.b3 {Pushing the pawn is the natural way to defend it. We immediately undermine d4 with our c-pawn - this also frees the c6-square for our knight.} 5...c5 6.e3 {White may refuse to capture and simply support d4. We pin the defender with 6...Bg4 - adding central pressure before …e6.} 6...Bg4 7.Be2 {White may unpin the knight. We play 7…e6 to connect the pawn chain. We have ideas of …Bxf3 and cxd4.} 7...e6 8.Ne5 {White may misplay with a centralized knight and a discovered attack. We gladly take on e2.} 8...Bxe2 9.Qxe2 {When the queen recaptures, it no longer defends d4. We take on d4 - but the isn't to win the pawn on d4 right away.} 9...cxd4 10.exd4 {White recaptures.} 10...Nc6 {And now we put pressure on the pawn. 10...Qxd4 allows 11. Qb5+. But after 10...Nc6, we've got ideas of bringing a rook into c8 and developing our knight from g to e7. We've got pressure against the center and White's pieces, like the h4-bishop, don't have meaningful roles.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: 1. d4 Sidelines / Study 2: 2.Bg5 and 2.Nc3 Sidelines / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/dba0ef12-520e-49de-b17c-699cf02c44ae/studies/e0e44f2d-9663-4c04-a07a-44afe7e3e9a8/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the last study of this chapter! Here we analyze positions where White’s second move is either 2.Bg5 or 2.Nc3. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We respond symmetrically with 1…d5.} 1...d5 2.Bg5 {2.Bg5 is an immediate attempt to annoy us, pinning the e-pawn. The bishop won’t stay there long. We kick it with 2…h6.} 2...h6 3.Bh4 {The natural reaction is to keep the pin. We answer with 3...c6, defending d5 and giving the queen a diagonal - on b6 we will attack b2.} 3...c6 4.Nf3 {White may go for simple development with 4.Nf3. We can attack b2 with the queen here as well.} 4...Qb6 5.Qc1 {Another defense is 5.Qc1. The queen isn’t happy here. We attack the bishop with 5…g5. The point is simple: …g4 wins the d4-pawn - White cannot prevent it.} 5...g5 6.Bg3 {The bishop drops to g3. We remove the defender of d4 by pushing our g-pawn once again.} 6...g4 7.Ne5 {The knight centralizes to e5, but the square is unstable - we will remove one of its defenders. We take on d4 with the queen and already are a pawn up. All we need now is to catch up in development.} 7...Qxd4 8.e3 {8. e3 attacks the queen.} 8...Qc5 {Our queen is quite active on c5. Our next move will be ...Bg7 to protect the diagonal. ...Nd7 is good to challenge the e5-knight, and ...h5 to support our advance g4-pawn will help us keep that pawn there. All we have to do is catch up in development and use our extra pawn in the middlegame.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: 1. d4 Sidelines / Study 2: 2.Bg5 and 2.Nc3 Sidelines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/dba0ef12-520e-49de-b17c-699cf02c44ae/studies/e0e44f2d-9663-4c04-a07a-44afe7e3e9a8/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the last study of this chapter! Here we analyze positions where White’s second move is either 2.Bg5 or 2.Nc3. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We respond symmetrically with 1…d5.} 1...d5 2.Bg5 {2.Bg5 is an immediate attempt to annoy us, pinning the e-pawn. The bishop won’t stay there long. We kick it with 2…h6.} 2...h6 3.Bh4 {The natural reaction is to keep the pin. We answer with 3...c6, defending d5 and giving the queen a diagonal - on b6 we will attack b2.} 3...c6 4.e3 {4.e3 is the most popular move, connecting White’s pawn chain. And this is where our setup shines - we attack the vulnerable b2-pawn with the queen after the early bishop development.} 4...Qb6 5.b3 {White may defend b2 by pushing it, but that creates plenty of dark-square weaknesses. Since the knight isn’t on f3 to fight for e5, we can play 5…e5 here.} 5...e5 6.dxe5 {Many players just take...} 6...Qb4+ {But as we've seen, the bishop is falling.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: 1. d4 Sidelines / Study 2: 2.Bg5 and 2.Nc3 Sidelines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/dba0ef12-520e-49de-b17c-699cf02c44ae/studies/e0e44f2d-9663-4c04-a07a-44afe7e3e9a8/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the last study of this chapter! Here we analyze positions where White’s second move is either 2.Bg5 or 2.Nc3. White begins with the Queen’s Pawn opening. We respond symmetrically with 1…d5.} 1...d5 2.Bg5 {2.Bg5 is an immediate attempt to annoy us, pinning the e-pawn. The bishop won’t stay there long. We kick it with 2…h6.} 2...h6 3.Bh4 {The natural reaction is to keep the pin. We answer with 3...c6, defending d5 and giving the queen a diagonal - on b6 we will attack b2.} 3...c6 4.e3 {4.e3 is the most popular move, connecting White’s pawn chain. And this is where our setup shines - we attack the vulnerable b2-pawn with the queen after the early bishop development.} 4...Qb6 5.Qc1 {White may defend b2 with 5.Qc1, but the queen is passive. Without Nf3, we can undermine d4 in a very tricky way, with 5...e5 - White should not capture.} 5...e5 6.dxe5 {Many players will fall for this one.} 6...Qb4+ {This check picks up the bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The English Opening (Part 1) / Study 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d6e64563-7e88-42cd-b58e-0c538b1ba56c/studies/10b95a51-c2c1-4d06-b761-1665e020a86f/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 8! In this and in the next chapter, we will analyze what to do when White opens with the English (1.c4) instead of the traditional Queen’s pawn systems. Chapter 8 covers White’s setup with knights on c3 and f3, followed by 3.e3. White opens with the 4th most popular move in chess, 1.c4, discouraging the immediate ...d5. 1...e6 prepares the central break we want.} 1...e6 2.Nc3 {White develops the queen’s knight and adds pressure on d5. We calmly continue with 2...d5, now properly supported. White has different options, but everything eventually leads back to the main line - other attempts are either inferior or already seen in previous chapters!} 2...d5 3.g3 {3.g3 is actually a mistake - let’s see why. White wants to fianchetto, but before they finish developing: We immediately play 3...d4!, kicking the knight from c3. If White isn’t precise, trouble comes quickly.} 3...d4 4.Na4 {White may retreat to the rim. Now we seize space with 4...e5!, something rarely possible in these structures. Our light-squared bishop will develop smoothly.} 4...e5 5.Bg2 {White keeps developing, but the knight remains stuck on a4. We solidify with 5...c5, taking away the b4 square.} 5...c5 6.d3 {White opens the diagonal for the bishop. We choose 6...Nc6, an active developing move. We don’t mind doubled c-pawns - White would be trading their only active piece!} 6...Nc6 7.Nf3 {Instead, White develops normally. We mirror with 7...Nf6.} 7...Nf6 8.O-O {After White castles...} 8...h6 {First we play 8...h6, disallowing Bg5 by White before we continue developing. As we have shown, 3.g3 by White has lead them nowhere. Now they have a stuck knight on a4, a lack of space and it is not clear how they will continue. On the other hand, we are ready to develop the bishop to d6 and castle, with a better position.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The English Opening (Part 1) / Study 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d6e64563-7e88-42cd-b58e-0c538b1ba56c/studies/10b95a51-c2c1-4d06-b761-1665e020a86f/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 8! In this and in the next chapter, we will analyze what to do when White opens with the English (1.c4) instead of the traditional Queen’s pawn systems. Chapter 8 covers White’s setup with knights on c3 and f3, followed by 3.e3. White opens with the 4th most popular move in chess, 1.c4, discouraging the immediate ...d5. 1...e6 prepares the central break we want.} 1...e6 2.Nc3 {White develops the queen’s knight and adds pressure on d5. We calmly continue with 2...d5, now properly supported. White has different options, but everything eventually leads back to the main line - other attempts are either inferior or already seen in previous chapters!} 2...d5 3.e3 {3.e3 is the main move and White’s best plan here. Of course, 3.d4 simply transposes to the QGD structures we already know. We develop with 3...Nf6, reinforcing the center.} 3...Nf6 4.Nf3 {White mirrors our development. And we implement our classic idea with 4...a6! We want to capture on c4 and expand on the queenside with ...b5 and ...Bb7.} 4...a6 5.b3 {White prevents ...dxc4 and prepares to recapture with the pawn. The dark-squared bishop wants to head to b2, since it has no other good square. We continue developing. With no Bg5 issues, 5...Bd6 is perfectly placed.} 5...Bd6 6.d4 {White challenges the center early. We prevent the c5 expansion and prepare to fianchetto with 6...b6.} 6...b6 7.Bb2 {White completes queenside development by fianchettoing their bishop. We do the same.} 7...Bb7 8.g3 {White goes for a double fianchetto - let’s learn the antidote. We start by capturing on c4, opening our bishop.} 8...dxc4 9.bxc4 {As White wants to fianchetto on g2, they recapture with the pawn. This way they also keep pawns in the center, which is always important. However, having played 9.bxc4, White now has only 2 pawns on the queenside, while we have 3. We finish queenside development with 9...Nbd7. Next we want to castle and play for the critical ...c5 push, supported by the knight.} 9...Nbd7 10.Bg2 {White continues with their plan. We castle.} 10...O-O 11.O-O {White castles as well... Now we strike with 11...c5!, grabbing space and creating tension. White shouldn’t capture - it helps us activate our pieces.} 11...c5 12.d5 {For that reason, White tries to close down the center and grab more space with 12.d5. We capture, planning ...b5 and ...c4 when the pawn recaptures.} 12...exd5 13.Nxd5 {White captures with the knight, opening up the dark-squared bishop. We go for the knight trade.} 13...Nxd5 14.cxd5 {And White finally recaptures with the pawn. Now we begin our queenside expansion. The 3-1 pawn majority is enormous - plus we now have a passed c-pawn.} 14...b5 15.e4 {15.e4 grabs more space in the center and defends d5. However, White would love to push e5 and completely take control of the center, which would be a huge problem! 15...Re8 stops it and targets e4.} 15...Re8 16.Qc2 {White activates the queen, connects the rooks and defends the pawn on e4, while also putting pressure on our c-pawn.} 16...c4 {16...c4 defends our pawn and frees up the c5-square as an excellent outpost for our pieces. Our play will definitely focus on the queenside with ideas of bringing the rook and trying to utilize the 3-1 pawn advantage. However, White will focus on the two strong center pawns, probably bring the rooks to the center and try to push! It's a balanced but extremely sharp game with a lot of chances for both sides.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The English Opening (Part 1) / Study 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d6e64563-7e88-42cd-b58e-0c538b1ba56c/studies/10b95a51-c2c1-4d06-b761-1665e020a86f/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 8! In this and in the next chapter, we will analyze what to do when White opens with the English (1.c4) instead of the traditional Queen’s pawn systems. Chapter 8 covers White’s setup with knights on c3 and f3, followed by 3.e3. White opens with the 4th most popular move in chess, 1.c4, discouraging the immediate ...d5. 1...e6 prepares the central break we want.} 1...e6 2.Nc3 {White develops the queen’s knight and adds pressure on d5. We calmly continue with 2...d5, now properly supported. White has different options, but everything eventually leads back to the main line - other attempts are either inferior or already seen in previous chapters!} 2...d5 3.e3 {3.e3 is the main move and White’s best plan here. Of course, 3.d4 simply transposes to the QGD structures we already know. We develop with 3...Nf6, reinforcing the center.} 3...Nf6 4.Nf3 {White mirrors our development. And we implement our classic idea with 4...a6! We want to capture on c4 and expand on the queenside with ...b5 and ...Bb7.} 4...a6 5.b3 {White prevents ...dxc4 and prepares to recapture with the pawn. The dark-squared bishop wants to head to b2, since it has no other good square. We continue developing. With no Bg5 issues, 5...Bd6 is perfectly placed.} 5...Bd6 6.d4 {White challenges the center early. We prevent the c5 expansion and prepare to fianchetto with 6...b6.} 6...b6 7.Bb2 {White completes queenside development by fianchettoing their bishop. We do the same.} 7...Bb7 8.g3 {White goes for a double fianchetto - let’s learn the antidote. We start by capturing on c4, opening our bishop.} 8...dxc4 9.bxc4 {As White wants to fianchetto on g2, they recapture with the pawn. This way they also keep pawns in the center, which is always important. However, having played 9.bxc4, White now has only 2 pawns on the queenside, while we have 3. We finish queenside development with 9...Nbd7. Next we want to castle and play for the critical ...c5 push, supported by the knight.} 9...Nbd7 10.Bg2 {White continues with their plan. We castle.} 10...O-O 11.O-O {White castles as well... Now we strike with 11...c5!, grabbing space and creating tension. White shouldn’t capture - it helps us activate our pieces.} 11...c5 12.d5 {For that reason, White tries to close down the center and grab more space with 12.d5. We capture, planning ...b5 and ...c4 when the pawn recaptures.} 12...exd5 13.cxd5 {If White chooses not to trade the knights on d5, we simply continue with our plan.} 13...b5 {13...b5 grabs more space on the queenside. Now the knight on c3 is also a target for our pawns. The position is -1.0. White should definitely trade the knights on d5!} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The English Opening (Part 1) / Study 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d6e64563-7e88-42cd-b58e-0c538b1ba56c/studies/10b95a51-c2c1-4d06-b761-1665e020a86f/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 8! In this and in the next chapter, we will analyze what to do when White opens with the English (1.c4) instead of the traditional Queen’s pawn systems. Chapter 8 covers White’s setup with knights on c3 and f3, followed by 3.e3. White opens with the 4th most popular move in chess, 1.c4, discouraging the immediate ...d5. 1...e6 prepares the central break we want.} 1...e6 2.Nc3 {White develops the queen’s knight and adds pressure on d5. We calmly continue with 2...d5, now properly supported. White has different options, but everything eventually leads back to the main line - other attempts are either inferior or already seen in previous chapters!} 2...d5 3.e3 {3.e3 is the main move and White’s best plan here. Of course, 3.d4 simply transposes to the QGD structures we already know. We develop with 3...Nf6, reinforcing the center.} 3...Nf6 4.Nf3 {White mirrors our development. And we implement our classic idea with 4...a6! We want to capture on c4 and expand on the queenside with ...b5 and ...Bb7.} 4...a6 5.b3 {White prevents ...dxc4 and prepares to recapture with the pawn. The dark-squared bishop wants to head to b2, since it has no other good square. We continue developing. With no Bg5 issues, 5...Bd6 is perfectly placed.} 5...Bd6 6.Bb2 {White develops naturally on the long diagonal. After 6...b6 our own bishop will soon reach b7 as well.} 6...b6 7.Be2 {White completes kingside development. We fianchetto our light-squared bishop.} 7...Bb7 8.O-O {Before the battle begins, the kings must be safe... White castles. So do we.} 8...O-O 9.d4 {White strikes in the center, gaining space with all pieces developed. We finish piece development with 9...Nbd7, controlling key central squares.} 9...Nbd7 10.Rc1 {White activates the rook to the c-file, preparing for potential pressure if the file opens. We connect our rooks as well with 10...Qe7. This line is highly positional - both sides improve everything before committing.} 10...Qe7 11.Re1 {White keeps optimizing their pieces and might aim for e4 soon, which would be very strong for them. We prevent that with 11...Ne4!, our most active piece, solidly supported.} 11...Ne4 12.Bd3 {White improves the position of their bishop and targets our knight, with possible ideas of trading.} 12...f5 {However, 12...f5 completely solidifies the knight! Now we have a lot of space on the board and have made it impossible for White to capture on e4, as ...fxe4 would activate our rook and simply give us too much advantage. White will probably rely on capturing on d5 and opening up the c-file. We'll play our other knight to f6 and maybe even go for ...c5.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The English Opening (Part 1) / Study 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d6e64563-7e88-42cd-b58e-0c538b1ba56c/studies/10b95a51-c2c1-4d06-b761-1665e020a86f/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 8! In this and in the next chapter, we will analyze what to do when White opens with the English (1.c4) instead of the traditional Queen’s pawn systems. Chapter 8 covers White’s setup with knights on c3 and f3, followed by 3.e3. White opens with the 4th most popular move in chess, 1.c4, discouraging the immediate ...d5. 1...e6 prepares the central break we want.} 1...e6 2.Nc3 {White develops the queen’s knight and adds pressure on d5. We calmly continue with 2...d5, now properly supported. White has different options, but everything eventually leads back to the main line - other attempts are either inferior or already seen in previous chapters!} 2...d5 3.e3 {3.e3 is the main move and White’s best plan here. Of course, 3.d4 simply transposes to the QGD structures we already know. We develop with 3...Nf6, reinforcing the center.} 3...Nf6 4.Nf3 {White mirrors our development. And we implement our classic idea with 4...a6! We want to capture on c4 and expand on the queenside with ...b5 and ...Bb7.} 4...a6 5.Be2 {5.Be2 does nothing to stop our idea and loses a tempo. We continue with 5...dxc4.} 5...dxc4 6.Bxc4 {White recaptures, but this is what we wanted. 6...b5! hits the bishop and opens b7 for ours.} 6...b5 7.Be2 {The bishop retreats. Before developing our light-squared bishop, we take more space with 7...c5!, grabbing full queenside control.} 7...c5 8.O-O {White castles. We continue with 8...Bb7.} 8...Bb7 9.d4 {White has been passive enough and strikes in the center!} 9...Nbd7 {Having no need to capture and make it easier for White to coordinate pieces, we simply develop and support the c5-square with our knight. Next, we will play ...Rc8, ...Be7 and castle. The engine claims that the position is equal, but from a human standpoint I would argue that we are already better!} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The English Opening (Part 1) / Study 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d6e64563-7e88-42cd-b58e-0c538b1ba56c/studies/10b95a51-c2c1-4d06-b761-1665e020a86f/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 8! In this and in the next chapter, we will analyze what to do when White opens with the English (1.c4) instead of the traditional Queen’s pawn systems. Chapter 8 covers White’s setup with knights on c3 and f3, followed by 3.e3. White opens with the 4th most popular move in chess, 1.c4, discouraging the immediate ...d5. 1...e6 prepares the central break we want.} 1...e6 2.Nc3 {White develops the queen’s knight and adds pressure on d5. We calmly continue with 2...d5, now properly supported. White has different options, but everything eventually leads back to the main line - other attempts are either inferior or already seen in previous chapters!} 2...d5 3.e3 {3.e3 is the main move and White’s best plan here. Of course, 3.d4 simply transposes to the QGD structures we already know. We develop with 3...Nf6, reinforcing the center.} 3...Nf6 4.Nf3 {White mirrors our development. And we implement our classic idea with 4...a6! We want to capture on c4 and expand on the queenside with ...b5 and ...Bb7.} 4...a6 5.cxd5 {White captures on d5… We recapture, maintaining healthy central control.} 5...exd5 6.d4 {White continues with the natural 6.d4, placing another pawn in the center and grabbing space.} 6...Bd6 {However, after 6...Bd6 we have developed the kingside and are ready to castle with little to no problems in the position. Notice how this position is almost the same as the positions of Chapter 2, with the c1-bishop being stuck as a difference! That means that this way of playing is far from optimal for White.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The English Opening (Part 1) / Study 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d6e64563-7e88-42cd-b58e-0c538b1ba56c/studies/10b95a51-c2c1-4d06-b761-1665e020a86f/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 8! In this and in the next chapter, we will analyze what to do when White opens with the English (1.c4) instead of the traditional Queen’s pawn systems. Chapter 8 covers White’s setup with knights on c3 and f3, followed by 3.e3. White opens with the 4th most popular move in chess, 1.c4, discouraging the immediate ...d5. 1...e6 prepares the central break we want.} 1...e6 2.Nc3 {White develops the queen’s knight and adds pressure on d5. We calmly continue with 2...d5, now properly supported. White has different options, but everything eventually leads back to the main line - other attempts are either inferior or already seen in previous chapters!} 2...d5 3.e3 {3.e3 is the main move and White’s best plan here. Of course, 3.d4 simply transposes to the QGD structures we already know. We develop with 3...Nf6, reinforcing the center.} 3...Nf6 4.Nf3 {White mirrors our development. And we implement our classic idea with 4...a6! We want to capture on c4 and expand on the queenside with ...b5 and ...Bb7.} 4...a6 5.d4 {5.d4 simply transposes the position back to Chapter 3.} 5...Be7 {We develop, ready to castle. You know how to play this position!} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The English Opening (Part 1) / Study 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d6e64563-7e88-42cd-b58e-0c538b1ba56c/studies/10b95a51-c2c1-4d06-b761-1665e020a86f/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 8! In this and in the next chapter, we will analyze what to do when White opens with the English (1.c4) instead of the traditional Queen’s pawn systems. Chapter 8 covers White’s setup with knights on c3 and f3, followed by 3.e3. White opens with the 4th most popular move in chess, 1.c4, discouraging the immediate ...d5. 1...e6 prepares the central break we want.} 1...e6 2.Nc3 {White develops the queen’s knight and adds pressure on d5. We calmly continue with 2...d5, now properly supported. White has different options, but everything eventually leads back to the main line - other attempts are either inferior or already seen in previous chapters!} 2...d5 3.cxd5 {If White chooses to capture the pawn on d5 immediately...} 3...exd5 {We simply recapture. White has made it easier for our light-squared bishop to develop! We have already seen positions like this in earlier chapters.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The English Opening (Part 1) / Study 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/d6e64563-7e88-42cd-b58e-0c538b1ba56c/studies/10b95a51-c2c1-4d06-b761-1665e020a86f/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 8! In this and in the next chapter, we will analyze what to do when White opens with the English (1.c4) instead of the traditional Queen’s pawn systems. Chapter 8 covers White’s setup with knights on c3 and f3, followed by 3.e3. White opens with the 4th most popular move in chess, 1.c4, discouraging the immediate ...d5. 1...e6 prepares the central break we want.} 1...e6 2.Nc3 {White develops the queen’s knight and adds pressure on d5. We calmly continue with 2...d5, now properly supported. White has different options, but everything eventually leads back to the main line - other attempts are either inferior or already seen in previous chapters!} 2...d5 3.g3 {3.g3 is actually a mistake - let’s see why. White wants to fianchetto, but before they finish developing: We immediately play 3...d4!, kicking the knight from c3. If White isn’t precise, trouble comes quickly.} 3...d4 4.Ne4 {4.Ne4 centralizes the knight and it looks like White is doing fine...} 4...f5 {But 4...f5 traps the knight! All of the squares the knight could escape to are covered by our pawns and pieces. Oops!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 1: White Plays Nf3 and Bg2 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/e3a639cd-7afc-4b8f-8f25-446db20c0481/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 9! In this chapter, just like in the previous one, we will cover the English with 1.c4 by White. However, in Chapter 9 we focus on White placing the kingside knight on f3 and often playing g3 on the third move. As we will see, one of our key ideas is capturing on c4 and playing around our c4-pawn. White plays the English Opening. We reply with 1...e6, ready to transpose into the QGD if White plays 2.d4.} 1...e6 2.Nf3 {Instead, with 2.Nf3, White chooses a move order that can transpose into many different openings. We grab space in the center with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.g3 {The Nf3–g3 setup is definitely an interesting idea by White. With the bishop on the long h1–a8 diagonal, White aims for better central control and a generally safer king. However, 3.g3 loses a tempo and allows us to capture on c4! Other moves like 3.e3 or 3.b3 were already covered in Chapter 7. Since White’s bishop is going to g2, it will be harder for White to regain the c4-pawn. We will probably try to hang on to it.} 3...dxc4 4.Bg2 {White continues developing. The position clearly resembles Catalan-like structures. 4...Nd7 is perhaps an unusual developing move, bringing out the queenside knight and temporarily blocking some of our own pieces. However, we plan to place the knight on b6 to defend the c4-pawn.} 4...Nd7 5.O-O {White has completed kingside development and castles. We develop the kingside knight to its natural square.} 5...Ngf6 6.Na3 {Instead of activating the queen, White might try to increase the pressure on our pawn with the knight on move 6. Once again, we remove the knight, now forcing White to double their pawns on the a-file!} 6...Bxa3 7.bxa3 {Forced. We get the knight out of d7, 7...Nb6. Now our queen and light-squared bishop have room to develop. Also, we add another defender to the c4-pawn.} 7...Nb6 8.Bb2 {Having captured on a3 with the pawn, White now has room to activate their own bishop on the b2-square. Once we bring the king to safety…} 8...O-O 9.Qc2 {...White develops the queen, putting pressure on the key c4-pawn. But it is defended, for now. With no ...b5 and ...Bb7 idea being possible here, we simply develop our bishop to d7. In the future, we might try to reroute it to a4 and pressure White’s queen. We must not forget about the undefended b7-pawn, as it can become a target in many positions!} 9...Bd7 10.Ng5 {Another approach for White at the beginning of the middlegame is 10.Ng5. Aside from threatening to win the b7-pawn, White is threatening something even more poisonous: Bxf6 followed by Qxh7# is a huge checkmating idea! That’s why 10...Ba4 is the key move to remember in this variation. We move the bishop with tempo, counterattacking White’s queen.} 10...Ba4 11.Qb1 {White moves the queen away, but keeps it on the b1–h7 diagonal, recreating the checkmating threat. Luckily, 11...Nbd7 reinforces the knight on f6. Now if Bxf6 happens, we will recapture with the knight and cover the h7-pawn. We are safe for now…} 11...Nbd7 12.Bxb7 {...And our precise play may cause White to blunder! Bxb7 is a huge mistake. Let’s exploit it! We move the rook out of danger and attack the bishop on b7. Notice that White has three pieces on the open b-file! White should never have opened it.} 12...Rb8 13.Bg2 {White moves the bishop away, but that is not the end of their troubles. Out of nowhere, 13...c3 attacks the pinned bishop on b2! We also clear the c4-square for our knight to reroute to.} 13...c3 14.dxc3 {Having no other choice, White captures.} 14...Ne5 {14...Ne5 proves our whole point. We have neutralized all of White's threats and have huge threats of our own! ...Nc4 and ...Qd2 both pressure the bishop on b2, which is pinned and blocked in by White's own pawn! ...Qd2 even attacks the knight on g5! The evaluation of this position is -6. We are winning!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 1: White Plays Nf3 and Bg2 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/e3a639cd-7afc-4b8f-8f25-446db20c0481/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 9! In this chapter, just like in the previous one, we will cover the English with 1.c4 by White. However, in Chapter 9 we focus on White placing the kingside knight on f3 and often playing g3 on the third move. As we will see, one of our key ideas is capturing on c4 and playing around our c4-pawn. White plays the English Opening. We reply with 1...e6, ready to transpose into the QGD if White plays 2.d4.} 1...e6 2.Nf3 {Instead, with 2.Nf3, White chooses a move order that can transpose into many different openings. We grab space in the center with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.g3 {The Nf3–g3 setup is definitely an interesting idea by White. With the bishop on the long h1–a8 diagonal, White aims for better central control and a generally safer king. However, 3.g3 loses a tempo and allows us to capture on c4! Other moves like 3.e3 or 3.b3 were already covered in Chapter 7. Since White’s bishop is going to g2, it will be harder for White to regain the c4-pawn. We will probably try to hang on to it.} 3...dxc4 4.Bg2 {White continues developing. The position clearly resembles Catalan-like structures. 4...Nd7 is perhaps an unusual developing move, bringing out the queenside knight and temporarily blocking some of our own pieces. However, we plan to place the knight on b6 to defend the c4-pawn.} 4...Nd7 5.O-O {White has completed kingside development and castles. We develop the kingside knight to its natural square.} 5...Ngf6 6.Qc2 {6.Qc2 develops the queen and puts pressure on the currently undefended c4-pawn! And we make use of our early ...Nd7 idea and play 6...Nb6. Our c4-pawn is now defended, and our queen and light-squared bishop have room to develop.} 6...Nb6 7.a4 {White tries to create play on the queenside and kick out our knight… 7...a5 simply prevents a5 from White. The queenside is successfully locked!} 7...a5 8.Ne5 {Here comes Ne5 once again, with an attack on c4. But we know what to do! The queen is active on d4, attacks the knight, and defends c4 at the same time.} 8...Qd4 9.Nf3 {With no better move, White retreats.} 9...Qc5 {In the variation with Ne5 on move 7...Qe4 was recommended, trying to trade queens. That's also fine here, but since White has commited their queenside with a4, ...Qc5 is more active with possible plans of queenside play in the future. Next, we will develop and castle!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 1: White Plays Nf3 and Bg2 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/e3a639cd-7afc-4b8f-8f25-446db20c0481/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 9! In this chapter, just like in the previous one, we will cover the English with 1.c4 by White. However, in Chapter 9 we focus on White placing the kingside knight on f3 and often playing g3 on the third move. As we will see, one of our key ideas is capturing on c4 and playing around our c4-pawn. White plays the English Opening. We reply with 1...e6, ready to transpose into the QGD if White plays 2.d4.} 1...e6 2.Nf3 {Instead, with 2.Nf3, White chooses a move order that can transpose into many different openings. We grab space in the center with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.g3 {The Nf3–g3 setup is definitely an interesting idea by White. With the bishop on the long h1–a8 diagonal, White aims for better central control and a generally safer king. However, 3.g3 loses a tempo and allows us to capture on c4! Other moves like 3.e3 or 3.b3 were already covered in Chapter 7. Since White’s bishop is going to g2, it will be harder for White to regain the c4-pawn. We will probably try to hang on to it.} 3...dxc4 4.Bg2 {White continues developing. The position clearly resembles Catalan-like structures. 4...Nd7 is perhaps an unusual developing move, bringing out the queenside knight and temporarily blocking some of our own pieces. However, we plan to place the knight on b6 to defend the c4-pawn.} 4...Nd7 5.O-O {White has completed kingside development and castles. We develop the kingside knight to its natural square.} 5...Ngf6 6.Qc2 {6.Qc2 develops the queen and puts pressure on the currently undefended c4-pawn! And we make use of our early ...Nd7 idea and play 6...Nb6. Our c4-pawn is now defended, and our queen and light-squared bishop have room to develop.} 6...Nb6 7.Ne5 {White might implement the now familiar Ne5 idea again. By attacking the c4-pawn one more time, White also puts pressure on b7 and makes it harder for us to develop. However, we have 7...Qd4! We suddenly centralize the queen, defend our pawn, and attack the e5-knight at the same time!} 7...Qd4 8.Nf3 {White admits they have no better option and retreats, attacking our queen. Since we want our c4-pawn to survive, we try trading queens with 8...Qe4, as that would remove one of White’s attackers from c4. If White captures, we will have a knight in the center of the board with no problems in the position.} 8...Qe4 9.Qd1 {White passively retreats, but keeps the queens alive.} 9...Be7 {And we simply develop our bishop to e7 and are ready to castle and develop our light-squared bishop, centralize the rooks and enjoy a comfortable position! The computer claims it's equal but we have more activity and space. I would much rather play Black here!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 1: White Plays Nf3 and Bg2 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/e3a639cd-7afc-4b8f-8f25-446db20c0481/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 9! In this chapter, just like in the previous one, we will cover the English with 1.c4 by White. However, in Chapter 9 we focus on White placing the kingside knight on f3 and often playing g3 on the third move. As we will see, one of our key ideas is capturing on c4 and playing around our c4-pawn. White plays the English Opening. We reply with 1...e6, ready to transpose into the QGD if White plays 2.d4.} 1...e6 2.Nf3 {Instead, with 2.Nf3, White chooses a move order that can transpose into many different openings. We grab space in the center with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.g3 {The Nf3–g3 setup is definitely an interesting idea by White. With the bishop on the long h1–a8 diagonal, White aims for better central control and a generally safer king. However, 3.g3 loses a tempo and allows us to capture on c4! Other moves like 3.e3 or 3.b3 were already covered in Chapter 7. Since White’s bishop is going to g2, it will be harder for White to regain the c4-pawn. We will probably try to hang on to it.} 3...dxc4 4.Bg2 {White continues developing. The position clearly resembles Catalan-like structures. 4...Nd7 is perhaps an unusual developing move, bringing out the queenside knight and temporarily blocking some of our own pieces. However, we plan to place the knight on b6 to defend the c4-pawn.} 4...Nd7 5.O-O {White has completed kingside development and castles. We develop the kingside knight to its natural square.} 5...Ngf6 6.Na3 {Instead of activating the queen, White might try to increase the pressure on our pawn with the knight on move 6. Once again, we remove the knight, now forcing White to double their pawns on the a-file!} 6...Bxa3 7.bxa3 {Forced. We get the knight out of d7, 7...Nb6. Now our queen and light-squared bishop have room to develop. Also, we add another defender to the c4-pawn.} 7...Nb6 8.Bb2 {Having captured on a3 with the pawn, White now has room to activate their own bishop on the b2-square. Once we bring the king to safety…} 8...O-O 9.Qc2 {...White develops the queen, putting pressure on the key c4-pawn. But it is defended, for now. With no ...b5 and ...Bb7 idea being possible here, we simply develop our bishop to d7. In the future, we might try to reroute it to a4 and pressure White’s queen. We must not forget about the undefended b7-pawn, as it can become a target in many positions!} 9...Bd7 10.Ne5 {One of White’s ideas is 10.Ne5. White is targeting both of our pawns on c4 and b7 at the same time! However, this is a slight inaccuracy. Let’s continue with our idea of hitting the queen on the c2-square! As we can see after 10...Ba4, White’s d2-pawn is weak, and pushing the queen away from it may allow us to capture it.} 10...Ba4 11.Qc3 {However, White plays 11.Qc3, lining up with the bishop on b2 and still defending the pawn. Our b7-pawn is still hanging! 11...c6 blocks the bishop from capturing on b7.} 11...c6 12.Nxc4 {However, should White choose to capture on c4... We trade on c4.} 12...Nxc4 13.Qxc4 {White is forced to recapture.} 13...Bb5 {And this is the key idea to remember! Even though we lost the c4-pawn, we have 13...Bb5, attacking the queen and targeting the e2-pawn behind her! The only way for White to defend against that is to trade on f6 and play Qe4. However, with the two weak pawns on the queenside against our three, that might not be the best idea!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 1: White Plays Nf3 and Bg2 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/e3a639cd-7afc-4b8f-8f25-446db20c0481/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 9! In this chapter, just like in the previous one, we will cover the English with 1.c4 by White. However, in Chapter 9 we focus on White placing the kingside knight on f3 and often playing g3 on the third move. As we will see, one of our key ideas is capturing on c4 and playing around our c4-pawn. White plays the English Opening. We reply with 1...e6, ready to transpose into the QGD if White plays 2.d4.} 1...e6 2.Nf3 {Instead, with 2.Nf3, White chooses a move order that can transpose into many different openings. We grab space in the center with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.g3 {The Nf3–g3 setup is definitely an interesting idea by White. With the bishop on the long h1–a8 diagonal, White aims for better central control and a generally safer king. However, 3.g3 loses a tempo and allows us to capture on c4! Other moves like 3.e3 or 3.b3 were already covered in Chapter 7. Since White’s bishop is going to g2, it will be harder for White to regain the c4-pawn. We will probably try to hang on to it.} 3...dxc4 4.Bg2 {White continues developing. The position clearly resembles Catalan-like structures. 4...Nd7 is perhaps an unusual developing move, bringing out the queenside knight and temporarily blocking some of our own pieces. However, we plan to place the knight on b6 to defend the c4-pawn.} 4...Nd7 5.Qc2 {Another approach for White is 5.Qc2, developing the queen to a safer square and targeting our c4-pawn. This is where ...Nd7 becomes useful, as we now defend c4 from b6.} 5...Nb6 6.a4 {As in the main line, White tries to kick our knight out. We won’t let them, 6...a5!} 6...a5 7.Na3 {This line is similar to the main line. White attacks the c4-pawn with one more piece, making it hard for us to defend. That’s why we remove the knight!} 7...Bxa3 8.Rxa3 {Naturally, White recaptures with the rook. After 8...Nf6 we have developed the kingside and are ready to castle. In the future, we might centralize the knight on f6 and possibly jump to the dangerous b4-square.} 8...Nf6 9.O-O {White castles...} 9...Nfd5 {...and we do just that! We'll castle, try to place the knight on the b4-square and deal with White's d3- or b3-pawn breaks, probably by capturing!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 1: White Plays Nf3 and Bg2 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/e3a639cd-7afc-4b8f-8f25-446db20c0481/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 9! In this chapter, just like in the previous one, we will cover the English with 1.c4 by White. However, in Chapter 9 we focus on White placing the kingside knight on f3 and often playing g3 on the third move. As we will see, one of our key ideas is capturing on c4 and playing around our c4-pawn. White plays the English Opening. We reply with 1...e6, ready to transpose into the QGD if White plays 2.d4.} 1...e6 2.Nf3 {Instead, with 2.Nf3, White chooses a move order that can transpose into many different openings. We grab space in the center with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.g3 {The Nf3–g3 setup is definitely an interesting idea by White. With the bishop on the long h1–a8 diagonal, White aims for better central control and a generally safer king. However, 3.g3 loses a tempo and allows us to capture on c4! Other moves like 3.e3 or 3.b3 were already covered in Chapter 7. Since White’s bishop is going to g2, it will be harder for White to regain the c4-pawn. We will probably try to hang on to it.} 3...dxc4 4.Bg2 {White continues developing. The position clearly resembles Catalan-like structures. 4...Nd7 is perhaps an unusual developing move, bringing out the queenside knight and temporarily blocking some of our own pieces. However, we plan to place the knight on b6 to defend the c4-pawn.} 4...Nd7 5.O-O {White has completed kingside development and castles. We develop the kingside knight to its natural square.} 5...Ngf6 6.Qc2 {6.Qc2 develops the queen and puts pressure on the currently undefended c4-pawn! And we make use of our early ...Nd7 idea and play 6...Nb6. Our c4-pawn is now defended, and our queen and light-squared bishop have room to develop.} 6...Nb6 7.a4 {White tries to create play on the queenside and kick out our knight… 7...a5 simply prevents a5 from White. The queenside is successfully locked!} 7...a5 8.Rd1 {8.Rd1 places the rook on the central d-file with ideas of going for the d3-break in the future. We develop with 8...Bd6 and block any potential pins from White on the d-file later on. Since White hasn’t made any serious threats yet and has little control of the center, we might decide to take some central space ourselves!} 8...Bd6 9.Na3 {However, White targets the c4-pawn with the now well-known 9.Na3. As we don’t have a good way of defending c4 in this variation, we choose to grab space with 9...e5 instead. Now our light-squared bishop can come to life!} 9...e5 10.Nxc4 {White finally takes the c4-pawn. Since we don’t want White to trade for our bishop on d6, we capture on c4.} 10...Nxc4 11.Qxc4 {The queen recaptures....} 11...O-O {...And we castle, with an equal position. Even though White has regained the pawn, they have let us develop and grab some space in the process! The queen on c4 can easily become a target for moves like ...Be6. Also, we will develop our queen, move our rooks to the center and battle!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 1: White Plays Nf3 and Bg2 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/e3a639cd-7afc-4b8f-8f25-446db20c0481/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 9! In this chapter, just like in the previous one, we will cover the English with 1.c4 by White. However, in Chapter 9 we focus on White placing the kingside knight on f3 and often playing g3 on the third move. As we will see, one of our key ideas is capturing on c4 and playing around our c4-pawn. White plays the English Opening. We reply with 1...e6, ready to transpose into the QGD if White plays 2.d4.} 1...e6 2.Nf3 {Instead, with 2.Nf3, White chooses a move order that can transpose into many different openings. We grab space in the center with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.g3 {The Nf3–g3 setup is definitely an interesting idea by White. With the bishop on the long h1–a8 diagonal, White aims for better central control and a generally safer king. However, 3.g3 loses a tempo and allows us to capture on c4! Other moves like 3.e3 or 3.b3 were already covered in Chapter 7. Since White’s bishop is going to g2, it will be harder for White to regain the c4-pawn. We will probably try to hang on to it.} 3...dxc4 4.Bg2 {White continues developing. The position clearly resembles Catalan-like structures. 4...Nd7 is perhaps an unusual developing move, bringing out the queenside knight and temporarily blocking some of our own pieces. However, we plan to place the knight on b6 to defend the c4-pawn.} 4...Nd7 5.O-O {White has completed kingside development and castles. We develop the kingside knight to its natural square.} 5...Ngf6 6.Qc2 {6.Qc2 develops the queen and puts pressure on the currently undefended c4-pawn! And we make use of our early ...Nd7 idea and play 6...Nb6. Our c4-pawn is now defended, and our queen and light-squared bishop have room to develop.} 6...Nb6 7.a4 {White tries to create play on the queenside and kick out our knight… 7...a5 simply prevents a5 from White. The queenside is successfully locked!} 7...a5 8.Na3 {The knight is developed to the edge of the board, and now two pieces are pressuring our c4-pawn. It’s not easy to defend, so… We remove the knight! As we can see, the c4-pawn is a critical pawn in the position, and we should do everything in our power to keep it alive, as it greatly restricts White’s development!} 8...Bxa3 9.Rxa3 {The rook recaptures, as bxa3 would only double White’s pawns and ruin their queenside pawn structure. Also, the rook supports the d3-square if White wants to challenge our c4-pawn with d3. Since White has committed with a4, 9...Nfd5 centralizes our knight and threatens to jump to b4, which would be an excellent outpost!} 9...Nfd5 10.d3 {And White has had enough of our c4-pawn! 10.d3, although a simple move, changes a lot. Now we are left with very few options… The most logical one is simply to capture, as it was very hard to defend c4!} 10...cxd3 11.Rxd3 {This is the other point of the rook lift to a3. White now activates the rook on the central d-file and is pinning our knight to our queen!} 11...Qe7 {11...Qe7 simply moves the queen out of the way and threatens our knight to come to b4 again. White has already castled but needs to deal with our threats and develop the dark-squared bishop. On the other hand, we are yet to castle and figure out the development of our own light-squared bishop. One of the plans could be to go for a quick ...f6 and ...e5, opening the diagonal for the bishop!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 1: White Plays Nf3 and Bg2 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/e3a639cd-7afc-4b8f-8f25-446db20c0481/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 9! In this chapter, just like in the previous one, we will cover the English with 1.c4 by White. However, in Chapter 9 we focus on White placing the kingside knight on f3 and often playing g3 on the third move. As we will see, one of our key ideas is capturing on c4 and playing around our c4-pawn. White plays the English Opening. We reply with 1...e6, ready to transpose into the QGD if White plays 2.d4.} 1...e6 2.Nf3 {Instead, with 2.Nf3, White chooses a move order that can transpose into many different openings. We grab space in the center with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.g3 {The Nf3–g3 setup is definitely an interesting idea by White. With the bishop on the long h1–a8 diagonal, White aims for better central control and a generally safer king. However, 3.g3 loses a tempo and allows us to capture on c4! Other moves like 3.e3 or 3.b3 were already covered in Chapter 7. Since White’s bishop is going to g2, it will be harder for White to regain the c4-pawn. We will probably try to hang on to it.} 3...dxc4 4.Bg2 {White continues developing. The position clearly resembles Catalan-like structures. 4...Nd7 is perhaps an unusual developing move, bringing out the queenside knight and temporarily blocking some of our own pieces. However, we plan to place the knight on b6 to defend the c4-pawn.} 4...Nd7 5.Qa4 {Instead of going for the safer route and castling, White chooses a more aggressive approach. 5.Qa4 pins our knight to our king, making ...Nb6 impossible, and attacks c4 at the same time. It’s hard to defend c4 now… Although a calm-looking move, 5...a6 prepares ...b5 in the future, defending the c4-pawn. If White captures now and we push ...b5, that would actually speed up our queenside development, as we’ll soon see.} 5...a6 6.Nc3 {After 6.Nc3, 6...b5 would be a blunder! The knight could simply capture, and 7...axb5 would leave our rook hanging. Whenever we are trying to play ...b5, we must always look out for tactics like this, especially with our rook being undefended and the White bishop controlling this dangerous diagonal! So we just continue developing instead, 6...Nf6.} 6...Nf6 7.O-O {Before doing anything else, White brings the king to safety. We complete kingside development, 7...Be7, and are ready to castle.} 7...Be7 8.Qxc4 {But as soon as White finally captures the c4-pawn...} 8...b5 {...Here comes 8...b5, with tempo on the queen! Next, we will play ...Bb7 and castle. In the future, we might even consider queenside expansion with ...b4 and ...a5. The position is sharp but equal!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 1: White Plays Nf3 and Bg2 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/e3a639cd-7afc-4b8f-8f25-446db20c0481/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 9! In this chapter, just like in the previous one, we will cover the English with 1.c4 by White. However, in Chapter 9 we focus on White placing the kingside knight on f3 and often playing g3 on the third move. As we will see, one of our key ideas is capturing on c4 and playing around our c4-pawn. White plays the English Opening. We reply with 1...e6, ready to transpose into the QGD if White plays 2.d4.} 1...e6 2.Nf3 {Instead, with 2.Nf3, White chooses a move order that can transpose into many different openings. We grab space in the center with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.g3 {The Nf3–g3 setup is definitely an interesting idea by White. With the bishop on the long h1–a8 diagonal, White aims for better central control and a generally safer king. However, 3.g3 loses a tempo and allows us to capture on c4! Other moves like 3.e3 or 3.b3 were already covered in Chapter 7. Since White’s bishop is going to g2, it will be harder for White to regain the c4-pawn. We will probably try to hang on to it.} 3...dxc4 4.Bg2 {White continues developing. The position clearly resembles Catalan-like structures. 4...Nd7 is perhaps an unusual developing move, bringing out the queenside knight and temporarily blocking some of our own pieces. However, we plan to place the knight on b6 to defend the c4-pawn.} 4...Nd7 5.O-O {White has completed kingside development and castles. We develop the kingside knight to its natural square.} 5...Ngf6 6.Qa4 {Having castled, White might choose to bring out the queen now, with similar ideas to what we saw in the 5.Qa4 variation. In this line, White chooses to capture immediately before moving the knight to c3. We prepare ...b5 with 6...a6.} 6...a6 7.Qxc4 {The pawn falls… But 7...b5 comes with tempo again, allowing us to develop the bishop on the next move.} 7...b5 8.Qc2 {The queen moves out of the way.} 8...Bb7 {And we activate our bishop on b7. Next, we will develop the dark-squared bishop, castle, and possibly play on the queenside with ...Rc8 and ...c5.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 2: Other 4th Moves / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/710d9d78-3c45-4761-9aa4-152ecefc55ed/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 9! In this study, we will go over the 4th-move deviations by White, such as 4.Qa4+, 4.Nc3 and 4.Na3. More often than not, White will opt for the 4.Bg2 approach, but it’s important to know these lines as well! White opens with 1.c4. 1...e6 signals that we are prepared for QGD! But White has other plans…} 1...e6 2.Nf3 {Instead of the QGD, White develops the knight to f3. As we know, 2.Nf3 is extremely flexible, leaving White with many options to choose from in the future. We move forward with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.g3 {The move 3.g3 is the traditional English approach by White, delaying d4. As in the last study, we know what to do! We take on c4, with hopes of ...a6 and ...b5 in the next few moves. White now has three main options.} 3...dxc4 4.Qa4+ {The queen activates to the edge of the board and forks our king and pawn. Rather than blocking with the bishop, we block with the knight. The knight on d7 will serve as great reinforcement for the other knight on f6, since we are planning to develop our light-squared bishop to b7 anyway, as you will see.} 4...Nd7 5.Qxc4 {Naturally, the queen captures. And here is our main 5...a6 idea! As we already know, we want to play ...b5 with tempo on the queen, followed by ...Bb7.} 5...a6 6.Bg2 {White opts for the calmer approach, finishing kingside development and getting ready to castle. We move forward with our plan. Don’t forget to develop the bishop immediately after playing 6...b5, as the rook on a8 is vulnerable!} 6...b5 7.Qc2 {The queen moves out of the way. And we finally develop the bishop to b7. In the near future, we might be looking to expand on the queenside with moves like ...c5.} 7...Bb7 8.O-O {Once White castles… We simply develop the kingside knight to f6. However, that gives White time to challenge our queenside!} 8...Ngf6 9.a4 {White is trying to undermine our queenside pawn structure! But we just continue with our plan...} 9...c5 {We expand on the queenside. The b5-pawn is nicely protected by the a6-pawn, and we wouldn't mind a rook trade on the a-file should it open up. We have excellent play on the queenside and might follow it up with moves like ...Qb6. Of course, we will develop with ...Be7 and ...O-O first.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 2: Other 4th Moves / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/710d9d78-3c45-4761-9aa4-152ecefc55ed/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 9! In this study, we will go over the 4th-move deviations by White, such as 4.Qa4+, 4.Nc3 and 4.Na3. More often than not, White will opt for the 4.Bg2 approach, but it’s important to know these lines as well! White opens with 1.c4. 1...e6 signals that we are prepared for QGD! But White has other plans…} 1...e6 2.Nf3 {Instead of the QGD, White develops the knight to f3. As we know, 2.Nf3 is extremely flexible, leaving White with many options to choose from in the future. We move forward with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.g3 {The move 3.g3 is the traditional English approach by White, delaying d4. As in the last study, we know what to do! We take on c4, with hopes of ...a6 and ...b5 in the next few moves. White now has three main options.} 3...dxc4 4.Qa4+ {The queen activates to the edge of the board and forks our king and pawn. Rather than blocking with the bishop, we block with the knight. The knight on d7 will serve as great reinforcement for the other knight on f6, since we are planning to develop our light-squared bishop to b7 anyway, as you will see.} 4...Nd7 5.Qxc4 {Naturally, the queen captures. And here is our main 5...a6 idea! As we already know, we want to play ...b5 with tempo on the queen, followed by ...Bb7.} 5...a6 6.Bg2 {White opts for the calmer approach, finishing kingside development and getting ready to castle. We move forward with our plan. Don’t forget to develop the bishop immediately after playing 6...b5, as the rook on a8 is vulnerable!} 6...b5 7.Qc2 {The queen moves out of the way. And we finally develop the bishop to b7. In the near future, we might be looking to expand on the queenside with moves like ...c5.} 7...Bb7 8.O-O {Once White castles… We simply develop the kingside knight to f6. However, that gives White time to challenge our queenside!} 8...Ngf6 9.b3 {White could also choose to play 9.b3 and develop the bishop to the b2-square. After 9...Be7 our plan remains the same: expand on the queenside, develop and castle! Again, in this line we can specifically consider putting the rook on the c-file, as White has not played a4.} 9...Be7 10.Bb2 {White will surely continue with Bb2.} 10...c5 {We can take a moment to expand on the c-file because 10...c5 is sufficiently supported. We'll continue with ...Qb6 and ...0-0. Our rooks will most likely have to be centralized in open or semi-open files like the d- and c-files.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 2: Other 4th Moves / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/710d9d78-3c45-4761-9aa4-152ecefc55ed/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 9! In this study, we will go over the 4th-move deviations by White, such as 4.Qa4+, 4.Nc3 and 4.Na3. More often than not, White will opt for the 4.Bg2 approach, but it’s important to know these lines as well! White opens with 1.c4. 1...e6 signals that we are prepared for QGD! But White has other plans…} 1...e6 2.Nf3 {Instead of the QGD, White develops the knight to f3. As we know, 2.Nf3 is extremely flexible, leaving White with many options to choose from in the future. We move forward with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.g3 {The move 3.g3 is the traditional English approach by White, delaying d4. As in the last study, we know what to do! We take on c4, with hopes of ...a6 and ...b5 in the next few moves. White now has three main options.} 3...dxc4 4.Qa4+ {The queen activates to the edge of the board and forks our king and pawn. Rather than blocking with the bishop, we block with the knight. The knight on d7 will serve as great reinforcement for the other knight on f6, since we are planning to develop our light-squared bishop to b7 anyway, as you will see.} 4...Nd7 5.Qxc4 {Naturally, the queen captures. And here is our main 5...a6 idea! As we already know, we want to play ...b5 with tempo on the queen, followed by ...Bb7.} 5...a6 6.Bg2 {White opts for the calmer approach, finishing kingside development and getting ready to castle. We move forward with our plan. Don’t forget to develop the bishop immediately after playing 6...b5, as the rook on a8 is vulnerable!} 6...b5 7.Qc2 {The queen moves out of the way. And we finally develop the bishop to b7. In the near future, we might be looking to expand on the queenside with moves like ...c5.} 7...Bb7 8.O-O {Once White castles… We simply develop the kingside knight to f6. However, that gives White time to challenge our queenside!} 8...Ngf6 9.d4 {White strikes in the center! But this plays directly into our hands… Now the ...c5 push is even more potent! It challenges White’s undefended d-pawn and threatens to open up the c-file and attack White!} 9...c5 10.Rd1 {The rook defends, with plans of maybe capturing now and pinning our knight to our queen.} 10...Rc8 {And we put our plan in motion! Now the c-pawn is supported by our rook. We would like to capture on d4 as that would open up an attack on White's queen! After that, we'll develop the dark-squared bishop, maybe even to c5, and castle. We are better in this position.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 2: Other 4th Moves / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/710d9d78-3c45-4761-9aa4-152ecefc55ed/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 9! In this study, we will go over the 4th-move deviations by White, such as 4.Qa4+, 4.Nc3 and 4.Na3. More often than not, White will opt for the 4.Bg2 approach, but it’s important to know these lines as well! White opens with 1.c4. 1...e6 signals that we are prepared for QGD! But White has other plans…} 1...e6 2.Nf3 {Instead of the QGD, White develops the knight to f3. As we know, 2.Nf3 is extremely flexible, leaving White with many options to choose from in the future. We move forward with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.g3 {The move 3.g3 is the traditional English approach by White, delaying d4. As in the last study, we know what to do! We take on c4, with hopes of ...a6 and ...b5 in the next few moves. White now has three main options.} 3...dxc4 4.Qa4+ {The queen activates to the edge of the board and forks our king and pawn. Rather than blocking with the bishop, we block with the knight. The knight on d7 will serve as great reinforcement for the other knight on f6, since we are planning to develop our light-squared bishop to b7 anyway, as you will see.} 4...Nd7 5.Qxc4 {Naturally, the queen captures. And here is our main 5...a6 idea! As we already know, we want to play ...b5 with tempo on the queen, followed by ...Bb7.} 5...a6 6.Bg2 {White opts for the calmer approach, finishing kingside development and getting ready to castle. We move forward with our plan. Don’t forget to develop the bishop immediately after playing 6...b5, as the rook on a8 is vulnerable!} 6...b5 7.Qc2 {The queen moves out of the way. And we finally develop the bishop to b7. In the near future, we might be looking to expand on the queenside with moves like ...c5.} 7...Bb7 8.O-O {Once White castles… We simply develop the kingside knight to f6. However, that gives White time to challenge our queenside!} 8...Ngf6 9.d4 {White strikes in the center! But this plays directly into our hands… Now the ...c5 push is even more potent! It challenges White’s undefended d-pawn and threatens to open up the c-file and attack White!} 9...c5 10.dxc5 {If White chooses to capture instead of defending with the rook, this only helps us.} 10...Bxc5 {We've now activated the bishop and are ready to castle! Apart from that, the rook is coming to c8, our bishops are very active, and the queen is ready to jump into the game! Who wouldn't like this position?} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 2: Other 4th Moves / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/710d9d78-3c45-4761-9aa4-152ecefc55ed/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 9! In this study, we will go over the 4th-move deviations by White, such as 4.Qa4+, 4.Nc3 and 4.Na3. More often than not, White will opt for the 4.Bg2 approach, but it’s important to know these lines as well! White opens with 1.c4. 1...e6 signals that we are prepared for QGD! But White has other plans…} 1...e6 2.Nf3 {Instead of the QGD, White develops the knight to f3. As we know, 2.Nf3 is extremely flexible, leaving White with many options to choose from in the future. We move forward with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.g3 {The move 3.g3 is the traditional English approach by White, delaying d4. As in the last study, we know what to do! We take on c4, with hopes of ...a6 and ...b5 in the next few moves. White now has three main options.} 3...dxc4 4.Qa4+ {The queen activates to the edge of the board and forks our king and pawn. Rather than blocking with the bishop, we block with the knight. The knight on d7 will serve as great reinforcement for the other knight on f6, since we are planning to develop our light-squared bishop to b7 anyway, as you will see.} 4...Nd7 5.Qxc4 {Naturally, the queen captures. And here is our main 5...a6 idea! As we already know, we want to play ...b5 with tempo on the queen, followed by ...Bb7.} 5...a6 6.a4 {White plays 6.a4, thinking they’ve stopped us from achieving our ...b5 push! But have they? Absolutely not! Despite 6.a4, we still push on the queenside with similar plans! Let’s see why 6...b5 is possible.} 6...b5 7.Qe4 {Instead of blundering, White moves the queen out of the way to the center of the board and hits our rook on a8.} 7...Rb8 {We simply slide one square, supporting the pawn on b5. Although we lack in development, we'll make use of White bringing out the queen early and develop with tempo! Moves such as ...Ngf6 and ...Bb7 attack the queen and activate our pieces at the same time.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 2: Other 4th Moves / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/710d9d78-3c45-4761-9aa4-152ecefc55ed/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 9! In this study, we will go over the 4th-move deviations by White, such as 4.Qa4+, 4.Nc3 and 4.Na3. More often than not, White will opt for the 4.Bg2 approach, but it’s important to know these lines as well! White opens with 1.c4. 1...e6 signals that we are prepared for QGD! But White has other plans…} 1...e6 2.Nf3 {Instead of the QGD, White develops the knight to f3. As we know, 2.Nf3 is extremely flexible, leaving White with many options to choose from in the future. We move forward with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.g3 {The move 3.g3 is the traditional English approach by White, delaying d4. As in the last study, we know what to do! We take on c4, with hopes of ...a6 and ...b5 in the next few moves. White now has three main options.} 3...dxc4 4.Qa4+ {The queen activates to the edge of the board and forks our king and pawn. Rather than blocking with the bishop, we block with the knight. The knight on d7 will serve as great reinforcement for the other knight on f6, since we are planning to develop our light-squared bishop to b7 anyway, as you will see.} 4...Nd7 5.Qxc4 {Naturally, the queen captures. And here is our main 5...a6 idea! As we already know, we want to play ...b5 with tempo on the queen, followed by ...Bb7.} 5...a6 6.a4 {White plays 6.a4, thinking they’ve stopped us from achieving our ...b5 push! But have they? Absolutely not! Despite 6.a4, we still push on the queenside with similar plans! Let’s see why 6...b5 is possible.} 6...b5 7.axb5 {If White chooses to capture, thinking that's exactly what they wanted, they are losing on the spot!} 7...axb5 {We simply recapture! Although our own rook is hanging, White's queen and rook are attacked at the same time! White can't defend both. Oops!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 2: Other 4th Moves / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/710d9d78-3c45-4761-9aa4-152ecefc55ed/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 9! In this study, we will go over the 4th-move deviations by White, such as 4.Qa4+, 4.Nc3 and 4.Na3. More often than not, White will opt for the 4.Bg2 approach, but it’s important to know these lines as well! White opens with 1.c4. 1...e6 signals that we are prepared for QGD! But White has other plans…} 1...e6 2.Nf3 {Instead of the QGD, White develops the knight to f3. As we know, 2.Nf3 is extremely flexible, leaving White with many options to choose from in the future. We move forward with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.g3 {The move 3.g3 is the traditional English approach by White, delaying d4. As in the last study, we know what to do! We take on c4, with hopes of ...a6 and ...b5 in the next few moves. White now has three main options.} 3...dxc4 4.Na3 {And the third possibility we will analyze in this chapter is 4.Na3. White attacks our c4-pawn from the edge of the board instead of with the queen. As we have seen in the previous study, more often than not we will capture this knight! 4...Bxa3 removes the attacker.} 4...Bxa3 5.Qa4+ {Instead of recapturing immediately and doubling their a-pawns, White brings out the queen and checks! They will probably capture the bishop with the queen next. 5...b5 is a smart move! Even though it seems like we are giving away a pawn with check, Qxb5 would be met by ...c6! The queen would be deflected from the bishop and we would save it.} 5...b5 6.Qxa3 {Instead, White goes ahead with their plan and captures with the queen on a3. Having played ...b5 with tempo, we now have room to develop our queenside bishop to b7!} 6...Bb7 7.Bg2 {White also fianchettos their light-squared bishop. We somehow need to block the queen’s vision of the f8-square in order to castle. The best way to do that would be ...c5, expanding on the queenside at the same time! But we need to support it first with 7...Nd7.} 7...Nd7 8.O-O {Once White castles...} 8...c5 {We move forward with our plan! Now we will finish kingside development and castle. There could be some ...b4 ideas in the future, expanding on the queenside even more, so White has to be careful!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 2: Other 4th Moves / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/710d9d78-3c45-4761-9aa4-152ecefc55ed/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 9! In this study, we will go over the 4th-move deviations by White, such as 4.Qa4+, 4.Nc3 and 4.Na3. More often than not, White will opt for the 4.Bg2 approach, but it’s important to know these lines as well! White opens with 1.c4. 1...e6 signals that we are prepared for QGD! But White has other plans…} 1...e6 2.Nf3 {Instead of the QGD, White develops the knight to f3. As we know, 2.Nf3 is extremely flexible, leaving White with many options to choose from in the future. We move forward with 2...d5.} 2...d5 3.g3 {The move 3.g3 is the traditional English approach by White, delaying d4. As in the last study, we know what to do! We take on c4, with hopes of ...a6 and ...b5 in the next few moves. White now has three main options.} 3...dxc4 4.Nc3 {Another 4th-move idea for White instead of bringing out the queen early is to develop the knight to c3. The knight now covers the b5-square and makes it difficult to push ...b5 right away. However, this approach is more passive and gives us everything we want! That’s why we play the key 4...a6 move! Now ...b5 will be supported by a pawn.} 4...a6 5.Bg2 {With no good way to stop our plan, White continues development. After 5...b5 we have a nice cluster of pawns on the queenside! However, with the light-squared bishop being on g2, there is always danger on the h1–a8 diagonal.} 5...b5 6.Ne5 {That's why White centralizes the knight and puts pressure on our rook on a8.} 6...Ra7 {We simply slide out of danger, with plans of ...Bb7. The position is equal but sharp!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 3: White Plays 2.g3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/f9a49150-488e-4bd9-b32b-9dcde47e4f15/lines"]

1.c4 {In the last study of Chapter 9, we look at the possible 2.g3 move. Instead of the flexible 2.Nf3, White quickly develops the light-squared bishop, controls the h1–a8 diagonal, and prepares to recapture on c4 early. White begins with the English and takes space in the center. We reply with 1...e6, supporting the …d5 push.} 1...e6 2.g3 {Rather than 2.d4 and entering QGD territory, White goes for a classical English setup with 2.g3 and Bg2. We continue with 2...d5.} 2...d5 {We still continue with 2...d5.} 3.Bg2 {White completes the fianchetto and puts pressure on the center. As we’ve learned, we capture on c4 aiming to follow with …a6 to defend the pawn.} 3...dxc4 4.Qa4+ {But White has other plans! 4.Qa4+ checks the king and attacks our c4-pawn. We cannot defend it. We block with 4...Nd7, keeping the option of …Nb6.} 4...Nd7 5.Qxc4 {Once White captures on c4… We continue with our main idea 5…a6, preparing …b5. But we must be careful: since White hasn’t played Nf3, a premature …b5 leaves the a8-rook vulnerable.} 5...a6 6.Nf3 {White may ignore the …b5 idea - or forget that it works, and caslte. Even so, we are safe. We play 6...b5 with tempo on the queen.} 6...b5 7.Qc6 {If the queen attacks our rook on a8… 7...Rb8 steps out of danger and makes …Bb7 come with tempo. If Ne5, then …Ne7 wins for Black because the queen will be chased and eventually distracted from defending the e5-knight.} 7...Rb8 8.O-O {That’s why White often castles. After 8…Bb7, fianchettoing our bishop…} 8...Bb7 9.Qc2 {The queen is forced back. We expand normally with 9...c5 - this is the structure you should expect.} 9...c5 10.d3 {The 10.d3 idea for Bf4 shouldn't be concerning. The rook can just go to c8. We continue with kingside development, 10...Ngf6.} 10...Ngf6 11.Bf4 {The bishop develops and attacks our rook.} 11...Rc8 {And the rook goes opposite the queen. This line isn't that common. You should spend most of your brain power memorizing more popular move orders like the previous studies or stuff like in Chapter 1-2 and the Anti-London. But if you ever do get something like this, the ideas stand. ...Be7, ...0-0, ...Qb6 for queenside control.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 3: White Plays 2.g3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/f9a49150-488e-4bd9-b32b-9dcde47e4f15/lines"]

1.c4 {In the last study of Chapter 9, we look at the possible 2.g3 move. Instead of the flexible 2.Nf3, White quickly develops the light-squared bishop, controls the h1–a8 diagonal, and prepares to recapture on c4 early. White begins with the English and takes space in the center. We reply with 1...e6, supporting the …d5 push.} 1...e6 2.g3 {Rather than 2.d4 and entering QGD territory, White goes for a classical English setup with 2.g3 and Bg2. We continue with 2...d5.} 2...d5 {We still continue with 2...d5.} 3.Bg2 {White completes the fianchetto and puts pressure on the center. As we’ve learned, we capture on c4 aiming to follow with …a6 to defend the pawn.} 3...dxc4 4.Qa4+ {But White has other plans! 4.Qa4+ checks the king and attacks our c4-pawn. We cannot defend it. We block with 4...Nd7, keeping the option of …Nb6.} 4...Nd7 5.Qxc4 {Once White captures on c4… We continue with our main idea 5…a6, preparing …b5. But we must be careful: since White hasn’t played Nf3, a premature …b5 leaves the a8-rook vulnerable.} 5...a6 6.Qc2 {Many grandmasters bring the queen back to avoid …b5 with tempo. Their queen and bishop are well placed, which is why engines like the position for White. I suggest 6…c5, gaining space and controlling d4. If White plays d4, we take.} 6...c5 7.Nf3 {White may also try to just develop the kingside as soon as possible. Without the c-knight, we gain full control over d5 with 7...Ngf6.} 7...Ngf6 8.O-O {Let’s say they castle… Then 8...b5 is very strong.} 8...b5 9.Ne5 {If White tries a discovered attack… We simply block on d5, 9...Nd5.} 9...Nd5 10.Nc6 {Some players may be tempted to continue attacking. But after 10...Qb6 the knight on c6 is completely trapped.} 10...Qb6 11.Bxd5 {White could insert this capture to defend for one more move...} 11...exd5 {But after we recapture, the knight will be lost on the next move. It simply cannot get out.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The English Opening (Part 2) / Study 3: White Plays 2.g3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/c4d12a37-30f6-4e3a-8e06-d8cf12361b11/studies/f9a49150-488e-4bd9-b32b-9dcde47e4f15/lines"]

1.c4 {In the last study of Chapter 9, we look at the possible 2.g3 move. Instead of the flexible 2.Nf3, White quickly develops the light-squared bishop, controls the h1–a8 diagonal, and prepares to recapture on c4 early. White begins with the English and takes space in the center. We reply with 1...e6, supporting the …d5 push.} 1...e6 2.g3 {Rather than 2.d4 and entering QGD territory, White goes for a classical English setup with 2.g3 and Bg2. We continue with 2...d5.} 2...d5 {We still continue with 2...d5.} 3.Bg2 {White completes the fianchetto and puts pressure on the center. As we’ve learned, we capture on c4 aiming to follow with …a6 to defend the pawn.} 3...dxc4 4.Qa4+ {But White has other plans! 4.Qa4+ checks the king and attacks our c4-pawn. We cannot defend it. We block with 4...Nd7, keeping the option of …Nb6.} 4...Nd7 5.Qxc4 {Once White captures on c4… We continue with our main idea 5…a6, preparing …b5. But we must be careful: since White hasn’t played Nf3, a premature …b5 leaves the a8-rook vulnerable.} 5...a6 6.Qc2 {Many grandmasters bring the queen back to avoid …b5 with tempo. Their queen and bishop are well placed, which is why engines like the position for White. I suggest 6…c5, gaining space and controlling d4. If White plays d4, we take.} 6...c5 7.Nc3 {7.Nc3 is best now. The knight controls key squares but cannot jump into them effectively. We develop with 7...Ngf6, controlling e4 and d5.} 7...Ngf6 8.Nf3 {White develops the other knight and prepares to castle. We prepare castling as well, 8...Be7.} 8...Be7 9.O-O {White castles kingside.} 9...O-O {And we'll castle kingside as well. We have ideas of ...Qc7 and ...b5 to activate our pieces on the queenside. We'll go ...Bb7 but we'll always keep in mind that our rook could be attacked with a knight discovery, so we have to be careful.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Steiner Variation (c4 and e4) / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/10276994-7b3f-476a-80fe-8cf4206459af/studies/e348e9e9-9ab0-4515-a895-d23f00bd0709/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 10! We’re going to see what happens if White plays the English, but chooses to play e4 on move 2. This transposes into the French, Steiner Variation. The idea is maximum control of d5. It may not be the best approach, since we can play ...d5 anyway, but we have to be prepared because White can play a gambit. White begins with 1.c4, the English. And we respond with 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.e4 {Now White plays 2.e4, going for maximum control of d5. This is the Steiner Variation of the French. These moves don’t really stop ...d5, since our pawn push is sufficiently supported. I suggest we follow through with 2...d5, the pawn break that’s most natural in our setup.} 2...d5 3.cxd5 {3.cxd5 is the most popular move. White’s idea is that they’re taking our central pawn with a flank pawn. We’ll recapture, of course.} 3...exd5 4.exd5 {The most popular continuation that you should expect is White just trading both pawns on d5. At this point, you’ll want to avoid 4...Qxd5, because 5.Nc3 will come with a tempo. Instead, you should attack the pawn with the knight, 4...Nf6 is our move.} 4...Nf6 5.Bb5+ {5.Bb5+ isn’t the most popular, but it’s very logical because it may give Black some coordination issues. But we’ll just block with the knight on d7. We won’t have any coordination issues because our knight will go to b6, where it will pressure the d5-pawn.} 5...Nbd7 6.Nc3 {White will usually try to make winning back the d-pawn difficult. But we’ll argue that if we get to castle, the pin will be eradicated and we’ll take the d5-pawn in no time. So we prepare castling with 6...Bd6.} 6...Bd6 7.Qe2+ {Hence White may try to continue causing coordination issues by giving another check. But the check doesn’t achieve anything, we can just block with the queen.} 7...Qe7 8.Qxe7+ {White takes. We recapture, and we’re castling next.} 8...Bxe7 9.Nf3 {White may prepare castling themselves. We now castle, with the idea of ...Nb6 to put pressure on the d5-pawn.} 9...O-O 10.Bxd7 {Giving up the bishop to remove our knight is an idea White may have. But this just gives us the bishop pair, allowing us to get the pawn back quite quickly.} 10...Bxd7 11.O-O {If castling...} 11...Bg4 {We can go for the positional threat of doubling the f-pawns and we're getting a rook into d8 soon enough to win the d5-pawn back. Because of the bishop pair, Black has a slight edge in this position.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Steiner Variation (c4 and e4) / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/10276994-7b3f-476a-80fe-8cf4206459af/studies/e348e9e9-9ab0-4515-a895-d23f00bd0709/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 10! We’re going to see what happens if White plays the English, but chooses to play e4 on move 2. This transposes into the French, Steiner Variation. The idea is maximum control of d5. It may not be the best approach, since we can play ...d5 anyway, but we have to be prepared because White can play a gambit. White begins with 1.c4, the English. And we respond with 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.e4 {Now White plays 2.e4, going for maximum control of d5. This is the Steiner Variation of the French. These moves don’t really stop ...d5, since our pawn push is sufficiently supported. I suggest we follow through with 2...d5, the pawn break that’s most natural in our setup.} 2...d5 3.cxd5 {3.cxd5 is the most popular move. White’s idea is that they’re taking our central pawn with a flank pawn. We’ll recapture, of course.} 3...exd5 4.exd5 {The most popular continuation that you should expect is White just trading both pawns on d5. At this point, you’ll want to avoid 4...Qxd5, because 5.Nc3 will come with a tempo. Instead, you should attack the pawn with the knight, 4...Nf6 is our move.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {5.Nc3 is the most popular move. But this just allows us to take on d5. 6.Nxd5 presents no problems, so let’s just focus on other moves.} 5...Nxd5 6.Bc4 {The first one we should see is 6.Bc4. Here, we can attack the c4-bishop by retreating the knight to b6.} 6...Nb6 7.Bb3 {We force the bishop back. And we can develop our other knight to c6, controlling the d4-square.} 7...Nc6 8.Nf3 {White’s most natural attempt of developing and playing d4, 8.Nf3 runs into our next move. We can give a check, 8...Qe7+ and force a piece into e2.} 8...Qe7+ 9.Qe2 {If the queen blocks, Black has more useful moves to make before the queen trade. 9...Bg4, for example, carries the positional threat of doubling the f-pawns.} 9...Bg4 10.Qxe7+ {If White is forced to initiate the queen trade, they will help Black develop.} 10...Bxe7 {After 10...Bxe7, all our pieces are out. We still have the positional threat of capturing on f3. It's hard to stop this plan and it's tough for White to develop. We've got options of castling either kingside or queenside, depending on what White does. ...0-0 may be better if there's pressure on f7, but if Bd1, ...0-0-0.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Steiner Variation (c4 and e4) / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/10276994-7b3f-476a-80fe-8cf4206459af/studies/e348e9e9-9ab0-4515-a895-d23f00bd0709/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 10! We’re going to see what happens if White plays the English, but chooses to play e4 on move 2. This transposes into the French, Steiner Variation. The idea is maximum control of d5. It may not be the best approach, since we can play ...d5 anyway, but we have to be prepared because White can play a gambit. White begins with 1.c4, the English. And we respond with 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.e4 {Now White plays 2.e4, going for maximum control of d5. This is the Steiner Variation of the French. These moves don’t really stop ...d5, since our pawn push is sufficiently supported. I suggest we follow through with 2...d5, the pawn break that’s most natural in our setup.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White can try to gain some space with 3.e5. As we’ll see, this pawn lacks support. Gaining space and controlling d4with 3...c5 is good for us here.} 3...c5 4.cxd5 {White can go for this capture. But going for exchanges isn’t always a good thing. Sometimes, trades help the other side. In this case, the trades help us. We recapture and have two strong pawns controlling the center.} 4...exd5 5.d4 {White can try pushing and supporting e5. But our c-pawn already controls d4. We can add pressure on d4 with 5...Nc6. We don’t need to capture on d4 right away because the structure will collapse on itself, unforced.} 5...Nc6 6.Nf3 {White could develop their knight now, supporting d4 and e5. And we can insert the capture here to misplace the knight on d4.} 6...cxd4 7.Nxd4 {White takes. The knight on d4 no longer defends the e5-pawn, so we can attack it with our queen, 7...Qe7. This is what I meant by misplacing the knight, there will be two attackers on e5.} 7...Qe7 8.Nxc6 {The most natural continuation is for White to trade on c6 and defend e5 momentarily. Once we recapture, we’re attacking e5 and we have fixed our structure, which is now the solid c6–d5 pawn chain.} 8...bxc6 9.f4 {9. f4 defends e5, yet it's not enough to keep things together. The e5-pawn is overextended.} 9...f6 {9...f6 successfully undermines it. ...Nh6 for ...Nf7 will help us add pressure on e5, but White really cannot defend it if not with awkward queen moves. The queen on d4 could be targetted with ...Nh6 to f5. And the queen on e2 could be forced to e5 with ...fxe5. Our c6-d5 structure is hard to undermine, so we'll control the center the entire game.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Steiner Variation (c4 and e4) / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/10276994-7b3f-476a-80fe-8cf4206459af/studies/e348e9e9-9ab0-4515-a895-d23f00bd0709/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 10! We’re going to see what happens if White plays the English, but chooses to play e4 on move 2. This transposes into the French, Steiner Variation. The idea is maximum control of d5. It may not be the best approach, since we can play ...d5 anyway, but we have to be prepared because White can play a gambit. White begins with 1.c4, the English. And we respond with 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.e4 {Now White plays 2.e4, going for maximum control of d5. This is the Steiner Variation of the French. These moves don’t really stop ...d5, since our pawn push is sufficiently supported. I suggest we follow through with 2...d5, the pawn break that’s most natural in our setup.} 2...d5 3.cxd5 {3.cxd5 is the most popular move. White’s idea is that they’re taking our central pawn with a flank pawn. We’ll recapture, of course.} 3...exd5 4.Qb3 {And this is officially the Orthoschnapp Gambit. White is inviting you to take on e4. If you take, their idea can be Bc4 to attack f7. Declining the gambit with 1...c6 is safe. We don’t want to allow White quick development with tempi.} 4...c6 5.exd5 {White usually takes on d5. We recapture, and, as a result, we have a symmetrical pawn structure. Both sides have an isolated queen’s pawn. But our d-pawn controls the c4-square, which prevents Bc4.} 5...cxd5 6.Nc3 {White can develop with a tempo, attacking the pawn on d5. But we can just defend it with our knight.} 6...Nf6 7.d4 {White could play 7.d4 to develop their dark-squared bishop. They also control some key squares with their d4-pawn. But we can attack White’s center with a tempo as well with 7...Nc6. After declining the gambit, Black gets natural development and a rather safe position. Symmetrical positions are often safe.} 7...Nc6 8.Nf3 {White has to defend d4. We should break any potential Bg5 pins with 8...Be7 and get ready to castle.} 8...Be7 9.Bg5 {The 9.Bg5 move is still popular even if it isn’t a pin. The idea is to try to remove the defender of d5. But we can just castle, because if White takes on f6, we recapture with the bishop and the e-file is open. Besides that, the bishop and knight would be attacking d4 in return.} 9...O-O 10.Bxf6 {So just to illustrate that idea… We can recapture and we’re attacking d4. Many players go for this continuation because it looks natural, but trading d-pawns isn’t an equal trade: Black is up in development and has a safer king!} 10...Bxf6 11.Nxd5 {11.Nxd5 is losing because of Black’s better pieces and White’s less safe king. The refutation begins with 11...Qa5+.} 11...Qa5+ 12.Nc3 {And after the knight blocks… We can win the d-pawn back, 12...Nxd4.} 12...Nxd4 13.Nxd4 {White recaptures...} 13...Bxd4 {And the bishop recaptures. The king is in a pretty tough spot in the middle of the board. If 0-0-0, the king is put on the open c-file and moves like ...Be6 to attack the queen exist. If Be2, ...Be6 and ...Rae8 are ideas. Going for the d-pawn isn't a good choice for White.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Steiner Variation (c4 and e4) / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/10276994-7b3f-476a-80fe-8cf4206459af/studies/e348e9e9-9ab0-4515-a895-d23f00bd0709/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 10! We’re going to see what happens if White plays the English, but chooses to play e4 on move 2. This transposes into the French, Steiner Variation. The idea is maximum control of d5. It may not be the best approach, since we can play ...d5 anyway, but we have to be prepared because White can play a gambit. White begins with 1.c4, the English. And we respond with 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.e4 {Now White plays 2.e4, going for maximum control of d5. This is the Steiner Variation of the French. These moves don’t really stop ...d5, since our pawn push is sufficiently supported. I suggest we follow through with 2...d5, the pawn break that’s most natural in our setup.} 2...d5 3.exd5 {White could choose to take with the e-pawn. We will, of course, recapture.} 3...exd5 4.Nf3 {White may also play with 4.Nf3, going for kingside development before anything else. But this neglects the center, which gives us an opportunity. We can prevent White from going d4. If we go 4...d4 ourselves, we control the c3-square, preventing Nc3. Pawn pushes like this are only possible when we’re confident we’ll be able to hold on to the pawn.} 4...d4 5.d3 {After White goes d3 to support their c4-pawn… We can also defend our own pawn with 5...c5. It seems our pawn duo is quite solid in the center and cannot be easily attacked.} 5...c5 6.Be2 {White could continue with their plan of kingside development before anything else. But we’re quite happy with how things have turned out in the center and can continue with 6...Nf6.} 6...Nf6 7.O-O {After White castles, they have completed kingside development. However, sometimes being in a hurry to do something in chess makes you ignore other things that may be going on. In this case, the center. 7...Nc6 supports the central structure and helps control the critical b4-square.} 7...Nc6 8.Re1 {White may put a rook on the e-file, but they don't even have a useful discovered check!} 8...Be7 {Still, we should just prepare castling. We'll play moves like ...h6 to control the g5-square and if Bf4, ...Bd6 to trade bishops. We can even go ...a5 to control the b4-square even further.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Steiner Variation (c4 and e4) / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/10276994-7b3f-476a-80fe-8cf4206459af/studies/e348e9e9-9ab0-4515-a895-d23f00bd0709/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 10! We’re going to see what happens if White plays the English, but chooses to play e4 on move 2. This transposes into the French, Steiner Variation. The idea is maximum control of d5. It may not be the best approach, since we can play ...d5 anyway, but we have to be prepared because White can play a gambit. White begins with 1.c4, the English. And we respond with 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.e4 {Now White plays 2.e4, going for maximum control of d5. This is the Steiner Variation of the French. These moves don’t really stop ...d5, since our pawn push is sufficiently supported. I suggest we follow through with 2...d5, the pawn break that’s most natural in our setup.} 2...d5 3.cxd5 {3.cxd5 is the most popular move. White’s idea is that they’re taking our central pawn with a flank pawn. We’ll recapture, of course.} 3...exd5 4.exd5 {The most popular continuation that you should expect is White just trading both pawns on d5. At this point, you’ll want to avoid 4...Qxd5, because 5.Nc3 will come with a tempo. Instead, you should attack the pawn with the knight, 4...Nf6 is our move.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {5.Nc3 is the most popular move. But this just allows us to take on d5. 6.Nxd5 presents no problems, so let’s just focus on other moves.} 5...Nxd5 6.Qb3 {6.Qb3 attacks the knight as well, however, it places the queen on a vulnerable square. For instance, 6...Nb6 controls the c4-square, which prevents Bc4, and also blocks the queen’s view of b7, which enables ideas like ...Be6.} 6...Nb6 7.Nf3 {White may just continue with development. But our development is much more active, 7...Nc6, with ideas of gaining tempi on White’s pieces. } 7...Nc6 8.Bb5 {If White tries developing actively by creating a pin on one of the knights...} 8...Qe7+ {We can disrupt their coordination with 8...Qe7+. All the options of dealing with this check are unpleasant. Be2 makes the bishop retreat. Ne2 places the knight on a worse square, and moving the king loses castling rights.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Steiner Variation (c4 and e4) / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/10276994-7b3f-476a-80fe-8cf4206459af/studies/e348e9e9-9ab0-4515-a895-d23f00bd0709/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 10! We’re going to see what happens if White plays the English, but chooses to play e4 on move 2. This transposes into the French, Steiner Variation. The idea is maximum control of d5. It may not be the best approach, since we can play ...d5 anyway, but we have to be prepared because White can play a gambit. White begins with 1.c4, the English. And we respond with 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.e4 {Now White plays 2.e4, going for maximum control of d5. This is the Steiner Variation of the French. These moves don’t really stop ...d5, since our pawn push is sufficiently supported. I suggest we follow through with 2...d5, the pawn break that’s most natural in our setup.} 2...d5 3.e5 {White can try to gain some space with 3.e5. As we’ll see, this pawn lacks support. Gaining space and controlling d4with 3...c5 is good for us here.} 3...c5 4.Nf3 {White can develop the kingside knight. Yet we can add control of the d4-square a bit more with 4...Nc6.} 4...Nc6 5.d4 {If d4… We can take the pawn, e5 is now really weak.} 5...cxd4 6.cxd5 {Inserting this capture doesn't help.} 6...Qxd5 {We've got superior piece play and pressure, e5 will surely fall. Our d4-pawn cannot be easily dislodged. White could try trading with Qb3 to defend the e5-pawn. But taking on b3 with ...Bb4+ and ...Bc5 ideas to overprotect d4 is good enough. ...Nge7 for ...Ng6 is an idea to attack e5.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Steiner Variation (c4 and e4) / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/10276994-7b3f-476a-80fe-8cf4206459af/studies/e348e9e9-9ab0-4515-a895-d23f00bd0709/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 10! We’re going to see what happens if White plays the English, but chooses to play e4 on move 2. This transposes into the French, Steiner Variation. The idea is maximum control of d5. It may not be the best approach, since we can play ...d5 anyway, but we have to be prepared because White can play a gambit. White begins with 1.c4, the English. And we respond with 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.e4 {Now White plays 2.e4, going for maximum control of d5. This is the Steiner Variation of the French. These moves don’t really stop ...d5, since our pawn push is sufficiently supported. I suggest we follow through with 2...d5, the pawn break that’s most natural in our setup.} 2...d5 3.exd5 {White could choose to take with the e-pawn. We will, of course, recapture.} 3...exd5 4.d4 {White could try to put their pawn on d4. This is quite sound, White wants to control the center. We can just be solid and defend d5 one more time with our knight, 4...Nf6.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {White could add an attacker, but the pawn is defended by two pieces. If there isn’t anything more productive to do, development is the name of the game: 5...Be7 is our continuation. You’ll notice there is tension between the d5- and c4-pawns. We want to keep the tension until White moves their f1-bishop, to make them move it again to recapture on c4.} 5...Be7 6.Nf3 {White may continue development, delaying the light-squared bishop’s development. But we’ll also delay the capture on c4 and just get castled.} 6...O-O 7.Be2 {When White moves their light-squared bishop, this should be our cue to capture. It's time to take on c4 and make White have to move their bishop again.} 7...dxc4 8.Bxc4 {White now captures. White has lost some time in this operation. And we can pin the f3-knight with 8...Bg4, provoking another bishop move to unpin! Most importantly, there’s pressure on d4.} 8...Bg4 9.Be3 {White could now add a defender to d4. And we can add an attacker, 9...Nc6.} 9...Nc6 10.O-O {White castles...} 10...Qd7 {We're going to be centralizing the rooks. We've got ideas of ...a6 to keep pieces out of b5. ...Bd6 will also improve our bishop's diagonal. We've got a middlegame that's about equal, but we've got some pressure against the IQP.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Steiner Variation (c4 and e4) / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/10276994-7b3f-476a-80fe-8cf4206459af/studies/e348e9e9-9ab0-4515-a895-d23f00bd0709/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 10! We’re going to see what happens if White plays the English, but chooses to play e4 on move 2. This transposes into the French, Steiner Variation. The idea is maximum control of d5. It may not be the best approach, since we can play ...d5 anyway, but we have to be prepared because White can play a gambit. White begins with 1.c4, the English. And we respond with 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.e4 {Now White plays 2.e4, going for maximum control of d5. This is the Steiner Variation of the French. These moves don’t really stop ...d5, since our pawn push is sufficiently supported. I suggest we follow through with 2...d5, the pawn break that’s most natural in our setup.} 2...d5 3.cxd5 {3.cxd5 is the most popular move. White’s idea is that they’re taking our central pawn with a flank pawn. We’ll recapture, of course.} 3...exd5 4.exd5 {The most popular continuation that you should expect is White just trading both pawns on d5. At this point, you’ll want to avoid 4...Qxd5, because 5.Nc3 will come with a tempo. Instead, you should attack the pawn with the knight, 4...Nf6 is our move.} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {5.Nc3 is the most popular move. But this just allows us to take on d5. 6.Nxd5 presents no problems, so let’s just focus on other moves.} 5...Nxd5 6.Qf3 {6.Qf3 is another way of adding an attacker on the d5-knight, but this isn’t particularly good. 6.Qf3 is refuted by a check on e7 with the queen. This will disrupt White’s coordination. If Nge2, the bishop on f1 is blocked and White’s development isn’t promising.} 6...Qe7+ 7.Be2 {The most natural way for White to block runs into more threats. After 7...Nb4 we threaten ...Nc2+.} 7...Nb4 8.Kd1 {If the king is forced to stop ...Nc2, this isn't good news for White.} 8...N8c6 {The other knight can join the party. If Nd5, we trade knights on d5 and attack the queen with ...Be6. At any rate, we want to go ...Be6 and play ...0-0-0 to place our rook opposite the king.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Steiner Variation (c4 and e4) / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/10276994-7b3f-476a-80fe-8cf4206459af/studies/e348e9e9-9ab0-4515-a895-d23f00bd0709/lines"]

1.c4 {Welcome to Chapter 10! We’re going to see what happens if White plays the English, but chooses to play e4 on move 2. This transposes into the French, Steiner Variation. The idea is maximum control of d5. It may not be the best approach, since we can play ...d5 anyway, but we have to be prepared because White can play a gambit. White begins with 1.c4, the English. And we respond with 1...e6.} 1...e6 2.e4 {Now White plays 2.e4, going for maximum control of d5. This is the Steiner Variation of the French. These moves don’t really stop ...d5, since our pawn push is sufficiently supported. I suggest we follow through with 2...d5, the pawn break that’s most natural in our setup.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {3.Nc3 is a bad move. It also neglects the d4-square. We can push our pawn up to d4 with tempo.} 3...d4 4.Nce2 {There are no good squares for the knight, but e2 is the lesser evil. Still, on e2 it blocks the f1-bishop and really just clutters the White position. We can support the d4-pawn with 4...e5.} 4...e5 5.d3 {5.d3 is the most popular move. But we’ve got 5...Bb4+. White will have to block with their bishop.} 5...Bb4+ 6.Bd2 {And once they block… Our idea is to defend the bishop with the a-pawn, going for complete dark-square control.} 6...a5 7.a3 {If White wants to pressure the bishop… We’ll just take on d2. Black’s position is solid because we’ll go for a dark-square complex, placing all our pawns on dark squares. The dark-squared bishop was White’s good bishop.} 7...Bxd2+ 8.Qxd2 {Once White recaptures, the queen won’t be so relevant on the dark squares, so it will most likely have to move again. With 8...f6 we “connect four”. The d4-pawn that spearheads the structure is super powerful.} 8...f6 9.f4 {Undermining the pawn chain with 9.f4 is not an easy task.} 9...Nh6 {We have ideas of ...Ng4. The queenside knight's journey will be ...Nd7 to c5. The queen will also operate on the safe dark squares with ...Qd6. We'll castle kingside and enjoy our stronger central control and superior pawn chain.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: White Plays The Reti (1.Nf3) / Study 1: Reverse London / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/3f0e7f37-7c9b-4aaf-89f2-68f345170185/studies/c31ac611-6ff4-4889-98da-a457557f3848/lines"]

1.Nf3 {Greetings! Welcome to the very last chapter of our QGD course for Black, Chapter 11! In this chapter, we will cover the lines after 1.Nf3, the Réti Opening by White. The first study deals with White developing their kingside before grabbing space in the center, which allows us to play 3...Bf5, the so-called Reverse London. Instead of placing a pawn in the center, White opts for 1.Nf3, the third most popular opening move in chess. White doesn’t want to commit to any specific opening yet and can transpose to many different playing styles on move 2. We respond naturally with 1...d5, putting a pawn in the center.} 1...d5 2.g3 {White continues with 2.g3, delaying the moves d4 and c4, but developing the kingside quickly. We’ll develop naturally, 2...Nf6.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg2 {White has developed rapidly and is already prepared to castle. However, having placed no pawns in the center of the board, White allows us to develop our bishop before playing ...e6! The bishop will be very active on the f5-square, instead of being blocked in by our own pawns.} 3...Bf5 4.c4 {White creates tension in the center! We have to defend the d5-pawn. 4...e6 is the right move, natural and solid, supporting d5. We also open up the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop.} 4...e6 5.Qb3 {5.Qb3 by White activates the queen and puts pressure on the weak points in our position. 5...Qc8 is an important move to remember! Although we relinquish some control of the d5-square, 5...Qc8 is the best defensive move against the pressure on b7.} 5...Qc8 6.O-O {Another line worth exploring is 6.O-O by White. Instead of capturing right away, they bring the king to safety first. We reinforce the center with 6...c6.} 6...c6 7.Nc3 {A normal developing move, pressuring the center. We develop ourselves, 7...Nbd7.} 7...Nbd7 8.d4 {Now White strikes in the center! However, that loosens up the e4- and c4-squares, which we can use to our advantage soon. We play 8...h6 to control the g5-square.} 8...h6 9.Bf4 {White takes control of the h2–c7 diagonal. The bishop on f4 now covers some important dark squares in our position. Since developing to d6 is impossible now, we develop the bishop to e7, ready to castle.} 9...Be7 10.Rac1 {Since our queen is on c8, White wants to capture on d5 and open up the c-file for the rook. Before going to battle, we castle…} 10...O-O 11.cxd5 {...and White puts their plan in motion with cxd5. However, let’s not allow them to open the c-file! We capture with the e-pawn instead, keeping it closed and maintaining a grip on the e4-square. Also, we might have ideas of placing our knight on the c4-square, which we also control.} 11...exd5 12.Ne5 {Having their own control of the e5-square, White jumps there with the knight, grabbing even more space. Instead of trading on White’s terms, we rotate our knight to the queenside, 12...Nb6 is our move.} 12...Nb6 13.a4 {13.a4 expands on the queenside and tries to kick our knight out. Before White achieves that, we support the c4-square one more time with 13...Be6. Our knight will be ready to jump there!} 13...Be6 14.a5 {The a-pawn still attacks our knight and grabs more space on the queenside...} 14...Nc4 {...But we go 14...Nc4, maintaining a solid position and attacking the a5-pawn. The position is balanced, but could become quite sharp, with possibility of a knight trade happening at any moment!} *

[Event "Chapter 11: White Plays The Reti (1.Nf3) / Study 1: Reverse London / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/3f0e7f37-7c9b-4aaf-89f2-68f345170185/studies/c31ac611-6ff4-4889-98da-a457557f3848/lines"]

1.Nf3 {Greetings! Welcome to the very last chapter of our QGD course for Black, Chapter 11! In this chapter, we will cover the lines after 1.Nf3, the Réti Opening by White. The first study deals with White developing their kingside before grabbing space in the center, which allows us to play 3...Bf5, the so-called Reverse London. Instead of placing a pawn in the center, White opts for 1.Nf3, the third most popular opening move in chess. White doesn’t want to commit to any specific opening yet and can transpose to many different playing styles on move 2. We respond naturally with 1...d5, putting a pawn in the center.} 1...d5 2.g3 {White continues with 2.g3, delaying the moves d4 and c4, but developing the kingside quickly. We’ll develop naturally, 2...Nf6.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg2 {White has developed rapidly and is already prepared to castle. However, having placed no pawns in the center of the board, White allows us to develop our bishop before playing ...e6! The bishop will be very active on the f5-square, instead of being blocked in by our own pawns.} 3...Bf5 4.O-O {White has chosen the calmer route and castles without creating tension in the center. This simply poses no problems for us; we will open up the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop, develop the other knight, sometimes be even more solid with ...c6, and castle! Just like 3.e3 or 4.e3 is played after Bf4 in the London, here we play 4...e6, connecting the pawns after taking the bishop out.} 4...e6 5.d3 {5.d3 is the most popular move and the one worth preparing against. In the London, the bishop usually goes to d3 and aims at h7. But when there’s a pawn on g6, the bishop goes to e2. In the Reverse London, we would love for our dark-squared bishop to have a long diagonal as well. But White has a pawn on g3, so there’s no use playing ...Bd6, we instead play 5...Be7.} 5...Be7 6.c4 {White could undermine our d5-pawn with the move c4. With 6...c6, we create the strong “diamond” pawn structure.} 6...c6 7.Qb3 {It’s also quite possible that White doesn’t insert the trade on d5. Instead, they could just go after the b7-pawn. Our antidote is the same: we defend with 7...Qc8 because we want to avoid getting attacked by the dark-squared bishop.} 7...Qc8 8.Nc3 {White will usually develop. And many times we have this move, 8...Na6, to attack the queen.} 8...Na6 9.cxd5 {If White captures...} 9...exd5 {This is a direct transposition to 6. c4 and 7. cxd5.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: White Plays The Reti (1.Nf3) / Study 1: Reverse London / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/3f0e7f37-7c9b-4aaf-89f2-68f345170185/studies/c31ac611-6ff4-4889-98da-a457557f3848/lines"]

1.Nf3 {Greetings! Welcome to the very last chapter of our QGD course for Black, Chapter 11! In this chapter, we will cover the lines after 1.Nf3, the Réti Opening by White. The first study deals with White developing their kingside before grabbing space in the center, which allows us to play 3...Bf5, the so-called Reverse London. Instead of placing a pawn in the center, White opts for 1.Nf3, the third most popular opening move in chess. White doesn’t want to commit to any specific opening yet and can transpose to many different playing styles on move 2. We respond naturally with 1...d5, putting a pawn in the center.} 1...d5 2.g3 {White continues with 2.g3, delaying the moves d4 and c4, but developing the kingside quickly. We’ll develop naturally, 2...Nf6.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg2 {White has developed rapidly and is already prepared to castle. However, having placed no pawns in the center of the board, White allows us to develop our bishop before playing ...e6! The bishop will be very active on the f5-square, instead of being blocked in by our own pawns.} 3...Bf5 4.O-O {White has chosen the calmer route and castles without creating tension in the center. This simply poses no problems for us; we will open up the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop, develop the other knight, sometimes be even more solid with ...c6, and castle! Just like 3.e3 or 4.e3 is played after Bf4 in the London, here we play 4...e6, connecting the pawns after taking the bishop out.} 4...e6 5.d3 {5.d3 is the most popular move and the one worth preparing against. In the London, the bishop usually goes to d3 and aims at h7. But when there’s a pawn on g6, the bishop goes to e2. In the Reverse London, we would love for our dark-squared bishop to have a long diagonal as well. But White has a pawn on g3, so there’s no use playing ...Bd6, we instead play 5...Be7.} 5...Be7 6.c4 {White could undermine our d5-pawn with the move c4. With 6...c6, we create the strong “diamond” pawn structure.} 6...c6 7.Qb3 {It’s also quite possible that White doesn’t insert the trade on d5. Instead, they could just go after the b7-pawn. Our antidote is the same: we defend with 7...Qc8 because we want to avoid getting attacked by the dark-squared bishop.} 7...Qc8 8.Nc3 {White will usually develop. And many times we have this move, 8...Na6, to attack the queen.} 8...Na6 9.Nh4 {Some people will go for this positional approach and hunt your bishop. We can therefore push the pawn and attack the c3-knight. The knight will have to spend at least a couple of moves before becoming relevant again.} 9...d4 10.Nxf5 {Even though White can insert this capture… We just recapture, and our f-pawn controls the e4-square. White still has to move the knight away.} 10...exf5 11.Na4 {If the knight goes to the rim...} 11...Nc7 {Rest assured, it won't be a good piece on a4. We've got ...Ne6 to support the d4-pawn and control the c5-square even more. ...0-0 is also coming and our pieces have more natural squares. The g2-bishop is blunted by our c6-b7 pawn chain.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: White Plays The Reti (1.Nf3) / Study 1: Reverse London / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/3f0e7f37-7c9b-4aaf-89f2-68f345170185/studies/c31ac611-6ff4-4889-98da-a457557f3848/lines"]

1.Nf3 {Greetings! Welcome to the very last chapter of our QGD course for Black, Chapter 11! In this chapter, we will cover the lines after 1.Nf3, the Réti Opening by White. The first study deals with White developing their kingside before grabbing space in the center, which allows us to play 3...Bf5, the so-called Reverse London. Instead of placing a pawn in the center, White opts for 1.Nf3, the third most popular opening move in chess. White doesn’t want to commit to any specific opening yet and can transpose to many different playing styles on move 2. We respond naturally with 1...d5, putting a pawn in the center.} 1...d5 2.g3 {White continues with 2.g3, delaying the moves d4 and c4, but developing the kingside quickly. We’ll develop naturally, 2...Nf6.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg2 {White has developed rapidly and is already prepared to castle. However, having placed no pawns in the center of the board, White allows us to develop our bishop before playing ...e6! The bishop will be very active on the f5-square, instead of being blocked in by our own pawns.} 3...Bf5 4.O-O {White has chosen the calmer route and castles without creating tension in the center. This simply poses no problems for us; we will open up the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop, develop the other knight, sometimes be even more solid with ...c6, and castle! Just like 3.e3 or 4.e3 is played after Bf4 in the London, here we play 4...e6, connecting the pawns after taking the bishop out.} 4...e6 5.d3 {5.d3 is the most popular move and the one worth preparing against. In the London, the bishop usually goes to d3 and aims at h7. But when there’s a pawn on g6, the bishop goes to e2. In the Reverse London, we would love for our dark-squared bishop to have a long diagonal as well. But White has a pawn on g3, so there’s no use playing ...Bd6, we instead play 5...Be7.} 5...Be7 6.Nbd2 {6.Nbd2 is the most common idea after 5.d3. The point is to support the e4- and c4-pawn breaks. Here I recommend that, before castling, we create a safe square for our bishop with 6...h6. The light-squared bishop is our “London bishop”, and we should take care of it as much as we can.} 6...h6 7.Re1 {This is a natural move designed to support the e4-pawn break. Realistically, we cannot stop e4 from being played. So kingside castling and simple development is the best strategy.} 7...O-O 8.e4 {After 8.e4, we don’t take the pawn. Rather, we use that h7-square we prepared for our bishop.} 8...Bh7 9.e5 {Many players get excited and think that 9.e5, gaining space and kicking the knight out of f6, is a good move. But e5 is usually only good when White has attacking chances on the kingside. Here, it’s actually Black who is attacking! We escape danger with 9...Nfd7.} 9...Nfd7 10.d4 {The center is overextended. The pawns have advanced too much and White will not be able to hold it together. After 10...c5 and with ...Nc6 coming, the center will be attacked and undermined.} 10...c5 11.c3 {White can “connect four” to defend. 11 ...Nc6 adds another attacker on d4.} 11...Nc6 12.Nb3 {If White defends...} 12...c4 {We can force the knight back. White's attempt to defend their center have allowed us to gain space on the queenside. We'll now attack with ...b5 and ...b4, supported by ...a5 if needed. White doesn't have counterplay on the kingside because our h7-bishop is a strong defender.} *

