1.Nf3 {Greetings! Welcome to the very last chapter of our QGD course for Black, Chapter 11! In this chapter, we will cover the lines after 1.Nf3, the Réti Opening by White. The first study deals with White developing their kingside before grabbing space in the center, which allows us to play 3...Bf5, the so-called Reverse London. Instead of placing a pawn in the center, White opts for 1.Nf3, the third most popular opening move in chess. White doesn’t want to commit to any specific opening yet and can transpose to many different playing styles on move 2. We respond naturally with 1...d5, putting a pawn in the center.} 1...d5 2.g3 {White continues with 2.g3, delaying the moves d4 and c4, but developing the kingside quickly. We’ll develop naturally, 2...Nf6.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg2 {White has developed rapidly and is already prepared to castle. However, having placed no pawns in the center of the board, White allows us to develop our bishop before playing ...e6! The bishop will be very active on the f5-square, instead of being blocked in by our own pawns.} 3...Bf5 4.O-O {White has chosen the calmer route and castles without creating tension in the center. This simply poses no problems for us; we will open up the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop, develop the other knight, sometimes be even more solid with ...c6, and castle! Just like 3.e3 or 4.e3 is played after Bf4 in the London, here we play 4...e6, connecting the pawns after taking the bishop out.} 4...e6 5.d3 {5.d3 is the most popular move and the one worth preparing against. In the London, the bishop usually goes to d3 and aims at h7. But when there’s a pawn on g6, the bishop goes to e2. In the Reverse London, we would love for our dark-squared bishop to have a long diagonal as well. But White has a pawn on g3, so there’s no use playing ...Bd6, we instead play 5...Be7.} 5...Be7 6.Nbd2 {6.Nbd2 is the most common idea after 5.d3. The point is to support the e4- and c4-pawn breaks. Here I recommend that, before castling, we create a safe square for our bishop with 6...h6. The light-squared bishop is our “London bishop”, and we should take care of it as much as we can.} 6...h6 7.Re1 {This is a natural move designed to support the e4-pawn break. Realistically, we cannot stop e4 from being played. So kingside castling and simple development is the best strategy.} 7...O-O 8.e4 {After 8.e4, we don’t take the pawn. Rather, we use that h7-square we prepared for our bishop.} 8...Bh7 9.exd5 {If White trades… Recapturing with the pawn keeps a strong pawn in the center. You’ll notice White still has to develop the dark-squared bishop.} 9...exd5 10.Nb3 {They may try something like 10.Nb3.} 10...c5 {But we can use our c-pawn to control the d4-square, and get ready to bring our knight out from b to d7. ...Re8 is also an idea to contest the e-file. Black has good development and space.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: White Plays The Reti (1.Nf3) / Study 1: Reverse London / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/3f0e7f37-7c9b-4aaf-89f2-68f345170185/studies/c31ac611-6ff4-4889-98da-a457557f3848/lines"]

1.Nf3 {Greetings! Welcome to the very last chapter of our QGD course for Black, Chapter 11! In this chapter, we will cover the lines after 1.Nf3, the Réti Opening by White. The first study deals with White developing their kingside before grabbing space in the center, which allows us to play 3...Bf5, the so-called Reverse London. Instead of placing a pawn in the center, White opts for 1.Nf3, the third most popular opening move in chess. White doesn’t want to commit to any specific opening yet and can transpose to many different playing styles on move 2. We respond naturally with 1...d5, putting a pawn in the center.} 1...d5 2.g3 {White continues with 2.g3, delaying the moves d4 and c4, but developing the kingside quickly. We’ll develop naturally, 2...Nf6.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg2 {White has developed rapidly and is already prepared to castle. However, having placed no pawns in the center of the board, White allows us to develop our bishop before playing ...e6! The bishop will be very active on the f5-square, instead of being blocked in by our own pawns.} 3...Bf5 4.O-O {White has chosen the calmer route and castles without creating tension in the center. This simply poses no problems for us; we will open up the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop, develop the other knight, sometimes be even more solid with ...c6, and castle! Just like 3.e3 or 4.e3 is played after Bf4 in the London, here we play 4...e6, connecting the pawns after taking the bishop out.} 4...e6 5.d3 {5.d3 is the most popular move and the one worth preparing against. In the London, the bishop usually goes to d3 and aims at h7. But when there’s a pawn on g6, the bishop goes to e2. In the Reverse London, we would love for our dark-squared bishop to have a long diagonal as well. But White has a pawn on g3, so there’s no use playing ...Bd6, we instead play 5...Be7.} 5...Be7 6.Nbd2 {6.Nbd2 is the most common idea after 5.d3. The point is to support the e4- and c4-pawn breaks. Here I recommend that, before castling, we create a safe square for our bishop with 6...h6. The light-squared bishop is our “London bishop”, and we should take care of it as much as we can.} 6...h6 7.c4 {Some people decide on the c4-pawn break only after Nbd2. This is a logical move, in a way, because the d2-knight supports the c4 expansion. We reinforce d5 with our c-pawn.} 7...c6 8.cxd5 {If White takes… We still recapture with the e-pawn to keep the c-file closed.} 8...exd5 9.Qb3 {But there’s a slight nuance that works in our favor. Since the knight is on d2, we can play 8...Qb6. This move is possible because Be3 cannot be played. We can defend b7 and propose a queen trade.} 9...Qb6 10.Qxb6 {And if White trades, as they often do...} 10...axb6 {Our rook becomes active on the semi-open a-file. We'll continue with ...c5, to control the d4- and b4-squares, and with ...Nc6 to develop. We'll also castle and play ...Rfd8. Our pieces have better activity.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: White Plays The Reti (1.Nf3) / Study 1: Reverse London / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/3f0e7f37-7c9b-4aaf-89f2-68f345170185/studies/c31ac611-6ff4-4889-98da-a457557f3848/lines"]

1.Nf3 {Greetings! Welcome to the very last chapter of our QGD course for Black, Chapter 11! In this chapter, we will cover the lines after 1.Nf3, the Réti Opening by White. The first study deals with White developing their kingside before grabbing space in the center, which allows us to play 3...Bf5, the so-called Reverse London. Instead of placing a pawn in the center, White opts for 1.Nf3, the third most popular opening move in chess. White doesn’t want to commit to any specific opening yet and can transpose to many different playing styles on move 2. We respond naturally with 1...d5, putting a pawn in the center.} 1...d5 2.g3 {White continues with 2.g3, delaying the moves d4 and c4, but developing the kingside quickly. We’ll develop naturally, 2...Nf6.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg2 {White has developed rapidly and is already prepared to castle. However, having placed no pawns in the center of the board, White allows us to develop our bishop before playing ...e6! The bishop will be very active on the f5-square, instead of being blocked in by our own pawns.} 3...Bf5 4.O-O {White has chosen the calmer route and castles without creating tension in the center. This simply poses no problems for us; we will open up the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop, develop the other knight, sometimes be even more solid with ...c6, and castle! Just like 3.e3 or 4.e3 is played after Bf4 in the London, here we play 4...e6, connecting the pawns after taking the bishop out.} 4...e6 5.d3 {5.d3 is the most popular move and the one worth preparing against. In the London, the bishop usually goes to d3 and aims at h7. But when there’s a pawn on g6, the bishop goes to e2. In the Reverse London, we would love for our dark-squared bishop to have a long diagonal as well. But White has a pawn on g3, so there’s no use playing ...Bd6, we instead play 5...Be7.} 5...Be7 6.c4 {White could undermine our d5-pawn with the move c4. With 6...c6, we create the strong “diamond” pawn structure.} 6...c6 7.cxd5 {White can always insert the trade on d5. And we should recapture with the e-pawn to keep the c-file closed. The reason for this is that after a move like Qb3, we will defend our b7-pawn by placing our queen on the c-file, and we don’t want a rook coming to c1 to drive us away.} 7...exd5 8.Qb3 {So if 8.Qb3… We just defend b7 with 8...Qc8.} 8...Qc8 9.Nc3 {White will usually develop.} 9...Na6 {But our king will get castled soon enough. White may go for Re1 to play e4. A good plan for Black is to play ...Rd8, since after e4, we can play ...dxe4 and open up the d-file for our rook.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: White Plays The Reti (1.Nf3) / Study 1: Reverse London / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/3f0e7f37-7c9b-4aaf-89f2-68f345170185/studies/c31ac611-6ff4-4889-98da-a457557f3848/lines"]

1.Nf3 {Greetings! Welcome to the very last chapter of our QGD course for Black, Chapter 11! In this chapter, we will cover the lines after 1.Nf3, the Réti Opening by White. The first study deals with White developing their kingside before grabbing space in the center, which allows us to play 3...Bf5, the so-called Reverse London. Instead of placing a pawn in the center, White opts for 1.Nf3, the third most popular opening move in chess. White doesn’t want to commit to any specific opening yet and can transpose to many different playing styles on move 2. We respond naturally with 1...d5, putting a pawn in the center.} 1...d5 2.g3 {White continues with 2.g3, delaying the moves d4 and c4, but developing the kingside quickly. We’ll develop naturally, 2...Nf6.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg2 {White has developed rapidly and is already prepared to castle. However, having placed no pawns in the center of the board, White allows us to develop our bishop before playing ...e6! The bishop will be very active on the f5-square, instead of being blocked in by our own pawns.} 3...Bf5 4.c4 {White creates tension in the center! We have to defend the d5-pawn. 4...e6 is the right move, natural and solid, supporting d5. We also open up the diagonal for our dark-squared bishop.} 4...e6 5.Qb3 {5.Qb3 by White activates the queen and puts pressure on the weak points in our position. 5...Qc8 is an important move to remember! Although we relinquish some control of the d5-square, 5...Qc8 is the best defensive move against the pressure on b7.} 5...Qc8 6.cxd5 {White tries to use the “absence” of our queen from d8 to take on the center! We take back. Now our queen and bishop form a battery on the h3–c8 diagonal, with possible ideas of ...Bh3 in the future, trading for White’s useful light-squared bishop and possibly starting an attack.} 6...exd5 7.Nc3 {The knight on c3 puts even more pressure on the d5-square! 7...c6 is forced now, strengthening the center with a pawn. Now we’ve defended everything in the position and are ready to develop and castle!} 7...c6 8.d3 {8.d3 by White brings the bishop on c1 to life. In the future, White might try to go for the e4 break. For now, we simply develop, 8...Be7.} 8...Be7 9.O-O {White castles, with ideas of activating the rook on the central e-file. We get our king to safety too.} 9...O-O 10.Re1 {Once White has activated the rook and prepares e4....} 10...Na6 {A useful resource by us is 10...Na6. We develop the knight to the edge of the board with the simple plan of placing it on c5. From there it will target White's queen and add protection to the critical e4 square. Next, we might have plans of ...Re1, ...Rb1 (to defend our b-pawn) and going for the mentioned ...Bh3 trade!} *

[Event "Chapter 11: White Plays The Reti (1.Nf3) / Study 2: White Plays An Early c4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/3f0e7f37-7c9b-4aaf-89f2-68f345170185/studies/c9d7d486-9bab-4496-b851-e2cc56aec81c/lines"]

1.Nf3 {In the second study of Chapter 11, we will again take a look at the Réti, but this time with White choosing to play c4 on move 3! This is a unique move order we haven’t seen yet. White opens with the Réti, developing the knight to f3. We respond by controlling the center with a pawn.} 1...d5 2.g3 {2.g3 prepares the kingside fianchetto for White. We develop the knight to its natural f6 square.} 2...Nf6 3.c4 {Instead of occupying the center directly with pawns, White puts pressure on it with a flank pawn! However, we still continue with our usual 3...dxc4 plan. We want to play ...a6 and prepare the possible ...b5 in the future. As in the previous chapters, the c4-pawn restricts the vital d- and b-pawns from moving forward.} 3...dxc4 4.Bg2 {The main line is 4.Bg2, with White naturally continuing development. We also continue with our idea, 4...a6. However, with this move order, in the main line playing ...b5 might be too dangerous. With White lacking control of the center, we might instead resort to something like ...e5 to grab more central squares and coordinate our pieces more easily.} 4...a6 5.O-O {Once White has finished kingside development and castled… First we bring the knight out to c6, developing and supporting the ...e5 push.} 5...Nc6 6.Na3 {Another path to our c4-pawn might be the common 6.Na3 move by White. That allows the ...b5 push. Although we will open the diagonal for White’s bishop on g2, they have to be very careful, as you will see.} 6...b5 7.Ne5 {White thinks they’ve tricked us with 7.Ne5, attacking our pinned knight on c6 twice - but this is a big mistake! We capture the free knight and sacrifice our rook! Our idea is that once the bishop captures on a8, we can trap it!} 7...Nxe5 8.Bxa8 {With no better option now, White accepts the sacrifice. But 8...c6 completely cuts off the bishop’s mobility! Now all that’s left is to take it.} 8...c6 9.f4 {White lashes out with 9.f4, trying to deflect the knight from defending the c6-pawn. But that simply doesn’t work. The queen delivers check and defends c6 at the same time. Now our knight is free to move!} 9...Qb6+ 10.Kg2 {The king gets out of check...} 10...Ng6 {...And we simply bring the knight back to the kingside. Next, we will play ...Bd7 to defend the c6-pawn before attacking the imprisoned bishop with ...Qa7 and finally capturing it. We are winning. Bye, bye, bishop!} *

[Event "Chapter 11: White Plays The Reti (1.Nf3) / Study 2: White Plays An Early c4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/3f0e7f37-7c9b-4aaf-89f2-68f345170185/studies/c9d7d486-9bab-4496-b851-e2cc56aec81c/lines"]

1.Nf3 {In the second study of Chapter 11, we will again take a look at the Réti, but this time with White choosing to play c4 on move 3! This is a unique move order we haven’t seen yet. White opens with the Réti, developing the knight to f3. We respond by controlling the center with a pawn.} 1...d5 2.g3 {2.g3 prepares the kingside fianchetto for White. We develop the knight to its natural f6 square.} 2...Nf6 3.c4 {Instead of occupying the center directly with pawns, White puts pressure on it with a flank pawn! However, we still continue with our usual 3...dxc4 plan. We want to play ...a6 and prepare the possible ...b5 in the future. As in the previous chapters, the c4-pawn restricts the vital d- and b-pawns from moving forward.} 3...dxc4 4.Qa4+ {Instead of developing with 4.Bg2, White might choose to go after our pawn immediately with 4.Qa4+, before we get time to play ...a6. We simply block with 4...Nc6. As you will see in the main line as well, we want to play ...e5 and grab space in the center. Our knight supports that square.} 4...Nc6 5.Ne5 {Instead of taking the pawn, White might try to exploit the pin on c6 with 5.Ne5. But this is a horrible blunder by White. 5...Qd5 is the key move to remember! We fork the knight on e5 and the rook on h1, while also adding protection to our knight on c6.} 5...Qd5 6.Nxc6 {White takes on c6, but that doesn’t save them. We simply capture the rook on h1, unafraid of possible discovered attacks on c6 by White.} 6...Qxh1 7.Ne5+ {With no better option, the White knight retreats to e5, with a discovered check from the queen.} 7...c6 {But a simple 7...c6 crushes all of White's hopes. Our queen from h1 protects the c6 square, so White can't even try to sacrifice the knight with Nxc6. We are crushing here! White is lost.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: White Plays The Reti (1.Nf3) / Study 2: White Plays An Early c4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/3f0e7f37-7c9b-4aaf-89f2-68f345170185/studies/c9d7d486-9bab-4496-b851-e2cc56aec81c/lines"]

1.Nf3 {In the second study of Chapter 11, we will again take a look at the Réti, but this time with White choosing to play c4 on move 3! This is a unique move order we haven’t seen yet. White opens with the Réti, developing the knight to f3. We respond by controlling the center with a pawn.} 1...d5 2.g3 {2.g3 prepares the kingside fianchetto for White. We develop the knight to its natural f6 square.} 2...Nf6 3.c4 {Instead of occupying the center directly with pawns, White puts pressure on it with a flank pawn! However, we still continue with our usual 3...dxc4 plan. We want to play ...a6 and prepare the possible ...b5 in the future. As in the previous chapters, the c4-pawn restricts the vital d- and b-pawns from moving forward.} 3...dxc4 4.Bg2 {The main line is 4.Bg2, with White naturally continuing development. We also continue with our idea, 4...a6. However, with this move order, in the main line playing ...b5 might be too dangerous. With White lacking control of the center, we might instead resort to something like ...e5 to grab more central squares and coordinate our pieces more easily.} 4...a6 5.O-O {Once White has finished kingside development and castled… First we bring the knight out to c6, developing and supporting the ...e5 push.} 5...Nc6 6.Qc2 {White develops the queen and puts pressure on our pawn on c4. We can let them take it! 6...e5 grabs crucial space in the center and prepares to develop our pieces with tempo once White takes the pawn on c4.} 6...e5 7.Qxc4 {Naturally, the queen captures. And our light-squared bishop comes out to e6 with an attack on the queen! Notice how in the previous chapters we usually played ...b5 and developed our bishop to b7. Since White has chosen this move order, we instead grab central space and activate the bishop on e6!} 7...Be6 8.Qc2 {The queen moves out of the way. White has already moved the queen three times in the opening! We use White’s slow development to activate our own queen, 8...Qd7. Since we’ve rapidly developed our queenside pieces, we will make use of that and castle queenside.} 8...Qd7 9.Nc3 {White develops the knight to c3...} 9...O-O-O {...And we castle. This different approach by us and quick queenside castling allows us to launch an attack on the kingside with moves like ...h5! Also, our queen and bishop make a battery that can be used for an h3 trade, as we've seen in the previous study.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: White Plays The Reti (1.Nf3) / Study 2: White Plays An Early c4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/3f0e7f37-7c9b-4aaf-89f2-68f345170185/studies/c9d7d486-9bab-4496-b851-e2cc56aec81c/lines"]

1.Nf3 {In the second study of Chapter 11, we will again take a look at the Réti, but this time with White choosing to play c4 on move 3! This is a unique move order we haven’t seen yet. White opens with the Réti, developing the knight to f3. We respond by controlling the center with a pawn.} 1...d5 2.g3 {2.g3 prepares the kingside fianchetto for White. We develop the knight to its natural f6 square.} 2...Nf6 3.c4 {Instead of occupying the center directly with pawns, White puts pressure on it with a flank pawn! However, we still continue with our usual 3...dxc4 plan. We want to play ...a6 and prepare the possible ...b5 in the future. As in the previous chapters, the c4-pawn restricts the vital d- and b-pawns from moving forward.} 3...dxc4 4.Bg2 {The main line is 4.Bg2, with White naturally continuing development. We also continue with our idea, 4...a6. However, with this move order, in the main line playing ...b5 might be too dangerous. With White lacking control of the center, we might instead resort to something like ...e5 to grab more central squares and coordinate our pieces more easily.} 4...a6 5.O-O {Once White has finished kingside development and castled… First we bring the knight out to c6, developing and supporting the ...e5 push.} 5...Nc6 6.Na3 {Another path to our c4-pawn might be the common 6.Na3 move by White. That allows the ...b5 push. Although we will open the diagonal for White’s bishop on g2, they have to be very careful, as you will see.} 6...b5 7.b3 {7.b3 undermines our queenside pawn structure. We capture, as we don’t want to allow bxc4.} 7...cxb3 8.axb3 {White recaptures with the pawn, opening the a-file for the rook with some nasty intentions. Now Nxb5 is possible, since our a6-pawn is pinned to our rook on a8! Also, White might have various Ne5 ideas.} 8...Rb8 {We simply move out of the way, neutralizing both threats. Next, we'll activate our bishop to b7, play ...e6 and ...Be7 or ...Bd6 and castle. Equal position!} *

[Event "Chapter 11: White Plays The Reti (1.Nf3) / Study 2: White Plays An Early c4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/3f0e7f37-7c9b-4aaf-89f2-68f345170185/studies/c9d7d486-9bab-4496-b851-e2cc56aec81c/lines"]

1.Nf3 {In the second study of Chapter 11, we will again take a look at the Réti, but this time with White choosing to play c4 on move 3! This is a unique move order we haven’t seen yet. White opens with the Réti, developing the knight to f3. We respond by controlling the center with a pawn.} 1...d5 2.g3 {2.g3 prepares the kingside fianchetto for White. We develop the knight to its natural f6 square.} 2...Nf6 3.c4 {Instead of occupying the center directly with pawns, White puts pressure on it with a flank pawn! However, we still continue with our usual 3...dxc4 plan. We want to play ...a6 and prepare the possible ...b5 in the future. As in the previous chapters, the c4-pawn restricts the vital d- and b-pawns from moving forward.} 3...dxc4 4.Bg2 {The main line is 4.Bg2, with White naturally continuing development. We also continue with our idea, 4...a6. However, with this move order, in the main line playing ...b5 might be too dangerous. With White lacking control of the center, we might instead resort to something like ...e5 to grab more central squares and coordinate our pieces more easily.} 4...a6 5.O-O {Once White has finished kingside development and castled… First we bring the knight out to c6, developing and supporting the ...e5 push.} 5...Nc6 6.Qa4 {You might wonder why White doesn't activate the queen on a4, instead of attacking the pawn from c2.} 6...b5 {Because that gives us exactly what we want! 6...b5 protects our c4-pawn and attacks the queen at the same time. Now we will easily develop to b7 and enjoy an already slightly-better position. Definitely an inaccuracy by White!} *

[Event "Chapter 11: White Plays The Reti (1.Nf3) / Study 2: White Plays An Early c4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/3f0e7f37-7c9b-4aaf-89f2-68f345170185/studies/c9d7d486-9bab-4496-b851-e2cc56aec81c/lines"]

1.Nf3 {In the second study of Chapter 11, we will again take a look at the Réti, but this time with White choosing to play c4 on move 3! This is a unique move order we haven’t seen yet. White opens with the Réti, developing the knight to f3. We respond by controlling the center with a pawn.} 1...d5 2.g3 {2.g3 prepares the kingside fianchetto for White. We develop the knight to its natural f6 square.} 2...Nf6 3.c4 {Instead of occupying the center directly with pawns, White puts pressure on it with a flank pawn! However, we still continue with our usual 3...dxc4 plan. We want to play ...a6 and prepare the possible ...b5 in the future. As in the previous chapters, the c4-pawn restricts the vital d- and b-pawns from moving forward.} 3...dxc4 4.Qa4+ {Instead of developing with 4.Bg2, White might choose to go after our pawn immediately with 4.Qa4+, before we get time to play ...a6. We simply block with 4...Nc6. As you will see in the main line as well, we want to play ...e5 and grab space in the center. Our knight supports that square.} 4...Nc6 5.Qxc4 {White takes the pawn… And we continue with our plan of controlling the center, 5...e5.} 5...e5 6.Bg2 {A normal developing move by White, fianchettoing their light-squared bishop. Again, as in the main line, we develop the light-squared bishop to e6 instead of b7, with tempo!} 6...Be6 7.Qa4 {The queen moves back to a4, pinning our knight and creating the threat of Nxe5. That’s why 7...Bd6 develops and protects the e5-pawn.} 7...Bd6 8.O-O {White castles...} 8...O-O {...And so do we. We are developed and have achieved everything we wanted out of the opening! The position is still equal, however, with a lot of chess to be played.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: White Plays The Reti (1.Nf3) / Study 2: White Plays An Early c4 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/3f0e7f37-7c9b-4aaf-89f2-68f345170185/studies/c9d7d486-9bab-4496-b851-e2cc56aec81c/lines"]

1.Nf3 {In the second study of Chapter 11, we will again take a look at the Réti, but this time with White choosing to play c4 on move 3! This is a unique move order we haven’t seen yet. White opens with the Réti, developing the knight to f3. We respond by controlling the center with a pawn.} 1...d5 2.g3 {2.g3 prepares the kingside fianchetto for White. We develop the knight to its natural f6 square.} 2...Nf6 3.c4 {Instead of occupying the center directly with pawns, White puts pressure on it with a flank pawn! However, we still continue with our usual 3...dxc4 plan. We want to play ...a6 and prepare the possible ...b5 in the future. As in the previous chapters, the c4-pawn restricts the vital d- and b-pawns from moving forward.} 3...dxc4 4.Qa4+ {Instead of developing with 4.Bg2, White might choose to go after our pawn immediately with 4.Qa4+, before we get time to play ...a6. We simply block with 4...Nc6. As you will see in the main line as well, we want to play ...e5 and grab space in the center. Our knight supports that square.} 4...Nc6 5.Bg2 {Another possibility for White on move 5 is to continue developing with Bg2. Since our knight on c6 is pinned, we can’t play ...e5 yet. That’s why we first develop the light-squared bishop to d7, unpinning.} 5...Bd7 6.Qxc4 {Now White takes on c4, also getting the queen out of the way of our bishop… But that gives us time for our central 6...e5 push!} 6...e5 7.O-O {White brings the king to safety. And we simply develop, 7...Be7, getting ready to castle our king as well.} 7...Be7 8.Nc3 {White continues developing.} 8...O-O {And we simply castle and have reached a pleasant position. Next, we will try to move our bishop to e6 with a tempo on the queen, and then try to go for ...a6, preventing anything from coming to b5. In some positions in the longer run, we might try to follow up with ...Rb8 and ...b5.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: White Plays The Reti (1.Nf3) / Study 2: White Plays An Early c4 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68814342-f681-4b82-bb08-acf239720df8/chapters/3f0e7f37-7c9b-4aaf-89f2-68f345170185/studies/c9d7d486-9bab-4496-b851-e2cc56aec81c/lines"]

1.Nf3 {In the second study of Chapter 11, we will again take a look at the Réti, but this time with White choosing to play c4 on move 3! This is a unique move order we haven’t seen yet. White opens with the Réti, developing the knight to f3. We respond by controlling the center with a pawn.} 1...d5 2.g3 {2.g3 prepares the kingside fianchetto for White. We develop the knight to its natural f6 square.} 2...Nf6 3.c4 {Instead of occupying the center directly with pawns, White puts pressure on it with a flank pawn! However, we still continue with our usual 3...dxc4 plan. We want to play ...a6 and prepare the possible ...b5 in the future. As in the previous chapters, the c4-pawn restricts the vital d- and b-pawns from moving forward.} 3...dxc4 4.Qa4+ {Instead of developing with 4.Bg2, White might choose to go after our pawn immediately with 4.Qa4+, before we get time to play ...a6. We simply block with 4...Nc6. As you will see in the main line as well, we want to play ...e5 and grab space in the center. Our knight supports that square.} 4...Nc6 5.Bg2 {Another possibility for White on move 5 is to continue developing with Bg2. Since our knight on c6 is pinned, we can’t play ...e5 yet. That’s why we first develop the light-squared bishop to d7, unpinning.} 5...Bd7 6.Qxc4 {Now White takes on c4, also getting the queen out of the way of our bishop… But that gives us time for our central 6...e5 push!} 6...e5 7.O-O {White brings the king to safety. And we simply develop, 7...Be7, getting ready to castle our king as well.} 7...Be7 8.d4 {A sharper variation for White might be the quick d4, counter-attacking in the center and trying to open up the board before we get to castle. We have to react now! As we don’t want White to capture, we do.} 8...exd4 9.Nxd4 {The knight recaptures, opening up some pressure towards our c6-knight. That’s why we trade once again…} 9...Nxd4 10.Qxd4 {And White recaptures, centralizing the queen. Now our b7-pawn is hanging. So let’s defend it by pushing our c-pawn! But we’ve left ourselves vulnerable to some pressure on the d-file by White.} 10...c6 11.Rd1 {The rook is activated and controls the d-file together with the queen on d4. However, there are no concrete threats yet, so we just castle.} 11...O-O 12.Bg5 {12.Bg5 is a dangerous move! White targets some weak points in our position. The threat is Bxf6 followed by Qxd7. We need to be accurate!} 12...Be6 {The light-squared bishop is out of the way and the dark-squared bishop is ready to capture on f6 if needed! Also, our queen is soundly defended on d8. We've evaded all problems and have equalized, with plans of kicking out the bishop from g5, centralizing rooks after trading queens, and in the longer run placing our bishop on d5, trying to trade for White's bishop on g2. If White doesn't trade queens, Qb6, aiming at b2, is strong.} *